{"version":1,"lang":"en","places":{"Q7296":{"t":"Africa's rooftop, a snowy island above the plains","s":"Kilimanjaro belongs to no range: it rises alone, a broad dark volcano swelling out of the savanna. Its summit, Uhuru Peak on the Kibo cone, is 5,895 m high — the top of Africa, yet still about five kilometres below you. Look for the white rim of ice on Kibo, shrinking year by year but still there, just three degrees south of the equator.","w":"A vast lone dome with a flat, white-streaked top and the jagged Mawenzi peak on its east flank, near the Kenya border.","f":["Kilimanjaro is built from three volcanic cones: Kibo, Mawenzi and Shira.","It is the highest free-standing mountain in the world, rising about 4,900 m above the plains at its base.","Its ice fields have shrunk by more than 80% since the early 20th century."],"q":{"q":"Which of Kilimanjaro's three volcanic cones holds its summit?","a":"Kibo","x":["Mawenzi","Shira"]}},"Q39231":{"t":"Japan's lone volcano, white-capped and nearly symmetrical","s":"On a clear day Fuji is unmistakable: one broad, even cone standing by itself west of Tokyo, its upper slopes white from autumn to spring. It last erupted in 1707. The summit, 3,776 m, sits roughly seven kilometres below you, and in summer the snow shrinks to thin streaks in the gullies, leaving a dark, ash-coloured mountain.","w":"A lone snow-topped cone about 100 km south-west of Tokyo, with small lakes strung along its northern foot.","f":["The five lakes around Fuji's northern foot formed where old lava flows dammed the valleys.","The 1707 eruption blew out a side crater on the south-east flank that breaks the cone's symmetry.","Fuji became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2013 — as a cultural site, not a natural one."],"q":{"q":"How many lakes make up the famous group at Fuji's northern foot?","a":"Five","x":["Three","Seven"]}},"Q513":{"t":"The world's highest point, only about 2 km below you","s":"Everest rarely looks tallest from the air: it hides among giant neighbours, a dark pyramid often trailing a plume of snow blown east by the jet stream. Its summit, 8,848 m, is only about two kilometres below cruising height. The rock at the very top is limestone that formed on an ancient sea floor and was lifted as India pushed into Asia.","w":"A dark three-sided pyramid, often with a snow plume, rising above the glaciers on the Nepal–Tibet border.","f":["Tibetans call it Chomolungma; in Nepal it is Sagarmatha.","Tenzing Norgay and Edmund Hillary first reached the summit on 29 May 1953.","The English name, adopted in 1865, honours George Everest, a British surveyor-general of India."],"q":{"q":"Who reached the summit of Everest with Tenzing Norgay in 1953?","a":"Edmund Hillary","x":["George Mallory","Reinhold Messner"]}},"Q43512":{"t":"Earth's second summit, a pyramid of the Karakoram","s":"The name K2 is just a surveyor's label — peak 2 on a Karakoram list drawn up in 1856, when no widely used local name was found. It is the second-highest mountain on Earth, 8,611 m, a steep, almost perfect pyramid ringed by rivers of ice. Look for a sharp summit at the head of a long glacier, far from any road or town.","w":"A lone sharp pyramid at the head of broad glacier tongues, on the border crest of the Karakoram.","f":["The Italians Lino Lacedelli and Achille Compagnoni made the first ascent in 1954.","The first winter ascent came only in January 2021, by a team of Nepali climbers.","Its pyramid rises more than 3,000 m above the glaciers at its foot."],"q":{"q":"What does the 'K' in K2 stand for?","a":"Karakoram","x":["Kashmir","Kunlun"]}},"Q1374":{"t":"The Alps' sharpest tooth, facing all four compass points","s":"Even from cruise height the Matterhorn looks carved: a hooked pyramid whose four faces point almost exactly north, south, east and west. It stands on the Swiss–Italian border above Zermatt, 4,478 m high and roughly six and a half kilometres below you. On a sunny morning its east face is lit while the north face stays in shadow, so the peak looks sliced in two.","w":"A lone, hooked rock pyramid among glaciers, west of the big Monte Rosa massif on the Swiss–Italian border.","f":["Edward Whymper's party made the first ascent on 14 July 1865.","Italians call it Monte Cervino; in French it is Mont Cervin.","A summit ridge about 100 m long links two tops, a Swiss one and an Italian one."],"q":{"q":"From which Swiss village is the Matterhorn most often climbed?","a":"Zermatt","x":["Grindelwald","Saas-Fee"]}},"ne-pk-1159105947":{"t":"The volcano that buried Pompeii, still watching Naples","s":"Look for a cone inside a broken ring: Vesuvius grew within the collapsed crater of an older volcano, Monte Somma, whose ridge wraps around its north side. Naples spreads right up to its lower slopes, a dense grey carpet along the bay. On a clear day the summit crater, 1,281 m high, shows as a dark hole. Vesuvius has been quiet since 1944, but it is watched around the clock.","w":"A dark two-humped volcano on the Bay of Naples, with the city spread along the shore to its north-west.","f":["In AD 79 its ash buried Pompeii and Herculaneum, preserving the Roman towns for archaeologists.","It is the only volcano on mainland Europe to have erupted in the last hundred years.","Its observatory, founded in 1841, is the oldest volcano observatory in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which Roman town, buried in AD 79, lies at the foot of Vesuvius?","a":"Pompeii","x":["Ostia","Paestum"]}},"Q583":{"t":"Western Europe's highest dome, white all year","s":"Mont Blanc is less a spike than a huge white shoulder: a broad dome of ice on a massif shared by France, Italy and Switzerland. At about 4,807 m it is the highest point of the Alps, yet still more than six kilometres below you. On its north side long glaciers pour down toward the valley of Chamonix; on the Italian side the walls drop steeply to Courmayeur.","w":"A broad, rounded white dome above deep valleys, with long glacier tongues reaching toward Chamonix on its north-west side.","f":["Jacques Balmat and Michel-Gabriel Paccard first reached the summit in 1786.","The summit is a cap of ice, and its height shifts by a metre or two from year to year.","The Mont Blanc road tunnel, 11.6 km long, runs beneath the massif between France and Italy."],"q":{"q":"In which year was Mont Blanc first climbed?","a":"1786","x":["1865","1741"]}},"ne-pk-1159107423":{"t":"Europe's restless giant, often trailing a plume","s":"Etna is the dark giant of eastern Sicily, a broad volcano whose summit, about 3,300 m high, often smokes. Its lower slopes are green with orchards and vineyards, the upper ones black with fresh lava, and in winter snow lies over both. Look for a thin plume drifting downwind: Etna erupts so often that its height changes every few years.","w":"A vast dark cone above Sicily's east coast, north of Catania, often with a steam plume and snow on top.","f":["Etna covers about 1,200 km², and its base is roughly 140 km around.","It is the highest active volcano in Europe outside the Caucasus.","Etna became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2013."],"q":{"q":"Which city lies at the southern foot of Etna?","a":"Catania","x":["Palermo","Messina"]}},"ne-gl-antarctic-ice-sheet":{"t":"Most of the world's ice, on one frozen continent","s":"If your route crosses Antarctica, the whiteness below is not snow on land but ice kilometres thick. The Antarctic Ice Sheet covers about 14 million km² and holds roughly 90% of all the ice on Earth. Its surface is so smooth and bright that the horizon can vanish; only nunataks — rocky peaks poking through the ice — break the white.","w":"An endless white surface with faint wind ripples; dark rock shows only near the coast and along mountain ranges.","f":["If it all melted, global sea level would rise by about 58 metres.","The ice is about two kilometres thick on average.","Its weight has pressed the bedrock beneath it down by hundreds of metres."],"q":{"q":"Roughly what share of Earth's ice lies in the Antarctic Ice Sheet?","a":"About 90%","x":["About 50%","About 70%"]}},"ne-gl-greenland-ice-sheet":{"t":"A 3-kilometre-thick lid on the world's largest island","s":"Crossing the North Atlantic, many flights pass over this white desert. The ice sheet covers about 80% of Greenland, some 1.7 million km², and rises into a dome more than 3,000 m high. Near the coast it breaks into crevassed outlet glaciers that snake down to fjords full of icebergs; in summer, bright blue meltwater lakes dot its western edge.","w":"Flat white expanse inland; at the edges, dark mountains, glacier tongues and fjords speckled with icebergs.","f":["If the whole ice sheet melted, sea level would rise by roughly 7 metres.","Its weight has pushed the bedrock in the centre of Greenland below sea level.","Ice cores drilled through it hold a climate record reaching back more than 100,000 years."],"q":{"q":"How much would sea level rise if Greenland's ice sheet melted?","a":"About 7 metres","x":["About 1 metre","About 60 metres"]}},"Q1001294":{"t":"The coldest high desert on the planet","s":"Around the South Pole the ice rises into a vast, gently sloping plateau, mostly around 3,000 m high and as flat as a frozen sea. It is a true desert: each year the snowfall adds up to only a few centimetres of water. Vostok Station, on this plateau, recorded −89.2 °C in 1983 — the lowest air temperature ever measured at the Earth's surface.","w":"A featureless white plain to the horizon, scored only by long wind-carved snow ridges called sastrugi.","f":["The South Pole itself lies on the plateau, about 2,835 m above sea level.","Antarctica's fierce coastal winds start here, as heavy cold air slides off the plateau toward the sea.","Its cold, dry, still air makes it one of the best places on Earth for telescopes."],"q":{"q":"What temperature did Vostok Station record in 1983?","a":"−89.2 °C","x":["−71.2 °C","−79.8 °C"]}},"Q165440":{"t":"The white mountain that once topped the world's list","s":"Dhaulagiri means 'white mountain' in Sanskrit, and from the air it earns the name: a massive snowy wall rising straight out of central Nepal. Its main summit, about 8,170 m, is the seventh-highest on Earth. Just east of it the Kali Gandaki river cuts through the Himalaya between Dhaulagiri and Annapurna, carving one of the deepest valleys on the planet.","w":"A huge snowy massif west of the deep Kali Gandaki gorge, with Annapurna's white ridges on the other side.","f":["After surveys in 1808 it was believed for some 30 years to be the highest mountain in the world.","A Swiss-Austrian expedition made the first ascent in 1960.","The Kali Gandaki gorge beside it was for centuries a trade route between Tibet and India."],"q":{"q":"Which river gorge separates Dhaulagiri from Annapurna?","a":"Kali Gandaki","x":["Arun","Trisuli"]}},"Q130736":{"t":"The 'naked mountain' at the western end of the Himalaya","s":"Nanga Parbat stands apart: the westernmost 8,000-metre peak of the Himalaya, with the Indus curving around its foot. Its name means 'naked mountain', perhaps for walls too steep to hold snow. The Rupal Face on its south side rises about 4,600 m from valley to summit, one of the tallest mountain walls on Earth. The top, 8,125 m, is less than three kilometres below you.","w":"An isolated giant massif south of a bend in the Indus, with a sheer southern wall and glaciers spilling north.","f":["Hermann Buhl made the first ascent in 1953, reaching the summit alone.","It is the ninth-highest mountain on Earth.","The Indus flows past its northern flank at around 1,100 m — seven kilometres below the summit."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Nanga Parbat mean?","a":"Naked mountain","x":["White mountain","Mother of snows"]}},"Q82019":{"t":"Five treasures of snow above Sikkim and Nepal","s":"Kangchenjunga is not one summit but a crowded crown of them, five main tops on a single massif. At 8,586 m it is the third-highest mountain on Earth, and until the 1850s it was thought to be the highest. Look for a broad white wall towering over the green hills of Darjeeling and Sikkim; at sunrise its snow turns pink long before the valleys wake.","w":"A massive white massif rising above green foothills, north of Darjeeling on the Nepal–India border.","f":["Its name is usually translated from Tibetan as 'Five Treasures of the Snow'.","The first climbers, in 1955, stopped just short of the top out of respect for local beliefs.","From the hill town of Darjeeling, about 75 km away, it fills the northern horizon."],"q":{"q":"Where does Kangchenjunga rank among the world's highest mountains?","a":"Third","x":["Second","Fifth"]}},"Q604846":{"t":"A red tableland of some of Earth's oldest rock","s":"For hours on a flight across Australia the ground barely changes: rust-red sand, pale salt lakes and dark scrub, a few hundred metres above sea level. This is the Western Plateau, spread over most of the western two-thirds of the continent. Its bedrock is among the oldest on Earth, and the deserts on top — the Gibson, the Great Sandy, the Great Victoria — are combed with long dune lines.","w":"Endless red and ochre ground streaked with parallel dunes and dotted with white salt lakes that shine like mirrors.","f":["Zircon crystals from the Jack Hills in Western Australia are about 4.4 billion years old, the oldest known pieces of Earth.","Its dunes often run in parallel lines for tens of kilometres, aligned with the prevailing winds.","Salt lakes such as Lake Carnegie hold water only after rare heavy rains."],"q":{"q":"What record do zircons from Western Australia's Jack Hills hold?","a":"Oldest known Earth minerals","x":["Largest gold nuggets","Most radioactive rocks"]}},"Q179842":{"t":"The roof of the world, averaging over 4,500 m","s":"Below you lies the highest large plateau on Earth, averaging more than 4,500 m and covering about 2.5 million km². From cruise it looks like another planet: brown and ochre plains, turquoise lakes with salt-white shores, snowy ranges ruled across it. Asia's great rivers — the Yangtze, Yellow, Mekong, Indus and Brahmaputra among them — begin up here.","w":"Bare brown highland with scattered turquoise lakes and white ridges; the Himalaya forms its sharp southern rim.","f":["It holds so much glacier ice that it is often called the Third Pole.","Lhasa, in the south of the plateau, lies at about 3,650 m.","The plateau is still rising as India keeps pushing north into Asia."],"q":{"q":"Which nickname does the Tibetan Plateau get for its glaciers?","a":"The Third Pole","x":["The White Continent","The Frozen Crown"]}},"ne-gl-flade-isblink":{"t":"Greenland's biggest ice cap that stands on its own","s":"Near Greenland's north-eastern tip a separate dome of ice sits apart from the main ice sheet: Flade Isblink, some 8,000 km² of white. Its Danish name means roughly 'flat iceblink' — the glow that ice casts on low clouds. The ice is up to about 600 m thick, and parts of its edge surge forward from time to time, pushing ice out to sea.","w":"A smooth, low white dome on a peninsula of north-east Greenland, cut off from the main ice sheet by bare land.","f":["It is the largest ice cap in Greenland that is not joined to the main ice sheet.","Station Nord, the only inhabited place in the area, lies just to its north-west.","The whole area is part of Northeast Greenland National Park, the largest national park in the world."],"q":{"q":"What does the word 'iceblink' describe?","a":"The glow of ice on clouds","x":["A crack in sea ice","A glacier's calving wall"]}},"Q163758":{"t":"The highest point of the frozen continent","s":"Vinson is the summit of Antarctica, 4,892 m, a long snowy massif in the Sentinel Range of the Ellsworth Mountains, some 1,300 km from the South Pole. US Navy aircraft spotted it in 1958, and climbers first reached the top in 1966. From above the range looks like a spine of dark rock and ice breaking out of the ice sheet, with glaciers flowing away on both sides.","w":"A long, dark rock-and-ice ridge rising sharply above the flat ice sheet in the Ellsworth Mountains.","f":["It is named after Carl Vinson, a US congressman who backed Antarctic exploration.","An American expedition made the first ascent in December 1966.","Climbers fly in to a blue-ice runway at Union Glacier to start their approach."],"q":{"q":"In which mountains does Vinson Massif rise?","a":"Ellsworth Mountains","x":["Transantarctic Mountains","Prince Charles Mountains"]}},"Q1537198":{"t":"Twin summits of ice above Ladakh's Suru valley","s":"Nun and its twin, Kun, form the Nun Kun massif, a knot of ice in the western Himalaya of Ladakh. Nun is the higher of the pair at 7,135 m, only about four kilometres below you. Between the two tops lies a snowy plateau some 4 km long, and from above it looks like a white saddle slung between two peaks. Glaciers pour off both sides toward the Suru valley.","w":"Two white summits joined by a snowy plateau, with long glaciers running down to a green valley at their foot.","f":["Nun was first climbed in 1953 by Claude Kogan and Pierre Vittoz of a French–Swiss expedition.","Kun, the lower twin, is a little over 7,000 m high.","The Parkachik Glacier flows off the massif almost down to the road in the Suru valley."],"q":{"q":"What is the name of Nun's twin summit?","a":"Kun","x":["Stok Kangri","Kang Yatse"]}},"Q811064":{"t":"The crown of the Karakoram's jagged western wall","s":"Batura Sar tops a long, serrated wall of ice in the far west of the Karakoram, the Batura Muztagh. At 7,795 m it is the highest point of that wall, yet it is little known outside climbing circles. Below it lies one of the longest glaciers outside the polar regions, the Batura: a grey-and-white river of ice that ends right beside the Karakoram Highway in the Hunza valley.","w":"A long, serrated ice ridge north-west of the Hunza valley, with a broad glacier sliding east toward the river.","f":["The Batura Glacier is about 57 km long.","Muztagh means 'ice mountain' in Turkic languages.","The Karakoram Highway, one of the highest paved roads in the world, passes the glacier's snout."],"q":{"q":"Which famous road passes the snout of the Batura Glacier?","a":"Karakoram Highway","x":["Pamir Highway","Grand Trunk Road"]}},"Q6654097":{"t":"The Kunlun's highest peak, lost in a cold desert","s":"This is one of the emptiest corners of Asia. Liushi Shan, also called the Kunlun Goddess, is the highest peak of the Kunlun Mountains at 7,167 m, rising from bleak high desert on the border of Tibet and Xinjiang. From above, look for a cluster of white ice domes and glaciers in a sea of brown, wind-scoured ridges.","w":"A cluster of white ice domes on the northern edge of the Tibetan Plateau, surrounded by bare brown ridges.","f":["A Japanese university team made the first ascent in 1986.","In Chinese it is also known as Kunlun Nüshen Feng, 'Kunlun Goddess Peak'.","The Keriya River rises in this part of the Kunlun, flows north and vanishes into the Taklamakan Desert."],"q":{"q":"Liushi Shan is the highest peak of which mountain system?","a":"Kunlun Mountains","x":["Tian Shan","Karakoram"]}},"Q1050238":{"t":"Where the Himalaya ends and a great river turns","s":"Namcha Barwa marks the eastern end of the Himalaya, beside one of the strangest river bends on Earth: the Yarlung Tsangpo, flowing east across Tibet, swings around the mountain and plunges south through a gorge far deeper than the Grand Canyon. The peak is 7,782 m high, a steep white pyramid above dark, forested valleys, often wrapped in monsoon cloud.","w":"A steep white peak where the brown Tibetan plateau gives way to green, cloudy gorges; a river loops around its foot.","f":["It was the highest unclimbed peak in the world until a Chinese–Japanese team reached the top in 1992.","Gyala Peri, 7,294 m, rises only about 20 km away on the other side of the gorge.","Downstream from the gorge, the Yarlung Tsangpo becomes the Brahmaputra."],"q":{"q":"Which river bends sharply around Namcha Barwa?","a":"Yarlung Tsangpo","x":["Salween","Mekong"]}},"Q26794321":{"t":"A goddess inside a fortress of peaks","s":"Nanda Devi rises inside a natural fortress: a ring of mountains, broken only by a narrow gorge, encloses the high glacial basin known as the Sanctuary. The double-topped peak, 7,817 m, is the highest mountain lying entirely within India and is revered as the goddess Nanda. From above, look for the ring itself — a near-circle of white summits around a bowl of ice.","w":"A double-topped white peak inside a near-circle of snowy summits, in the Himalaya west of the Nepal border.","f":["Bill Tilman and Noel Odell made the first ascent in 1936.","Eric Shipton and Tilman were the first to force a way up the Rishi Ganga gorge into the Sanctuary, in 1934.","Nanda Devi National Park and the nearby Valley of Flowers form a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"What is the ring-walled basin around Nanda Devi called?","a":"The Sanctuary","x":["The Amphitheatre","The Citadel"]}},"Q41413":{"t":"The Pamirs' high point, a peak of three names","s":"For decades this was the highest point of the Soviet Union, first as Stalin Peak, then as Communism Peak. Since 1998 it has carried the name of Ismoil Somoni, a Samanid ruler of the 9th and 10th centuries. The summit, 7,495 m, crowns a massive white wall in the heart of the Pamirs, close to the Fedchenko Glacier, one of the great ice rivers of Asia.","w":"A massive white peak at a junction of ridges, west of the long, straight Fedchenko Glacier in the high Pamirs.","f":["Yevgeny Abalakov made the first ascent in 1933.","The Fedchenko Glacier nearby is about 77 km long, the longest outside the polar regions.","Tajikistan's currency, the somoni, is named after the same ruler."],"q":{"q":"What was the peak called from 1962 to 1998?","a":"Communism Peak","x":["Lenin Peak","Stalin Peak"]}},"Q1537157":{"t":"The easternmost 7,000-metre giant, above Sichuan","s":"Gongga, also known as Minya Konka, stands where the Tibetan highlands drop toward the Sichuan Basin. At 7,556 m it is the highest peak in Sichuan and the easternmost 7,000-metre summit on Earth. Look for a sharp white pyramid ringed by glaciers, with the deep Dadu River valley to the east — more than six kilometres of relief in a short distance.","w":"A sharp white pyramid with a skirt of glaciers, west of the deep Dadu River gorge in western Sichuan.","f":["An American team made the first ascent in 1932.","The Hailuogou Glacier on its eastern side reaches down into forest below 3,000 m.","Gongga is the highest point of the Daxue Shan, the 'Great Snowy Mountains'."],"q":{"q":"Which glacier descends from Gongga's east side into forest?","a":"Hailuogou","x":["Baltoro","Rongbuk"]}},"Q6362901":{"t":"A cloud-wrapped giant of the eastern Himalaya","s":"East of Bhutan the Himalaya grows wilder and wetter, and Kangto is one of its tallest summits, just over 7,000 m. It sits on the main crest, where the dry Tibetan uplands meet steep valleys draining south toward the Brahmaputra plains of Assam. Thick forest climbs its lower flanks, and monsoon cloud hides the peak for much of the summer, so a clear view is a rare prize.","w":"A white summit on the Himalayan crest north of the Brahmaputra plains, between dry brown highlands and forested valleys.","f":["Kangto remained unclimbed for decades; the first recorded ascent came only in 2025.","Its southern valleys drain toward the Kameng River, a tributary of the Brahmaputra.","The eastern Himalaya receives far more monsoon rain than the ranges around Everest."],"q":{"q":"Toward which great river do Kangto's southern valleys drain?","a":"Brahmaputra","x":["Ganges","Irrawaddy"]}},"Q1109058":{"t":"A mountain god guarding a turquoise lake","s":"Nyenchen Tanglha is the highest peak of a long range running across central Tibet north-west of Lhasa. Look for its white summit, 7,162 m, just south-east of Namtso, a huge salt lake of startling turquoise. In Tibetan tradition the mountain is a protector deity, and he and the lake are often spoken of as a couple.","w":"A white ridge just south-east of a large turquoise lake, Namtso, on the brown plateau north-west of Lhasa.","f":["Namtso, at its foot, lies about 4,700 m above sea level.","The lake covers nearly 2,000 km², one of the largest in Tibet.","The railway and highway from Qinghai to Lhasa run along the range's southern foot."],"q":{"q":"Which lake lies at the foot of Nyenchen Tanglha?","a":"Namtso","x":["Manasarovar","Yamdrok"]}},"Q1560829":{"t":"The 'great ice mountain' of Tibet's empty north","s":"Ulugh Muztagh means 'great ice mountain' in Uyghur, and it rules one of the loneliest places on Earth: the high, cold uplands on the border of Tibet and Xinjiang, far from any town. The summit, 6,973 m, crowns a compact knot of ice-covered ridges. Around it the ground is pale brown desert, dotted with salt lakes that flash white and blue.","w":"A compact cluster of white summits and glaciers rising from pale brown desert and scattered salt lakes.","f":["After his journey of 1895, the explorer George Littledale guessed it was over 7,700 m high.","A Chinese–American expedition made the first ascent in 1985 and measured its true height.","Its meltwater drains into closed basins and never reaches the sea."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Ulugh Muztagh mean?","a":"Great ice mountain","x":["White goddess","Father of rivers"]}},"Q229107":{"t":"A sacred rock pyramid that no one has climbed","s":"Kailash looks almost designed: a lone, four-sided dome of dark rock streaked with snow, standing apart in the far west of Tibet. It is sacred to Hindus, Buddhists, Jains and followers of Bon, and climbing it is not permitted. Pilgrims instead walk around it, a circuit of about 52 km. Just to the south, two big lakes lie side by side: Manasarovar and Rakshastal.","w":"A lone snow-streaked rock dome north of two large lakes lying side by side on the brown plateau.","f":["The Indus, Sutlej, Brahmaputra and Karnali all rise within about 100 km of it.","Lake Manasarovar, at about 4,590 m, is fresh, while its neighbour Rakshastal is salty.","The pilgrim circuit crosses a pass at about 5,600 m."],"q":{"q":"Which sacred freshwater lake lies just south of Mount Kailash?","a":"Manasarovar","x":["Namtso","Yamdrok"]}},"Q207132":{"t":"King of the Hindu Kush, above the Chitral valley","s":"Tirich Mir is the highest mountain of the Hindu Kush, 7,708 m, and it rules the valley of Chitral in northern Pakistan. From cruise height it looks like a great snowy tent with long ridges, standing north of the green thread of the Chitral river. Beyond it the land runs north toward Afghanistan's Wakhan Corridor, a narrow strip squeezed between high ranges.","w":"A massive snowy peak with long ridges, north of the town of Chitral and close to the Afghan border.","f":["A Norwegian expedition made the first ascent in 1950.","The Hindu Kush stretches about 800 km from northern Pakistan into central Afghanistan.","The town of Chitral lies about 40 km to its south."],"q":{"q":"Tirich Mir is the highest peak of which range?","a":"Hindu Kush","x":["Karakoram","Pamir"]}},"Q332762":{"t":"The northernmost 7,000-metre peak on Earth","s":"Jengish Chokusu, long known as Victory Peak, is the highest point of the Tian Shan, 7,439 m, on the border of Kyrgyzstan and China. No mountain over 7,000 m stands farther north. Look for a long, heavy ridge of ice rather than a spike; just to its north rises the sharper pyramid of Khan Tengri, whose marble summit glows pink at sunset.","w":"A long, heavy white ridge on the Kyrgyz–Chinese border, with the sharp pyramid of Khan Tengri about 16 km north.","f":["It was recognised as the Tian Shan's highest peak only in the 1940s; Khan Tengri had long been thought higher.","The South Inylchek Glacier at its foot is about 60 km long.","Jengish Chokusu is Kyrgyz for 'Victory Peak'."],"q":{"q":"Which sharp peak rises just north of Jengish Chokusu?","a":"Khan Tengri","x":["Lenin Peak","Muztagh Ata"]}},"Q271700":{"t":"Afghanistan's highest summit, on the Hindu Kush crest","s":"Noshaq is the highest point of Afghanistan and the second-highest mountain of the Hindu Kush, 7,492 m, standing right on the border with Pakistan. From above it shows as a broad white massif with several summits in a row, a short way north of Tirich Mir. Its northern glaciers drain into the Wakhan valley, where the rivers gather that later form the Amu Darya.","w":"A broad white massif with a row of summits on the Afghan–Pakistani border, just north of Tirich Mir.","f":["A Japanese expedition made the first ascent in 1960.","It stands just south of the Wakhan Corridor, a thin arm of Afghanistan reaching east to China."],"q":{"q":"Noshaq is the highest point of which country?","a":"Afghanistan","x":["Tajikistan","Iran"]}},"Q8536063":{"t":"The top of the Transhimalaya, Tibet's second wall","s":"North of the great Himalayan wall runs a second, lesser-known chain: the Transhimalaya, or Gangdise. Its highest summit is Lunpo Gangri, 7,095 m, an ice-capped massif in south-western Tibet. Look for a line of white peaks rising from brown uplands, with the upper Yarlung Tsangpo — the future Brahmaputra — winding through a broad valley to the south.","w":"An ice-capped massif north of the broad Yarlung Tsangpo valley, part of a white chain parallel to the Himalaya.","f":["Mount Kailash belongs to the same Gangdise range, about 300 km to the west.","The Swedish explorer Sven Hedin named the Transhimalaya after travelling through it in 1906–1908."],"q":{"q":"Which explorer gave the Transhimalaya its name?","a":"Sven Hedin","x":["Francis Younghusband","Nikolai Przhevalsky"]}},"ne-pk-1159105819":{"t":"The ice giant above the Karakoram Highway","s":"Kongur Tagh, 7,649 m, is a massive dome of ice at the western edge of the Tarim Basin, not far from Kashgar. Its neighbour to the south, Muztagh Ata, is rounder and more famous, but Kongur is higher. Between the two the Karakoram Highway runs past Karakul Lake, a blue mirror at about 3,600 m that reflects both peaks on calm days.","w":"A huge ice massif east of the Karakoram Highway, with the rounder Muztagh Ata to the south and a small blue lake between.","f":["A British team including Chris Bonington made the first ascent in 1981.","Kashgar, the old Silk Road oasis, lies roughly 100 km to the north-east.","Its summit rises more than 6,000 m above the floor of the Tarim Basin."],"q":{"q":"Which famous peak stands just south of Kongur Tagh?","a":"Muztagh Ata","x":["Khan Tengri","Tirich Mir"]}},"Q1322521":{"t":"The northern sentinel of Tibet's great gorge","s":"Gyala Peri and Namcha Barwa face each other across the Yarlung Tsangpo, only about 20 km apart. Between them the river squeezes through a gorge of extraordinary depth before turning south toward India. Gyala Peri, 7,294 m, is the northern guard: a white dome above slopes so wet and steep that dense forest climbs far up its sides.","w":"A white dome on the north side of a tight river bend, facing the higher Namcha Barwa across a dark gorge.","f":["Between Gyala Peri and Namcha Barwa the gorge is more than 5,000 m deep.","The botanist Frank Kingdon-Ward explored the gorge in 1924 in search of new plants."],"q":{"q":"Which peak faces Gyala Peri across the Yarlung Tsangpo gorge?","a":"Namcha Barwa","x":["Kangchenjunga","Nyenchen Tanglha"]}},"Q219986":{"t":"The highest mountain no one has climbed","s":"Gangkhar Puensum, 7,570 m, is the highest point of Bhutan and very likely the highest unclimbed mountain on Earth. Attempts in the 1980s failed, and since 1994 Bhutan has banned climbing peaks over 6,000 m out of respect for the belief that mountains are home to spirits. Look for a broad white summit on the northern border, above deep, forested valleys.","w":"A broad white summit on Bhutan's northern border with Tibet, above deep, green, forested valleys.","f":["Its name is usually translated as 'White Peak of the Three Spiritual Brothers'.","Since 2003 Bhutan has banned mountaineering altogether.","In 1999 a Japanese team climbed a neighbouring summit, Liankang Kangri, from the Tibetan side."],"q":{"q":"Since which year has Bhutan banned climbing peaks over 6,000 m?","a":"1994","x":["1974","2010"]}},"Q43105":{"t":"A double-humped volcano crowning the Caucasus","s":"Elbrus stands a little north of the main Caucasus ridge, and from the air its shape gives it away: two rounded white domes, the remains of a dormant volcano, joined by a saddle. The western summit, 5,642 m, is the highest point in Russia and is often counted as the top of Europe. Its ice cap feeds more than twenty glaciers that radiate outward like the spokes of a wheel.","w":"Two rounded snow domes side by side, standing just north of the jagged main Caucasus ridge.","f":["The eastern summit, 5,621 m, is only about 20 m lower than the western one.","Its last eruption is thought to have happened around AD 50.","Cable cars carry visitors from the Baksan valley up to about 3,800 m on its southern slope."],"q":{"q":"How many main summits does Elbrus have?","a":"Two","x":["One","Three"]}},"Q130018":{"t":"North America's summit, rising from near sea level","s":"Denali is the highest mountain in North America, about 6,200 m, and one of the bulkiest on Earth. It rises from lowlands only about 600 m above sea level, so its climb from base to summit — some 5,500 m — is greater than Everest's. Look for a huge white mass with long glaciers flowing out of it, dwarfing the rest of the Alaska Range.","w":"An enormous white massif towering over the Alaska Range, about 210 km north of Anchorage.","f":["Its name means 'the high one' in the Koyukon Athabaskan language.","An expedition led by Hudson Stuck and Harry Karstens made the first ascent in 1913.","The Kahiltna Glacier on its south side is about 70 km long, the longest in the Alaska Range."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Denali' mean in the Koyukon language?","a":"The high one","x":["The white one","Father of rivers"]}},"Q235539":{"t":"The Lower 48's highest summit, above a desert valley","s":"Mount Whitney is the highest point of the contiguous United States, 4,421 m, on the sharp eastern edge of the Sierra Nevada. Its granite wall drops steeply to the dry Owens Valley, more than three kilometres below. Only about 135 km to the south-east lies Badwater Basin in Death Valley, the lowest point in North America — the continent's high and low points almost within sight of each other.","w":"A saw-toothed granite crest above the brown Owens Valley, directly west of the small town of Lone Pine.","f":["Badwater Basin, 86 m below sea level, lies only about 135 km away.","Three fishermen from Lone Pine first reached the summit in 1873.","It is named after Josiah Whitney, head of the California Geological Survey."],"q":{"q":"Which low point lies about 135 km from Mount Whitney?","a":"Badwater Basin","x":["Salton Sea","Great Salt Lake"]}},"Q178167":{"t":"Australia's rooftop, a gentle granite hump","s":"Mount Kosciuszko is the highest mountain on mainland Australia, yet at 2,228 m it is a rounded summit that walkers reach from the top of a chairlift. From above, the Snowy Mountains look like a rumpled plateau, white for a few months each winter and grey-green the rest of the year. Look for the long blue arms of Lake Jindabyne and other reservoirs winding through the valleys below.","w":"A rounded, often snowy plateau south-west of Canberra, with long blue reservoirs winding through the valleys.","f":["The explorer Paweł Strzelecki named it in 1840 after the Polish patriot Tadeusz Kościuszko.","The Snowy Mountains Scheme, built from 1949 to 1974, sends the mountains' water through tunnels to power stations and farms.","Mainland Australia's only glacial lakes, including Blue Lake, lie near its summit."],"q":{"q":"Who was Mount Kosciuszko named after?","a":"A Polish patriot","x":["A British governor","A Scottish explorer"]}},"Q39739":{"t":"The highest peak on Earth outside Asia","s":"Aconcagua is the tallest mountain outside Asia, 6,959 m, and the summit of both the Western and Southern hemispheres. It rises in Argentina just east of the Chilean border, a huge brown-and-white massif rather than a slender spike. On flights between Santiago and Mendoza it often looms off the wing, barely four kilometres below you.","w":"A massive white-topped peak above the Andes' dry brown ridges, just north of the route between Santiago and Mendoza.","f":["Matthias Zurbriggen made the first recorded ascent in 1897.","Its normal route needs no technical climbing, only stamina and careful acclimatisation.","The name is often traced to Quechua words meaning 'stone sentinel', though its origin is debated."],"q":{"q":"Aconcagua is the highest peak outside which continent?","a":"Asia","x":["Africa","Europe"]}},"ne-pk-1159108759":{"t":"A lonely summit above Venezuela's rainforest","s":"Deep in Venezuela's Amazonas state, far from any road, Cerro La Raya rises to about 2,000 m above an unbroken green sea of forest. It belongs to the Guiana Highlands, a region of ancient sandstone mountains and flat-topped tepuis. Look for dark ridges poking through the canopy, often wrapped in afternoon cloud, with brown rivers winding away toward the Orinoco.","w":"Forested ridges and cloud-capped heights rising from unbroken rainforest, with brown rivers winding west toward the Orinoco.","f":["Venezuela's Amazonas state is larger than many European countries but very thinly populated.","In the same state the Casiquiare, a natural channel, links the Orinoco and Amazon river systems.","Nearby tepuis such as Cerro Autana have sheer walls rising straight out of the forest."],"q":{"q":"Which natural channel in Venezuela's Amazonas links the Orinoco and Amazon?","a":"Casiquiare","x":["Ventuari","Caroní"]}},"Q1108926":{"t":"Copper country on the roof of central Africa","s":"South-eastern DR Congo rises into the Katanga Plateau: high savanna and open woodland mostly over 1,000 m, crossed by the divide between Congo and Zambezi waters. Beneath it lie some of the richest copper and cobalt deposits on Earth. Look for enormous open-pit mines and tailings ponds — grey and turquoise scars — around the cities of Lubumbashi and Kolwezi.","w":"Green-brown savanna and woodland dotted with grey open-pit mines and bright turquoise ponds near Kolwezi and Lubumbashi.","f":["DR Congo produces around 70% of the world's cobalt, most of it from this region.","The Lualaba, the upper course of the Congo River, rises on the plateau.","The copper belt continues south across the border into Zambia's Copperbelt."],"q":{"q":"Which battery metal is mined heavily on the Katanga Plateau?","a":"Cobalt","x":["Nickel","Platinum"]}},"Q208807":{"t":"Tabletop mountains rising from the rainforest","s":"Across northern South America, sheer-sided table mountains called tepuis rise from the rainforest like islands, their flat tops often lost in cloud. They are carved from sandstone laid down about 1.7 billion years ago, among the oldest sedimentary rocks on the continent. Look for dark, cliff-edged blocks above the green, and in the rainy season thin white threads of waterfalls dropping from their rims.","w":"Flat-topped, cliff-edged blocks of dark rock standing above unbroken rainforest, often capped with cloud.","f":["Angel Falls, dropping 979 m from Auyán-tepui, is the highest uninterrupted waterfall in the world.","Mount Roraima, about 2,810 m, marks the meeting point of Venezuela, Brazil and Guyana.","Tepui is a Pemón word usually translated as 'house of the gods'."],"q":{"q":"Which record-breaking waterfall pours off a tepui here?","a":"Angel Falls","x":["Victoria Falls","Iguazu Falls"]}},"Q118899":{"t":"The old stone heart of Brazil","s":"Much of eastern and central Brazil is a vast upland of ancient rock, worn into long flat-topped plateaus called chapadas with steep edges. Brasília was built on it in the late 1950s, at over 1,000 m. From the air look for red-earth tablelands, cerrado savanna in olive and gold, and huge green circles of irrigated farmland laid out like coins on a table.","w":"Flat-topped red tablelands with steep edges, olive-green savanna and neat green circles of centre-pivot irrigation.","f":["The São Francisco, the longest river entirely within Brazil, flows north across the highlands for much of its course.","Brasília was inaugurated as the capital in 1960.","The cerrado savanna covering much of it is one of the most species-rich savannas on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Which is the longest river flowing entirely within Brazil?","a":"São Francisco","x":["Paraná","Tocantins"]}},"Q155197":{"t":"Taiga, rivers and old lava to the horizon","s":"Between the Yenisei and the Lena lies a vast raised upland, mostly 500 to 700 m high and cut by deep river valleys. Much of it is built from the Siberian Traps, lava that poured out about 250 million years ago. From cruise height it is endless larch taiga: dark green in summer, gold in autumn, and white under snow for most of the year.","w":"Endless dark taiga cut by winding rivers in deep valleys, with flat-topped uplands and, in autumn, golden larch.","f":["The Siberian Traps eruptions are linked to the greatest mass extinction in Earth's history, about 252 million years ago.","In 1908 the Tunguska explosion flattened some 2,000 km² of forest on the plateau.","Permafrost underlies nearly all of it, in places hundreds of metres deep."],"q":{"q":"Which 1908 event flattened forest on the Central Siberian Plateau?","a":"The Tunguska explosion","x":["The Sikhote-Alin meteorite","The Chelyabinsk meteor"]}},"Q560198":{"t":"Grassland under a huge sky, a kilometre up","s":"The Mongolian Plateau spreads across Mongolia and northern China at around 1,000 to 1,500 m: rolling steppe, bare hills and, to the south, the stony Gobi. Few places look so open from the air. Roads are faint tracks, rivers wander freely, and lakes shine like scattered coins. Ulaanbaatar, on the plateau at about 1,350 m, is the coldest national capital on Earth.","w":"Rolling green-brown steppe with few roads, meandering rivers and, to the south, pale stony desert.","f":["Ulaanbaatar has the lowest average temperature of any national capital.","Lake Khövsgöl, in the north, holds about 70% of Mongolia's fresh water.","At the Flaming Cliffs in the Gobi, scientists found the first recognised dinosaur egg nests in 1923."],"q":{"q":"Which is the coldest national capital, on this plateau?","a":"Ulaanbaatar","x":["Astana","Moscow"]}},"Q169966":{"t":"India's tilted tableland of ancient lava","s":"The Deccan fills most of southern India, a triangular plateau raised along its western edge by the Western Ghats and tilted toward the east. So its great rivers — the Godavari, Krishna and Kaveri — rise near the Arabian Sea yet flow right across to the Bay of Bengal. Much of the north-west is built of Deccan Traps basalt, poured out as lava about 66 million years ago.","w":"A dry brown tableland of flat-topped hills, reservoirs and rivers draining east; much greener in the monsoon months.","f":["Its name comes from the Sanskrit word dakshina, 'south'.","Hyderabad and Bengaluru both stand on the plateau, at about 500 and 900 m.","The Deccan Traps eruptions took place around the time the dinosaurs died out."],"q":{"q":"Where do most of the Deccan's great rivers flow?","a":"East to the Bay of Bengal","x":["West to the Arabian Sea","North to the Ganges"]}},"Q1908889":{"t":"Where the Amazon forest meets the fields","s":"Mato Grosso means 'thick bush' in Portuguese, and this plateau sits in the heart of Brazil, where Amazon rainforest gives way to cerrado savanna. From the air it is a patchwork: huge rectangles of soy and maize, strips of forest along the rivers, and red dirt roads ruled straight for kilometres. Its rivers split two ways, north to the Amazon and south toward the Pantanal.","w":"A vast chessboard of green and tan fields with ruler-straight roads, cut by dark lines of forest along rivers.","f":["The Xingu and the rivers that form the Tapajós, big Amazon tributaries, rise on this plateau.","The Xingu Indigenous Park, founded in 1961, shows from above as a great block of forest ringed by farmland.","Mato Grosso state is Brazil's leading soybean producer."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Mato Grosso' mean in Portuguese?","a":"Thick bush","x":["Great river","High plain"]}},"Q7680307":{"t":"A snowy ice dome above the Ross Sea coast","s":"Talos Dome is a broad, gentle summit of the East Antarctic ice, about 2,300 m high, inland from the coast of northern Victoria Land. Unlike the bone-dry domes of the deep interior, it catches a good deal of snow from storms off the Ross Sea and the Southern Ocean. That leaves thick, readable yearly layers in its ice, and scientists have drilled deep into them.","w":"A barely noticeable swell in the white ice sheet, with the dark peaks of Victoria Land between it and the coast.","f":["A European ice-coring project, TALDICE, drilled more than 1,600 m into the dome in the 2000s.","Its ice core holds a climate record of roughly 250,000 years."],"q":{"q":"Which sea lies toward the coast from Talos Dome?","a":"Ross Sea","x":["Weddell Sea","Amundsen Sea"]}},"Q470049":{"t":"An icy upland named on a 1939 flight","s":"American Highland is a high, ice-covered upland in East Antarctica, beside the vast valley of the Lambert Glacier. The American explorer Lincoln Ellsworth flew over it in 1939 and gave it its name. From above it is a smooth white slope crossed by faint ridges, falling toward the giant ice stream of the Lambert and the flat Amery Ice Shelf beyond.","w":"A smooth, faintly ridged white upland sloping toward a vast glacier valley and the flat Amery Ice Shelf.","f":["The Lambert Glacier nearby is one of the largest glaciers on Earth, several hundred kilometres long.","Ellsworth's 1939 flight took off from his expedition ship, the Wyatt Earp.","The Amery Ice Shelf, fed by the Lambert, floats at the head of Prydz Bay."],"q":{"q":"Who named the American Highland after a flight in 1939?","a":"Lincoln Ellsworth","x":["Richard Byrd","Hubert Wilkins"]}},"Q5881339":{"t":"Named for the pilot of the first flight across Antarctica","s":"This broad ice plateau in West Antarctica carries the name of Herbert Hollick-Kenyon, who in 1935 flew Lincoln Ellsworth across the continent for the first time. The crossing took about two weeks, because they had to land on the open ice several times to wait out storms. Below you is an endless, gently rolling white expanse with hardly a rock in sight.","w":"A featureless, gently rolling white plateau between the Ellsworth Mountains and the ranges of Marie Byrd Land.","f":["The 1935 flight ran out of fuel short of the Bay of Whales, and the two men walked the last stretch.","Ellsworth named the plateau after his pilot."],"q":{"q":"What did Herbert Hollick-Kenyon do in 1935?","a":"Flew across Antarctica","x":["Reached the South Pole","Mapped the Ross Sea"]}},"Q370807":{"t":"A white upland named after an expedition backer","s":"The Rockefeller Plateau is a vast, ice-buried upland in West Antarctica, inland from the Ross Ice Shelf. Richard Byrd's expedition flew over it in 1934 and named it after John D. Rockefeller Jr., one of Byrd's sponsors. From above there is only smooth white ice to the horizon; the bedrock lies well over a kilometre down, much of it below sea level.","w":"Smooth white ice to the horizon, east of the flat Ross Ice Shelf, with no rock visible at all.","f":["Much of the bedrock beneath West Antarctica's ice lies below sea level.","Byrd's base, Little America, stood on the Ross Ice Shelf to the west."],"q":{"q":"Whose expedition discovered and named the Rockefeller Plateau?","a":"Richard Byrd's","x":["Roald Amundsen's","Ernest Shackleton's"]}},"Q3045552":{"t":"A year-round outpost on the ice, 3,200 m up","s":"Dome C is one of the high summits of the East Antarctic ice, about 3,200 m above sea level, where France and Italy run Concordia, a station staffed all year. Winter temperatures here fall below −80 °C, and for months the sun never rises. An ice core drilled at Dome C holds a record of Earth's climate reaching back some 800,000 years.","w":"A flat white summit of the ice sheet with nothing to mark it — the station is far too small to see.","f":["The European EPICA project drilled 3,270 m of ice here, spanning about 800,000 years.","Space agencies study Concordia's winter crews to learn how people cope with isolation on long missions.","In 2025 the Beyond EPICA project reached ice over a million years old at nearby Little Dome C."],"q":{"q":"Which two countries run Concordia Station at Dome C?","a":"France and Italy","x":["Germany and Norway","UK and Belgium"]}},"Q593197":{"t":"The highest summit of Antarctica's ice","s":"Dome A is the highest point of the Antarctic ice sheet, about 4,090 m above sea level, deep in East Antarctica. Under some three kilometres of ice lie the Gamburtsev Mountains, a range roughly the size of the Alps that no one has ever seen. China set up its Kunlun Station here in 2009, and satellites have found some of Earth's coldest surface temperatures nearby.","w":"An utterly flat white horizon; the summit of the ice rises so gently that you cannot pick it out.","f":["Soviet scientists discovered the buried Gamburtsev Mountains in 1958 with seismic soundings.","Satellite readings on the ice ridge near Dome A have dropped below −90 °C.","A Chinese traverse team first reached Dome A in 2005."],"q":{"q":"What lies hidden beneath the ice at Dome A?","a":"The Gamburtsev Mountains","x":["Lake Vostok","The Wilkes Basin"]}},"Q2007598":{"t":"Japan's station on a summit of the ice","s":"Dome Fuji, also called Dome F, is a high summit of the East Antarctic ice sheet, about 3,800 m up, far inland in Queen Maud Land. Japan opened a research station here in 1995 and drilled deep into the ice beneath it; the cores reach back roughly 700,000 years. Up here the air is so cold and still that temperatures below −79 °C have been recorded.","w":"A broad white summit of the ice sheet, featureless to the horizon, far inland from the coast of Queen Maud Land.","f":["Japanese expeditions named the dome after Mount Fuji.","Only a few centimetres of water a year fall here as snow, making it one of the driest places on Earth.","The ice ridge between Dome Fuji and Dome A holds some of the coldest surface temperatures ever measured."],"q":{"q":"Which country runs the research station at Dome Fuji?","a":"Japan","x":["China","Norway"]}},"ne-gl-academy-of-sciences-ice-cap":{"t":"Russia's largest ice cap, a round lid on an Arctic island","s":"Near the top of Severnaya Zemlya, an almost perfectly round dome of ice covers most of Komsomolets Island. The Academy of Sciences Ice Cap is about 80 km across and some 5,500 km² in area, the largest single ice mass in Russia. From above it looks like a white lid dropped on a dark sea, its edges crumbling into ice cliffs and drifting floes.","w":"A round, smooth white dome filling most of an island, with ice cliffs along its edges and sea ice around.","f":["Severnaya Zemlya was one of the last archipelagos on Earth to be discovered, in 1913.","Georgy Ushakov and Nikolay Urvantsev first mapped the islands in 1930–1932, travelling by dog sled.","The ice is hundreds of metres thick at the centre of the dome."],"q":{"q":"On which island does the Academy of Sciences Ice Cap lie?","a":"Komsomolets Island","x":["Wrangel Island","Kotelny Island"]}},"ne-gl-agassiz-ice-cap":{"t":"An Arctic ice cap named for the Ice Age's discoverer","s":"On northern Ellesmere Island, Canada's northernmost, the Agassiz Ice Cap spreads over the high interior like poured icing. It carries the name of Louis Agassiz, the Swiss-born scientist who in the 1830s argued that great ice sheets had once covered much of the northern world. Look for a smooth white dome cut at its edges by dark rock ridges and glacier tongues sliding toward the fjords.","w":"A smooth white dome over Ellesmere's high interior, fringed by dark rock ridges and glacier tongues heading for the fjords.","f":["Ice cores drilled here since the 1970s record Arctic temperatures over thousands of years.","Ellesmere Island is the tenth-largest island in the world."],"q":{"q":"Louis Agassiz is famous for which idea?","a":"Past ice ages","x":["Continental drift","Natural selection"]}},"ne-gl-asgardfonna":{"t":"An ice plateau named after the home of the Norse gods","s":"In the north-east of Spitsbergen, between two long fjords, lies Åsgardfonna, a plateau of ice some 1,200 km² in area. Its name comes from Åsgard, the home of the gods in Norse myth, and neighbouring ice fields borrow names from the same stories. From above it is a high white tableland, with dark ridges and valley glaciers spilling off its sides.","w":"A high white ice plateau between two long fjords, Wijdefjorden to the west and Lomfjorden to the east.","f":["Nearby ice fields are called Valhallfonna and Balderfonna, also after Norse mythology.","Wijdefjorden, on its western side, is Svalbard's longest fjord, over 100 km long."],"q":{"q":"In Norse myth, what is Åsgard?","a":"The home of the gods","x":["The realm of the dead","The world of the giants"]}},"ne-gl-barnes-ice-cap":{"t":"The last surviving piece of the great Ice Age sheet","s":"In the middle of Baffin Island lies a smooth white dome some 150 km long, and it is older than it looks. The Barnes Ice Cap is the last remnant of the Laurentide Ice Sheet, which buried most of Canada during the last ice age, and its deepest layers still hold ice from that time. Around it the land is brown tundra scattered with thousands of lakes.","w":"A long, smooth white dome in the centre of Baffin Island, surrounded by brown tundra freckled with lakes.","f":["It covers about 6,000 km².","Scientists expect it to disappear within a few centuries as the Arctic warms.","Baffin Island is the fifth-largest island in the world."],"q":{"q":"The Barnes Ice Cap is a remnant of which ice sheet?","a":"The Laurentide Ice Sheet","x":["The Cordilleran Ice Sheet","The Greenland Ice Sheet"]}},"ne-gl-bering-glacier":{"t":"North America's longest glacier, a river of ice","s":"Flying along the Gulf of Alaska, look for a vast lobe of ice spreading onto the coastal plain: the Bering Glacier, roughly 190 km long, the longest and largest glacier in North America. It flows from the Bagley Icefield in the Chugach and Saint Elias mountains. Dark stripes of rock debris ripple across its surface, bent into waves by past surges.","w":"A huge ice lobe spilling onto the coastal plain east of the Copper River delta, streaked with dark, wavy lines.","f":["It is named after the explorer Vitus Bering and has surged forward several times in the past century.","At its snout lies Vitus Lake, crowded with floating icebergs."],"q":{"q":"Which icefield feeds the Bering Glacier?","a":"Bagley Icefield","x":["Harding Icefield","Juneau Icefield"]}},"ne-gl-brasvellbreen":{"t":"The ice wall that leapt 20 km in two years","s":"On Nordaustlandet, Svalbard's second-largest island, the Austfonna ice cap ends in a long, low cliff, and Bråsvellbreen is its biggest outlet. In 1936–38 it surged forward by up to 20 km along a 30 km front, one of the largest glacier surges ever recorded. In summer, meltwater pours off the ice cliff in dozens of waterfalls straight into the sea.","w":"A long, low ice cliff running along the sea, the edge of a huge white ice cap on Svalbard's second-largest island.","f":["Its Norwegian name means roughly 'sudden swell', after that surge.","Austfonna's ice front runs for well over 100 km, almost without a break.","Austfonna is one of the largest ice caps in Europe."],"q":{"q":"What did Bråsvellbreen do in 1936–38?","a":"Surged forward up to 20 km","x":["Retreated 20 km","Split into two glaciers"]}},"ne-gl-cambria-icefield":{"t":"An icefield feeding blue glaciers above Stewart","s":"South-east of the little port of Stewart, at the head of the long fjord of Portland Canal, the Cambria Icefield covers some 700 km² of the Coast Mountains. Its glaciers spill down on all sides into forested valleys. One of them, Bear Glacier, ends right beside a highway, its snout glowing an intense blue above a meltwater lake.","w":"A white icefield among jagged coastal peaks, glaciers radiating into green valleys, east of a long, narrow fjord.","f":["Portland Canal, the fjord below, is more than 100 km long and forms part of the Alaska–Canada border.","Stewart, at the fjord's head, calls itself Canada's northernmost ice-free port.","Its outlet glaciers have been retreating since the early 1900s."],"q":{"q":"Which Cambria glacier ends beside a highway near Stewart?","a":"Bear Glacier","x":["Athabasca Glacier","Mendenhall Glacier"]}},"ne-gl-columbia-icefield":{"t":"The rooftop of the Rockies, draining to three oceans","s":"The Columbia Icefield is the largest in the Rocky Mountains, some 200 km² of ice on the Continental Divide between Alberta and British Columbia. Its meltwater runs to three oceans: west to the Pacific, north to the Arctic, and east through Hudson Bay to the Atlantic. Look for the Athabasca Glacier, a broad white tongue ending near the Icefields Parkway.","w":"A high white plateau of ice ringed by rock peaks, with a broad glacier tongue reaching down toward a road.","f":["Mount Columbia, 3,747 m, on its rim, is the highest point in Alberta.","Snow Dome, on the icefield, is a triple divide: its meltwater can reach three different oceans.","The Athabasca Glacier has retreated more than 1.5 km since the late 1800s."],"q":{"q":"How many oceans does Columbia Icefield meltwater reach?","a":"Three","x":["Two","Four"]}},"ne-gl-devon-ice-cap":{"t":"Ice on the largest uninhabited island on Earth","s":"Devon Island, north of Lancaster Sound, is the largest island on Earth with no permanent residents, and its eastern part lies under the Devon Ice Cap, about 14,000 km² of white. West of the ice the land is a bare, rust-brown plateau, so Mars-like that scientists use the Haughton crater there to rehearse missions to the Red Planet. From the air the white-brown contrast is striking.","w":"A white dome covering the eastern end of the island, beside bare, rust-brown plateaus cut by canyons.","f":["The Haughton crater, about 23 km wide, was blasted out by an impact tens of millions of years ago.","The Haughton-Mars Project has run field seasons at the crater since 1997."],"q":{"q":"What makes Devon Island stand out?","a":"Largest uninhabited island","x":["Northernmost island","Largest Arctic island"]}},"ne-gl-edgeoyjokulen":{"t":"A white dome on Svalbard's wild eastern island","s":"Edgeøya, Svalbard's third-largest island, has no permanent residents, and much of its eastern part lies under ice caps such as Edgeøyjøkulen. Look for a smooth white dome whose edges break into short glaciers reaching the sea, while the west of the island shows bare, terraced brown plateaus. In winter the whole island merges with the frozen sea around it.","w":"A smooth white dome on a big island south-east of Spitsbergen, with short glaciers reaching the sea and bare brown uplands.","f":["The island is named after Thomas Edge, an English merchant in the 17th-century whaling trade.","Edgeøya lies within a nature reserve where polar bears roam freely."],"q":{"q":"Where does Edgeøya rank among Svalbard's islands by size?","a":"Third","x":["First","Fifth"]}},"ne-gl-harding-icefield":{"t":"A frozen sea spilling dozens of glaciers","s":"On the Kenai Peninsula south of Anchorage, the Harding Icefield buries some 1,800 km² of mountains, with only rocky summits poking through. More than 30 glaciers flow off it, several all the way down to the fjords of Kenai Fjords National Park. From above it is a white sea studded with dark islands of rock — nunataks — that ends abruptly at the green coast.","w":"A white expanse dotted with dark rock peaks, sending glacier tongues down to fjords on the Kenai Peninsula's south coast.","f":["It is named after US President Warren G. Harding, who visited Alaska in 1923.","Exit Glacier, near the town of Seward, is the one part of the icefield you can reach by road."],"q":{"q":"Which national park lies below the Harding Icefield?","a":"Kenai Fjords","x":["Glacier Bay","Denali"]}},"ne-gl-holtedahlfonna":{"t":"The ice field that feeds the glaciers of Kongsfjorden","s":"North-west Spitsbergen is crowned by Holtedahlfonna, the island's second-largest ice cap, about 1,400 km² of white. Its ice drains west through Kronebreen, one of Svalbard's fastest glaciers, which ends in a crumbling blue wall at the head of Kongsfjorden. On the same fjord stands Ny-Ålesund, one of the northernmost research settlements on Earth.","w":"A broad white ice field east of Kongsfjorden, sending a wide glacier into the head of the fjord.","f":["It is named after the Norwegian geologist Olaf Holtedahl.","Kronebreen can flow up to about 3 m a day.","Ny-Ålesund hosts research stations from about ten countries."],"q":{"q":"Which fast glacier drains Holtedahlfonna into Kongsfjorden?","a":"Kronebreen","x":["Negribreen","Bråsvellbreen"]}},"ne-gl-juneau-icefield":{"t":"The icefield behind Alaska's roadless capital","s":"Right behind Juneau, Alaska's capital, the mountains vanish under the Juneau Icefield, nearly 4,000 km² of ice straddling the US–Canada border. Dozens of glaciers pour off it: the Mendenhall reaches almost to the city's suburbs, and the Taku runs down toward the sea. The ice is one reason no road links Juneau to the rest of North America.","w":"A white plateau with dark peaks behind Juneau, glaciers spilling toward the sea, one ending in a lake by the town.","f":["Juneau is the only US state capital on the North American mainland with no road link to the rest of the continent.","Students have studied the icefield every summer since the late 1940s through the Juneau Icefield Research Program.","For decades the Taku was one of the few advancing glaciers in Alaska."],"q":{"q":"Which Juneau Icefield glacier ends in a lake near the city?","a":"Mendenhall Glacier","x":["Hubbard Glacier","Columbia Glacier"]}},"ne-gl-karpinsky-ice-cap":{"t":"Severnaya Zemlya's highest point is made of ice","s":"The Karpinsky Ice Cap is the largest of the seven ice caps on October Revolution Island, about 2,800 km². Its summit, at about 965 m, is the highest point of the whole Severnaya Zemlya archipelago — and it is ice, not rock. Look for a broad dome sloping toward the Laptev Sea, its edges split into outlet glaciers and bays choked with sea ice most of the year.","w":"A broad white dome on the eastern side of a large Arctic island, with glaciers reaching into ice-choked bays.","f":["It is named after Alexander Karpinsky, a geologist who became the first elected president of the Russian Academy of Sciences.","October Revolution Island is the largest island of Severnaya Zemlya."],"q":{"q":"What forms the highest point of Severnaya Zemlya?","a":"An ice cap","x":["A volcano","A rock pinnacle"]}},"ne-gl-kluane-ice-cap":{"t":"The biggest icefields outside the polar regions","s":"Between Yukon and Alaska lies a sea of ice that swallows whole mountain ranges: the icefields of the Saint Elias Mountains, the largest outside the polar regions. Rising from them is Mount Logan, 5,959 m, Canada's highest peak and one of the bulkiest mountains on Earth. From above look for rivers of ice many kilometres wide, striped with dark moraines like highways.","w":"Endless white icefields with dark rock peaks and huge striped glaciers, inland from the Gulf of Alaska.","f":["Kluane National Park and its neighbours form a single UNESCO World Heritage site straddling the Canada–US border.","Mount Logan is thought to have the largest base of any non-volcanic mountain on Earth.","The Hubbard Glacier, fed from these icefields, calves into the sea near Yakutat."],"q":{"q":"Which mountain, rising from these icefields, is Canada's highest?","a":"Mount Logan","x":["Mount Robson","Mount Saint Elias"]}},"ne-gl-lyell-icefield":{"t":"Five summits named for Swiss mountain guides","s":"South of the Columbia Icefield, another sheet of ice spreads along the Continental Divide: the Lyell Icefield. Above it rise the five summits of Mount Lyell, named after the geologist Charles Lyell; the individual peaks honour Swiss guides who worked in the Canadian Rockies. Look for a white plateau feeding glaciers west into British Columbia and east into the valleys of Banff National Park.","w":"A white ice plateau on the Rockies' crest south of the larger Columbia Icefield, with glaciers pouring off both sides.","f":["Charles Lyell, whose name it bears, was one of the founders of modern geology.","Its eastern glaciers feed the North Saskatchewan River, whose water ends up in Hudson Bay."],"q":{"q":"Which river is fed by the Lyell Icefield's eastern glaciers?","a":"North Saskatchewan","x":["Athabasca","Bow"]}},"ne-gl-malaspina-glacier":{"t":"A giant fan of ice spread over the Alaskan plain","s":"Look for a vast, round lobe of ice sprawled across the coastal plain at the foot of Mount Saint Elias: the Malaspina, the largest piedmont glacier in the world. The Seward Glacier squeezes out of the mountains through a gap and spreads like dough on a table, about 65 km wide. Its surface is marbled with dark bands of moraine, folded into zigzags by repeated surges.","w":"A huge rounded lobe of ice on the coastal plain, marbled with dark zigzag stripes, west of Yakutat Bay.","f":["It covers about 3,900 km², more than the US state of Rhode Island.","It is named after Alessandro Malaspina, who explored this coast for Spain in 1791.","Its bed lies below sea level, so the ocean could one day flood in if the ice thins enough."],"q":{"q":"What kind of glacier is the Malaspina?","a":"A piedmont glacier","x":["A tidewater glacier","A cirque glacier"]}},"ne-gl-manson-icefield":{"t":"A lonely ice sheet on Ellesmere's southern shore","s":"Along the south-east coast of Ellesmere Island, at the top of Baffin Bay, the Manson Icefield covers roughly 6,000 km² of mountains. From above it is a broad white plateau whose glaciers drop steeply into the sea, calving icebergs that drift slowly south. There are no roads or settlements on the ice, and in winter it merges with the frozen sea into a single white plain.","w":"A broad white plateau on Ellesmere's south-east coast, with glaciers dropping steeply into dark, ice-strewn water.","f":["Grise Fiord, Canada's northernmost civilian community, lies on the same southern coast of Ellesmere.","Just offshore, the North Water polynya is a stretch of sea that stays partly open even in winter."],"q":{"q":"Which is Canada's northernmost civilian community, on Ellesmere?","a":"Grise Fiord","x":["Resolute","Alert"]}},"ne-gl-monarch-icefield":{"t":"A crown of ice above the Bella Coola valley","s":"South-east of the fjord town of Bella Coola, the Coast Mountains vanish under the Monarch Icefield, the northernmost of a chain of big icefields in the Pacific Ranges. Its namesake, Monarch Mountain, 3,555 m, towers over the peaks around it. From above, look for a white plateau with dark summits, glaciers spilling toward deep green valleys, and the long fjords of the coast beyond.","w":"A white icefield with dark rock summits south-east of a long fjord, glaciers draining toward deep green valleys.","f":["Henry Hall and Hans Fuhrer made the first ascent of Monarch Mountain in 1936.","The larger Ha-Iltzuk Icefield, the biggest of this group, lies to the south.","The Bella Coola valley below is known for grizzly bears fishing for salmon."],"q":{"q":"Which fjord town lies north-west of the Monarch Icefield?","a":"Bella Coola","x":["Prince Rupert","Kitimat"]}},"ne-gl-mount-brown-icefield":{"t":"Home of a famous mountain-height blunder","s":"Around Athabasca Pass on the Continental Divide lies the Mount Brown Icefield, a quiet sheet of ice in the backcountry south of Jasper. Its namesake has a story: in 1827 the Scottish botanist David Douglas declared Mount Brown and its neighbour Mount Hooker to be about 16,000 feet high, the tallest peaks in North America. For decades maps repeated the claim. Both are in fact well under 3,500 m.","w":"A white icefield among dark peaks on the Rockies' crest, beside a low notch that marks an old fur-trade pass.","f":["Athabasca Pass was a main fur-trade route across the Rockies in the early 1800s.","Mount Brown is named after the botanist Robert Brown, known for describing Brownian motion.","The myth of the giant peaks lasted until climbers and surveyors checked them in the 1890s."],"q":{"q":"What did David Douglas get wrong about Mount Brown in 1827?","a":"Its height","x":["Its name","Its location"]}},"ne-gl-muller-ice-cap":{"t":"Arctic ice on an island of fossil forests","s":"Axel Heiberg Island, west of Ellesmere, is crowned by two ice caps, and the Müller is the northern one. Its outlet glaciers creep down toward long fjords. Nearby, White Glacier has been measured every year since 1959, one of the longest such records in the Arctic. Elsewhere on the island lie mummified tree stumps from forests that grew here some 45 million years ago.","w":"A broad white ice cap in northern Axel Heiberg Island, glaciers reaching down toward long, dark fjords.","f":["The island is named after Axel Heiberg, a Norwegian brewery director who funded Otto Sverdrup's expedition.","McGill University has run a summer research station at Expedition Fiord since 1960."],"q":{"q":"What remarkable fossils have been found on Axel Heiberg Island?","a":"Mummified tree stumps","x":["Dinosaur footprints","Mammoth skeletons"]}},"ne-gl-myrdalsjokull":{"t":"An ice cap hiding the volcano Katla","s":"Near Iceland's southern tip, a round white cap of ice sits above black sand plains: Mýrdalsjökull, about 500 km². Beneath it lies Katla, one of Iceland's most powerful volcanoes, which last erupted in a big way in 1918, sending floods of meltwater across the coast. Just to the west is the smaller Eyjafjallajökull, whose 2010 eruption grounded flights across Europe for days.","w":"A rounded white ice cap above black sand plains on Iceland's south coast, with smaller Eyjafjallajökull just to the west.","f":["The 1918 Katla flood carried so much sediment that it pushed the shoreline several kilometres out to sea.","Vík, near its southern foot, is the southernmost village in Iceland.","Mýrdalsjökull is Iceland's fourth-largest glacier."],"q":{"q":"Which volcano lies beneath Mýrdalsjökull?","a":"Katla","x":["Hekla","Grímsvötn"]}},"ne-gl-negribreen":{"t":"A glacier that wakes up about once a century","s":"On Spitsbergen's east coast, Negribreen flows into the wide Storfjorden, a broad river of ice with a heavily crevassed surface. It is a surge-type glacier: in 1935–36 it lurched almost 12 km forward in a single year. In 2016 it woke again, and by 2017 parts were racing along at about 20 m a day. From above look for a chaotic, shattered surface of white ice blocks.","w":"A broad, heavily crevassed glacier on Spitsbergen's east coast, ending in an ice cliff in the wide Storfjorden.","f":["In its 1935–36 surge the front advanced along a stretch about 15 km wide.","Surging glaciers are common in Svalbard; many lie quiet for decades between surges."],"q":{"q":"How far did Negribreen lurch forward in 1935–36?","a":"About 12 km","x":["About 1 km","About 50 km"]}},"ne-gl-north-ellesmere-ice-cap":{"t":"Ice at the very top of North America","s":"The northern end of Ellesmere Island is some of the northernmost land on Earth, and much of it lies under ice caps and icefields. Here, in Quttinirpaaq National Park, rise the highest mountains of the Canadian Arctic, including Barbeau Peak, 2,616 m. From above look for white plateaus broken by dark nunataks and, along the coast, the ragged remains of ancient floating ice shelves.","w":"White plateaus pierced by dark peaks, fringed along the Arctic Ocean coast by the remains of floating ice shelves.","f":["Cape Columbia, on Ellesmere's north coast, is the northernmost point of Canada.","Quttinirpaaq means 'top of the world' in Inuktitut.","Barbeau Peak, 2,616 m, is the highest mountain in Nunavut."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Quttinirpaaq' mean in Inuktitut?","a":"Top of the world","x":["Land of ice","Place of bears"]}},"ne-gl-northern-patagonian-ice-field":{"t":"Where a glacier meets the sea closest to the equator","s":"The Northern Patagonian Ice Field is the smaller of Patagonia's two great ice fields, yet it still drapes some 4,000 km² of ice over the Andes of southern Chile. Its highest point, Monte San Valentín, about 4,000 m, is the tallest summit in Patagonia. On its western side the San Rafael Glacier reaches Laguna San Rafael, a lagoon open to the Pacific, at only about 46.7° south.","w":"An icy plateau on the Andes of southern Chile, glaciers spilling west to a milky lagoon and east toward turquoise lakes.","f":["San Rafael is often called the tidewater glacier closest to the equator.","Lake General Carrera, east of the ice field, is the largest lake in Chile."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest summit of the Northern Patagonian Ice Field?","a":"Monte San Valentín","x":["Cerro Fitz Roy","Cerro Torre"]}},"ne-gl-penny-ice-cap":{"t":"The ice cap of 'the land that never melts'","s":"On Baffin Island's Cumberland Peninsula, the Penny Ice Cap spreads some 6,000 km² of white just north of the Arctic Circle. It is the heart of Auyuittuq National Park — 'the land that never melts' in Inuktitut. From its edges glaciers pour down into deep U-shaped valleys and fjords, walled by some of the tallest granite cliffs on Earth.","w":"A broad white ice cap feeding glaciers into deep, steep-walled valleys, just north of the Arctic Circle on Baffin Island.","f":["Mount Thor, in the park, has one of the tallest sheer cliffs on Earth, about 1,250 m.","Akshayuk Pass, a well-known trekking route, cuts right across the peninsula below the ice."],"q":{"q":"What does Auyuittuq mean in Inuktitut?","a":"The land that never melts","x":["The land of the bears","The great white wall"]}},"ne-gl-prince-of-wales-icefield":{"t":"A vast ice sheet on Ellesmere, facing Greenland","s":"The Prince of Wales Icefield spreads along the eastern side of central Ellesmere Island, roughly 20,000 km² of ice — larger than many small countries. Across the channels to the east lies Greenland. From above, look for an immense white plateau split by outlet glaciers that snake down to the sea, releasing icebergs into the ice-choked straits.","w":"An immense white plateau on eastern Ellesmere, with glaciers snaking east to the sea channels that separate it from Greenland.","f":["It is one of the largest icefields in the Canadian Arctic.","Smith Sound and Nares Strait, off its eastern side, link Baffin Bay with the Arctic Ocean."],"q":{"q":"Which land lies across the strait east of this icefield?","a":"Greenland","x":["Baffin Island","Devon Island"]}},"ne-gl-sargent-icefield":{"t":"Glaciers pouring straight into Prince William Sound","s":"On the eastern Kenai Peninsula, the Sargent Icefield caps the mountains above the maze of fjords and islands of Prince William Sound. Its glaciers spill down steep valleys right to the sea, calving icebergs into quiet green inlets. Some carry unexpected names: Princeton Glacier, for example, was named in 1909 after the university.","w":"A white icefield above a maze of fjords and forested islands, with glaciers ending at the water's edge.","f":["It is named after Rufus Harvey Sargent, a US Geological Survey mapmaker who surveyed the Kenai Peninsula.","Chenega Glacier, one of its outlets, takes its name from nearby Chenega Island."],"q":{"q":"Which sound do the Sargent Icefield's glaciers flow into?","a":"Prince William Sound","x":["Norton Sound","Kotzebue Sound"]}},"ne-gl-southern-patagonian-ice-field":{"t":"The largest ice in the south outside Antarctica","s":"For some 350 km along the Andes, the mountains of Patagonia disappear under one continuous sheet of ice, the largest in the Southern Hemisphere outside Antarctica. On a clear day you can follow glaciers flowing west to Pacific fjords and east to milky turquoise lakes in Argentina. Granite spires such as Fitz Roy and the Torres del Paine rise from its edges like broken teeth.","w":"A continuous white sheet on the Andes, glaciers streaming west to fjords and east into turquoise lakes like Lago Argentino.","f":["Perito Moreno Glacier, one of its outlets, ends in an ice wall about 5 km wide in Lago Argentino.","Pío XI, on the Chilean side, is the largest glacier in South America."],"q":{"q":"Which famous glacier of this ice field ends in Lago Argentino?","a":"Perito Moreno","x":["San Rafael","Pío XI"]}},"ne-gl-steacie-ice-cap":{"t":"The southern ice cap of an uninhabited Arctic island","s":"Axel Heiberg Island, west of Ellesmere, carries two big ice caps, and the Steacie covers the island's southern highlands. From above it is a broad white dome, its outlet glaciers winding between dark, flat-topped plateaus toward the fjords. Nobody lives on the island; in summer only a few research camps dot its valleys, and in winter the sea around it freezes solid.","w":"A broad white dome over southern Axel Heiberg Island, glaciers winding between dark flat-topped plateaus to the fjords.","f":["More than a quarter of Axel Heiberg Island, some 43,000 km² in all, lies under ice.","Several glaciers on the island's west side have kept advancing for decades, against the global trend."],"q":{"q":"Axel Heiberg Island lies next to which larger island?","a":"Ellesmere Island","x":["Baffin Island","Victoria Island"]}},"ne-gl-stikine-icecap":{"t":"An icefield split by a wild river","s":"Along the Alaska–British Columbia border near Wrangell and Petersburg, the Stikine Icecap buries the Coast Mountains in white. The Stikine River slices right through the range here, one of only a few rivers that do. On the Alaskan side LeConte Glacier reaches the sea, calving icebergs into LeConte Bay. Look too for Devils Thumb, a sharp rock spire rising from the ice on the border.","w":"A broad white icefield on the coastal mountains east of Petersburg, cut by a wide river valley and fjords full of icebergs.","f":["LeConte Glacier is the southernmost tidewater glacier in the Northern Hemisphere.","John Muir compared the Stikine valley to 'a Yosemite a hundred miles long'."],"q":{"q":"Which glacier here is the Northern Hemisphere's southernmost tidewater glacier?","a":"LeConte Glacier","x":["Mendenhall Glacier","Hubbard Glacier"]}},"ne-gl-university-ice-cap":{"t":"One of seven ice caps on a remote Arctic island","s":"October Revolution Island, the largest of Severnaya Zemlya, wears seven ice caps, and the University Ice Cap lies south of the biggest, the Karpinsky. Its dome rises to about 800 m. Between the caps the bare ground is stony polar desert, brown and grey in summer, with no trees or shrubs at all. In winter everything merges with the frozen seas into one white expanse.","w":"A white dome on a large Arctic island, flanked by stony brown polar desert and other ice caps to the north.","f":["Severnaya Zemlya separates the Kara Sea from the Laptev Sea.","The archipelago was mapped only in 1930–32, by a four-man expedition led by Georgy Ushakov."],"q":{"q":"Which two seas does Severnaya Zemlya separate?","a":"The Kara and Laptev seas","x":["The Barents and Kara seas","The Laptev and East Siberian"]}},"ne-gl-vatnajokull":{"t":"Iceland's giant ice cap, sitting on volcanoes","s":"Vatnajökull covers about 8% of Iceland, some 7,700 km² of ice up to nearly a kilometre thick. From above it is a white plateau spilling glacier tongues in every direction, some ending in lagoons full of icebergs near the south coast. Beneath the ice lie active volcanoes, including Grímsvötn and Bárðarbunga, whose eruptions can melt the ice and unleash sudden floods.","w":"A vast white ice cap in south-east Iceland, glacier tongues radiating outward and a lagoon of icebergs by the coast.","f":["Hvannadalshnúkur, on its southern rim, is Iceland's highest peak at about 2,110 m.","Jökulsárlón, the lagoon at the foot of one of its glaciers, is crowded with floating icebergs.","Vatnajökull National Park became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2019."],"q":{"q":"Which Icelandic volcano lies beneath Vatnajökull?","a":"Grímsvötn","x":["Hekla","Katla"]}},"ne-gl-vestfonna":{"t":"The western twin of Svalbard's great ice caps","s":"Nordaustlandet, Svalbard's second-largest island, is mostly wrapped in ice, split between two great caps. Vestfonna, the western one, covers about 2,500 km²: a low white dome crossed by ridges and outlet glaciers that end in ice cliffs along the fjords. Its bigger sibling, Austfonna, spreads to the east, and together they make the island look frosted from above.","w":"A low white dome on the western half of a large, ice-covered island north-east of Spitsbergen.","f":["Nordaustlandet has no permanent residents and is protected as a nature reserve.","Walruses haul out on beaches along the island's coasts."],"q":{"q":"Which is Vestfonna's larger neighbour on the same island?","a":"Austfonna","x":["Holtedahlfonna","Åsgardfonna"]}},"Q358755":{"t":"A huge snowy volcano named after a rare bird","s":"Mount Takahe is a broad shield volcano rising alone from the ice of Marie Byrd Land, 3,460 m high and with a volume of about 780 km³. From above it looks like a gently swelling white dome with a wide, shallow crater on top. Its last known eruption was about 7,500 years ago, and layers of its ash turn up in Antarctic ice cores.","w":"A broad, gently rounded snow dome rising alone from the flat ice, with a wide shallow crater at its summit.","f":["It is named after the takahē, a rare flightless bird of New Zealand.","Its summit caldera is about 8 km across."],"q":{"q":"What is Mount Takahe named after?","a":"A flightless bird","x":["A ship","A mountaineer"]}},"Q924312":{"t":"Antarctica's highest volcano, with a broken crater","s":"Mount Sidley is the tallest of the Executive Committee Range, a line of volcanoes standing in the ice of Marie Byrd Land, and at 4,285 m it is the highest volcano in Antarctica. Its south side is torn open by a caldera about 5 km wide, a dramatic bite out of the white cone. Richard Byrd spotted it from the air in 1934.","w":"A massive white volcano in a north–south line of snowy cones, its southern flank cut by a huge crater.","f":["Byrd named it after Mabelle Sidley, daughter of one of his expedition's sponsors.","It is the Antarctic entry on the 'Volcanic Seven Summits', the highest volcano on each continent.","The first recorded ascent was made in 1990."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest volcano in Antarctica?","a":"Mount Sidley","x":["Mount Erebus","Mount Takahe"]}},"Q3321842":{"t":"A giant beside Amundsen's road to the Pole","s":"In November 1911 Roald Amundsen's party climbed from the Ross Ice Shelf up the Axel Heiberg Glacier toward the polar plateau, and beside their route stood this massive peak. Amundsen named it after Fridtjof Nansen, the Norwegian explorer who had lent him his ship, the Fram. At 4,069 m it is one of the great summits of the Queen Maud Mountains, a wall of rock and ice across the way to the Pole.","w":"A massive ice-clad peak at the edge of the Ross Ice Shelf, beside a steep glacier climbing toward the polar plateau.","f":["Amundsen reached the South Pole on 14 December 1911, about a month after passing this mountain.","The Axel Heiberg Glacier beside it climbs roughly 3,000 m from the ice shelf to the plateau."],"q":{"q":"Who named Mount Fridtjof Nansen in 1911?","a":"Roald Amundsen","x":["Robert Falcon Scott","Ernest Shackleton"]}},"Q19459387":{"t":"The high point above Pangong's blue lake","s":"Kangju Kangri is the highest summit of the Pangong Range, 6,725 m, a white crest in the high desert of eastern Ladakh. Its most striking neighbour is water rather than rock: Pangong Tso, a narrow lake some 130 km long at about 4,200 m, lies at its foot. From above the lake is an unmistakable ribbon of blue that shifts from turquoise to deep navy with the light.","w":"A white crest in bare brown mountains beside a long, narrow, vividly blue lake that winds away to the east.","f":["Pangong Tso is brackish, yet it freezes over in winter.","Kangju Kangri was first climbed in 1983.","Villagers on the lake's southern shore also call the peak Thinguru."],"q":{"q":"Which long high-altitude lake lies at the foot of Kangju Kangri?","a":"Pangong Tso","x":["Tso Moriri","Namtso"]}},"Q28794266":{"t":"The 'white mountain' of the central Kunlun","s":"Aktag means 'white mountain' in Turkic, a plain name for a remote summit in the Kunlun, the long range that walls the Tibetan Plateau off from the Tarim Basin. At 6,748 m it is among the highest peaks of the Kunlun, rising in one of the least visited corners of Asia. From above look for a white massif over khaki ridges, with the sandy Tarim lowlands far to the north.","w":"A white massif rising from bare khaki ridges, with the pale desert lowlands of the Tarim Basin far to the north.","f":["The Kunlun runs for more than 2,000 km along the northern rim of the Tibetan Plateau.","North of the range lies the Taklamakan, one of the largest sand deserts in the world."],"q":{"q":"What does the Turkic name Aktag mean?","a":"White mountain","x":["Blue lake","Red rock"]}},"ne-pk-1159105667":{"t":"The sacred giant the Yellow River bends around","s":"Amne Machin is the holy mountain of the Golog Tibetans, a broad massif of ice rising to 6,282 m from the grasslands of Qinghai. The Yellow River, flowing east from its source, swings in a great loop around the range before turning north. Pilgrims walk a long circuit around it. From above look for a white crest of glaciers ringed by high green pastures.","w":"A broad ice massif in rolling green-brown grassland, with a big river looping around it in a wide bend.","f":["Early 20th-century explorers guessed it might rival Everest; modern surveys put it at 6,282 m.","Amne means 'grandfather' in the local Amdo dialect, an honorific for the mountain's protector deity."],"q":{"q":"Which great river loops around the Amne Machin range?","a":"Yellow River","x":["Yangtze","Mekong"]}},"Q724367":{"t":"Siberia's highest peak, twin-topped and white","s":"Belukha is the highest mountain of the Altai and of all Siberia, 4,506 m, straddling the border of Russia and Kazakhstan. Its name comes from its whiteness: two snowy summits joined by a saddle, draped in glaciers that feed the Katun, one of the rivers that form the Ob. From above, look for a bright white crown standing over greener, lower ridges and turquoise lakes.","w":"A twin-summited white massif above green valleys and turquoise lakes, on the Russia–Kazakhstan border in the Altai.","f":["The Katun River begins at the Gebler Glacier on Belukha's southern side.","The Tronov brothers made the first ascent of the eastern summit in 1914.","Belukha is part of the Golden Mountains of Altai, a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"Which river rises from the glaciers of Belukha?","a":"Katun","x":["Yenisei","Irtysh"]}},"Q4062402":{"t":"A lonely ice massif in Tibet's far west","s":"Far out in the dry uplands of western Tibet, the Nganglong massif rises to about 6,720 m, a knot of ice with more than 40 peaks over 6,000 m. It stands roughly 85 km east-north-east of the town of Ali, in a land of gravel plains and salt lakes. From above, look for a cluster of white summits and glaciers set in a vast brown landscape.","w":"A cluster of white summits and glaciers in a vast brown upland of gravel plains, north-east of the town of Ali.","f":["The massif holds 37 glaciers, two of them more than 6 km long.","Modern Chinese maps give about 6,720 m, although older sources still quote 7,315 m."],"q":{"q":"Which town lies about 85 km west-south-west of the Nganglong massif?","a":"Ali","x":["Lhasa","Shigatse"]}},"Q5530346":{"t":"Where the Yangtze is born, in Tibet's ice","s":"The longest river in Asia starts here. Meltwater from glaciers on Geladaindong, the highest peak of the Tanggula Mountains at 6,621 m, gathers into the Tuotuo River, the traditional source of the Yangtze. From this cold, empty upland the water will travel more than 6,000 km to the sea at Shanghai. Look for a white massif of glaciers above braided silver streams on the brown plateau.","w":"A white massif of glaciers above braided silver streams spreading across the brown plateau of central Tibet.","f":["The Jianggendiru Glacier on its south-west flank is usually named as the Yangtze's source.","East of the peak, the Qinghai–Tibet railway crosses the Tanggula range at about 5,070 m."],"q":{"q":"Which great river begins on Geladaindong's glaciers?","a":"Yangtze","x":["Yellow River","Mekong"]}},"Q2102394":{"t":"A remote ice peak of the eastern Karakoram","s":"This summit of the eastern Karakoram, charted at 6,934 m and known as Shahi Kangri, stands above the upper Shyok valley in Ladakh, west of the high, empty plains of Aksai Chin. It is a world of ice and bare rock: glaciers pour down between brown ridges, and the braided Shyok threads its gravel bed below. The higher Saser Kangri group rises to the south.","w":"Glaciers and white crests above a braided grey river valley, with bare brown plateaus stretching away to the east.","f":["Saser Kangri I, the highest summit of the neighbouring Saser group, reaches about 7,670 m.","The Shyok River, flowing below, is a major tributary of the Indus."],"q":{"q":"The Shyok River below this peak is a tributary of which river?","a":"Indus","x":["Ganges","Brahmaputra"]}},"Q2118899":{"t":"The biggest ice field of Tibet's empty north","s":"In the heart of the Changtang, the high, cold and almost empty north of Tibet, Purog Kangri rises to 6,929 m above a dome of ice some 400 km² in area. It is the largest ice field of the northern Tibetan Plateau. From above it looks like a white starfish on a brown plain, its glacier arms reaching out toward shallow lakes and salt flats.","w":"A white, star-shaped ice field with radiating glacier lobes on the brown plateau, surrounded by lakes and salt flats.","f":["In 2000 Chinese and American scientists drilled ice cores here, the longest 213 m deep.","The ice field holds about 52 km³ of ice."],"q":{"q":"In which remote region of Tibet does Purog Kangri lie?","a":"The Changtang","x":["Kham","Amdo"]}},"Q4848635":{"t":"The unclimbed crown of a hidden Tibetan range","s":"In the wet, deeply cut south-east of Tibet rises the Kangri Garpo range, and Bairiga, 6,882 m, is its highest peak. It has never been climbed. The range was so little known that until 2009 none of its 47 summits above 6,000 m had been reached. From above look for a chain of white peaks feeding long glaciers, their tongues reaching down toward dark forested valleys.","w":"A chain of white peaks with long glaciers reaching down toward dark, forested valleys in south-eastern Tibet.","f":["The botanist-explorer Frank Kingdon-Ward photographed the peak in 1933, when it was known as Choembo.","The Lhagu Glacier, the largest in the range, is about 30 km long."],"q":{"q":"Bairiga is the highest peak of which range?","a":"Kangri Garpo","x":["Nyenchen Tanglha","Gangdise"]}},"Q572801":{"t":"A modest mountain with famously savage weather","s":"At 1,917 m Mount Washington is small by world standards, yet it is the highest peak in the north-eastern United States and notorious for its weather. Storm tracks from several directions converge over the Presidential Range, and in April 1934 observers on the summit measured a gust of 372 km/h. From above look for a bare grey summit ridge above New Hampshire's dark green forests, often white from October to May.","w":"A bare grey summit ridge above endless dark forest in northern New Hampshire, the tallest of a chain of treeless peaks.","f":["The 1934 gust of 231 mph stood as the world's strongest measured wind for over 60 years.","The Mount Washington Cog Railway, opened in 1869, was the world's first mountain-climbing cog railway.","The harsh climate keeps the treeline here at only about 1,300 m."],"q":{"q":"What record did Mount Washington's summit set in 1934?","a":"Strongest measured wind gust","x":["Lowest temperature","Deepest snowfall"]}},"Q1858056":{"t":"The finish line of the Appalachian Trail","s":"Katahdin rises alone from the forests and lakes of northern Maine, a granite massif of 1,606 m with a bare, bouldery top. Its name means 'the greatest mountain' in the Penobscot language. For hikers it is the northern end of the Appalachian Trail, which runs about 3,500 km from Georgia. From above, look for its grey summit plateau and sharp ridges above dark green woods.","w":"A lone grey granite massif rising from dark forest dotted with countless lakes in northern Maine.","f":["Henry David Thoreau climbed toward its summit in 1846 and wrote about it in The Maine Woods.","Former governor Percival Baxter bought the land around it and gave it to Maine as a park.","The trail's southern end is Springer Mountain in Georgia."],"q":{"q":"Which long-distance trail ends on Katahdin?","a":"Appalachian Trail","x":["Pacific Crest Trail","Continental Divide Trail"]}},"Q748355":{"t":"A lone volcano ruling northern California","s":"Mount Shasta stands alone, a huge white cone rising some 3,000 m above the surrounding country of northern California. At 4,322 m it is the second-highest peak of the Cascades and the largest of their stratovolcanoes by volume. Look for a smaller cone, Shastina, bulging from its western flank; on a clear day the pair can be seen from well over 100 km away.","w":"A lone white volcanic cone with a smaller cone on its western shoulder, rising far above forested plateaus.","f":["Shastina, its satellite cone, would rank among the Cascades' tallest volcanoes on its own.","Its Whitney Glacier is the longest glacier in California."],"q":{"q":"What is the name of Shasta's satellite cone?","a":"Shastina","x":["Lassen Peak","Black Butte"]}},"Q2078034":{"t":"A wall of peaks with no foothills at all","s":"The Tetons rise straight out of the flat valley of Jackson Hole with no foothills in between, and Grand Teton, 4,199 m, is the sharpest tooth among them. The range stands about 2,100 m above the valley floor, pushed up along a fault that is still active. From above look for a jagged grey line of peaks, with the blue ovals of Jackson Lake and smaller glacial lakes along its eastern foot.","w":"A short, jagged range rising abruptly from a flat valley, with lakes along its eastern foot, just south of Yellowstone.","f":["French-speaking fur trappers called the peaks les trois tétons, 'the three breasts'.","The Teton Range is among the youngest in the Rocky Mountains, less than 10 million years old.","Yellowstone National Park lies just to the north."],"q":{"q":"Which valley lies at the eastern foot of the Tetons?","a":"Jackson Hole","x":["Yosemite Valley","Sun Valley"]}},"Q275662":{"t":"Portland's snowy volcano, skiable even in summer","s":"Mount Hood is Oregon's highest peak, about 3,400 m, a sharp snowy volcano some 80 km east of Portland that crowns the city's skyline on clear days. It is still considered potentially active, with steam vents near the summit. On its south slope the Palmer Snowfield keeps skiers busy well into summer, and Timberline Lodge, built in the 1930s, sits just below the treeline.","w":"A sharp, lone white cone rising above dark forest, east of Portland and south of the Columbia River Gorge.","f":["A British naval officer named it in 1792 after Admiral Samuel Hood.","Its last significant eruptions came in the 1780s, shortly before Europeans arrived.","Timberline Lodge played the exterior of the hotel in the film The Shining."],"q":{"q":"Which city sees Mount Hood on its skyline, about 80 km away?","a":"Portland","x":["Seattle","Boise"]}},"Q756244":{"t":"A snowy giant east of Santiago","s":"Tupungato is a massive, snow-capped volcano over 6,500 m high, straddling the border of Chile and Argentina east of Santiago. It is an old, eroded giant; its smaller, younger neighbour, Tupungatito, has erupted repeatedly in the last two centuries. From above look for a broad white massif rising from a tangle of dry brown Andean ridges, with green vineyards in the valleys on the Argentine side.","w":"A broad white massif on the Chile–Argentina border, rising above dry brown ridges about 80 km east of Santiago.","f":["Matthias Zurbriggen and Stuart Vines made the first ascent in 1897.","The Argentine wine district of Tupungato, in the Uco Valley, takes its name from the volcano."],"q":{"q":"Who made the first ascent of Tupungato in 1897?","a":"Zurbriggen and Vines","x":["Whymper and Carrel","Hillary and Tenzing"]}},"Q233836":{"t":"The highest volcano on Earth, in the driest desert","s":"Ojos del Salado is the highest volcano on Earth, nearly 6,900 m, rising on the border of Chile and Argentina at the edge of the Atacama. The air here is so dry that the mountain carries only thin patches of snow despite its height. Steaming vents near the summit show it is not extinct. From above look for a broad, pale massif in a moonscape of ochre, rust and white salt flats.","w":"A broad, pale volcanic massif in a moonscape of ochre and rust, with white salt flats spread around it.","f":["It is also the highest point of Chile.","A small crater lake near the summit, at about 6,390 m, is often called the highest lake in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which country's highest point is Ojos del Salado?","a":"Chile","x":["Peru","Bolivia"]}},"Q214916":{"t":"An Inca shrine on a volcano in the sky","s":"Llullaillaco rises to 6,739 m on the border of Chile and Argentina, in one of the driest places on Earth. Five centuries ago the Inca climbed to its summit to build a shrine, and in 1999 archaeologists found three remarkably preserved Inca burials there — the highest archaeological site in the world. From above look for a lone cone with thin snow above rust-red desert and salt flats.","w":"A lone, sharp volcanic cone with thin snow patches above rust-red desert and bright salt flats in the Atacama.","f":["The three Inca children found on the summit are now kept in a museum in Salta, Argentina.","The air is so dry that snow on the summit often turns straight to vapour without melting."],"q":{"q":"What did archaeologists find on Llullaillaco's summit in 1999?","a":"Inca burials","x":["Spanish silver","Dinosaur bones"]}},"Q148521":{"t":"The snowy guardian on La Paz's skyline","s":"Illimani is the mountain that seems to appear in every photo of La Paz: a long white wall with several summits, towering over the city and the high plain of El Alto. At about 6,400 m it is the highest peak of the Cordillera Real and the second-highest in Bolivia. From above look for a massive glacier-clad ridge rising south-east of the city, with the dark gash of La Paz's canyon below.","w":"A long, glacier-clad ridge with several summits, south-east of La Paz and the flat, built-up plateau of El Alto.","f":["Martin Conway and two Alpine guides climbed its south summit in 1898.","La Paz and El Alto together form one of the highest large urban areas on Earth, between about 3,600 and 4,100 m."],"q":{"q":"Which city lies at the foot of Illimani?","a":"La Paz","x":["Cusco","Quito"]}},"Q272593":{"t":"Bolivia's highest summit, a lone volcanic cone","s":"Nevado Sajama rises alone from the Altiplano, a white-topped cone of 6,542 m and the highest point of Bolivia. Just to the west, on the Chilean border, two more volcanoes stand side by side, Parinacota and Pomerape, known together as the Payachatas. On Sajama's lower slopes grows one of the highest forests on Earth, made of small, twisted queñua trees.","w":"A lone, snow-capped volcanic cone on the brown Altiplano, with twin white cones on the Chilean border to its west.","f":["Sajama National Park, created in 1939, was Bolivia's first national park.","Ice cores drilled on its summit in 1997 reach back roughly 25,000 years."],"q":{"q":"What name is given to the twin volcanoes west of Sajama?","a":"Payachatas","x":["Licancabur","Aucanquilcha"]}},"Q758574":{"t":"A volcano once mined almost to its summit","s":"Aucanquilcha is a big, pale volcano of 6,176 m in the Atacama highlands of northern Chile, and for decades it was a workplace. Sulphur was dug near its summit, at about 5,950 m — for years the highest mine in the world. In 1972 a switchback road for 20-tonne trucks was built almost to the top. From above look for pale yellow-grey slopes above bright white salt flats.","w":"A broad pale volcano in the dry highlands near the Bolivian border, with white salt flats spread at its foot.","f":["The mine opened in 1913, and at first llamas carried the sulphur down the mountain.","The sulphur was refined far below at the camp of Amincha, about 3,900 m, linked by an aerial cableway."],"q":{"q":"What was mined near the summit of Aucanquilcha?","a":"Sulphur","x":["Copper","Silver"]}},"Q777794":{"t":"The sacred ice peak beside Rainbow Mountain","s":"Ausangate is the great white summit of southern Peru, 6,372 m, rising about 100 km south-east of Cusco. For the Quechua people it is an apu, a mountain spirit, and every year thousands of pilgrims gather in a valley near it for the festival of Qoyllur Rit'i. Close by stands Vinicunca, the 'Rainbow Mountain', striped in red, ochre and green.","w":"A massive white peak rising above brown puna grassland and small turquoise lakes, south-east of Cusco.","f":["Ausangate is the highest peak of the Cordillera Vilcanota.","Qoyllur Rit'i, held each year before Corpus Christi, blends Andean and Catholic traditions.","Vinicunca's colours come from layers of mineral-rich sediment."],"q":{"q":"Which famous striped mountain lies near Ausangate?","a":"Vinicunca","x":["Huayna Picchu","El Misti"]}},"Q946035":{"t":"The largest ice cap in the tropics","s":"Coropuna is a broad volcanic massif in southern Peru, more than 6,300 m high, crowned by several summits and a sheet of ice. That ice, around 44 km², is the most extensive in the tropics, and its meltwater feeds valleys and farms far below. From above look for a big white cap sprawled over a dark volcanic plateau, north-west of Arequipa.","w":"A sprawling white ice cap with several summits on a dark volcanic plateau, north-west of Arequipa.","f":["Coropuna is the highest volcano in Peru.","The Inca held it sacred, and ruins of their shrines lie on its slopes.","Scientists expect its ice to keep supplying water for roughly another century."],"q":{"q":"What record does Coropuna's ice cap hold?","a":"Largest ice cap in the tropics","x":["Oldest ice in South America","Highest glacier on Earth"]}},"Q200935":{"t":"The highest summit in the tropics","s":"Huascarán is the highest mountain in Peru and in all of the Earth's tropics, 6,768 m. It crowns the Cordillera Blanca, the 'white range', the most heavily glaciated range in the tropics. From above look for a twin-topped white massif towering over the green valley of the Santa River, with turquoise glacial lakes tucked into the side valleys.","w":"A twin-summited white massif above a long green valley, in a chain of ice peaks running north–south in central Peru.","f":["Annie Peck reached its north summit in 1908, a landmark climb for women in mountaineering.","Huascarán National Park became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1985.","The Cordillera Blanca holds hundreds of glacial lakes."],"q":{"q":"Which range does Huascarán crown?","a":"Cordillera Blanca","x":["Cordillera Real","Cordillera Vilcanota"]}},"Q14081":{"t":"The summit farthest from the centre of the Earth","s":"Measured from sea level, Chimborazo's 6,267 m is nowhere near Everest. But the Earth bulges at the equator, and this Ecuadorian volcano stands just south of it, so its summit is the point on the planet's surface farthest from the Earth's centre — about 2 km farther out than Everest's. From above look for a broad, glacier-capped dome rising alone over the highlands.","w":"A broad, glacier-capped volcanic dome standing alone above green and brown highlands, south-west of Quito.","f":["In 1802 Alexander von Humboldt climbed to nearly 5,900 m on its slopes, then a world altitude record.","Edward Whymper and his guides Jean-Antoine and Louis Carrel made the first ascent in 1880."],"q":{"q":"What makes Chimborazo's summit special?","a":"Farthest point from Earth's centre","x":["Highest point of the Andes","Highest active volcano"]}},"Q27489229":{"t":"A pale limestone tableland above the Mediterranean","s":"Between the Mediterranean and Egypt's Western Desert, the Libyan Plateau spreads as a pale, stony limestone tableland cut by dry wadis. Along the coast it ends in a steep escarpment above a thin strip of shore. From above look for bleached sand-and-stone colours broken only by winding ravines, with the deep blue sea to the north and emptiness to the south.","w":"A pale, flat stony plateau cut by dry ravines, ending in a steep escarpment above the blue Mediterranean.","f":["To the south-east lies the Qattara Depression, whose floor sinks to about 133 m below sea level.","The plateau's limestone formed on the floor of a sea millions of years ago.","Siwa Oasis, famous in antiquity for its oracle of Amun, lies at the plateau's southern edge."],"q":{"q":"Which deep depression lies south-east of the Libyan Plateau?","a":"Qattara Depression","x":["Danakil Depression","Turpan Depression"]}},"Q1881042":{"t":"India's mineral heartland of forests and waterfalls","s":"East-central India rises into the Chota Nagpur Plateau, a hilly tableland of ancient rock patched with sal forest. It holds much of India's coal and iron ore, so from the air you may spot black scars of open-cast mines and the haze of steel towns. In the monsoon its rivers tumble off the plateau edges in waterfalls, and reservoirs fill the valleys.","w":"Rolling green-brown hills and forest patches, with dark open-cast mines, reservoirs and a few hazy industrial towns.","f":["Jamshedpur, one of India's first planned industrial cities, grew around a steel works founded here in 1907.","The Damodar valley on the plateau has long been one of India's main coalfields.","Its sal forests are home to many of India's Adivasi communities."],"q":{"q":"Which steel city grew up on the Chota Nagpur Plateau from 1907?","a":"Jamshedpur","x":["Kanpur","Surat"]}},"Q2740752":{"t":"The highland that keeps the rain off the sertão","s":"In north-eastern Brazil, just inland from the green Atlantic coast, the land steps up onto the Borborema Plateau, rolling uplands several hundred metres high. Moist ocean winds drop their rain on its seaward side, so beyond it lies the dry sertão, a land of thorny caatinga scrub. From above you can often see the change: greener to the east, dusty grey-brown to the west.","w":"Rolling uplands inland from the coast: greener on the eastern side, grey-brown dry scrub to the west.","f":["Campina Grande, on the plateau, hosts one of Brazil's biggest São João festivals every June.","Caatinga, the dry thorny forest beyond, is found only in Brazil."],"q":{"q":"Which dry scrubland lies west of the Borborema Plateau?","a":"Caatinga","x":["Pampa","Pantanal"]}},"Q916997":{"t":"South Africa's vast 'land of thirst'","s":"The Karoo covers a huge part of South Africa's interior: a semi-desert of flat plains and flat-topped hills capped with dark dolerite. Its name is usually traced to a Khoekhoe word for a dry, thirsty land. From above it is a palette of khaki, rust and grey, crossed by long straight roads and the occasional green thread of a river.","w":"Flat khaki plains dotted with flat-topped dark hills, crossed by long straight roads and dry riverbeds.","f":["Its rocks are rich in fossils of mammal-like reptiles more than 250 million years old.","The MeerKAT radio telescope, part of the Square Kilometre Array project, stands in the Karoo."],"q":{"q":"Which giant radio-telescope project is being built in the Karoo?","a":"Square Kilometre Array","x":["Atacama Large Millimeter Array","Event Horizon Telescope"]}},"Q482274":{"t":"Cameroon's water tower, draining three ways","s":"Across central Cameroon runs the Adamawa Plateau, a high grassy upland mostly around 1,000 m, dotted with old volcanic cones and crater lakes. It is often called Cameroon's water tower: rivers born here flow north toward Lake Chad, west to the Niger by way of the Benue, and south toward the Atlantic and the Congo basin. From above look for green savanna, wooded valleys and red earth.","w":"A green, rolling upland of savanna and wooded valleys, dotted with small volcanic cones and crater lakes.","f":["It is named after Modibo Adama, a Fulani leader who founded an emirate here in the early 19th century.","Ngaoundéré, the main town on the plateau, is the northern end of the railway from Douala."],"q":{"q":"Why is the Adamawa Plateau called Cameroon's 'water tower'?","a":"Many rivers rise there","x":["It stores water in dams","It has Africa's highest rainfall"]}},"Q776345":{"t":"Angola's high plateau, where the Okavango is born","s":"The Bié Plateau is the high heart of Angola, rolling grassland and woodland mostly 1,500–1,800 m above the sea. Its rains feed rivers that set off in every direction: the Kwanza to the Atlantic, the Cunene south toward Namibia, and the Cubango, which becomes the Okavango and ends in the sands of Botswana. From above look for green-and-ochre uplands threaded with meandering rivers.","w":"Rolling green-and-ochre uplands and woodland, laced with meandering rivers and wetlands in central Angola.","f":["The Okavango never reaches the sea; it spreads into a vast inland delta in Botswana.","Huambo, on the plateau at about 1,700 m, is one of Angola's largest cities."],"q":{"q":"Which famous river starts on the Bié Plateau as the Cubango?","a":"Okavango","x":["Limpopo","Orange"]}},"Q1321468":{"t":"An old worn plateau riddled with caves and springs","s":"The Ozarks are not really mountains but an old plateau, deeply cut by rivers into forested hills. The limestone beneath is riddled with caves and springs, some of the largest in North America. From above look for dark green, crinkled hill country with winding clear rivers and big, branching reservoirs — dammed valleys shaped like spilled ink.","w":"Crinkled, dark green forested hills cut by winding rivers, with large reservoirs spreading in branching, fingered shapes.","f":["Missouri, much of it in the Ozarks, has more than 7,000 known caves.","Big Spring in Missouri releases around a billion litres of water on an average day.","The Buffalo River in Arkansas became the first national river in the United States, in 1972."],"q":{"q":"Which Ozark river became the first US 'national river' in 1972?","a":"Buffalo River","x":["White River","Arkansas River"]}},"Q4492616":{"t":"A rolling lava plateau of lakes and pine forest","s":"Between the Coast Mountains and the Rockies, central British Columbia flattens into the Fraser Plateau, a rolling upland of pine forest, meadows and countless lakes, much of it resting on old lava flows. Along its eastern edge the Fraser River cuts a deep trench on its way to the Pacific. From above look for a green-grey carpet speckled with lakes and slashed by that sharp river canyon.","w":"A rolling green-grey upland speckled with lakes, cut on its eastern side by the deep trench of the Fraser River.","f":["The Fraser, about 1,375 km long, is the longest river entirely within British Columbia.","In the 2000s a mountain pine beetle outbreak killed vast areas of pine here, turning forests red and then grey."],"q":{"q":"Which ocean does the Fraser River finally reach?","a":"Pacific","x":["Arctic","Atlantic"]}},"Q15241934":{"t":"The continent's ancient shield rock, full of lakes","s":"The Laurentian Upland is the worn-down core of the Canadian Shield, a vast sweep of ancient rock around Hudson Bay and the Great Lakes. Ice-age glaciers scraped it bare and left behind a maze of lakes, bogs and rocky knolls. From above, especially near Lake Superior, look for dark forest scattered with thousands of lakes shining like broken glass.","w":"Dark forest and bare rock scattered with countless lakes, especially around the shores of Lake Superior.","f":["Much of its rock is more than 2.5 billion years old, among the oldest in North America.","Minnesota's Mesabi Range, on the upland, has long been one of the world's great iron-ore districts.","Lake Superior, on its southern edge, is the largest freshwater lake in the world by area."],"q":{"q":"Which is the largest freshwater lake by area, on the upland's edge?","a":"Lake Superior","x":["Lake Victoria","Lake Huron"]}},"Q293730":{"t":"A land of wind-blown yellow dust, carved into gullies","s":"The Loess Plateau is built of dust: fine yellow silt blown in from the deserts of Central Asia over hundreds of thousands of years, piled in places more than 100 m thick. Rain carves it easily, so from above it looks like a crumpled yellow-brown sheet shot through with branching gullies and neat terraced fields. The silt washing off it gives the Yellow River its colour and its name.","w":"Crumpled yellow-brown land cut by countless branching gullies, with contour-terraced hillsides and a muddy river.","f":["The plateau covers about 640,000 km², larger than Spain.","Millions of people have lived in yaodong, homes dug into the loess hillsides.","A restoration project from the 1990s terraced slopes and replanted hillsides, sharply cutting erosion."],"q":{"q":"What gives the Yellow River its colour?","a":"Silt from the Loess Plateau","x":["Algae in the water","Sulphur springs"]}},"Q1126906":{"t":"Gentle hills of black earth, where great rivers begin","s":"South of Moscow the land swells gently into the Central Russian Upland, hills only a couple of hundred metres high but important on the map: the Oka, the Don and tributaries of the Dnieper all start here. Its soils include rich black chernozem. From above look for a patchwork of huge fields cut by branching, tree-lined ravines and, in the south, vast open-pit iron mines.","w":"A vast patchwork of fields cut by tree-lined, branching ravines, with huge grey open-pit mines in the south.","f":["The Kursk Magnetic Anomaly beneath it is one of the largest iron-ore deposits on Earth.","The Don rises near Novomoskovsk, south of Moscow, and flows about 1,900 km to the Sea of Azov.","Its ravines grow fast because the soft soils erode easily once the forest is cleared."],"q":{"q":"Which river rises here and flows to the Sea of Azov?","a":"Don","x":["Volga","Dnieper"]}},"Q1286":{"t":"A white crescent across the heart of Europe","s":"From cruise height the Alps look like crumpled white paper laid across Europe: sharp ridges, dark valleys and glaciers poured into the gaps. The arc runs about 1,200 km, from the Mediterranean near Nice almost to Vienna. Even Mont Blanc, the highest summit at about 4,800 m, sits some six kilometres below you.","w":"White ridges broken by deep valleys, with long lakes such as Garda, Como and Geneva at their feet.","f":["The Aletsch Glacier in Switzerland is the longest in the Alps: about 20 km of ice.","Around 14 million people live in the Alps.","The Gotthard Base Tunnel, opened in 2016, runs 57 km beneath the Alps — the longest railway tunnel in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which is the longest glacier in the Alps?","a":"The Aletsch Glacier","x":["The Mer de Glace","The Pasterze"]}},"Q5451":{"t":"The highest wall on Earth, almost at your altitude","s":"No other mountains come this close to your wing. The Himalayas rise from the green plains of India in steps — foothills, forested ridges, then a white wall — and Everest, at 8,849 m, reaches to within about two kilometres of cruise height. The range is still growing as India keeps pushing north into Asia.","w":"A white wall of peaks between the green plains of India and the brown, high Tibetan Plateau.","f":["Himalaya means “abode of snow” in Sanskrit.","Ten of the world’s fourteen peaks above 8,000 m are in the Himalayas; the other four are in the Karakoram.","Marine fossils are found near Everest’s summit: its top layers are limestone laid down on an ancient sea floor."],"q":{"q":"What does the Sanskrit name “Himalaya” mean?","a":"Abode of snow","x":["Roof of the world","Mountain of the gods"]}},"Q5456":{"t":"A 7,000-km spine down the whole of South America","s":"Seven thousand kilometres long, the Andes change character as you fly along them: glaciers and fjords in Patagonia, a wall of snow above Santiago, and in Bolivia a high plateau, the Altiplano, where Salar de Uyuni lies like a sheet of white paper. No mountain chain on any continent is longer.","w":"Snowy peaks and volcanic cones on one side, desert or rainforest on the other; in Bolivia, dazzling white salt flats.","f":["Aconcagua, at 6,961 m, is the highest mountain outside Asia.","Salar de Uyuni is so flat that it is used to calibrate satellite altimeters.","La Paz, in the Bolivian Andes at around 3,600 m, is the highest seat of government in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Andes?","a":"Aconcagua","x":["Chimborazo","Huascarán"]}},"Q5463":{"t":"Where North America’s rivers choose their ocean","s":"Somewhere along these ridges runs the Continental Divide: rain on one side flows to the Pacific, on the other towards the Atlantic or the Arctic. From the air the Rockies are range after range of grey-brown peaks, dark with pine forest and streaked with snow well into summer. They stretch some 4,800 km, from northern British Columbia to New Mexico.","w":"Parallel ridges of grey rock and dark forest, snowfields on the peaks, and flat sagebrush basins between the ranges.","f":["Mount Elbert in Colorado, 4,401 m, is the highest peak of the Rockies.","Yellowstone, in the Wyoming Rockies, holds about half of the world’s active geysers.","By mountain standards the Rockies are young: their main uplift began around 80 million years ago."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Rocky Mountains?","a":"Mount Elbert","x":["Pikes Peak","Longs Peak"]}},"Q42070":{"t":"Stony, cold and vast — a desert of dinosaur eggs","s":"Expect beige, grey and rust rather than golden dunes: most of the Gobi is bare rock and gravel, scored by dry riverbeds. It is a cold desert, lying high on a plateau across Mongolia and northern China, with winters that can drop below −30 °C. Sand seas do exist, in patches, and from above they stand out as golden ripples in the grey.","w":"Flat grey-brown gravel plains with dry riverbeds, patches of golden dunes and white salt pans.","f":["In 1923 explorers at the Gobi’s Flaming Cliffs found nests of eggs that were the first to be recognised as dinosaur eggs.","In Badain Jaran, in the Chinese Gobi, dunes rise to about 500 m, with small lakes lying between them.","Wild Bactrian camels survive in the Gobi; they are a separate species, not runaway domestic camels."],"q":{"q":"What covers most of the Gobi’s surface?","a":"Bare rock and gravel","x":["Sand dunes","Salt marsh"]}},"Q6583":{"t":"Almost the size of the USA — and once green","s":"You can fly for hours and still be over the Sahara. At about 9 million km² it is the largest hot desert on Earth, painted in every shade from white salt to black basalt and orange sand. Dunes cover only part of it; the rest is stony plateaus, gravel plains and dark volcanic mountains.","w":"Endless tan and orange ground: look for rippled dune fields, dark volcanic massifs and pale dry lakebeds.","f":["Until about 5,000 years ago much of the Sahara was savanna with lakes; rock paintings show giraffes and cattle.","Saharan dust blown across the Atlantic helps fertilise the Amazon rainforest.","“Sahara” simply comes from the Arabic word for “desert”, so “Sahara Desert” says the same thing twice."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Sahara” mean in Arabic?","a":"Desert","x":["Sand","Sun"]}},"Q5474":{"t":"Celestial Mountains around a lake that never freezes","s":"Tian Shan means “Celestial Mountains” in Chinese, and from above the name fits: snowy ranges roll for some 2,500 km across Central Asia. In their Kyrgyz heart lies Issyk-Kul, a deep blue lake about 180 km long sitting at 1,600 m. Despite the altitude and harsh winters, it never freezes — its water is slightly salty.","w":"Rows of snowy ridges running east–west; north of them, the long blue oval of Issyk-Kul.","f":["Jengish Chokusu, also called Victory Peak, is the highest summit of the Tian Shan at 7,439 m.","Issyk-Kul is one of the deepest lakes in the world, reaching about 670 m.","Khan Tengri glows pinkish-red at sunset because its summit pyramid is made of marble."],"q":{"q":"Which Tian Shan lake never freezes despite its altitude?","a":"Issyk-Kul","x":["Song-Kul","Balkhash"]}},"Q35600":{"t":"A modest ridge that divides two continents","s":"Don’t expect peaks: the Urals are so old and worn down that from cruise height they look like low, forested wrinkles. Yet this line, running some 2,500 km from the Arctic to the steppes of Kazakhstan, is where Europe traditionally ends and Asia begins. Around Yekaterinburg the mountains are so low that the Trans-Siberian crosses them almost unnoticed.","w":"A band of dark taiga and low ridges running north–south; east of Yekaterinburg and Chelyabinsk, a scatter of lakes.","f":["The Urals rose around 300 million years ago, when Europe and Siberia collided — long before the Alps existed.","The highest peak, Narodnaya, reaches only 1,895 m.","The malachite columns of St Isaac’s Cathedral in St Petersburg are faced with stone from the Urals."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Urals?","a":"Narodnaya","x":["Yamantau","Manaraga"]}},"Q5477":{"t":"A snowy wall between the Black Sea and the Caspian","s":"Between two seas rises a single, sharp-edged barrier: the Greater Caucasus runs about 1,200 km from the Black Sea to the Caspian, with a white crest along its spine. In the east, over Dagestan, it breaks into a maze of deep canyons and high plateaus. Elbrus, a dormant volcano with two summits, is usually counted as Europe’s highest peak at 5,642 m.","w":"A continuous line of snowy peaks running north-west to south-east between two seas.","f":["Dagestan means “land of mountains”, and more than 30 languages are spoken in its valleys.","Elbrus’s two summits stand about 1.5 km apart and differ in height by only about 20 m.","Kazbek, 5,054 m, is also an old volcano; its white cone rises above the Darial Gorge."],"q":{"q":"Which peak is usually counted as Europe’s highest?","a":"Elbrus","x":["Mont Blanc","Kazbek"]}},"Q60140":{"t":"A former island continent that crashed into Asia","s":"The land below you was once an island continent. Tens of millions of years ago it drifted north across the ocean and rammed into Asia, crumpling the crust into the Himalayas — and it is still pushing, a few centimetres a year. From above, look for the flat Ganges plain in the north and the old, worn Deccan plateau in the south.","w":"Flat, intensely farmed plains in the north; brown plateaus, rivers and reservoirs further south; at night, countless clusters of lights.","f":["The Indian plate still moves north by about 5 cm a year.","The Deccan Traps, layers of lava up to 2 km thick, poured out about 66 million years ago.","Around a quarter of all people on Earth live on the subcontinent."],"q":{"q":"Which mountains rose when India collided with Asia?","a":"The Himalayas","x":["The Urals","The Zagros"]}},"Q31945":{"t":"The world’s largest peninsula, tilted towards the Gulf","s":"Seen from above, Arabia is a giant tilted slab: mountains rise along the Red Sea in the west, and the land slopes gently east to the Gulf. In between lie gravel plains, black lava fields and the dunes of the Empty Quarter. Saudi Arabia, which fills most of the peninsula, has no permanent rivers at all.","w":"Beige plains and orange dune seas, dark mountain ridges along the Red Sea coast and black lava fields inland.","f":["At more than 3 million km², Arabia is the largest peninsula on Earth.","Arabia is slowly pulling away from Africa: the Red Sea widens by about a centimetre a year.","The Empty Quarter in its south is the largest continuous sand desert on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Which is the largest peninsula in the world?","a":"The Arabian Peninsula","x":["The Indochina Peninsula","Scandinavia"]}},"Q27611":{"t":"A land bridge built by volcanoes","s":"Two oceans, one narrow strip of land: on a clear day you may see water on both sides of the plane. A chain of volcanoes runs along the Pacific side, many of them near-perfect cones, while the Caribbean coast is low, green and swampy. When this land bridge closed about 3 million years ago, animals began crossing between the Americas.","w":"A string of volcanic cones along the Pacific coast, with lakes such as Nicaragua and Atitlán shining between them.","f":["Lake Nicaragua is the largest lake in Central America, and bull sharks swim in it.","Tajumulco in Guatemala, 4,220 m, is the highest peak in Central America.","Guatemala alone has more than 30 volcanoes."],"q":{"q":"Which Central American lake is home to bull sharks?","a":"Lake Nicaragua","x":["Lake Atitlán","Lake Yojoa"]}},"Q185605":{"t":"Antarctica’s long finger pointing at South America","s":"Ice-capped mountains, glaciers spilling into the sea and a coast shattered into islands: the Antarctic Peninsula reaches some 1,300 km north towards Cape Horn. It is the mildest and most visited corner of the continent, and one of the fastest-warming places on Earth. Along its eastern side, vast ice shelves float on the Weddell Sea.","w":"Jagged, snow-covered peaks along a narrow spine, and glaciers flowing into a dark sea dotted with icebergs.","f":["In 2002 part of the Larsen Ice Shelf larger than Luxembourg broke apart in about five weeks.","The Drake Passage between the peninsula and Cape Horn is about 800 km wide.","Most tourists who reach Antarctica come here, on ships from Ushuaia."],"q":{"q":"Which sea lies east of the Antarctic Peninsula?","a":"The Weddell Sea","x":["The Ross Sea","The Amundsen Sea"]}},"Q319671":{"t":"A 3,500-km wall holding back the polar ice","s":"Dark rock peaks break through the white here, marking one of the longest mountain ranges on Earth. The Transantarctic Mountains cross the whole continent, splitting East Antarctica from West, and hold back the thick East Antarctic ice sheet like a dam. Glaciers squeeze through the gaps between the summits and flow down to the Ross Ice Shelf.","w":"Rows of dark, flat-topped peaks poking through the ice, with broad glaciers flowing between them.","f":["Both Shackleton and Scott reached the polar plateau by climbing the Beardmore Glacier, about 200 km long.","Bones of a crested dinosaur, Cryolophosaurus, were found high on Mount Kirkpatrick in this range.","Coal seams and fossil Glossopteris leaves found here show that Antarctica was once forested."],"q":{"q":"Which glacier did Shackleton and Scott climb to the polar plateau?","a":"The Beardmore Glacier","x":["The Axel Heiberg Glacier","The Lambert Glacier"]}},"Q192583":{"t":"Australia’s green seam between coast and outback","s":"This is less a wall than a long, worn backbone: the Great Dividing Range runs some 3,500 km down eastern Australia, and from the air you notice the change of colour more than the mountains. On the coast side lie green forest and river valleys; inland, the land fades to dry brown plains. Its western rivers flow away from the nearby sea, towards the Murray–Darling or the dry heart of the continent.","w":"Green, forested ridges near the east coast; inland of them, farmland and scrub turning to brown plains.","f":["Mount Kosciuszko, 2,228 m, is its highest point and the highest in mainland Australia.","The Blue Mountains near Sydney are part of the range; their blue haze comes from eucalyptus oil in the air."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Great Dividing Range?","a":"Mount Kosciuszko","x":["Mount Townsend","Mount Bogong"]}},"Q21195":{"t":"A peninsula still rising after the Ice Age","s":"From cruise height Scandinavia is a mosaic of dark forest and countless lakes, with mountains along the Norwegian side and deep fjords cutting into the Atlantic coast. All of it once lay under an ice sheet up to about 3 km thick. Freed of that weight, the land is still rebounding — in places by nearly a centimetre a year.","w":"Dark green forest speckled with hundreds of lakes; to the west, snowy mountains and fjords like narrow blue cracks.","f":["Sweden’s High Coast has risen about 285 m since the ice melted, the greatest such rebound known.","Sweden alone has close to 100,000 lakes.","At North Cape the midnight sun shines for more than two months each summer."],"q":{"q":"Why is the land of Scandinavia still rising?","a":"The weight of Ice Age ice has gone","x":["Volcanic activity below it","A collision of tectonic plates"]}},"Q27617163":{"t":"Between India and China, a land of ridges and rivers","s":"Its old name, Indochina, says it all: the land between India and China. From above you can read its structure — long mountain ridges fanning south from the Himalayas, with great rivers in the valleys between. The Mekong, Chao Phraya, Salween and Irrawaddy all run roughly north to south. In the dry season, smoke from burning fields often softens the view.","w":"Parallel green ridges running north–south, brown rivers winding between them, rice fields shining in the valleys.","f":["Cambodia’s Tonle Sap River reverses its flow every rainy season, and the lake it drains swells several times over.","The Mekong flows through or along six countries: China, Myanmar, Laos, Thailand, Cambodia and Vietnam.","Thailand and Vietnam are among the world’s largest rice exporters."],"q":{"q":"Which river reverses its flow every rainy season?","a":"The Tonle Sap","x":["The Chao Phraya","The Irrawaddy"]}},"Q12837":{"t":"A sun-baked tableland at the edge of Europe","s":"On the map Iberia is almost a square, and much of its interior is a lofty tableland, the Meseta, where ochre wheat fields and olive groves run to the horizon. Mountain ranges ring it and cross it, and the Pyrenees seal it off from the rest of Europe. At the southern tip, only about 14 km of sea separate it from Africa.","w":"Ochre and green plains broken by bare, rocky sierras, and reservoirs with branching, finger-like shapes.","f":["Madrid, at about 650 m, is one of the highest capital cities in Europe.","Spain has more than 1,000 large dams, one of the highest counts in the world.","Near Almería, plastic greenhouses cover so much land that they show up as a white patch from space."],"q":{"q":"How wide is the Strait of Gibraltar at its narrowest?","a":"About 14 km","x":["About 4 km","About 40 km"]}},"Q181528":{"t":"The Sahara’s driest corner, where years pass without rain","s":"This eastern part of the Sahara, spread across Egypt, Libya and Sudan, is among the driest places on Earth — some spots go years without a drop of rain. From above it is a study in beige: flat gravel plains, the long parallel dunes of the Great Sand Sea, and the brown sandstone plateau of the Gilf Kebir rising like an island.","w":"Pale sand drawn into long, straight dune lines running north–south, and the dark, flat-topped Gilf Kebir plateau.","f":["A carved piece of yellow Libyan desert glass, found only in this region, sits in a pectoral from Tutankhamun’s tomb.","The Cave of Swimmers in the Gilf Kebir has ancient paintings of people who seem to be swimming — in today’s driest desert."],"q":{"q":"Whose tomb held a jewel set with Libyan desert glass?","a":"Tutankhamun’s","x":["Ramesses II’s","Khufu’s"]}},"Q37056":{"t":"Where Eurasia runs out of land","s":"You are over the very top of Eurasia. Taymyr’s tip, Cape Chelyuskin, is the northernmost point of any continent’s mainland; across a narrow strait lie only the islands of Severnaya Zemlya and the pack ice. Below you: treeless tundra, the low Byrranga Mountains and a web of lakes and rivers that stay frozen for most of the year.","w":"Flat, treeless tundra laced with lakes, and the long, low ridges of the Byrranga Mountains running east–west.","f":["Musk oxen, gone from here for thousands of years, were brought back from Canada and Alaska in the 1970s and thrived.","Taymyr is home to the largest herd of wild reindeer in Eurasia.","Cape Chelyuskin was first reached in 1742 by Semyon Chelyuskin, travelling by dog sled."],"q":{"q":"What is the northernmost point of mainland Eurasia?","a":"Cape Chelyuskin","x":["Cape Dezhnev","North Cape"]}},"Q488266":{"t":"“The end of the land”, freckled with countless lakes","s":"In the Nenets language Yamal means “the end of the land”, and from above the name feels right: a flat, pale tundra reaching north into the Kara Sea, so pocked with lakes that it seems more water than ground. Nenets herders still cross it with their reindeer, travelling hundreds of kilometres each year between summer and winter pastures.","w":"A flat peninsula between the Kara Sea and the Gulf of Ob, scattered with thousands of round lakes.","f":["Beneath Yamal lie some of the largest natural gas fields on Earth.","In 2014 a crater tens of metres wide appeared in the tundra, blown out by gas from thawing permafrost.","The peninsula is about 700 km long."],"q":{"q":"What does “Yamal” mean in the Nenets language?","a":"The end of the land","x":["Land of reindeer","White water"]}},"Q993":{"t":"A land of fire and ice on the edge of the Pacific","s":"White cones, one after another, some trailing a thin plume: Kamchatka has around 30 active volcanoes, one of the densest clusters on Earth. On a clear day you can pick out several at once, rising from dark forest and tundra. The tallest, Klyuchevskaya Sopka, reaches about 4,750 m — the highest active volcano in Eurasia.","w":"Snow-capped volcanic cones in a row along the peninsula’s spine and east coast, often with steam or ash at the top.","f":["The Valley of Geysers, discovered in 1941, is one of the largest geyser fields in the world.","Kamchatka is about 1,200 km long, yet no road links it to the rest of Russia.","The Volcanoes of Kamchatka have been a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1996."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest active volcano in Eurasia?","a":"Klyuchevskaya Sopka","x":["Koryaksky","Shiveluch"]}},"Q18758":{"t":"Asia’s long green tail, reaching for the equator","s":"Mainland Asia ends at the tip of this peninsula, barely 150 km from the equator. From above, its spine is a line of rainforest-covered mountains; on either side the green turns into the neat, dotted rows of oil palm and rubber plantations. Off the west coast runs the Strait of Malacca, busy with ships day and night.","w":"A forested mountain spine down the middle, geometric plantations on both sides, and ships dotting the Strait of Malacca to the west.","f":["Tanjung Piai, at the peninsula’s tip, is the southernmost point of mainland Asia.","The Strait of Malacca is one of the busiest shipping lanes in the world.","Gunung Tahan, 2,187 m, is the highest peak of Peninsular Malaysia."],"q":{"q":"Which is the southernmost point of mainland Asia?","a":"Tanjung Piai","x":["Cape Cà Mau","Cape Comorin"]}},"Q319288":{"t":"Africa’s rooftop, where the Nile gets its flood","s":"A vast green-and-brown plateau, mostly above 1,500 m, rises out of the surrounding lowlands like a fortress. Its flat tops are farmed right to the edge, then drop into canyons — the Blue Nile Gorge is up to 1.5 km deep. Every summer, monsoon rain on these highlands sends down the flood that has fed Egypt for millennia.","w":"A high tableland of fields and eucalyptus groves, split by deep brown canyons; Lake Tana shines to the north.","f":["Most of the Nile water that reaches Egypt falls as rain on the Ethiopian Highlands.","Arabica coffee comes from here: it still grows wild in Ethiopia’s forests.","Addis Ababa, at about 2,350 m, is one of the highest capital cities in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which lake is the source of the Blue Nile?","a":"Lake Tana","x":["Lake Victoria","Lake Turkana"]}},"Q167021":{"t":"Mountains folded like a rumpled carpet","s":"From above, the Zagros are one of the clearest pictures of how mountains are made: long parallel ridges curve for hundreds of kilometres like folds in a pushed rug, as Arabia presses into Iran. In the south, dark round blotches stand out between the folds — salt domes, where ancient salt has squeezed up through the rock and flowed out like a glacier.","w":"Long, curving parallel ridges like wrinkles in cloth, with round dark salt domes between them near the Gulf coast.","f":["The Zagros run about 1,500 km, from near the Turkish border to the Strait of Hormuz.","The Middle East’s first oil strike came in the Zagros foothills, at Masjed Soleyman, in 1908.","Goats were domesticated in the Zagros around 10,000 years ago."],"q":{"q":"Which collision is folding up the Zagros?","a":"Arabia pushing into Eurasia","x":["India pushing into Asia","Africa pushing into Europe"]}},"Q5955":{"t":"Golden mountains where four countries meet","s":"Snowy peaks, turquoise rivers and larch forest shading into steppe: the Altai sit in the middle of Asia, where Russia, Kazakhstan, China and Mongolia come together. Look for the milky turquoise ribbon of the Katun River and the long, narrow blade of Lake Teletskoye. Belukha, the highest peak at 4,506 m, wears a white, twin-headed cap all year.","w":"White peaks above dark larch forest, the turquoise Katun River and the narrow blue strip of Lake Teletskoye.","f":["The Golden Mountains of Altai have been a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1998.","Ancient Pazyryk burials, sealed in permafrost, preserved tattooed human skin about 2,400 years old.","Belukha is the highest point in Siberia."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Altai?","a":"Belukha","x":["Munku-Sardyk","Khan Tengri"]}},"Q293713":{"t":"The ridge where forest ends and the steppe begins","s":"Look for a sharp change of colour: the Greater Khingan divides the damp, green Manchurian side in the east from the dry grasslands of the Mongolian Plateau in the west. The range itself is gentle, rarely much above 2,000 m, but it catches the summer monsoon and casts a rain shadow over the steppe beyond. It runs about 1,200 km from north to south.","w":"Low, forested ridges running north–south, with greener fields to the east and tawny steppe to the west.","f":["Its northern slopes carry one of China’s largest forests, mostly of larch.","West of the range lie the Hulunbuir grasslands, some of the finest pastures in China."],"q":{"q":"What lies to the west of the Greater Khingan?","a":"The Mongolian Plateau","x":["The Manchurian Plain","The North China Plain"]}},"Q214284":{"t":"A remote divide between the Arctic and the Pacific","s":"Dark taiga, bare stony summits and barely a road: the Stanovoy Range is one of Siberia’s emptiest places. It is the region’s great watershed — rivers on its north side flow to the Lena and the Arctic, those on the south to the Amur and the Pacific. In winter the ridges turn white and the valleys vanish under snow.","w":"Long, dark-green ridges with pale rocky tops running east–west, and winding rivers in empty valleys.","f":["For nearly two centuries after the 1689 Treaty of Nerchinsk, these mountains roughly marked the border between Russia and China.","The railway to Yakutsk crosses the range near Neryungri, a coal-mining town in southern Yakutia."],"q":{"q":"Which great river takes the water from the Stanovoy’s north side?","a":"The Lena","x":["The Yenisei","The Kolyma"]}},"Q26813923":{"t":"The Himalayas’ first and youngest wrinkle","s":"Coming from the Indian plains, these are the first hills you meet: low, green, steep-sided ridges rising abruptly from the flat Terai. The Siwaliks are the youngest and outermost fold of the Himalayas, made of gravel and sand washed down from the high peaks and then pushed up again. Behind them rise higher ranges, and on a clear day the snows.","w":"The first line of low, forested hills rising sharply from the flat, farmed plain, parallel to the snowy peaks beyond.","f":["The name is usually explained as “belonging to Shiva”.","Their rocks are rich in fossils of ancient apes, elephants and giraffes, some more than 10 million years old.","In Nepal these hills are called the Chure range."],"q":{"q":"Where do the Siwalik Hills lie within the Himalayas?","a":"At the outermost southern edge","x":["Along the main snowy crest","On the Tibetan side"]}},"Q161750":{"t":"The Roof of the World, cut by giant glaciers","s":"Locals called it Bam-i-Dunya, the Roof of the World, and from the air it looks the part: a high, bare knot of mountains where the Tian Shan, Hindu Kush and Karakoram all meet. In the eastern Pamirs even the valley floors lie around 4,000 m. The Fedchenko Glacier, more than 70 km long, is the longest glacier outside the polar regions.","w":"A dense knot of snowy peaks and long glaciers; to the east, high, bare brown plateaus with turquoise lakes.","f":["Ismoil Somoni Peak, 7,495 m, is the highest point of the Pamirs.","Lake Karakul, at about 3,900 m, fills a crater left by a meteorite impact millions of years ago.","The Pamir Highway crosses the Ak-Baital Pass at about 4,650 m, one of the highest roads in the world."],"q":{"q":"What is the traditional nickname of the Pamirs?","a":"The Roof of the World","x":["The Abode of Snow","The Celestial Mountains"]}},"Q5472":{"t":"A snowy wall on the old roads of Central Asia","s":"A sea of sharp, snowy peaks cut by deep valleys stretches about 800 km from central Afghanistan into northern Pakistan. For thousands of years traders, pilgrims and armies threaded the Hindu Kush’s high passes between Central Asia and India. Tirich Mir, the highest summit at 7,708 m, rises near its eastern end.","w":"Row upon row of jagged white peaks, with narrow green valleys and braided grey rivers deep below.","f":["Lapis lazuli mined in these mountains for more than 6,000 years ended up in the jewellery of Egyptian pharaohs.","Alexander the Great crossed the Hindu Kush on his way to India in the 4th century BC."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Hindu Kush?","a":"Tirich Mir","x":["Nanga Parbat","Noshaq"]}},"Q5469":{"t":"K2’s kingdom of rock, ice and giant glaciers","s":"Few places outside the polar regions hold this much mountain ice: glaciers such as the Siachen and the Baltoro, each over 60 km long, fill the Karakoram’s valleys like frozen motorways striped with dark rock. Above them stand K2 and three more peaks over 8,000 m. The name is usually translated from Turkic as “black gravel”.","w":"Long, straight glaciers with dark stripes of rock down their middle, between clusters of sharp granite peaks.","f":["K2, 8,611 m, is the second-highest mountain on Earth.","Some Karakoram glaciers have held steady or even grown while most mountain glaciers shrink — the “Karakoram anomaly”.","The Karakoram Highway crosses the range at the Khunjerab Pass, about 4,700 m high."],"q":{"q":"Which is the second-highest mountain on Earth?","a":"K2","x":["Kangchenjunga","Lhotse"]}},"Q187871":{"t":"The stern northern rampart of the Tibetan Plateau","s":"Look for the line where the sand stops. The Kunlun rise like a rampart along the northern edge of the Tibetan Plateau, with the yellow Tarim Basin and the Taklamakan desert at their feet and cold, high Tibet behind them. The range runs some 3,000 km, and in Chinese myth its peaks were the home of immortals.","w":"A sharp boundary: a yellow desert basin on the north side, snow-streaked grey ridges and the high plateau to the south.","f":["The Qinghai–Tibet Railway crosses the Kunlun at a pass about 4,770 m high.","Nephrite jade from the Kunlun rivers near Hotan has been prized in China for thousands of years.","In Chinese legend the Kunlun were the home of Xiwangmu, the Queen Mother of the West."],"q":{"q":"Which basin lies at the northern foot of the western Kunlun?","a":"The Tarim Basin","x":["The Sichuan Basin","The Dzungarian Basin"]}},"Q130135":{"t":"The barrier between the Mediterranean and the Sahara","s":"Green on one side, red and ochre on the other: the Atlas Mountains hold back the Sahara from the Mediterranean coast for some 2,500 km, from Morocco’s Atlantic shore to Tunisia. Their northern slopes catch rain and even snow, while the southern flanks look scorched. In the Middle Atlas, dark cedar forests shelter the Barbary macaque, the only macaque living in Africa.","w":"Snow-dusted ridges with dark cedar forest on the north side, and bare red and ochre slopes on the Saharan side.","f":["Jebel Toubkal, 4,167 m, in the High Atlas, is the highest peak in North Africa.","The Atlas cedar grows wild only in these mountains of Morocco and Algeria.","The mountains are named after Atlas, the Titan of Greek myth who holds up the sky."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Atlas Mountains?","a":"Jebel Toubkal","x":["Jebel Ayachi","Mount Chélia"]}},"Q93332":{"t":"Ancient ridges combed into parallel lines","s":"Look down for stripes: in the Ridge-and-Valley country the Appalachians form long, forested ridges running side by side for hundreds of kilometres, as if the land had been combed. These are some of the oldest mountains on Earth, raised more than 250 million years ago and once far higher than today. Erosion has worn them down to rounded, green hills.","w":"Long, parallel forested ridges with farmed valleys between them, running south-west to north-east.","f":["The Appalachian Trail follows the range for about 3,500 km, from Georgia to Maine.","The Blue Ridge owes its blue haze to natural compounds released by its trees.","Mount Mitchell in North Carolina, 2,037 m, is the highest peak in the eastern United States."],"q":{"q":"Why do the Blue Ridge Mountains look blue?","a":"Haze from compounds released by trees","x":["Blue-tinted granite","Reflections from many lakes"]}},"Q929910":{"t":"America’s wild northern rampart above the Arctic plain","s":"Above the Arctic Circle, the Brooks Range sweeps across northern Alaska for about 1,000 km: grey, treeless peaks, braided rivers and hardly a settlement anywhere. To the north the land drops to the flat Arctic coastal plain, dotted with countless lakes. In summer the snow melts from the valleys; in winter everything lies white under a low sun.","w":"Grey, jagged, treeless ridges running east–west and wide braided riverbeds; to the north, flat tundra full of lakes.","f":["Gates of the Arctic National Park, in the Brooks Range, has no roads or trails at all.","The Trans-Alaska Pipeline crosses the range at Atigun Pass, about 1,400 m high.","Its crest divides rivers flowing to the Arctic Ocean from those heading to the Bering Sea."],"q":{"q":"Which pipeline crosses the Brooks Range at Atigun Pass?","a":"The Trans-Alaska Pipeline","x":["The Keystone Pipeline","The Colonial Pipeline"]}},"Q920070":{"t":"Ice, rainforest and fjords on Canada’s Pacific edge","s":"Between the Pacific and the interior of British Columbia rises a wall of granite peaks and icefields some 1,600 km long. Fjords push deep inland between them, and their lower slopes are cloaked in dense temperate rainforest. On a clear day you look down on glaciers feeding milky rivers that wind to the sea.","w":"Jagged snowy peaks and icefields, with long fjords and green valleys cutting in from the Pacific coast.","f":["Mount Waddington, 4,019 m, is the highest peak lying entirely within British Columbia.","The range is built largely of granite and forms one of the largest granite bodies on Earth.","The Skeena and Stikine rivers slice right through the range on their way to the Pacific."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak lying entirely within British Columbia?","a":"Mount Waddington","x":["Mount Robson","Mount Fairweather"]}},"Q4558":{"t":"A line of lone white volcanoes above dark forest","s":"The Cascades are easy to spot: a chain of big, isolated snow cones — Baker, Rainier, Adams, Hood, Shasta — standing high above the forest from British Columbia to northern California. Between them, the range is dark green on its rainy western side and brown and dry on the east. In the north, the jagged North Cascades hold hundreds of glaciers.","w":"Solitary white volcanic cones in a north–south line far above the forest; green western slopes, dry brown eastern ones.","f":["Mount Rainier, 4,392 m, carries more glacier ice than any other peak in the contiguous United States.","Crater Lake in Oregon fills the collapsed top of a Cascade volcano and is the deepest lake in the US, about 590 m.","The range is named after the cascades of the Columbia River, where it cuts through the mountains."],"q":{"q":"Which lake fills the collapsed top of a Cascade volcano?","a":"Crater Lake","x":["Lake Tahoe","Lake Chelan"]}},"Q156684":{"t":"Home of Denali, North America’s giant","s":"Even from cruise height Denali looks enormous: at 6,190 m it is North America’s highest peak, and it rises from lowlands only a few hundred metres above sea level, so its full bulk is on show. Around it the Alaska Range curves some 650 km across southern Alaska, a white wall of ice and rock above spruce forest and tundra.","w":"One massive white peak towering above all the others, with long glaciers streaming from a curved wall of snowy summits.","f":["From base to summit Denali rises about 5,500 m — more than Everest does above the Tibetan Plateau.","The Kahiltna Glacier, the climbers’ route to Denali, is about 70 km long.","The mountain has two names in use: Denali and Mount McKinley."],"q":{"q":"How high is Denali, North America’s highest peak?","a":"6,190 m","x":["5,642 m","6,961 m"]}},"Q40556":{"t":"Africa’s sharp eastern tip, jutting into the ocean","s":"On the map it is exactly what its name says: a horn thrust out into the sea. The Horn of Africa juts east between the Gulf of Aden and the Indian Ocean — a dry land of red-brown plains, thorny scrub and long, straight beaches. From above it is mostly ochre, crossed by the green threads of the Shabelle and Juba rivers.","w":"Ochre and red-brown plains with sparse scrub, a long sandy coastline, and thin green ribbons along the rivers.","f":["Ras Hafun in Somalia is the easternmost point of mainland Africa.","Somalia has the longest coastline of any country on the African mainland, over 3,000 km.","Frankincense and myrrh, prized in the ancient world, come from trees that grow wild on the Horn’s dry hills."],"q":{"q":"Which is the easternmost point of mainland Africa?","a":"Ras Hafun","x":["Cape Agulhas","Ras ben Sakka"]}},"Q23522":{"t":"A knot of mountains wrapped in blue seas","s":"Look down and you’ll see why travel here was always slow: the Balkan Peninsula is a jumble of mountain ranges, deep valleys and high basins, with the Adriatic on one side and the Aegean and Black Sea on the other. The main north–south routes follow the valleys of the Morava and the Vardar. The peninsula takes its name from the Balkan Mountains in Bulgaria.","w":"Tightly packed green and grey ridges, small basins with towns and fields, and deep river valleys running north–south.","f":["The word “Balkan” is usually traced to a Turkish term for a chain of wooded mountains.","Musala in Bulgaria, 2,925 m, is the highest peak of the peninsula, just ahead of Mount Olympus.","Lake Ohrid is one of the oldest lakes in Europe, more than a million years old."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Balkan Peninsula?","a":"Musala","x":["Mount Olympus","Triglav"]}},"Q1359850":{"t":"Kazakhstan’s “Hungry Steppe”, flat to every horizon","s":"Between the Chu River and Lake Balkhash lies a clay-and-gravel plateau so empty that it is also known as the Northern Hungry Steppe. From above it is a vast, flat sheet of grey-beige, crossed by faint tracks and dotted with white salt pans. Only in spring, for a few short weeks, does a green tinge spread across it.","w":"A featureless grey-beige plain with white salt pans; Lake Balkhash to the north-east, the Chu River’s green thread to the south-west.","f":["Betpak-Dala is home to one of Kazakhstan’s main herds of saiga, the antelope with a trunk-like nose.","The desert covers about 75,000 km²."],"q":{"q":"Which animal with a trunk-like nose roams Betpak-Dala?","a":"The saiga","x":["The goitered gazelle","The kulan"]}},"Q169132":{"t":"The Land of Five Rivers, a patchwork of fields","s":"Punjab means “five waters” in Persian, after the five rivers — the Jhelum, Chenab, Ravi, Beas and Sutlej — that cross this flat plain to join the Indus. From above it is one of the most intensely farmed landscapes on Earth: a fine patchwork of wheat and rice fields laced with a dense grid of irrigation canals. At night, towns and villages sparkle everywhere.","w":"A flat, finely divided patchwork of green and gold fields, straight canals and wide, braided riverbeds.","f":["The Indus basin irrigation network, fed by Punjab’s rivers, is one of the largest in the world.","Lahore, on the Ravi, is famous for its Mughal fort and Shalimar Gardens, both listed by UNESCO.","Punjab is often called the breadbasket of the subcontinent."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Punjab mean?","a":"Five waters","x":["Green land","Great plain"]}},"Q229269":{"t":"The Empty Quarter: the largest sand sea on Earth","s":"Nothing but sand for hundreds of kilometres: the Rub’ al Khali, the Empty Quarter, is the largest continuous sand desert in the world, about 650,000 km². From above, look for giant dunes lined up like waves, some over 200 m high, with pale salt flats in the hollows between them. Its colours shift from yellow to deep orange-red.","w":"Endless rows of orange dunes like frozen waves, with white or grey salt flats in the corridors between them.","f":["In 1930–31 Bertram Thomas made the first recorded crossing by a Westerner, travelling by camel.","Wilfred Thesiger crossed it twice in the 1940s and described it in his classic book “Arabian Sands”.","The Rub’ al Khali is larger than France."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Rub’ al Khali” mean?","a":"The Empty Quarter","x":["The Red Sands","The Great Sea"]}},"Q375816":{"t":"Brazil’s “white forest” that turns green in days","s":"In the dry season the Caatinga looks grey and lifeless from above: thorny scrub that has dropped every leaf, dotted with cacti. Its name comes from the Tupi for “white forest”. But when the rains arrive, the whole landscape flushes green within days. This semi-arid land of north-eastern Brazil exists nowhere else on Earth.","w":"Grey-brown scrubland in the dry season, bright green after rain, with reservoirs shining like scattered mirrors.","f":["The Caatinga is the only major biome that lies entirely within Brazil.","The mandacaru, a tall columnar cactus, is a symbol of this dry back country, the sertão.","Serra da Capivara, in the Caatinga, has one of the largest concentrations of prehistoric rock art in the Americas."],"q":{"q":"What does the Tupi name “Caatinga” mean?","a":"White forest","x":["Dry land","Thorny field"]}},"Q47700":{"t":"Rust-red sand beneath a sea of grass and thorn","s":"The Kalahari is not the bare dune field you might imagine: its deep red sand is held down by grass and thorn trees, so from above it looks like rust-coloured ground brushed with pale green. Long parallel dunes run for kilometres, lined up by the wind. Far to the north, the same sand swallows the Okavango River, which never reaches the sea.","w":"Red-brown ground speckled with grey-green scrub, long straight dune ridges and white pans of dry salt lakes.","f":["The Okavango Delta, where a river vanishes into the Kalahari sand, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","The sand’s red colour comes from a thin coating of iron oxide on each grain.","The San people have lived in the Kalahari for many thousands of years."],"q":{"q":"Which river ends in the Kalahari without reaching the sea?","a":"The Okavango","x":["The Zambezi","The Orange"]}},"Q2913098":{"t":"Dark peaks stranded in a white ocean of ice","s":"Here the Transantarctic Mountains fray into their last, lonely ranges: dark summits and ridges sticking out of an endless white sheet, near the inner edge of the Filchner–Ronne Ice Shelf. The Pensacola Mountains stretch some 450 km. They were discovered only in 1956, spotted from a US Navy aircraft.","w":"Scattered dark rock ridges and peaks poking through flat, endless white ice.","f":["The mountains are named after the US Naval Air Station at Pensacola, Florida.","The Dufek Massif in this group is one of the largest layered igneous intrusions on Earth."],"q":{"q":"What are the Pensacola Mountains named after?","a":"A US Navy air station","x":["A Spanish explorer","A whaling ship"]}},"Q1139110":{"t":"The roof of Antarctica, crowned by Mount Vinson","s":"The highest mountains of the coldest continent rise here as a sharp, dark crest above the West Antarctic ice. Their Sentinel Range holds Mount Vinson, Antarctica’s highest peak at 4,892 m, and a string of other summits over 4,000 m. From above, the black rock faces and long glaciers stand out sharply against the white.","w":"A sharp north–south line of dark peaks and ridges above flat white ice, with long glaciers streaming off both sides.","f":["Lincoln Ellsworth discovered the range in 1935, during the first flight across Antarctica.","Climbers reach Vinson via a seasonal camp on the Union Glacier, where planes land on bare blue ice."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak in Antarctica?","a":"Mount Vinson","x":["Mount Erebus","Mount Kirkpatrick"]}},"Q1796029":{"t":"Amundsen’s stairway to the South Pole","s":"In November 1911 Roald Amundsen and his team climbed through these mountains with their dog sledges, up the steep Axel Heiberg Glacier to the polar plateau. From here the South Pole lies only about 400 km away. From above, the Queen Maud Mountains are a jumble of dark peaks and broad glaciers pouring down to the flat Ross Ice Shelf.","w":"Dark, rugged peaks between wide glaciers that flow north onto the smooth white Ross Ice Shelf.","f":["Amundsen named the range after Queen Maud of Norway.","The Axel Heiberg Glacier is named after a Norwegian brewer who helped pay for Amundsen’s expeditions.","Amundsen’s team reached the South Pole on 14 December 1911."],"q":{"q":"Which glacier did Amundsen climb through these mountains?","a":"The Axel Heiberg Glacier","x":["The Beardmore Glacier","The Scott Glacier"]}},"Q842139":{"t":"A misty green spine that hid an unknown animal","s":"Cloud often clings to this long green spine between the Mekong valley and the coast of Vietnam. The Annamites run some 1,100 km along the border of Laos and Vietnam, their steep forested ridges so remote that in 1992 scientists discovered a large mammal entirely new to science: the saola, a shy forest ox with long, straight horns.","w":"Steep, forest-green ridges, often capped with cloud, separating the hills of Laos from Vietnam’s narrow coastal plain.","f":["In Vietnamese the range is called Truong Son, “the long mountains”.","Son Doong cave, in Vietnam’s Phong Nha–Ke Bang park in these mountains, has the largest known cave passage in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which large mammal was discovered in the Annamites in 1992?","a":"The saola","x":["The kouprey","The gaur"]}},"ne-rg-1730074073":{"t":"An ocean of green threaded with brown rivers","s":"Below you the Amazon rainforest — the selva — rolls to every horizon, a green so continuous it looks like moss. The only breaks are rivers: wide, coffee-coloured and looping back on themselves, leaving crescent-shaped lakes where old bends were cut off. In the afternoon, towering rain clouds bubble up over the canopy.","w":"Unbroken green canopy, brown rivers in huge loops and crescent-shaped oxbow lakes; in the afternoon, storm towers above.","f":["Iquitos, in the Peruvian selva, is often called the largest city in the world that cannot be reached by road.","Upstream of Iquitos, the Marañón and Ucayali rivers join to form the Amazon.","In Spanish, selva simply means “forest” or “jungle”."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers join in Peru to form the Amazon?","a":"The Marañón and the Ucayali","x":["The Napo and the Putumayo","The Madeira and the Negro"]}},"Q748435":{"t":"Genghis Khan’s sacred mountains, birthplace of rivers","s":"Here the Siberian taiga reaches south into Mongolia: dark larch forest on the slopes, open grassy valleys below. The Khentii are the heartland of the Mongols — Burkhan Khaldun, the mountain Genghis Khan is said to have worshipped, rises among them. Three great rivers, the Onon, Kherlen and Tuul, begin in these hills.","w":"Dark forested ridges with open, grassy valleys; Ulaanbaatar spreads along the Tuul at the south-western edge.","f":["The Great Burkhan Khaldun Mountain was listed by UNESCO in 2015 as a sacred landscape.","The Kherlen flows east into Lake Hulun in China, while the Onon becomes part of the Amur system."],"q":{"q":"Which sacred Khentii mountain is linked to Genghis Khan?","a":"Burkhan Khaldun","x":["Otgontenger","Khüiten Peak"]}},"Q22449163":{"t":"Remote northern ranges famous for green jade","s":"Rounded grey peaks, dark spruce valleys and hardly a light at night: the Cassiar Mountains straddle the border of northern British Columbia and Yukon. Their rocks hold rich deposits of nephrite jade, making this one of the world’s main sources of the green stone. Few roads cross them; the Stewart–Cassiar Highway winds past the hamlet of Jade City.","w":"Rounded grey-green peaks, broad forested valleys and scattered lakes, with no towns in sight.","f":["Jade City, a tiny settlement on the Stewart–Cassiar Highway, sells nephrite mined in these mountains.","The Cassiar gold rush of the 1870s first drew miners into these remote valleys."],"q":{"q":"Which green stone is mined in the Cassiar Mountains?","a":"Nephrite jade","x":["Malachite","Emerald"]}},"Q1774690":{"t":"Kenya’s only true desert, a salt-white ancient lakebed","s":"East of Lake Turkana the land flattens into a pale, almost white expanse: the Chalbi, often called Kenya’s only true desert. It is the floor of an ancient lake, and after rare rains it floods into a shallow sheet of water before drying back to a cracked, salty crust. Gabbra herders bring their camels to the few oases on its edge.","w":"A flat, pale grey-white basin east of jade-green Lake Turkana, with the forested hump of Mount Marsabit to the south-east.","f":["Lake Turkana, just to the west, is the largest permanent desert lake in the world.","The Chalbi is so flat and bare that heat mirages often make it look like a lake even when it is dry."],"q":{"q":"Which big lake lies just west of the Chalbi Desert?","a":"Lake Turkana","x":["Lake Naivasha","Lake Victoria"]}},"Q624170":{"t":"Dark mountains between the Nile and the Red Sea","s":"Unlike the sandy west, Egypt’s Eastern Desert is a land of jagged, dark mountains and dry valleys — wadis — running down to the Red Sea on one side and to the Nile on the other. From above, look for the sudden contrast: turquoise sea, a thin strip of coast, then a chaos of brown and black peaks. The ancient Egyptians mined gold here.","w":"Jagged brown and black mountains cut by pale, branching wadis, with the turquoise Red Sea along the eastern edge.","f":["The Turin Papyrus Map, drawn about 1150 BC, shows quarries and gold mines here — perhaps the oldest geological map known.","The Romans quarried rare purple porphyry for their emperors at Mons Porphyrites, in these mountains."],"q":{"q":"Which rare stone did the Romans quarry in the Eastern Desert?","a":"Imperial porphyry","x":["Carrara marble","Lapis lazuli"]}},"Q22041906":{"t":"A lava plateau in southern Brazil, cut by sheer canyons","s":"About 130 million years ago, as South America and Africa began to split, lava flooded this region layer after layer, building a plateau more than a kilometre thick in places. Its edge, the Serra Geral, drops in steep escarpments split by deep canyons such as Itaimbezinho. On top, look for dark stands of Paraná pine among the fields.","w":"A high, flat plateau of fields and dark pine groves ending in steep cliffs and canyons; its rivers flow west, away from the sea.","f":["The Iguaçu Falls pour over the same layers of basalt, on the plateau’s western side.","The Paraná pine, whose flat-topped crowns dot the plateau, belongs to a tree family that grew alongside the dinosaurs.","The same lava flows appear across the ocean in Namibia, torn apart when the Atlantic opened."],"q":{"q":"Which famous waterfall pours over the same basalt layers?","a":"Iguaçu Falls","x":["Angel Falls","Kaieteur Falls"]}},"Q22529966":{"t":"Snowy ranges and long lakes in Canada’s inland rainforest","s":"West of the Rockies, British Columbia’s interior rises into another tangle of snowy ranges — the Selkirks, Monashees, Purcells and Cariboos, together called the Columbia Mountains. Between them lie long, narrow lakes such as Kootenay, Arrow and Slocan, like strips of blue ribbon. The western slopes catch so much snow and rain that they grow a rare inland rainforest of giant cedars.","w":"Tight clusters of snowy peaks separated by long, narrow blue lakes running north–south, with dark forest on the slopes.","f":["Rogers Pass, where the railway and the Trans-Canada Highway cross the Selkirks, gets about 10 m of snow in a typical winter.","The Columbia River loops right around the northern Selkirks in a giant curve known as the Big Bend.","Their eastern edge is the Rocky Mountain Trench, a straight valley hundreds of kilometres long."],"q":{"q":"Which pass takes the railway and highway across the Selkirks?","a":"Rogers Pass","x":["Kicking Horse Pass","Yellowhead Pass"]}},"Q747843":{"t":"Mongolia’s green heart, where an empire’s capital stood","s":"In the middle of Mongolia the land lifts into the Khangai: broad, rounded mountains where larch forest clings only to the north-facing slopes, so the hills look striped dark and light. Herders have grazed these pastures for thousands of years. At the range’s eastern foot, in the Orkhon Valley, stood Karakorum, the 13th-century capital of the Mongol Empire.","w":"Broad, rounded mountains with dark forest on the north slopes and pale grass on the south, and green river valleys between.","f":["The Orkhon Valley, with the ruins of Karakorum, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","Otgontenger, the highest peak of the Khangai, is revered by Mongolians as a sacred mountain.","Rivers from the Khangai’s northern slopes flow through the Selenga into Lake Baikal."],"q":{"q":"Which old imperial capital stood at the foot of the Khangai?","a":"Karakorum","x":["Xanadu","Samarkand"]}},"Q863798":{"t":"The wall that splits China into north and south","s":"Cross this range and you cross a climate line: the Qinling split China into a dry, wheat-growing north and a wet, rice-growing south. From above, yellow loess and the plain of Xi’an lie on one side, green terraced valleys on the other. In the bamboo forests on its slopes live giant pandas and golden snub-nosed monkeys.","w":"A long east–west wall of forested ridges, with brownish plains around Xi’an to the north and greener hills to the south.","f":["Together with the Huai River, the Qinling mark China’s traditional boundary between north and south.","Taibai Shan, about 3,770 m, is the highest peak in the eastern half of mainland China.","Xi’an, at the range’s northern foot, was the starting point of the Silk Road."],"q":{"q":"Which river pairs with the Qinling as China’s north–south line?","a":"The Huai River","x":["The Yellow River","The Pearl River"]}},"Q20155858":{"t":"A stone desert as flat and dark as a slate roof","s":"Not every desert is sand. A hamada is a bare, rocky plateau swept clean by wind, and the Hamada du Draa is a classic: a vast, flat pavement of dark stones south of the Draa valley. From above it looks like slate or scorched leather, broken only by dry riverbeds. Along its northern edge runs the Draa, a river that rarely reaches the sea.","w":"A dark, flat stony plain to the horizon, cut by pale dry riverbeds, with the green Draa oases to the north.","f":["The Draa is Morocco’s longest river, about 1,100 km, yet it reaches the Atlantic only in rare wet years.","Desert varnish — a thin dark film of manganese and iron — gives the hamada’s stones their black sheen."],"q":{"q":"What is a hamada?","a":"A bare, rocky desert plateau","x":["A field of shifting dunes","A dried-up salt lake"]}},"Q272521":{"t":"A desert within the desert, once home to the loneliest tree","s":"Ténéré means “desert” in the Tuareg language — a desert within the Sahara. East of the Aïr Mountains it spreads as a vast, smooth sea of pale sand, some of the flattest and emptiest land on Earth. For decades a single acacia stood here, the only tree for some 400 km and a landmark for caravans, until a truck knocked it down in 1973.","w":"An immense, smooth sheet of pale yellow sand with few landmarks; the dark Aïr Mountains rise along its western edge.","f":["What remains of the Tree of Ténéré stands in the National Museum in Niamey; a metal sculpture marks its old spot.","At Gadoufaoua the shifting sands have uncovered dinosaur skeletons and the giant crocodile Sarcosuchus.","Salt caravans still cross the Ténéré to the oasis of Bilma."],"q":{"q":"What happened to the famous Tree of Ténéré in 1973?","a":"A truck knocked it down","x":["Lightning struck it","It was cut for firewood"]}},"Q307143":{"t":"A golden sea of dunes, frozen mid-storm","s":"Few views from the air say “Sahara” like this one: a golden sea of dunes stretching across eastern Algeria into Tunisia. Look for the patterns — long ridges, crescents and, in places, huge star-shaped dunes with arms radiating from a peak. Beyond its northern edge, the white salt crust of Chott el Djerid gleams in the sun.","w":"Golden dunes in regular patterns — ridges, crescents and stars — with the white salt flat of Chott el Djerid to the north.","f":["Sets for Tatooine, the desert planet of Star Wars, were built near Tozeur and Nefta on the erg’s Tunisian edge.","“Erg”, a word taken from Arabic, is what geographers call a large sea of dunes.","Hassi Messaoud, Algeria’s largest oilfield, lies at the erg’s western edge; at night its gas flares glow."],"q":{"q":"Which film saga shot desert scenes on the erg’s Tunisian edge?","a":"Star Wars","x":["Lawrence of Arabia","Dune"]}},"Q2064799":{"t":"The Sahara’s great empty basin, far from any oasis","s":"Here lies one of the most remote corners of the Sahara: El Djouf, a vast basin of dunes and gravel on the border of Mauritania and Mali, with no roads, villages or permanent wells across much of it. From above, look for endless parallel dune ridges running north-east to south-west, combed by the steady trade wind. Its Arabic name means “the belly” — a hollow in the desert.","w":"Endless parallel orange dune ridges and gravel plains, with no roads, towns or green patches anywhere in sight.","f":["On its north-eastern edge lies Taoudenni, where salt has been cut in slabs and carried by camel to Timbuktu for centuries.","The French naturalist Théodore Monod spent decades exploring this empty basin by camel."],"q":{"q":"Which salt-mining oasis lies on El Djouf’s north-eastern edge?","a":"Taoudenni","x":["Bilma","Siwa"]}},"Q855658":{"t":"A high desert of cactus, gypsum dunes and the Rio Grande","s":"Much of the Chihuahuan Desert lies on high plateaus between two Mexican mountain ranges, so it is cooler than you might expect, and most of its rain falls in summer storms. From above it is a mosaic of tan basins, dark volcanic hills and dry lakebeds. On the Texas border the Rio Grande makes its Big Bend, carving deep canyons through the ranges.","w":"Tan basins with dark volcanic hills and pale dry lakebeds; the Rio Grande’s green ribbon winds through canyons.","f":["White Sands, at the desert’s northern end in New Mexico, is the largest gypsum dune field in the world.","The Chihuahuan Desert is one of the richest places on Earth for cactus species."],"q":{"q":"Which field of white gypsum dunes lies in the Chihuahuan Desert?","a":"White Sands","x":["Death Valley","Great Sand Dunes"]}},"Q189429":{"t":"The land of the saguaro, surprisingly green","s":"For a desert, the Sonoran looks surprisingly green from above: two rainy seasons a year feed a dense scatter of shrubs, trees and giant cacti. It spreads across Arizona, California and north-western Mexico, broken by rugged little mountain ranges. Look for the sharp green squares of irrigated farms along the Colorado and Gila rivers, and the vast grid of Phoenix to the east.","w":"Olive-dotted desert basins between small, jagged ranges, bright irrigated fields along the rivers, and Phoenix’s sprawling grid.","f":["The giant saguaro cactus grows wild almost only in the Sonoran Desert.","A saguaro can grow more than 12 m tall and live for over 150 years.","Phoenix, in the heart of the desert, is one of the largest cities in the United States."],"q":{"q":"Which giant cactus is the symbol of the Sonoran Desert?","a":"The saguaro","x":["The Joshua tree","The prickly pear"]}},"Q1670841":{"t":"A desert cradled in the Yellow River’s great loop","s":"Look for the Yellow River swinging north in a huge, almost rectangular loop: the land inside it is the Ordos Plateau, and its sandy south is the Mu Us Desert. Over recent decades much of the Mu Us has been fixed with planted shrubs and trees, so from above you may see as much grey-green as yellow. The Great Wall once ran along its southern edge.","w":"A plateau of pale sand patches and grey-green scrub inside the Yellow River’s big loop, crossed by straight roads.","f":["Kubuqi, the desert on the northern side of the Ordos, is known for huge solar farms laid out on the sand.","The Ordos Plateau is rich in coal, making it one of China’s main energy regions."],"q":{"q":"Which river loops around the Ordos Plateau?","a":"The Yellow River","x":["The Yangtze","The Amur"]}},"Q132956":{"t":"A sea of shifting sand ringed by snowy mountains","s":"The Taklamakan fills the Tarim Basin like sand poured into a bowl: dunes in every direction, walled in by the snowy Tian Shan to the north and the Kunlun to the south. It is one of the largest shifting-sand deserts on Earth. Old Silk Road oases such as Kashgar and Hotan ring its edges, fed by meltwater streams that die out in the sand.","w":"Huge dune fields in a closed basin, ringed by snowy mountains and green oasis strips where rivers leave the foothills.","f":["The name is popularly translated as “go in and you won’t come out”, though its real origin is uncertain.","The Tarim Desert Highway crosses the dunes for more than 500 km, protected by planted belts of shrubs.","The Tarim River, fed by mountain meltwater, runs along the desert’s northern edge and never reaches the sea."],"q":{"q":"Which mountains wall in the Taklamakan to the north?","a":"The Tian Shan","x":["The Kunlun","The Altai"]}},"Q483134":{"t":"A mountainous peninsula with a rugged eastern spine","s":"About 70% of the Korean Peninsula is hills and mountains, and from above you can see how they crowd towards the east: a long spine, the Baekdudaegan, runs close to the eastern coast, while the land slopes west to broad plains and a shore of islands and vast mudflats. In autumn the forests turn every shade of red and gold.","w":"Forested ridges crowding the east coast, flatter farmland and cities in the west, and a western shore of islands and mudflats.","f":["The tidal flats of the west coast are a UNESCO World Heritage Site; tides there can rise more than 8 m.","From north to south the peninsula is about 1,000 km long."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how much of the Korean Peninsula is mountainous?","a":"About 70%","x":["About 30%","About 50%"]}},"Q584279":{"t":"North Africa’s highest wall, snow above the desert","s":"From Marrakesh you can see its snow in winter, and from above the High Atlas is a sharp, rust-and-white barrier running some 700 km across Morocco. To the north lie green plains and the Atlantic; to the south the desert begins almost at once. Deep gorges such as Todgha and Dadès cut its southern flank, with thin green strips of palm oases below.","w":"A rust-red mountain wall topped with snow in winter; narrow green oasis strips wind along the valleys on its southern side.","f":["The road from Marrakesh to Ouarzazate crosses the range at the Tizi n’Tichka pass, about 2,260 m high.","Aït Benhaddou, a fortified earthen village on the southern side, is a UNESCO site and a favourite film location."],"q":{"q":"Which pass carries the road from Marrakesh to Ouarzazate?","a":"Tizi n’Tichka","x":["Tizi n’Test","Col du Zad"]}},"Q183295":{"t":"The Dragon Mountains, a basalt wall around Lesotho","s":"Seen from above, the Drakensberg is a single colossal cliff: the edge of a high basalt plateau that drops more than a kilometre to the green hills of KwaZulu-Natal. On top lies Lesotho, a mountain kingdom where even the lowest ground is about 1,400 m above sea level. In winter the rim of the escarpment is often dusted with snow.","w":"A long, dark escarpment edge: a flat, bare high plateau on one side, folded green foothills far below on the other.","f":["Its Zulu name, uKhahlamba, means “barrier of spears”; Drakensberg is Afrikaans for “dragon mountains”.","Thabana Ntlenyana in Lesotho, 3,482 m, is the highest peak in southern Africa.","The Maloti-Drakensberg Park holds thousands of rock paintings made by the San people."],"q":{"q":"What does the Zulu name uKhahlamba mean?","a":"Barrier of spears","x":["Dragon’s back","Mountain of thunder"]}},"Q5191296":{"t":"Green hills where Central Africa’s rivers rush to the sea","s":"A band of low, rainforest-covered hills runs parallel to the Atlantic coast of Central Africa, from Equatorial Guinea through Gabon towards the mouth of the Congo. Rivers crossing it tumble down in rapids, and the mighty Congo itself breaks through in the Livingstone Falls, a chain of cataracts below Kinshasa. From above it is an almost unbroken carpet of dark green.","w":"Low, rolling hills under dense green rainforest a short way inland from the Atlantic, with brown rivers cutting through them.","f":["Gabon’s Monts de Cristal National Park, at the northern end of the range, is one of Africa’s richest places for plant species.","Below Kinshasa the Congo drops about 270 m through the Livingstone Falls on its way to the ocean."],"q":{"q":"Which cataracts carry the Congo through these hills to the sea?","a":"The Livingstone Falls","x":["The Victoria Falls","The Boyoma Falls"]}},"Q309104":{"t":"Dark volcanoes rising from the heart of the Sahara","s":"In the middle of the Sahara, dark volcanic mountains rise out of the sand like islands. The Tibesti in northern Chad holds the highest summit of the Sahara, Emi Koussi, at about 3,400 m — a huge shield volcano whose crater is some 12 km across. Nearby lies Trou au Natron, a deep caldera whose floor is streaked white with salt.","w":"Dark brown and black volcanic massifs rising from pale sand, with wide craters and white salt crusts inside some calderas.","f":["Trou au Natron is about 1 km deep, and its floor is crusted with white natron salt.","Rock art in the Tibesti shows cattle and giraffes from the time when the Sahara was green."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest summit of the Sahara?","a":"Emi Koussi","x":["Mount Tahat","Jebel Toubkal"]}},"Q23836082":{"t":"Black volcanic spires in the heart of the Algerian Sahara","s":"Out of the Algerian Sahara rises the Ahaggar, or Hoggar: a high granite plateau crowned with dark volcanic peaks and needle-like plugs of old lava, weathered into fantastic shapes. It is the home ground of the Tuareg, and the town of Tamanrasset sits at its foot. Its highest peak, Mount Tahat, reaches about 2,900 m.","w":"A dark, rugged highland of black peaks and spires rising from pale desert, with dry valleys radiating outwards.","f":["On the Assekrem plateau, about 2,700 m up, stands the hermitage the French monk Charles de Foucauld built in 1911.","Wild Saharan olive trees survive in the Ahaggar’s high valleys, relics of a wetter past."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Ahaggar?","a":"Mount Tahat","x":["Emi Koussi","Assekrem"]}},"Q170383":{"t":"Dark granite islands in a sea of sand","s":"The Aïr rises from the Sahara like a cluster of dark islands: granite massifs, some almost perfectly round, surrounded by yellow sand. The rings are the roots of ancient volcanoes laid bare by erosion, and they show up clearly from the air. Between them, dry valleys hold acacias and a few Tuareg villages; the old caravan town of Agadez lies at the southern edge.","w":"Dark, rounded rock massifs, some ring-shaped, standing out against pale sand, with the smooth Ténéré dunes to the east.","f":["The Aïr and Ténéré Natural Reserves together form one of the largest protected areas in Africa, listed by UNESCO.","The old town of Agadez, with its tall mud-brick minaret, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],"q":{"q":"Which caravan town lies at the southern edge of the Aïr?","a":"Agadez","x":["Timbuktu","Tamanrasset"]}},"Q404600":{"t":"Forested hills that the Amur River slices in two","s":"Low, rolling and dark with forest, the Lesser Khingan rise between the plains of Manchuria and the Amur. The range is modest, mostly under 1,000 m, but here the great Amur cuts straight through it in a narrow, winding gorge. The hills are covered with Korean pine, larch and birch, turning gold and red in autumn.","w":"Low, dark-green forested hills with the broad Amur River squeezing through them in a winding gorge.","f":["At the range’s western foot lies Wudalianchi, a cluster of volcanoes that last erupted in 1719–21, damming a chain of five lakes.","Korean pine, whose seeds are sold as pine nuts, is one of the range’s main trees."],"q":{"q":"Which great river cuts through the Lesser Khingan in a gorge?","a":"The Amur","x":["The Songhua","The Ussuri"]}},"Q155322":{"t":"Tiger country on Russia’s Pacific shore","s":"A long, forested range runs some 1,000 km along Russia’s Pacific coast, north of Vladivostok. Its forests are unique: northern taiga mixes with southern broadleaf woods, Amur tigers share them with brown and Himalayan bears, and spruce grows beside wild grapes and lianas. From above it is a sea of ridges, dark green in summer and flecked with gold in autumn.","w":"Endless parallel forested ridges between the Ussuri valley and the sea, with small river mouths along the coast.","f":["On 12 February 1947 one of the largest iron meteorite showers ever seen fell here, leaving dozens of craters.","Central Sikhote-Alin is a UNESCO World Heritage Site that protects the Amur tiger’s home."],"q":{"q":"What fell on the Sikhote-Alin in February 1947?","a":"An iron meteorite shower","x":["Volcanic ash","A giant hailstorm"]}},"Q215917":{"t":"A frozen arc beside the Pole of Cold","s":"A great arc of bare, jagged mountains curves some 1,200 km along the east bank of the lower Lena, rising steeply above its broad, braided valley. Beyond the range lie Verkhoyansk and Oymyakon, the coldest inhabited places in the Northern Hemisphere, where winter can bring −67 °C. In summer the snow retreats to the summits; in winter everything is white and silent.","w":"A long wall of grey, snow-streaked peaks east of the wide, braided Lena, with empty, larch-dotted valleys between them.","f":["Verkhoyansk, beyond the range, recorded −67.8 °C in 1892, one of the lowest temperatures ever measured in an inhabited place.","Summer there can top +30 °C — a swing of about 100 degrees across the year.","Permafrost beneath the range reaches hundreds of metres deep."],"q":{"q":"Which town beyond the range is famed as a Pole of Cold?","a":"Verkhoyansk","x":["Norilsk","Vorkuta"]}},"Q1072879":{"t":"A snowy wall above the Silk Road’s Hexi Corridor","s":"Along the north-eastern edge of the Tibetan Plateau, the Qilian Mountains form a long snowy wall, their glaciers feeding the rivers that water the Hexi Corridor below. That narrow strip of oases, squeezed between the mountains and the Gobi, was the main route of the Silk Road into China. At the range’s northern foot, the Zhangye hills are striped red, orange and yellow.","w":"A long, white-capped range to the south, a green strip of oases and fields along its foot, and the grey-brown Gobi beyond.","f":["The Hexi Corridor was the main Silk Road route between China’s heartland and Central Asia.","Jiayuguan fort, the western end of the Ming Great Wall, stands at the foot of the Qilian."],"q":{"q":"Which Silk Road corridor runs along the Qilian’s northern foot?","a":"The Hexi Corridor","x":["The Wakhan Corridor","The Dzungarian Gate"]}},"Q1156557":{"t":"Green hills between the Yangtze and the Huai","s":"Between the Yangtze and the Huai River, the Dabie Mountains rise as a forested knot of peaks in central China, often wrapped in mist. They look gentle from above — green ridges, terraced tea slopes and reservoirs in the valleys — but their rocks tell a violent story: they were dragged more than 100 km down into the Earth and then brought back up.","w":"A compact cluster of green, forested ridges with reservoirs in the valleys, between two broad river plains.","f":["Tiny diamonds and coesite in the Dabie rocks prove they were once buried more than 100 km deep.","The mountains divide the basins of the Yangtze and the Huai.","Lu’an Guapian, a well-known Chinese green tea, is grown on the Dabie slopes."],"q":{"q":"How deep were the Dabie Mountains’ rocks once buried?","a":"More than 100 km","x":["About 1 km","About 10 km"]}},"Q1195285":{"t":"The ridges that shield southern China from winter","s":"Across southern China runs a jumble of rounded, forested ranges, the Nanling. They divide the Yangtze basin from the Pearl River basin and shelter Guangdong from the coldest winter winds, so subtropical crops grow on their southern side. From above, look for green hills scarred with red earth, rice terraces and, towards Guilin, jagged limestone pinnacles.","w":"Rounded green ridges running east–west, red earth and rice terraces, and sharp limestone pinnacles to the west near Guilin.","f":["Nanling simply means “southern ridges” in Chinese.","The Lingqu Canal, dug in the 3rd century BC, crossed these mountains to link the Yangtze and Pearl River systems."],"q":{"q":"Which two river basins do the Nanling divide?","a":"The Yangtze and the Pearl","x":["The Yellow and the Huai","The Mekong and the Red"]}},"Q4527":{"t":"India’s rain-soaked green wall above the Arabian Sea","s":"Running some 1,600 km parallel to India’s west coast, the Western Ghats are the first thing the summer monsoon hits: their seaward slopes are drenched and deep green, while the land behind them lies in a rain shadow. Around the Nilgiri Hills they rise to a high, rolling plateau of tea gardens and grassland. Just to the south, the Palghat Gap cuts a rare low pass through the wall.","w":"A steep, dark-green escarpment parallel to the coast, tea gardens on the Nilgiri plateau and the wide Palghat Gap to the south.","f":["The Western Ghats are a UNESCO World Heritage Site and one of the world’s biodiversity hotspots.","The neelakurinji shrub blooms here only once every 12 years, turning whole slopes blue-purple.","Anamudi, 2,695 m, is the highest peak in India south of the Himalayas."],"q":{"q":"How often does the neelakurinji of the Western Ghats bloom?","a":"Once every 12 years","x":["Once every 3 years","Once every 50 years"]}},"Q465055":{"t":"Broken hills where India’s great rivers break through","s":"Unlike the continuous wall of the Western Ghats, the Eastern Ghats are a broken chain of old, worn hills along India’s east coast. The great rivers of the Deccan — the Godavari, Krishna and Mahanadi — cut right through them on their way to the Bay of Bengal, fanning out into fertile deltas. In the north, forested hills around the Araku Valley grow coffee.","w":"Low, forested, broken hills parallel to the east coast, with wide rivers cutting through them to green deltas.","f":["The Borra Caves in the Eastern Ghats are among the largest caves in India.","Chilika Lake, near the northern end of the Ghats, is India’s largest coastal lagoon."],"q":{"q":"Where do the rivers that cut through the Eastern Ghats flow?","a":"Into the Bay of Bengal","x":["Into the Arabian Sea","Into the Gulf of Khambhat"]}},"Q271435":{"t":"The old line between northern and southern India","s":"For centuries Indians saw the Vindhya as the dividing line between north and south — the Gangetic plains on one side, the Deccan on the other. From above it is less a range than a long, broken escarpment of flat-topped sandstone hills. Along its southern foot flows the Narmada, one of the few big Indian rivers that runs west.","w":"A long line of flat-topped, scrub-covered sandstone hills and cliffs, with the Narmada valley along the southern side.","f":["The Bhimbetka rock shelters in the Vindhya foothills hold prehistoric paintings and are a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","The Buddhist stupas of Sanchi, begun in the 3rd century BC, stand on a hilltop in this region."],"q":{"q":"Which west-flowing river runs along the Vindhya’s southern foot?","a":"The Narmada","x":["The Godavari","The Chambal"]}},"Q207692":{"t":"Red cliffs, a winding river and the home of rock tea","s":"On the border of Fujian and Jiangxi, the Wuyi Mountains rise as forested ridges and bare red sandstone cliffs. The Nine-Bend River loops between them, and on narrow terraces among the rocks grows Wuyi rock tea, including the famous Da Hong Pao. From above, look for red rock walls standing out against the deep green.","w":"Dense green forest broken by bare red cliffs and domes, with a winding river threading between them.","f":["The Wuyi Mountains are a UNESCO World Heritage Site for both their nature and their culture.","Da Hong Pao tea from the few ancient bushes here has fetched record prices at auction.","The Neo-Confucian philosopher Zhu Xi taught in these mountains in the 12th century."],"q":{"q":"Which famous tea comes from the Wuyi Mountains?","a":"Da Hong Pao","x":["Longjing","Pu’er"]}},"Q128500":{"t":"The marmot mountains on the Kazakh–Chinese border","s":"A long, bare ridge runs east–west along the border of Kazakhstan and China, rising abruptly from dry steppe. The Tarbagatai take their name from the Mongolian word for marmot, and those burrowing animals still whistle on the grassy slopes. To the north lies the blue sheet of Lake Zaysan; to the south, the Dzungarian Basin opens out.","w":"A lone east–west ridge, snow-streaked in winter, rising from brown steppe, with Lake Zaysan to the north-east.","f":["The range’s highest point, Mount Tastau, falls just short of 3,000 m.","South of the range lies the Dzungarian Gate, a windswept pass that has long linked Central Asia and China."],"q":{"q":"Which animal gave the Tarbagatai their name?","a":"The marmot","x":["The golden eagle","The wild horse"]}},"Q682334":{"t":"The golden mountains at the edge of Tibet","s":"Altyn-Tagh means “golden mountains” in Turkic — a fitting name for these bare, sun-baked ridges. The range forms the north-eastern rim of the Tibetan Plateau, dropping steeply to the Tarim Basin and the salt flats of Lop Nur. Along it runs a huge fault, where the plateau slides sideways past the rest of Asia.","w":"Bare, tawny ridges rising steeply south of the desert, with the pale, ear-shaped salt flats of Lop Nur to the north-east.","f":["The Altyn Tagh Fault runs for about 1,600 km, one of the longest active faults in Asia.","Lop Nur, a dried-up lake, is ringed with salt bands that make it look like a giant ear from space.","The Lop Nur area is one of the last refuges of the wild Bactrian camel."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Altyn-Tagh mean?","a":"Golden mountains","x":["Black mountains","Heavenly mountains"]}},"Q186547":{"t":"Norway’s high spine of ice, fjords and bare plateaus","s":"The Scandinavian Mountains run about 1,700 km along the peninsula, mostly through Norway. From above they are less a line of peaks than a vast, bare highland scraped by ice, scattered with lakes and snowfields and slashed on the west by deep fjords. In Jotunheimen, the “home of the giants”, the land rises to the highest summits in northern Europe.","w":"Bare grey-brown plateaus with snowfields and countless lakes, deep fjords cutting in from the west, glaciers on the highest ground.","f":["Galdhøpiggen, 2,469 m, in Jotunheimen, is the highest peak in Scandinavia.","Jostedalsbreen is the largest glacier in mainland Europe.","Musk oxen roam the Dovrefjell plateau, brought back from Greenland in the mid-20th century."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak in Norway?","a":"Galdhøpiggen","x":["Glittertind","Snøhetta"]}},"Q1288":{"t":"A great forested arc wrapped around Transylvania","s":"The Carpathians curve through Central and Eastern Europe in an arc about 1,500 km long, and from above you can see how they cradle the Transylvanian Basin like a horseshoe. Their slopes carry some of Europe’s largest surviving beech and spruce forests, home to many brown bears, wolves and lynx. Only in the Tatras do they rise to sharp, alpine-looking peaks.","w":"A curving band of dark-forested ridges, with the patchwork fields and towns of the Transylvanian Basin inside the arc.","f":["Gerlachovský štít in Slovakia’s High Tatras, 2,655 m, is the highest peak of the Carpathians.","Romania’s Carpathians hold one of the largest brown bear populations in Europe.","The Danube cuts through the range at the Iron Gates, a gorge on the border of Serbia and Romania."],"q":{"q":"Where does the Danube cut through the Carpathians?","a":"The Iron Gates","x":["The Wachau Valley","The Schlögen Loop"]}},"Q377264":{"t":"The long, bony spine of Italy","s":"The Apennines run some 1,200 km through Italy, from Liguria to the toe of Calabria, and from above they are its unmistakable backbone: grey limestone ridges, beech forest and towns clustered on hilltops. They reach their highest in Abruzzo, at the Gran Sasso. Nearby, look for a strangely flat oval of fields ringed by mountains — the bed of Lake Fucino, drained in the 19th century.","w":"A grey-green mountain spine down the middle of Italy; in Abruzzo, the flat oval of the drained Fucino basin among the peaks.","f":["Corno Grande in the Gran Sasso massif, 2,912 m, is the highest peak of the Apennines.","Under Emperor Claudius the Romans dug a 5.6-km tunnel to drain Lake Fucino, but it was fully drained only in the 1870s."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Apennines?","a":"Corno Grande","x":["Monte Vettore","Monte Amaro"]}},"Q12431":{"t":"A wall between Spain and France, with a country inside","s":"From the Atlantic to the Mediterranean, the Pyrenees form an almost unbroken wall about 430 km long between Spain and France. The French side is greener and steeper; the Spanish side is drier, with long ridges and ochre valleys. Tucked high in the middle is Andorra, a small country of deep valleys and ski slopes.","w":"A continuous snowy crest from sea to sea, with greener valleys on the French side and drier ochre ones on the Spanish side.","f":["Aneto, 3,404 m, on the Spanish side, is the highest peak of the Pyrenees.","The waterfall in the Cirque de Gavarnie drops about 420 m, one of the tallest in Europe.","Andorra la Vella, at about 1,020 m, is the highest capital city in Europe."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Pyrenees?","a":"Aneto","x":["Monte Perdido","Vignemale"]}},"Q5513":{"t":"The deepest lake on Earth, a blue crescent in Siberia","s":"Look for a long blue crescent pressed between dark mountain ridges — that's Baikal, more than 600 km from end to end. Its deepest point is 1,642 m down, deeper than any other lake on Earth. In late winter the ice turns glassy and pale turquoise, split by cracks that run for kilometres.","w":"A narrow crescent of deep blue, or white ice in winter, running south-west to north-east between mountains.","f":["Baikal holds roughly a fifth of all the unfrozen fresh water on Earth's surface.","More than 300 rivers flow into it, but only one — the Angara — flows out.","It is home to the nerpa, a seal that lives nowhere else in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which river is the only one flowing out of Lake Baikal?","a":"The Angara","x":["The Selenga","The Yenisei"]}},"Q23883":{"t":"The lowest water on Earth, and it keeps sinking","s":"The shore of the Dead Sea is the lowest dry land on the planet, more than 430 m below sea level — and the water drops by roughly a metre a year. It is so salty that people float without trying. From above, the deep-blue northern basin gives way to pale geometric salt pans in the south.","w":"A slim blue lake in a deep desert trough east of Jerusalem, with turquoise and white salt ponds at its southern end.","f":["Its water is roughly ten times saltier than the ocean — too salty for fish.","The Jordan River is its main source, and nothing flows out: water leaves only by evaporation.","The southern basin is now mostly a patchwork of evaporation ponds for potash and salt."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how far below sea level is the Dead Sea's shore?","a":"More than 400 m","x":["About 100 m","About 250 m"]}},"Q788":{"t":"A frozen ocean where the ice itself drifts","s":"From a polar flight the Arctic Ocean looks like a cracked white plate: pack ice broken by dark lanes of open water called leads. It is the smallest and shallowest ocean, yet under the North Pole the sea is more than 4 km deep. In summer, pools of meltwater dot the ice with blue.","w":"Endless white pack ice laced with dark cracks; in summer, turquoise meltwater ponds and patches of open sea.","f":["There is no land at the North Pole — only drifting sea ice a few metres thick.","In 1893–1896 Nansen's ship Fram was deliberately frozen in and drifted with the ice across the Arctic.","Near the pole the sun stays up for about six months, then stays below the horizon for six."],"q":{"q":"What lies under the ice at the North Pole?","a":"Ocean more than 4 km deep","x":["A rocky island","A shallow shelf sea"]}},"Q7354":{"t":"The ocean that runs all the way around the world","s":"Few flights ever cross it, which makes this view rare. The Southern Ocean rings Antarctica with no land in its way, so its current circles the globe endlessly from west to east. Look for flat-topped icebergs — some are tens of kilometres long — and, in winter, a white apron of sea ice larger than the continent itself.","w":"Dark, wind-streaked water with scattered white icebergs; farther south, a solid rim of sea ice around Antarctica.","f":["The Antarctic Circumpolar Current carries more water than any other current on Earth.","Sailors named its stormy latitudes the Roaring Forties, the Furious Fifties and the Screaming Sixties.","Its northern boundary is usually drawn at 60° South."],"q":{"q":"In which direction does the Antarctic Circumpolar Current flow?","a":"West to east","x":["East to west","South to north"]}},"Q98":{"t":"Bigger than all the world's land put together","s":"Hours of blue with barely a speck of land: this is the Pacific, covering about a third of the planet. Its deepest point, the Challenger Deep, lies nearly 11 km down — roughly as far below the surface as you are above it. Magellan called it the “peaceful sea” after the calm water he met in 1520.","w":"Deep navy water to every horizon, broken only by rows of cloud and, now and then, the pale ring of an atoll.","f":["The Pacific is larger than all the land on Earth combined.","Volcanoes and earthquake zones rim much of it — the so-called Ring of Fire.","Across the tropics it stretches nearly halfway around the globe."],"q":{"q":"Who named the Pacific after its calm, “peaceful” water?","a":"Ferdinand Magellan","x":["James Cook","Vasco da Gama"]}},"Q1239":{"t":"The warm ocean that powers the monsoon","s":"Sun glint on the Indian Ocean can blaze like hammered metal for minutes on end. This is the warmest of the oceans, and it is closed off in the north by Asia rather than open to the Arctic. That shape drives the monsoon: each summer the heating continent pulls in moist ocean air, bringing the rains to South Asia.","w":"Open blue ocean with a bright path of sun glint; in the tropics, rows of puffy cumulus over the water.","f":["It covers about a fifth of the world's ocean area.","The Maldives, in its north, are some 1,200 small coral islands grouped into 26 atolls.","It is the only ocean named after a country — India."],"q":{"q":"How does the Indian Ocean rank in size among the oceans?","a":"Third largest","x":["Second largest","Fourth largest"]}},"Q97":{"t":"An ocean still growing, a few centimetres a year","s":"Down the middle of the Atlantic, hidden under kilometres of water, runs the Mid-Atlantic Ridge — a volcanic seam where the sea floor is pulling apart. The ocean grows wider by a few centimetres every year, roughly as fast as your fingernails. At the surface, the sun's glint picks out the long, even lines of swell.","w":"Open ocean ribbed with long swell, under drifting cloud fields; the sun's reflection shows the texture of the waves.","f":["Iceland and the Azores are parts of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge that rise above the waves.","The North Atlantic is the busiest stretch of oceanic airspace in the world.","Its name comes from Atlas, the Titan of Greek myth who held up the sky."],"q":{"q":"Which island sits right on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge?","a":"Iceland","x":["Ireland","Madeira"]}},"Q4918":{"t":"The sea in the middle of the land","s":"Its famous deep blue comes from poverty, not richness: the water carries few nutrients and little plankton, so sunlight sinks deep. The Mediterranean joins the Atlantic only through the 14-km gap at Gibraltar. About six million years ago its link to the ocean closed and the sea almost dried out, leaving thick salt layers still buried under its floor.","w":"Intense cobalt-blue water between Europe and Africa, often with a sharp silver sun glint and scattered rocky islands.","f":["Its name comes from the Latin for “in the middle of the land”.","Tides across most of it are small, usually well under half a metre.","More water evaporates than rivers bring in, so it is saltier than the open Atlantic."],"q":{"q":"About how wide is the Strait of Gibraltar at its narrowest?","a":"About 14 km","x":["About 3 km","About 50 km"]}},"Q5484":{"t":"A “sea” that is really the world's largest lake","s":"Call it a sea, but it is the largest body of water on Earth with no outlet to the ocean — bigger than Germany. Its two ends are nothing alike: the shallow north, fed by the Volga, often freezes in winter, while the south plunges past 1,000 m. On the eastern shore look for Kara-Bogaz-Gol, a vivid turquoise lagoon.","w":"A huge inland sea with a pale, silty north and deep blue south; on its east side, the round turquoise Kara-Bogaz-Gol.","f":["Its surface lies about 28 m below the level of the world ocean.","The Volga brings in roughly four-fifths of all the river water the Caspian receives.","For centuries it has been the world's main source of sturgeon and black caviar."],"q":{"q":"Which river supplies most of the Caspian Sea's water?","a":"The Volga","x":["The Ural","The Kura"]}},"Q166":{"t":"A turquoise surface over an oxygen-free deep","s":"In late spring the Black Sea can turn milky turquoise, as blooms of chalk-plated plankton swirl across it. The depths are another world: below about 200 m there is no oxygen at all, and ancient wooden ships lie there almost untouched. It reaches the Mediterranean only through the narrow Bosphorus and Dardanelles.","w":"A broad oval sea between Turkey, the Balkans and the Caucasus; in early summer, pale turquoise swirls on dark water.","f":["Nearly 90% of its water holds no oxygen, only dissolved hydrogen sulphide.","In 2018 an intact Greek trading ship about 2,400 years old was found on its floor.","The Danube reaches it through a vast delta, one of Europe's largest wetlands."],"q":{"q":"What is missing from the Black Sea's deep water?","a":"Oxygen","x":["Salt","Nitrogen"]}},"Q159183":{"t":"A warm, deep nursery for typhoons","s":"This is some of the warmest open water on the planet, and it has a stormy talent: many of the Pacific's fiercest typhoons are born here. The sea is fenced by island chains — the Philippines, Taiwan, the Ryukyus and Japan to the west and north, the Marianas to the east. Far below the surface, trenches cut more than 10 km deep.","w":"Deep indigo ocean dotted with towering cumulus; island arcs mark its edges — Luzon and Taiwan to the west, the Marianas to the east.","f":["The warm Kuroshio current sets off along its western edge and runs north past Japan.","The Philippine Trench, along its western side, drops to more than 10 km.","The sea sits on a tectonic plate of its own, the Philippine Sea Plate."],"q":{"q":"Which warm current runs north along the Philippine Sea's western edge?","a":"The Kuroshio","x":["The Oyashio","The Humboldt Current"]}},"Q82931":{"t":"Home of the Great Barrier Reef","s":"The Coral Sea earns its name on its western side, where the Great Barrier Reef runs for about 2,300 km along Australia's coast. From above, the reefs look like turquoise brushstrokes and pale rings scattered on deep blue, with white surf on their seaward edges. Farther east the water deepens to a dark indigo.","w":"Turquoise reef patches and white surf lines off north-east Australia, fading into deep open blue to the east.","f":["The Great Barrier Reef is made of nearly 3,000 separate reefs and hundreds of islands.","It is the largest structure built by living things, and it can be seen from orbit.","The reef has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1981."],"q":{"q":"About how long is the Great Barrier Reef?","a":"About 2,300 km","x":["About 600 km","About 5,000 km"]}},"Q33254":{"t":"“The Ditch” between Australia and New Zealand","s":"Australians and New Zealanders call it “the Ditch”, but it is roughly 2,000 km across — about three hours in the air. The Tasman is known for rough water, stirred up by westerly winds from the Southern Ocean. Its name honours Abel Tasman, the Dutch navigator who crossed it in 1642 and sighted New Zealand.","w":"Open grey-blue water between two coastlines, often streaked with whitecaps and bands of cloud moving in from the west.","f":["Lord Howe Island, in its western part, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Nearby Ball's Pyramid, a volcanic stack 562 m tall, rises straight out of the sea.","On its floor lies the Lord Howe Rise, part of Zealandia, a continent that is mostly underwater."],"q":{"q":"What do Australians and New Zealanders nickname the Tasman Sea?","a":"The Ditch","x":["The Puddle","The Gap"]}},"Q38684":{"t":"The world's largest bay, fed by the Ganges","s":"At the head of the bay, the Ganges and Brahmaputra pour out so much silt that the sea turns café-au-lait brown far from shore. This is the largest bay on Earth, and at its top lies the world's biggest delta, fringed by the tangled green of the Sundarbans mangroves. Farther south the silt settles and the water turns deep blue.","w":"A vast triangle of sea between India and Myanmar; near Bangladesh, brown silt plumes swirl into the blue.","f":["The Sundarbans on its northern shore are the largest mangrove forest in the world.","Beneath the bay lies the Bengal Fan, a pile of river sediment some 3,000 km long.","River water makes its surface noticeably less salty than the open ocean."],"q":{"q":"Which rivers build the huge delta at the head of the Bay of Bengal?","a":"Ganges and Brahmaputra","x":["Indus and Narmada","Irrawaddy and Salween"]}},"Q37660":{"t":"One of the busiest seaways on the planet","s":"In clear weather, look for faint white scratches on the blue — the wakes of ships. A huge share of the world's sea trade crosses this water between the Strait of Malacca, China, Japan and Korea. Scattered across the middle are low coral reefs and atolls, which show as turquoise rings where deep water suddenly gives way to shallows.","w":"Wide blue sea between Vietnam, the Philippines and southern China, with ship wakes and small turquoise reef rings.","f":["Roughly a third of all global shipping passes through it.","The Mekong and the Pearl River both empty into it — the latter right beside Hong Kong.","Its deepest basin goes down more than 5,000 m."],"q":{"q":"Which great river's delta opens onto the South China Sea?","a":"The Mekong","x":["The Irrawaddy","The Ganges"]}},"Q27092":{"t":"Cloud streets by day, squid-boat lights by night","s":"On a winter flight, look for neat parallel lines of cloud streaming across the water from the Asian mainland: icy monsoon winds pick up moisture here and dump it as deep snow on Japan's western coast. At night the sea can sparkle with points of light — fishing boats using bright lamps to lure squid.","w":"An enclosed sea between Japan and the Asian mainland; in winter, rows of cloud stripes blowing towards Japan.","f":["It connects to the open ocean only through a handful of narrow straits.","Its deepest basin reaches about 3,700 m.","In winter, sea ice forms in its far north, near the Tatar Strait."],"q":{"q":"What makes the Sea of Japan glitter at night?","a":"Lamps on squid-fishing boats","x":["Offshore oil platforms","Floating solar farms"]}},"Q58705":{"t":"Monsoon sea between Arabia and India","s":"Every summer the south-west monsoon sweeps across the Arabian Sea, stirring cold, nutrient-rich water to the surface off Oman — plankton blooms can then paint the sea green. For thousands of years sailors rode these seasonal winds between Arabia and India in wooden dhows. In the north, the Indus fans out into a muddy delta.","w":"A wide sea between the Arabian Peninsula and India; in summer, greenish plankton swirls may show off Oman.","f":["The Indus reaches the sea through a broad delta on Pakistan's coast.","Below the surface lies one of the largest oxygen-poor zones in any ocean.","A 1st-century Greek sailing guide, the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea, describes trade across it."],"q":{"q":"Which great river flows into the Arabian Sea?","a":"The Indus","x":["The Ganges","The Brahmaputra"]}},"Q131274":{"t":"Where the Mackenzie meets the Arctic ice","s":"For most of the year the Beaufort Sea is a white plain of drifting ice, turned slowly clockwise by a vast ocean eddy. In summer the ice pulls back from the coast, and the Mackenzie River spills a brown plume of silt from a delta braided with countless lakes. Look for that tea-coloured stain against the ice.","w":"Sea ice north of Alaska and Canada; in summer, a brown river plume and a lake-speckled delta on the Canadian shore.","f":["It is named after Admiral Francis Beaufort, who devised the wind-force scale.","The Mackenzie, Canada's longest river, builds a huge delta on its shore.","Bowhead whales pass through it every year on their seasonal migration."],"q":{"q":"The Beaufort Sea's namesake is known for devising what?","a":"A wind-force scale","x":["A map projection","A tide table"]}},"Q1247":{"t":"Turquoise shallows beside deep blue water","s":"The famous colours are real: over sandbanks and reefs the Caribbean glows turquoise and jade, with white sand showing through. Just off those shallows the floor drops away to deep blue — in the Cayman Trough it lies about 7,700 m down. The sea is fenced in by the arc of the Antilles, a chain of islands curving from Cuba almost to Venezuela.","w":"Turquoise and emerald shallows around islands, white sand bars, and a sharp edge where the water turns deep blue.","f":["It takes its name from the Caribs, a people of the Lesser Antilles.","The Mesoamerican Barrier Reef off Belize and Mexico is the largest reef system in the Americas.","The Panama Canal links it to the Pacific across a narrow isthmus."],"q":{"q":"Which people is the Caribbean Sea named after?","a":"The Caribs","x":["The Maya","The Taíno"]}},"Q12630":{"t":"A warm basin with a bird's-foot delta","s":"Where the Mississippi reaches the Gulf, its delta splays out like a bird's foot, trailing brown silt into the blue. The Gulf is a warm, nearly round basin, and at night hundreds of offshore oil and gas platforms scatter lights across it. Buried off Yucatán lies the crater of the asteroid that ended the age of the dinosaurs.","w":"A broad, rounded gulf; off Louisiana, the Mississippi's fingered delta and brown plume; at night, scattered platform lights offshore.","f":["The Chicxulub crater, about 180 km across, lies partly beneath its floor.","Padre Island, off the Texas coast, is the longest barrier island in the world.","The warm Loop Current flows in between Yucatán and Cuba and leaves past Florida."],"q":{"q":"What shape is the Mississippi Delta famous for?","a":"A bird's foot","x":["A perfect fan","A crescent"]}},"Q184189":{"t":"Iceberg highway between Greenland and Labrador","s":"Many flights between Europe and North America cross this cold sea between Greenland and Labrador. In spring and early summer, look for white specks on dark water: icebergs calved from Greenland's glaciers, riding the Labrador Current south towards Newfoundland. In winter, surface water here grows so cold and dense that it sinks deep, helping drive the ocean's slow global circulation.","w":"Dark open water between Greenland's southern tip and the Labrador coast; in spring, scattered white icebergs and drifting pack ice.","f":["Norse sagas describe Markland, a forested coast thought to be Labrador, reached around AD 1000.","The cold Labrador Current flows south along its western side, carrying icebergs and sea ice.","Cape Farewell, the southern tip of Greenland, marks its north-eastern corner."],"q":{"q":"Where do most icebergs in the Labrador Sea come from?","a":"Greenland's glaciers","x":["Antarctica","Hudson Bay"]}},"Q3040":{"t":"An inland sea that freezes over every winter","s":"Hudson Bay is a true sea set inside a continent, and each winter it freezes over almost completely. Look at its eastern coast: it bends in a smooth, near-perfect arc, as if traced around a giant plate. The land around the bay is still slowly rising, springing back now that the weight of the ice-age glaciers is gone.","w":"A vast, roughly round bay in northern Canada — white ice in winter, dark water in late summer; note the smooth eastern arc.","f":["The smooth curve of its eastern shore is known as the Nastapoka Arc.","The land around it is still rising by about a centimetre a year since the last ice age.","Each autumn polar bears gather near Churchill, waiting for the bay to freeze so they can hunt seals."],"q":{"q":"What are Churchill's polar bears waiting for each autumn?","a":"The bay to freeze over","x":["The salmon run","The first snowfall"]}},"Q37040":{"t":"Iceberg-strewn water between Baffin Island and Greenland","s":"On a clear day Baffin Bay is all contrast: Greenland's white ice cap and glacier tongues on one side, the snowy mountains of Baffin Island on the other. Icebergs from glaciers such as Ilulissat drift across the water like scattered sugar cubes. In winter most of the bay freezes — except the North Water in the far north, which stays open.","w":"A long bay between Greenland's ice-covered coast and Baffin Island; white icebergs dot the dark water in summer.","f":["The North Water is one of the largest patches of Arctic sea that stays open through the winter.","Ilulissat Icefjord on its Greenland shore, a UNESCO site, sends huge icebergs into the bay.","It is named after William Baffin, who sailed and charted it in 1616."],"q":{"q":"What is the North Water in Baffin Bay?","a":"Sea that stays ice-free in winter","x":["A deep undersea canyon","A freshwater lake on an island"]}},"Q180531":{"t":"Where glaciers pour down to the sea","s":"Few coasts look wilder from the air. Along the Gulf of Alaska, ice fields spill glaciers straight towards the sea, and the Malaspina Glacier spreads across the coastal plain in a huge lobe striped like marble cake. Where silty meltwater meets clear ocean, you may see a sharp line between milky green and deep blue.","w":"The curving southern coast of Alaska: snowy peaks, glacier tongues, and the vast rounded lobe of the Malaspina Glacier.","f":["The Malaspina is one of the world's largest piedmont glaciers — ice that spreads out over flat land.","Mount Saint Elias, 5,489 m high, rises close to the gulf's northern shore.","Kodiak Island, on its western side, is home to the huge Kodiak brown bear."],"q":{"q":"About how high is Mount Saint Elias, above the Gulf of Alaska?","a":"About 5,500 m","x":["About 3,500 m","About 7,500 m"]}},"Q23406":{"t":"A young ocean being born between Africa and Arabia","s":"The Red Sea is an ocean in the making: Africa and Arabia are slowly pulling apart, and the crack between them has flooded. From the air it's a long, narrow blue channel between desert coasts, fringed with turquoise reefs. In the north it splits around the Sinai into two horns — the Gulfs of Suez and Aqaba.","w":"A long, slender sea between brown desert shores, with turquoise coral fringes; at its top, two arms around the Sinai triangle.","f":["No permanent river flows into it, and it is one of the saltiest seas connected to the ocean.","Africa and Arabia are drifting apart here by roughly a centimetre a year.","Since 1869 the Suez Canal has linked its northern tip to the Mediterranean."],"q":{"q":"Which two gulfs form the Red Sea's northern “horns”?","a":"Suez and Aqaba","x":["Aden and Oman","Aqaba and Sidra"]}},"Q41602":{"t":"A northern sea whose ice reaches Hokkaido","s":"In winter the Sea of Okhotsk turns white far to the south: its drift ice reaches the coast of Hokkaido, at roughly the latitude of Venice. The sea is walled in by Kamchatka to the east, Sakhalin to the west and the long chain of Kuril volcanoes to the south-east. In summer, fog often hides the water.","w":"A broad northern sea between Kamchatka, Sakhalin and the Kuril arc; white with ice in winter, often under fog in summer.","f":["Winter drift ice here reaches Hokkaido's coast at around 44° North.","The Kuril Islands, a chain of volcanoes, separate it from the open Pacific.","Okhotsk, founded on its shore in the 1640s, was the first Russian settlement on the Pacific."],"q":{"q":"Which island's coast does Okhotsk drift ice reach in the south?","a":"Hokkaido","x":["Honshu","Kyushu"]}},"Q173343":{"t":"Home of the clearest seawater ever measured","s":"The Weddell Sea is ruled by ice. Its pack ice turns in a slow clockwise gyre that trapped Shackleton's ship Endurance in 1915 and crushed it; the crew all survived, and the wreck was found in 2022 some 3 km down. To the south lies the Filchner–Ronne Ice Shelf, a floating plain of ice ending in cliffs tens of metres high.","w":"A vast sheet of pack ice east of the Antarctic Peninsula, ending at the flat white cliff of a floating ice shelf.","f":["In 1986 a white disc lowered into its water stayed visible to 79 m — the clearest seawater ever recorded.","The Filchner–Ronne Ice Shelf at its southern end is one of the largest floating ice shelves on Earth.","It is named after James Weddell, a British sealer who sailed deep into it in 1823."],"q":{"q":"Whose ship was crushed by the Weddell Sea's ice in 1915?","a":"Ernest Shackleton's","x":["Roald Amundsen's","Robert Scott's"]}},"Q34675":{"t":"Shallow, warm, and dotted with man-made islands","s":"This is a shallow, bath-warm sea — on average only about 50 m deep, with summer surface water often above 30 °C. Look off Dubai for the unmistakable fronds of the palm-shaped artificial islands, and for Qatar jutting north like a thumb. At night, gas flares and city lights ring the water. Before oil, its wealth came from pearls.","w":"A shallow turquoise gulf between Iran and Arabia; spot Qatar's thumb-shaped peninsula and Dubai's palm-shaped islands.","f":["The Tigris and Euphrates reach it together through the Shatt al-Arab.","Its only outlet to the ocean is the Strait of Hormuz, less than 40 km wide at its narrowest.","For centuries pearl diving was the main livelihood of towns on its Arabian shore."],"q":{"q":"Which peninsula juts into the Persian Gulf like a thumb?","a":"Qatar","x":["Sinai","Kathiawar"]}},"Q164466":{"t":"The sea at the foot of the great ice barrier","s":"The Ross Sea ends in a wall. Its southern edge is the Ross Ice Shelf, a floating slab of ice roughly the size of France, whose cliffs early explorers simply called “the Barrier”. On Ross Island, Mount Erebus sends up steam from a lava lake in its crater. Nowhere does open sea reach closer to the South Pole.","w":"Dark water and pack ice ending at a flat, sharp-edged white plateau of floating ice; to the west, snowy volcanic Ross Island.","f":["The ice shelf's front rises tens of metres above the sea and runs for some 600 km.","Mount Erebus, 3,794 m high, is the southernmost active volcano on Earth.","Much of the Ross Sea has been a marine protected area since 2017."],"q":{"q":"The Ross Ice Shelf is roughly the size of which country?","a":"France","x":["Belgium","Ireland"]}},"Q5525":{"t":"The biggest lake wholly in Canada, on the Arctic Circle","s":"Great Bear Lake sprawls across the northern forest and tundra in five long arms, like a starfish pressed into the land. The Arctic Circle crosses its northern part, and ice often lingers on the water into July. It is the largest lake lying entirely inside Canada, and it is almost untouched: only one community, Déline, sits on its shore.","w":"A huge, many-armed lake in the far north-west of Canada, white or streaked with ice for much of the year.","f":["Its outflow, the Great Bear River, runs into the Mackenzie.","A 33-kg lake trout caught here in 1995 set a rod-and-reel world record.","The lake and its watershed form Tsá Tué, a UNESCO biosphere reserve."],"q":{"q":"Which line crosses Great Bear Lake?","a":"The Arctic Circle","x":["The 60th parallel","The Continental Divide"]}},"Q5539":{"t":"North America's deepest lake","s":"Great Slave Lake has two faces. The main western basin is broad and open, while the East Arm is a maze of cliffs, islands and deep channels — the lake reaches 614 m there, deeper than any other in North America. The territorial capital, Yellowknife, sits on the north shore, and the Mackenzie River slips out of the western end.","w":"A wide lake with a long, island-studded eastern arm; at night, Yellowknife's lights on the north shore.","f":["Its name comes from the Slavey, a Dene people, not from slavery.","In winter an ice road across the frozen lake links Yellowknife with the community of Dettah.","Ice covers the lake for roughly half of every year."],"q":{"q":"How deep is Great Slave Lake at its deepest?","a":"614 m","x":["214 m","1,014 m"]}},"Q3272":{"t":"A shallow giant left behind by an ice-age lake","s":"Lake Winnipeg stretches north for over 400 km, pinched at the waist where its northern and southern basins meet at the Narrows. For all its size it is shallow — about 12 m on average — and in summer, algae blooms can swirl across it in shades of green visible from the air. Its name means “muddy water” in Cree.","w":"A long lake north of the city of Winnipeg, pinched in the middle; often greenish in late summer.","f":["It is a remnant of glacial Lake Agassiz, which once covered more area than all of today's Great Lakes.","Its outflow, the Nelson River, carries its water north to Hudson Bay.","Its drainage basin reaches all the way to the Rocky Mountains."],"q":{"q":"What does the Cree name “Winnipeg” mean?","a":"Muddy water","x":["Big water","Lake of spirits"]}},"Q5492":{"t":"The shallow, warm Great Lake that feeds Niagara","s":"Lake Erie is the shallowest of the Great Lakes, so it warms fastest in summer and is the one most likely to freeze over in winter. From above, look for Long Point, a slender sand spit curving about 40 km out from the Canadian shore. The lake's water leaves eastward, over Niagara Falls.","w":"A long oval lake between Ontario and Ohio; look for the thin hooked spit of Long Point on the northern shore.","f":["It averages only about 19 m deep — far less than the other Great Lakes.","Point Pelee, jutting into its western part, is the southern tip of mainland Canada.","In late summer, green algae blooms in its western basin can be seen from space."],"q":{"q":"Which of the Great Lakes is the shallowest?","a":"Lake Erie","x":["Lake Ontario","Lake Huron"]}},"Q1062":{"t":"The last Great Lake before the sea","s":"This is the final step in the Great Lakes' staircase: water arrives down the Niagara River and leaves through the St Lawrence towards the Atlantic. Lake Ontario is the smallest of the five by area, yet deep enough that it rarely freezes over. At night, look for the dense glow of Toronto and its suburbs wrapped around the western shore.","w":"An oval lake with Toronto's urban sprawl on its north-west shore; at its eastern end, a scatter of islands.","f":["The St Lawrence leaves it among the Thousand Islands — in fact there are more than 1,800.","Its deepest point is about 244 m.","Winds crossing it dump heavy lake-effect snow on New York's Tug Hill Plateau."],"q":{"q":"Which river carries Lake Ontario's water to the Atlantic?","a":"The St Lawrence","x":["The Hudson","The Ottawa"]}},"Q1066":{"t":"The largest freshwater lake on Earth by area","s":"Lake Superior is big enough to have its own horizon: at 563 km long, even from cruise height you may not see it end to end. No freshwater lake on Earth covers more area, and its cold, clear water holds about a tenth of all the fresh water on the planet's surface. In winter, ice and snow trace its rocky outline in white.","w":"A vast lake shaped a little like a wolf's head facing west, with the long Keweenaw Peninsula jutting from its southern shore.","f":["A drop of water that enters it stays, on average, for almost 200 years.","Isle Royale, in its north-west, has hosted a study of wolves and moose since 1958 — one of the longest of its kind.","Its deepest point lies 406 m below the surface."],"q":{"q":"On average, how long does water stay in Lake Superior?","a":"Almost 200 years","x":["About 20 years","About 2 years"]}},"Q1169":{"t":"The only Great Lake wholly in the United States","s":"Lake Michigan hangs down from the Straits of Mackinac like a long blue pendant, with Chicago's grid pressed against its south-western corner. Its eastern shore is lined with pale sand dunes, some rising over 100 m above the water. Strictly speaking, it is one lake with Huron: the two meet at the Straits and share the same level.","w":"A long north–south lake: Chicago's sprawl at the south-west, pale dune shores along the east, the Mackinac straits at the top.","f":["Its eastern shore holds the largest freshwater sand dunes in the world.","The Mackinac Bridge, about 8 km long, spans the straits that link it to Lake Huron.","Its name comes from an Ojibwe word meaning “great water”."],"q":{"q":"Which lake is Lake Michigan hydrologically joined with?","a":"Lake Huron","x":["Lake Superior","Lake Erie"]}},"Q1383":{"t":"The Great Lake with tens of thousands of islands","s":"Lake Huron is the island lake of the five. Along its north and east, the granite shore breaks into thousands of rocky islets, and Manitoulin — the largest island in any freshwater lake on Earth — divides the main lake from the North Channel. Georgian Bay, almost a lake in its own right, opens to the east.","w":"A broad lake east of Michigan's mitten; the long island of Manitoulin in the north and Georgian Bay to the east.","f":["Counting all its islands, it has the longest shoreline of the Great Lakes.","Saginaw Bay cuts into Michigan's shore, separating the “thumb” from the rest of the mitten.","The French named it after the Huron, or Wendat, people."],"q":{"q":"Which Lake Huron island is the largest in any freshwater lake?","a":"Manitoulin","x":["Isle Royale","Mackinac Island"]}},"Q5532":{"t":"A lake with more fish species than any other","s":"Lake Malawi fills a long trench of the East African Rift, a slim blue blade about 580 km long hemmed in by steep escarpments. Beneath the surface swim more fish species than in any other lake on Earth — hundreds of brightly coloured cichlids, most of them found nowhere else. At night, fishermen's lamps can speckle the dark water.","w":"A long, narrow lake running north–south in the Rift Valley, with mountains rising steeply along its northern shores.","f":["Its deepest point is more than 700 m down.","Lake Malawi National Park, at its southern end, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","It is also called Lake Nyasa, and Lago Niassa in Mozambique."],"q":{"q":"Which fish make up most of Lake Malawi's famous variety?","a":"Cichlids","x":["Salmon","Sturgeons"]}},"Q5511":{"t":"The longest freshwater lake in the world","s":"Lake Tanganyika runs for about 670 km along the floor of the Rift Valley, farther than any other freshwater lake. It is narrow — about 50 km wide on average — but plunges to 1,470 m, second only to Baikal. Its long, steep shores are shared by four countries, and the water is a deep, clear blue.","w":"A very long, narrow lake running north–south in a deep rift, lined by steep mountain walls on both sides.","f":["Tanzania, DR Congo, Burundi and Zambia share its shores.","In 1871 Stanley found Livingstone at Ujiji, on its eastern shore.","It is among the oldest lakes on Earth, formed roughly 10 million years ago."],"q":{"q":"Which lake is deeper than Lake Tanganyika?","a":"Lake Baikal","x":["Lake Malawi","Lake Superior"]}},"Q5505":{"t":"Africa's largest lake, where the White Nile begins","s":"Lake Victoria is so wide it looks like an inland sea, with a ragged coastline of bays, peninsulas and green islands. It is Africa's largest lake, yet surprisingly shallow — only about 80 m at its deepest. The equator crosses its northern waters, and from its northern shore the White Nile slips out on its long journey to the Mediterranean.","w":"A huge lake straddling the equator, with a jagged shoreline, many islands and afternoon thunderclouds building over the water.","f":["It is the largest tropical lake in the world and the second-largest freshwater lake by area.","John Hanning Speke named it after Queen Victoria in 1858; “Nyanza” simply means “lake” in several local languages.","Uganda, Kenya and Tanzania share its waters."],"q":{"q":"Which great river flows out of Lake Victoria?","a":"The White Nile","x":["The Congo","The Blue Nile"]}},"Q173862":{"t":"A freshwater sea with a two-volcano island","s":"Look for Ometepe: an island shaped like a figure of eight, formed by two volcanic cones joined by a low neck of land. It rises from the largest lake in Central America, so wide that the Spanish called it the Mar Dulce, the sweet sea. Bull sharks have long swum here from the Caribbean, up the San Juan River.","w":"A broad lake in south-west Nicaragua; spot the twin volcanic cones of Ometepe island near its western shore.","f":["Ometepe's taller volcano, Concepción, is active and rises to about 1,600 m.","Near the city of Granada, hundreds of tiny islets, Las Isletas, are scattered along the shore.","The lake drains to the Caribbean through the San Juan River."],"q":{"q":"What forms the island of Ometepe?","a":"Two volcanoes","x":["A coral reef","A river delta"]}},"Q1134446":{"t":"A clear, island-studded lake of the Canadian Shield","s":"Reindeer Lake is pure Canadian Shield: a long sheet of dark, clear water scattered with rocky, forested islands, its shores carved into countless bays by ice-age glaciers. It stretches north–south through almost roadless forest. Its water drains south through the Reindeer River into the Churchill, and on to Hudson Bay.","w":"A long north–south lake with ragged shores and many rocky islands, set in dark forest in north-eastern Saskatchewan.","f":["It is the second-largest lake in Saskatchewan, after Lake Athabasca.","The Cree community of Southend sits at its southern tip.","Its cold, clear water is home to big lake trout and northern pike."],"q":{"q":"Where does Reindeer Lake's water finally end up?","a":"Hudson Bay","x":["The Pacific Ocean","The Gulf of Mexico"]}},"Q845876":{"t":"A maze of islands with a notch in the border","s":"From above, Lake of the Woods looks like two lakes in one: an open, rounded basin in the south, and in the north a shattered maze of some 14,500 rocky, forested islands. On its western side sits the Northwest Angle, a quirk of old map-making that leaves a piece of Minnesota north of the 49th parallel, reachable by land only through Canada.","w":"A lake on the Canada–US border: an open southern bay and a northern labyrinth of wooded islands.","f":["The Northwest Angle is the only part of the contiguous United States north of the 49th parallel.","Its water drains north through the Winnipeg River.","The town of Kenora, Ontario, sits at its northern outlet."],"q":{"q":"About how many islands does Lake of the Woods have?","a":"About 14,500","x":["About 1,500","About 4,500"]}},"Q122184":{"t":"The big lake that feeds Lake Superior","s":"Just north of Lake Superior lies a big, ragged lake scattered with hundreds of islands. Lake Nipigon is the largest lake entirely inside Ontario, and it drains south through the Nipigon River — the biggest river flowing into Superior. Around it stretches almost unbroken boreal forest, dark green in summer and white in winter.","w":"A large, island-dotted lake a short way north of Lake Superior's northern shore, surrounded by dark forest.","f":["The world-record brook trout, 6.6 kg, was caught in the Nipigon River in 1915.","The lake covers about 4,800 km²."],"q":{"q":"Into which Great Lake does Lake Nipigon drain?","a":"Lake Superior","x":["Lake Huron","Lake Michigan"]}},"Q645901":{"t":"The lake that gave a province its name","s":"Lake Manitoba is long and shallow, and halfway along it narrows to a slim neck called the Narrows. There, waves striking limestone shores make eerie booming sounds, which people heard as the drum of a spirit — the likely origin of the lake's name, and of the province named after it. It is nowhere much deeper than 7 m.","w":"A long, slender lake west of Lake Winnipeg, narrowing sharply halfway up; marshes fringe its southern end.","f":["Delta Marsh, on its southern shore, is one of the largest freshwater marshes in North America.","Its water flows on, through smaller rivers and lakes, into Lake Winnipeg."],"q":{"q":"What was named after Lake Manitoba?","a":"A Canadian province","x":["A U.S. state","A mountain range"]}},"Q934638":{"t":"Winnipeg's “little muddy” sister","s":"Three big lakes lie side by side in Manitoba, all remnants of the same vanished ice-age lake. Winnipegosis is the westernmost: long, narrow and shallow, with a shoreline of low points and quiet bays. Even its name is the little sister of Winnipeg's — roughly “little muddy water” in Cree. Its water flows south into Lake Manitoba.","w":"The westernmost of Manitoba's three great lakes: long and narrow, running north–south, west of Lake Winnipeg.","f":["It drains south into Lake Manitoba through the Waterhen River.","Lakes Winnipegosis, Manitoba and Winnipeg are all remnants of glacial Lake Agassiz.","It covers more than 5,000 km², yet over most of that area it is only a few metres deep."],"q":{"q":"Which lake does Lake Winnipegosis flow into directly?","a":"Lake Manitoba","x":["Lake Athabasca","Lake of the Woods"]}},"Q272463":{"t":"A northern lake with a desert on its shore","s":"Along the south shore of Lake Athabasca, the dark forest suddenly gives way to bare, pale sand: the Athabasca Sand Dunes, among the most northerly large active dunes on Earth. At the lake's western end, the Peace and Athabasca rivers build a vast green delta. The north shore is rocky Canadian Shield, pitted with small lakes.","w":"A long east–west lake on the Alberta–Saskatchewan border; look for pale patches of sand along its southern shore.","f":["The Peace–Athabasca Delta is one of the largest freshwater deltas in the world.","Fort Chipewyan, founded on its shore in 1788, is one of Alberta's oldest settlements.","Its water flows north via the Slave River towards Great Slave Lake and the Arctic."],"q":{"q":"What unusual landscape lies on Lake Athabasca's southern shore?","a":"Active sand dunes","x":["Hot springs","A volcanic crater"]}},"Q1458051":{"t":"Quebec's largest natural lake, split by islands","s":"Lake Mistassini looks as if it was raked by a giant comb: long and narrow, it runs north-east for more than 150 km, with a chain of slender islands dividing it lengthwise. The Cree community of Mistissini sits on its southern shore, and the lake drains west through the Rupert River into James Bay.","w":"A long, narrow lake running south-west to north-east in the forests of central Quebec, split down the middle by islands.","f":["Its name comes from the Cree for “big rock”.","It covers about 2,300 km², more than any other natural lake in Quebec."],"q":{"q":"Where does Lake Mistassini's water end up?","a":"James Bay","x":["The Gulf of St Lawrence","Lake Ontario"]}},"Q1267413":{"t":"Lake Huron's big bay of thirty thousand islands","s":"Georgian Bay is so large it is sometimes called the sixth Great Lake, though it is really an arm of Lake Huron. The Bruce Peninsula walls it off in the west, a finger of limestone cliffs above turquoise water. Along the eastern shore, pinkish granite breaks into the Thirty Thousand Islands, a glittering scatter from the air.","w":"A large bay east of Lake Huron, closed off by the finger of the Bruce Peninsula, with a maze of islands on its eastern shore.","f":["The Thirty Thousand Islands form the world's largest freshwater archipelago.","The Bruce Peninsula is part of the Niagara Escarpment, the same rock ridge that Niagara Falls pours over.","It was named in 1822 after King George IV."],"q":{"q":"Which peninsula separates Georgian Bay from the rest of Lake Huron?","a":"The Bruce Peninsula","x":["The Keweenaw Peninsula","The Door Peninsula"]}},"Q15288":{"t":"Europe's largest lake, rocky in the north","s":"Europe's largest lake has two faces. Its southern shore is low and marshy, but the north breaks into skerries — rocky islands and narrow inlets gouged by glaciers — where the water plunges to over 200 m. On the Valaam islands, in the north, stands a monastery founded in the Middle Ages.","w":"A huge lake north-east of St Petersburg, low-shored in the south; in the north, a rocky maze of islands and inlets.","f":["The Neva carries its water only about 74 km to the Baltic, straight through St Petersburg.","It is home to the Ladoga ringed seal, a subspecies found nowhere else.","The lake covers about 17,700 km² — more than half the size of Belgium."],"q":{"q":"Which river carries Lake Ladoga's water to the Baltic?","a":"The Neva","x":["The Svir","The Volkhov"]}},"Q134485":{"t":"A lake that is half fresh and half salty","s":"Lake Balkhash is a long, thin arc across the Kazakh steppe, some 600 km from end to end, and it is two lakes in one. The western half, fed by the Ili River, is nearly fresh; the eastern half, linked to it only by a narrow strait, is salty. From above, the two halves often show slightly different shades.","w":"A long, thin arc of water across the dry steppe of eastern Kazakhstan, with the green Ili delta near its western end.","f":["The Ili River, flowing in from the south, supplies most of its water.","The strait linking its two halves is only a few kilometres wide.","It has no outlet: water leaves only by evaporation."],"q":{"q":"Which river feeds Lake Balkhash?","a":"The Ili","x":["The Irtysh","The Syr Darya"]}},"Q125888":{"t":"A rift-valley lake the Nile passes through","s":"Lake Albert lies in a deep trough of the western Rift Valley, walled by steep escarpments — on the Congo side they rise abruptly for hundreds of metres. The Nile only brushes its northern tip: the Victoria Nile flows in below Murchison Falls, and the Albert Nile leaves a few kilometres away. The lake stretches about 160 km from south-west to north-east.","w":"A long, narrow lake between steep escarpments on the Uganda–Congo border, with green papyrus flats at its northern end.","f":["Samuel Baker reached it in 1864 and named it after Prince Albert, Queen Victoria's late husband.","The Semliki River flows in at its southern end, carrying water from the Rwenzori Mountains."],"q":{"q":"Who named Lake Albert in 1864?","a":"Samuel Baker","x":["John Hanning Speke","Henry Morton Stanley"]}},"Q173596":{"t":"Sweden's inland sea, split into two basins","s":"Sweden's biggest lake has two parts: the broad main basin and, to the south-west, the smaller Dalbosjön, almost cut off by two peninsulas reaching towards each other. Rocky islands and skerries dot the water, and at Karlstad on the northern shore the Klarälven river fans out into a delta. Vänern is the largest lake in the European Union.","w":"A huge lake in south-west Sweden, pinched into two basins by peninsulas, with rocky islands and a delta at Karlstad in the north.","f":["Its water leaves through the Göta älv, reaching the sea at Gothenburg.","The Göta Canal links it eastwards to Lake Vättern and on to the Baltic.","It covers about 5,600 km²."],"q":{"q":"Which city stands at the mouth of the river draining Vänern?","a":"Gothenburg","x":["Stockholm","Malmö"]}},"Q166162":{"t":"Europe's second-largest lake, with fingers in the north","s":"Look at Lake Onega's northern end: it splits into long, narrow bays lying side by side like the fingers of a hand, shaped by the ice sheets. The south is a broad, open basin. Among the northern islands lies Kizhi, whose wooden Church of the Transfiguration is crowned with 22 domes — far too small to spot from here, but worth knowing about.","w":"A big lake north-east of Ladoga, its northern end split into long parallel bays; Petrozavodsk on the western shore.","f":["The wooden churches of Kizhi are a UNESCO World Heritage site.","More than a thousand rock carvings, thousands of years old, cover granite ledges on its eastern shore.","The Svir River carries its water west into Lake Ladoga."],"q":{"q":"How many domes crown the Church of the Transfiguration on Kizhi?","a":"22","x":["12","33"]}},"Q116685":{"t":"The lake where the Blue Nile begins","s":"High on the Ethiopian plateau, about 1,800 m up, Lake Tana is the source of the Blue Nile, which slips out of its southern shore at Bahir Dar. Its islands and peninsulas shelter old monasteries with painted churches, and fishermen still paddle papyrus boats called tankwa. From the air it's a broad, rounded lake ringed by green farmland.","w":"A broad, roundish lake on the green Ethiopian highlands, with Bahir Dar and the Blue Nile's outflow at its southern tip.","f":["The Blue Nile supplies most of the water that flows down the Nile through Egypt.","About 30 km downstream, the Blue Nile plunges over the Tis Issat falls.","Some of its island monasteries date back to the 14th century."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows out of Lake Tana?","a":"The Blue Nile","x":["The White Nile","The Awash"]}},"Q35342":{"t":"A deep-blue lake at 3,800 m, with floating islands","s":"Lake Titicaca sits at about 3,810 m — more than a third of the way up to your cruising height — and its water looks an intense, dark blue in the thin mountain air. Beyond it rise the snowy peaks of Bolivia's Cordillera Real. On its Peruvian side, the Uros people live on floating islands built from totora reeds.","w":"A large, irregular deep-blue lake on the bare high plateau, with snow-capped mountains rising along its eastern side.","f":["It is often called the highest navigable lake in the world.","Its two basins are joined by the narrow Strait of Tiquina.","In Inca legend, the founders of the Inca dynasty rose from its waters."],"q":{"q":"What are the Uros people's floating islands made of?","a":"Totora reeds","x":["Balsa logs","Pumice stone"]}},"Q192770":{"t":"A labyrinth of water and 14,000 islands","s":"From above, Saimaa hardly looks like a lake at all — more like a jigsaw of blue channels, pine-dark islands and narrow sounds spread across eastern Finland. It has around 14,000 islands and a shoreline so convoluted that it runs to about 15,000 km. In winter the whole maze freezes into a white puzzle.","w":"A sprawling maze of lakes, channels and forested islands across south-eastern Finland, with no single open expanse.","f":["The Saimaa ringed seal lives here and nowhere else; only a few hundred remain.","The Saimaa Canal links it to the Gulf of Finland at Vyborg.","It is the largest lake in Finland."],"q":{"q":"Which rare animal lives only in Lake Saimaa?","a":"A ringed seal","x":["A freshwater dolphin","A giant salamander"]}},"Q375029":{"t":"A man-made “sea” over a drowned town","s":"Locals call it the Rybinsk Sea, and from the air it earns the name: a broad sheet of grey-blue water spreading north from the Volga over flat forest and fields. Created in the 1940s by damming the Volga and Sheksna, it was then the largest man-made lake in the world. Beneath it lies the old town of Mologa.","w":"A wide, shallow, many-armed reservoir north of Rybinsk, filling the flooded valleys of the Volga, Sheksna and Mologa.","f":["Its average depth is only about 5–6 m.","Rafts of floating peat, sometimes carrying bushes and small trees, drift across it.","The Darwin Nature Reserve on its shore was set up to study how the flooding changed nature."],"q":{"q":"Which town was flooded to create the Rybinsk Reservoir?","a":"Mologa","x":["Kostroma","Vologda"]}},"Q21890573":{"t":"Australia's lowest point, a salt pan that rarely fills","s":"Most of the time Kati Thanda–Lake Eyre is a vast, glaring-white salt pan about 15 m below sea level — the lowest point in Australia. Only after big rains far to the north does water spread across it, sometimes tinted pink by salt-loving algae. Then pelicans fly in from the coast, hundreds of kilometres away, to breed.","w":"A huge white salt pan in the South Australian outback, sometimes streaked with shallow blue or pink water.","f":["Its catchment covers about a sixth of Australia.","In 1964 Donald Campbell set a land speed record of about 649 km/h on its salt.","It has filled completely only a few times since Europeans first saw it."],"q":{"q":"How far below sea level is the bed of Kati Thanda–Lake Eyre?","a":"About 15 m","x":["About 150 m","About 400 m"]}},"Q178741":{"t":"A lake cut in two: pink on one side, blue on the other","s":"Look for a dead-straight line across the water: a railway causeway completed in 1959 splits Great Salt Lake in two. North of it the brine is so salty that microbes turn it pink or rusty red; south of it the water stays blue-green. The lake is what remains of Lake Bonneville, an ice-age lake that once covered much of western Utah.","w":"A large, shallow lake west of Salt Lake City, crossed by a straight causeway: pinkish-red water north, blue-green south.","f":["It is the largest saltwater lake in the Western Hemisphere.","Antelope Island, its largest island, is home to a free-roaming bison herd.","Tiny brine shrimp thrive in it, and their eggs are harvested as fish-farm feed."],"q":{"q":"What turns the north arm of Great Salt Lake pink?","a":"Salt-loving microbes","x":["Iron-rich mud","Reflected desert sand"]}},"Q276776":{"t":"A vanishing sea, turned into a new desert","s":"What you see is the ghost of a sea. In 1960 the Aral was the world's fourth-largest lake, but its rivers were diverted to irrigate cotton, and it shrank. The southern part split into remnants: a deep western basin and a pale, shifting eastern one that dried out almost entirely in 2014. Its old seabed is now a new desert, the Aralkum.","w":"A narrow strip of dark water in the west and pale salt flats to the east, ringed by old shorelines in the desert.","f":["The old fishing port of Moynaq now lies more than 100 km from the water, with rusting ships in the sand.","The former Vozrozhdeniya Island is now joined to the mainland.","Salt and dust blown off the dry seabed can travel hundreds of kilometres."],"q":{"q":"Why did the Aral Sea shrink?","a":"Its rivers were diverted for irrigation","x":["An earthquake drained it","A canal emptied it into the Caspian"]}},"Q876280":{"t":"The “little” lagoon that is 180 km long","s":"Its name means “little” in Tupi-Guarani, yet Lagoa Mirim stretches about 180 km along the coastal plain, shared by Brazil and Uruguay. Only a strip of sand dunes and marshes separates it from the Atlantic. From the air, look for its brownish, silty water beside the ocean's blue, with green rice paddies spread along its western shore.","w":"A long lagoon parallel to the Atlantic coast near the Brazil–Uruguay border, separated from the sea by a sandy strip.","f":["The São Gonçalo Channel links it to the even larger Lagoa dos Patos to the north-east.","The Taim wetlands, a reserve rich in waterbirds, lie between the lagoon and the sea."],"q":{"q":"What does “Mirim” mean in Tupi-Guarani?","a":"Small","x":["Deep","Salty"]}},"Q42191":{"t":"The “warm lake” of the Tian Shan that never freezes","s":"Issyk-Kul is a long sapphire oval held between two snowy ranges of the Tian Shan, about 1,600 m above sea level. Winters in these mountains are harsh, yet the lake never freezes — its name means “warm lake” in Kyrgyz. It is deep, slightly salty and strikingly clear, and for most of the year its blue is framed by white peaks.","w":"A long east–west oval of deep blue between two parallel snow-capped ranges in north-eastern Kyrgyzstan.","f":["It reaches about 668 m deep.","No river flows out of it, which is why its water is slightly salty.","It is one of the largest mountain lakes in the world."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Issyk-Kul mean?","a":"Warm lake","x":["Blue lake","Salt lake"]}},"Q3358037":{"t":"The part of the Aral that is coming back","s":"Unlike its southern neighbour, the North Aral Sea is a rare story of recovery. In 2005 the Kokaral Dam sealed it off from the shrinking south, so the Syr Darya's water now stays here. The level rose, the water grew less salty, and fish returned. From above it's a lobed blue lake set in pale desert, still well short of its old shores.","w":"A lobed blue lake in the desert of south-western Kazakhstan; a dam at its southern tip, the Syr Darya entering from the east.","f":["The Kokaral Dam, about 13 km long, holds its water back.","The water has crept back towards Aralsk, a port that had been left far inland.","Commercial fishing has returned to its waters."],"q":{"q":"What helped the North Aral Sea to recover?","a":"A dam","x":["Pumping from the Caspian","A desalination plant"]}},"Q19270":{"t":"A deep tropical basin in the Coral Triangle","s":"Ringed by Mindanao, Sulawesi and Borneo, this almost enclosed basin drops to more than 6,000 m. Its open water is a dark, velvety blue, but the edges shine turquoise wherever reefs fringe the islands. The Celebes Sea is part of the Coral Triangle, where more kinds of coral and reef fish live than anywhere else on Earth.","w":"A deep-blue sea between Mindanao, Sulawesi's long northern arm and north-east Borneo; volcanic islands along its eastern edge.","f":["“Celebes” is the old European name for the island of Sulawesi.","The Sulu Archipelago, a chain of small islands, separates it from the Sulu Sea."],"q":{"q":"“Celebes” is the old name of which island?","a":"Sulawesi","x":["Borneo","Mindanao"]}},"Q160194":{"t":"Coral atolls in the heart of the Philippine seas","s":"The Sulu Sea is ringed by islands. Look for the long, thin island of Palawan — it forms the north-western wall, like a green spine laid on the blue. In the middle of the open water, far from any coast, lie the Tubbataha Reefs: two atolls whose turquoise lagoons and white sand cays stand out sharply against the deep blue.","w":"A deep-blue sea between Palawan, Mindanao and the central Philippine islands, with small turquoise atolls in the middle.","f":["The Tubbataha Reefs have been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1993.","Its deep basin plunges to more than 5,000 m.","The Sulu Archipelago, which gives the sea its name, runs along its south-eastern edge."],"q":{"q":"Which long island forms the Sulu Sea's north-western edge?","a":"Palawan","x":["Luzon","Samar"]}},"Q47545":{"t":"An Arctic sea kept open by warm Atlantic water","s":"Although much of it lies north of the Arctic Circle, the Norwegian Sea stays almost free of ice all year: a branch of the Gulf Stream's warm water flows through it towards the Arctic. On the Norwegian side, look for the jagged peaks of the Lofoten Islands rising straight from the water like a row of shark's teeth.","w":"Open grey-blue sea off Norway's coast; to the east, the saw-toothed Lofoten chain and a coastline shredded into fjords.","f":["Off the tip of Lofoten swirls the Moskstraumen, the maelstrom that inspired Edgar Allan Poe and Jules Verne.","The Røst Reef off Lofoten is the largest cold-water coral reef yet discovered.","Its deep basins go down more than 3,000 m."],"q":{"q":"Which famous whirlpool swirls off the Lofoten Islands?","a":"The Moskstraumen","x":["The Corryvreckan","Charybdis"]}},"Q132868":{"t":"The Arctic's exit door for drifting sea ice","s":"Most of the sea ice that leaves the Arctic Ocean does so through here. Floes squeeze out through Fram Strait, between Greenland and Svalbard, then ride the East Greenland Current south along the coast in a long white band. East of that band the sea is dark and open. Far to the south rises Jan Mayen, crowned by the snowy volcano Beerenberg.","w":"Between Greenland and Svalbard: a white band of drifting pack ice along Greenland's coast, with open dark water to the east.","f":["Beerenberg on Jan Mayen, 2,277 m high, is the northernmost active volcano on land.","Fram Strait, at its northern end, is the main gateway through which Arctic sea ice drifts out.","In winter its surface water sinks deep, helping to drive the Atlantic's circulation."],"q":{"q":"Through which strait does most ice leave the Arctic Ocean?","a":"Fram Strait","x":["Bering Strait","Davis Strait"]}},"Q171510":{"t":"Deep blue sea of the original Spice Islands","s":"The Banda Sea is ringed by a great curving arc of volcanic islands, and in places its floor drops to more than 7,000 m. Scattered in it are the tiny Banda Islands — for centuries the only place on Earth where nutmeg grew, a spice so valuable that European powers fought over these specks of land.","w":"Deep indigo sea in eastern Indonesia, rimmed by volcanic islands; look for tiny green specks and cones in the open water.","f":["Under a 1667 treaty, England gave up its claim to Run, a tiny Banda nutmeg island, and kept Manhattan.","The Weber Deep, in the east of the sea, is more than 7 km deep."],"q":{"q":"Which spice once grew only on the Banda Islands?","a":"Nutmeg","x":["Cinnamon","Black pepper"]}},"Q41573":{"t":"A stormy bay between France's straight coast and Spain","s":"Look east across the Bay of Biscay and you may see one of Europe's straightest coastlines: over 200 km of almost unbroken sand along France's Landes coast, with the Dune du Pilat piled up near its northern end. The southern shore is the opposite — Spain's green, mountainous north coast. The bay is famous for sudden storms and big Atlantic swell.","w":"A wide bay framed by France's long, straight sandy coast to the east and Spain's mountainous shore to the south.","f":["The Dune du Pilat, about 100 m high, is the tallest sand dune in Europe.","Beyond the continental shelf its floor drops to more than 4,000 m.","Dolphins and whales feed here and are often seen from the ferries that cross it."],"q":{"q":"What is the tallest sand dune in Europe, on the Bay of Biscay?","a":"The Dune du Pilat","x":["The Råbjerg Mile","The Łeba dunes"]}},"Q165100":{"t":"The strait between Africa and Madagascar","s":"This broad channel separates Madagascar from mainland Africa by at least 400 km of deep blue water. It opened more than 100 million years ago, as Madagascar drifted away from Africa. From above, look for the Zambezi's brown plume on the African side, and at the northern end the volcanic Comoros.","w":"Wide blue water between Mozambique's low coast and Madagascar's western shore; the Comoros islands mark its northern end.","f":["Coelacanths, fish once thought extinct for 66 million years, live in deep water off the Comoros.","The channel is about 1,600 km long.","Humpback whales come here to breed during the southern winter."],"q":{"q":"Which “living fossil” fish lives off the Comoros?","a":"The coelacanth","x":["The lungfish","The sturgeon"]}},"Q41430":{"t":"Where the equator meets the prime meridian","s":"Somewhere in this warm, hazy gulf lies 0° latitude, 0° longitude — the spot where the equator crosses the prime meridian, marked only by a weather buoy. To the north the Niger spreads into a vast delta of mangroves and creeks, and at night its oil and gas flares glow like embers. Offshore, volcanic islands rise from the sea.","w":"A broad, warm gulf off West Africa; the Niger Delta's green maze in the north, and volcanic islands such as Bioko offshore.","f":["Bioko, São Tomé and Príncipe are volcanoes of the Cameroon Line, which runs from the mainland out to sea.","The Niger Delta is the largest river delta in Africa.","European sailors once named stretches of its shore the Gold Coast and the Ivory Coast."],"q":{"q":"Which two lines cross in the Gulf of Guinea?","a":"Equator and prime meridian","x":["Tropic of Cancer and prime meridian","Equator and 180th meridian"]}},"Q545":{"t":"A young, half-fresh sea born after the ice age","s":"The Baltic is barely a sea at all: fed by more than 200 rivers and linked to the ocean only through the narrow Danish straits, its water is brackish, and in the far north almost fresh. It formed after the last ice age, and for a time it was a lake. Look for Gotland and Öland, long limestone islands off Sweden.","w":"A large grey-blue inland sea in northern Europe; the long islands of Gotland and Öland lie off Sweden's south-east coast.","f":["Most of the world's amber is found along its south-eastern shores.","In cold winters its northern gulfs freeze over completely.","Its average depth is only about 55 m."],"q":{"q":"What washes up on the Baltic's south-eastern shores?","a":"Amber","x":["Jade","Coral"]}},"Q45823":{"t":"An Arctic sea kept half open by the Atlantic","s":"The Barents Sea is a sea of two halves. Warm Atlantic water keeps its south-western part open all winter — the port of Murmansk never freezes — while the north is locked in ice for much of the year. In August, plankton blooms can marble the water with milky turquoise, a sight satellites often capture.","w":"Open dark water in the south-west, pack ice in the north; the long, curved island of Novaya Zemlya walls it off in the east.","f":["It is named after Willem Barentsz, who wintered on Novaya Zemlya in 1596–97.","Its waters hold one of the world's largest stocks of cod.","Svalbard, Franz Josef Land and Novaya Zemlya frame it to the west, north and east."],"q":{"q":"Why does Murmansk's port stay ice-free in winter?","a":"Warm Atlantic water","x":["Hot springs under the bay","Salt from nearby mines"]}},"Q1693":{"t":"A shallow sea that was once dry land","s":"Much of the southern North Sea was dry land until about 8,000 years ago: hunters roamed a plain now called Doggerland, which joined Britain to the continent. Today the sea is shallow and busy. On clear days you may make out the regular grids of offshore wind farms and, along the Dutch, German and Danish coasts, the grey-brown tidal flats of the Wadden Sea.","w":"Grey-green shallow sea between Britain, Norway and the continent; tidal flats and barrier islands along the southern shore.","f":["The Wadden Sea is the largest unbroken system of tidal flats in the world.","Its average depth is only about 95 m.","Trawlers still sometimes haul up mammoth bones from the Dogger Bank."],"q":{"q":"What is the drowned land under the southern North Sea called?","a":"Doggerland","x":["Atlantis","Lyonesse"]}},"Q41735":{"t":"Between Britain and Ireland, with Man in the middle","s":"Right in the middle of the Irish Sea sits the Isle of Man, green and hilly, with its own ancient parliament. On clear days you can pick out Anglesey and the mountains of Snowdonia to the east, Dublin Bay to the west, and wide bays where the ebbing tide uncovers kilometres of sand.","w":"A grey-green sea between Ireland and Britain; the Isle of Man near its centre, Anglesey off the Welsh coast.","f":["The Isle of Man's parliament, Tynwald, traces its origins back more than a thousand years.","Morecambe Bay, on its English side, holds one of Britain's largest expanses of tidal sand and mud.","An old saying claims that from Snaefell, the Isle of Man's 621 m summit, you can see six kingdoms."],"q":{"q":"Which island sits near the middle of the Irish Sea?","a":"The Isle of Man","x":["The Isle of Wight","Arran"]}},"Q49364":{"t":"A shallow tropical sea over a drowned plain","s":"The Java Sea is remarkably shallow — on average less than 50 m deep — because it floods a former plain. In the ice ages, when sea levels were lower, you could have walked from Java to Borneo. Look for Jakarta's sprawl on the southern shore and, just offshore, the scatter of coral islets called the Thousand Islands.","w":"A warm, shallow, often greenish sea between Java and Borneo, busy with ships; Jakarta spreads along its southern shore.","f":["The Thousand Islands off Jakarta actually number a little over a hundred.","Its seabed is part of the Sunda Shelf, one of the largest continental shelves on Earth.","Karimunjawa, a group of coral islands in its middle, is a national park."],"q":{"q":"In the ice ages, you could walk from Java to which island?","a":"Borneo","x":["Sulawesi","New Guinea"]}},"Q47632":{"t":"Limestone towers, island chains and a lone volcano","s":"The Andaman Sea is framed by islands: the long Andaman and Nicobar chain in the west and, off Myanmar, the hundreds of forested isles of the Mergui Archipelago. Near Phuket, in Phang Nga Bay, sheer limestone towers rise straight out of green water. The Irrawaddy's muddy delta spreads across the north, staining the sea brown.","w":"Warm blue sea west of Thailand and Myanmar; island chains on both sides and brown river water in the north.","f":["Barren Island, in its western part, is the only confirmed active volcano in South Asia.","The Mergui Archipelago off Myanmar counts some 800 islands."],"q":{"q":"Which river's delta spreads into the northern Andaman Sea?","a":"The Irrawaddy","x":["The Mekong","The Chao Phraya"]}},"Q37960":{"t":"A sea tinted by the silt of China's rivers","s":"The name is no exaggeration: near the Chinese coast, silt from great rivers turns the water a murky yellow-brown that fades to green offshore. The Yellow Sea is shallow — about 44 m on average — and on the Korean side the tides are huge, retreating twice a day to bare kilometres of glistening mudflats.","w":"A shallow sea between China and Korea: yellow-brown water near the Chinese coast, broad grey mudflats along Korea's west.","f":["At Incheon the tide can rise and fall by more than 8 m.","Its tidal flats are a vital stopover for millions of migrating shorebirds.","Near the island of Jindo, very low tides briefly expose a path of seabed almost 3 km long."],"q":{"q":"What gives the Yellow Sea its colour?","a":"River silt","x":["Algae blooms","Sulphur springs"]}},"Q45341":{"t":"Where the Yangtze pours into the Pacific","s":"At the mouth of the Yangtze, beside Shanghai, the East China Sea turns the colour of milky tea: Asia's longest river pours out a vast plume of silt. South of the city, look for the thin line of the Hangzhou Bay Bridge running some 36 km across the water. Farther east the sea deepens and clears towards the Ryukyu Islands.","w":"Brown water fanning out from the Yangtze mouth near Shanghai, turning blue-green offshore towards the Ryukyu chain.","f":["The warm Kuroshio current runs north-east along its eastern edge.","Its wide continental shelf is mostly less than 200 m deep.","The Ryukyu Islands, strung out between Kyushu and Taiwan, form its eastern boundary."],"q":{"q":"Which great river empties into the East China Sea?","a":"The Yangtze","x":["The Yellow River","The Pearl River"]}},"Q159252":{"t":"The Arctic sea where the last mammoths lived","s":"North of the Bering Strait, the Chukchi Sea lies between Chukotka and Alaska, and the International Date Line runs straight through it — cross it and the calendar jumps a day. The sea is frozen for most of the year. On its western edge lies Wrangel Island, where woolly mammoths survived until roughly 4,000 years ago, long after they vanished elsewhere.","w":"Ice or dark open water north of the Bering Strait, between the hills of Chukotka and Alaska's flat north-west coast.","f":["Wrangel Island is a UNESCO site and a major denning ground for polar bears.","In autumn, thousands of Pacific walruses haul out on its beaches."],"q":{"q":"Until roughly when did mammoths survive on Wrangel Island?","a":"About 4,000 years ago","x":["About 40,000 years ago","About 400 years ago"]}},"Q61304":{"t":"The oil-rich southern pocket of the Gulf of Mexico","s":"Curving from Veracruz round to the Yucatán Peninsula, the Bay of Campeche is the Gulf of Mexico's southern pocket. Under its waters lies Cantarell, once one of the largest oil fields on Earth, and at night platform lights scatter across the sea. Along the southern shore, rivers spread into lagoons and mangrove-lined wetlands.","w":"The southern bight of the Gulf of Mexico, from Veracruz east to the Yucatán; platform lights offshore at night.","f":["Laguna de Términos, on its southern shore, is Mexico's largest coastal lagoon.","Veracruz, on its western shore, was founded by Hernán Cortés in 1519.","The Grijalva and Usumacinta rivers reach it through a shared delta of swamps and channels."],"q":{"q":"Who founded Veracruz on the Bay of Campeche?","a":"Hernán Cortés","x":["Francisco Pizarro","Christopher Columbus"]}},"Q128880":{"t":"A shallow sea over the land bridge to New Guinea","s":"Between northern Australia and New Guinea spreads the Arafura Sea — broad, warm and surprisingly shallow, mostly less than 80 m deep. In the ice ages it was dry land, and people and animals could walk between the two. From the air, look for green-brown water near the muddy coasts and the low, forested Aru Islands in the north.","w":"Wide, shallow, often greenish sea between Australia's Top End and New Guinea, with the low Aru Islands to the north.","f":["The Gulf of Carpentaria opens off its southern side.","Torres Strait, a maze of islands and reefs, links it to the Coral Sea."],"q":{"q":"What did the Arafura Sea floor connect during the ice ages?","a":"Australia and New Guinea","x":["Australia and Timor","New Guinea and Borneo"]}},"Q131418":{"t":"A sea named after the “eastern” island","s":"The Timor Sea takes its name from Timor, and Timor's name comes from the Malay word for “east”. Most of it is a broad, shallow shelf off northern Australia, but close to Timor the floor plunges into a trough more than 3,000 m deep. On the Australian side, big tides wash in and out among rust-red cliffs and mangrove creeks.","w":"Open blue-green sea between the mountainous island of Timor and Australia's red Kimberley and Top End coasts.","f":["The city of Darwin lies at its eastern edge.","Joseph Bonaparte Gulf, on its Australian side, is named after Napoleon's elder brother."],"q":{"q":"What does the Malay word behind the name “Timor” mean?","a":"East","x":["Island","Mountain"]}},"Q131217":{"t":"A warm, shallow gulf ringed by tropical islands","s":"The Gulf of Thailand is warm and shallow — about 45 m deep on average — and its water often looks jade-green rather than blue. Look for the islands of Ko Samui and Ko Pha Ngan off the western shore, and the large island of Phu Quoc near the eastern one. At night, fishing boats' lamps can scatter the dark water with light.","w":"A broad, often greenish gulf south of Bangkok, with the Malay Peninsula to the west and scattered islands off both shores.","f":["The Chao Phraya, the river of Bangkok, empties into its northern tip.","Its old name, the Gulf of Siam, is still used in some languages, including Russian.","Its seabed was dry land during the last ice age."],"q":{"q":"Which river of Bangkok flows into the Gulf of Thailand?","a":"The Chao Phraya","x":["The Mekong","The Irrawaddy"]}},"Q7343":{"t":"Turquoise lace stitched along Australia's coast","s":"Look for patches of turquoise and pale lace scattered over deep navy water, far off the Queensland coast. Each is a reef built by living coral, and together they form the largest coral reef system on Earth, running for about 2,300 km. It is so big that astronauts can pick it out from orbit.","w":"Turquoise and cream patches on dark blue sea, well offshore, lined up parallel to the Queensland coast.","f":["It is made up of roughly 3,000 separate reefs and about 900 islands.","UNESCO listed the reef as a World Heritage site in 1981.","Once a year, a few nights after a full moon in October or November, its corals release their eggs almost all at once."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how long is the Great Barrier Reef?","a":"About 2,300 km","x":["About 800 km","About 5,000 km"]}},"Q118841":{"t":"A mile-deep gash with Earth's history on its walls","s":"Even from 11 km up the Grand Canyon looks like a wound in the plateau: a jagged chasm of red, orange and cream layers, with the Colorado River a thin green-brown thread at the bottom. It is about 446 km long and up to 29 km wide. In places the river runs some 1,800 metres below the rim.","w":"A branching maze of red and cream cliffs cutting the flat plateau, the river hidden deep inside.","f":["The oldest rocks at the bottom of the canyon are nearly two billion years old.","The North Rim is about 300 m higher than the South Rim, gets far more snow and closes to visitors in winter.","The Grand Canyon became a US national park in 1919."],"q":{"q":"Which river carved the Grand Canyon?","a":"The Colorado","x":["The Rio Grande","The Snake"]}},"Q43278":{"t":"A waterfall that announces itself with a cloud","s":"Before you see the falls you may see their cloud: a white plume of spray rising above the Zambezi, visible from dozens of kilometres away when the river is high. Below it the water drops into a narrow chasm about 1.7 km long, then zigzags through a chain of gorges. Locally it is called Mosi-oa-Tunya, 'the smoke that thunders'.","w":"A white mist above the Zambezi, then a sharp zigzag of gorges below it, on the Zambia–Zimbabwe border.","f":["About 1,700 m wide and over 100 m high, it is one of the largest sheets of falling water in the world.","David Livingstone reached the falls in 1855 and named them after Queen Victoria.","The zigzag gorges downstream are former lips of the falls, left behind as the water cut its way back upstream."],"q":{"q":"What does the local name Mosi-oa-Tunya mean?","a":"The smoke that thunders","x":["The river that sings","The great white wall"]}},"Q34221":{"t":"Where Lake Erie spills over a cliff towards Ontario","s":"From the air, look for a white smudge of spray between Lake Erie and Lake Ontario: that is the Horseshoe Falls, curved just like its name. The Niagara River carries the outflow of four Great Lakes over the edge of the Niagara Escarpment here. The falls formed roughly 12,000 years ago, as the ice sheets melted back.","w":"A short river linking Lake Erie and Lake Ontario; the falls are a white patch where it bends.","f":["There are three falls: the big Horseshoe Falls on the Canadian side, and the American and Bridal Veil Falls next door.","The falls are about 50 m high; creeping upstream, they have cut a gorge some 11 km long.","At night much of the river is diverted to hydroelectric plants, so less water goes over the falls."],"q":{"q":"Which two lakes does the Niagara River connect?","a":"Erie and Ontario","x":["Huron and Erie","Superior and Michigan"]}},"Q36332":{"t":"Some 275 falls strung along one jungle cliff","s":"Iguaçu is not one waterfall but a whole horseshoe of them: around 275 separate cascades spread along nearly 3 km of cliff, on the border of Brazil and Argentina. From above, look for a brown river breaking into white foam inside green forest. The deepest point, the U-shaped Devil's Throat, swallows about half of the river's flow.","w":"White foam in green forest, just east of where the Iguaçu River meets the wide Paraná.","f":["The river's name comes from the Guarani words for 'big water'.","Both the Argentine and the Brazilian national parks around the falls are UNESCO World Heritage sites.","Downstream the Iguaçu joins the Paraná at a point where Brazil, Argentina and Paraguay meet."],"q":{"q":"Which two countries share Iguaçu Falls?","a":"Brazil and Argentina","x":["Brazil and Paraguay","Argentina and Uruguay"]}},"Q3783":{"t":"A café-au-lait river in an ocean of green","s":"Below you the Amazon, here still called the Solimões, is a broad café-au-lait band wandering through unbroken green, with side channels, islands and lakes along both banks. It carries about a fifth of all the river water that reaches the world's oceans. Not a single bridge crosses its main stream.","w":"A wide brown river with pale sandbars and dark lakes on either side, cutting through unbroken forest.","f":["Its average flow is around 200,000 cubic metres per second, more than the next several largest rivers combined.","At Manaus the dark Rio Negro joins it, and the two waters run side by side for kilometres before mixing.","In 1500 Vicente Yáñez Pinzón found fresh water at sea off its mouth and named the river the 'Sweet Sea'."],"q":{"q":"What is the Amazon called in Brazil above Manaus?","a":"Solimões","x":["Madeira","Tapajós"]}},"Q3392":{"t":"A green thread stitched through the Sahara","s":"Few sights from the air are as stark as this: a single blue-green line with a narrow fringe of fields and palms, and bare desert on both sides. Here in northern Sudan the Nile makes its Great Bend, flowing south-west for a few hundred kilometres before turning north again. Nubian kings built pyramids along this stretch.","w":"A thin dark line with a green fringe across pale desert, curving in a big S.","f":["At about 6,650 km, the Nile is one of the two longest rivers on Earth, alongside the Amazon.","Sudan has more pyramids than Egypt, around two hundred of them, smaller and steeper than Giza's.","Its water comes from two sources: the White Nile from the lakes of East Africa and the Blue Nile from Ethiopia."],"q":{"q":"Where do the White Nile and the Blue Nile meet?","a":"Khartoum","x":["Aswan","Juba"]}},"Q932289":{"t":"Africa's great green heart, cut by brown rivers","s":"For hundreds of kilometres there is almost nothing but forest: a dense green carpet, cut by brown rivers that loop and braid through it. This is the heart of the Congo basin, home to the second-largest tropical rainforest on Earth after the Amazon. By afternoon towering thunderclouds often build over it, fed by moisture the trees themselves breathe out.","w":"Unbroken dark green forest with meandering brown rivers; in the afternoon, clusters of tall white storm clouds.","f":["The Congo River crosses the equator twice as it curves round the basin.","Bonobos live only here, south of the Congo River.","In 2017 scientists mapped a huge peatland beneath its swamp forests, the largest tropical peatland known."],"q":{"q":"Which ape lives only south of the Congo River?","a":"Bonobo","x":["Chimpanzee","Orangutan"]}},"Q244451":{"t":"The greatest river basin on Earth, roofed in forest","s":"Look down and count the shades of green: canopy, swamp forest, pale patches of grassland, and rivers the colour of tea or milky coffee. This is the Amazon lowland, where forest still runs to the horizon in most directions. In places, straight roads with side tracks cut into it in a fishbone pattern, the mark of clearing for farms.","w":"Endless forest crossed by winding rivers; near roads, rectangular clearings arranged like a fishbone.","f":["The Amazon's drainage basin covers about 7 million km², the largest of any river on Earth.","Much of the rain here was first evaporated by the forest itself, recycled again and again as air moves west.","Dust blown across the Atlantic from the Sahara helps fertilise the forest's poor soils."],"q":{"q":"Where does much of the dust that fertilises the Amazon come from?","a":"The Sahara","x":["The Kalahari","The Atacama"]}},"Q797":{"t":"America's biggest state, and its emptiest","s":"Below you is the Alaskan interior: dark spruce forest, a glitter of lakes and the wide braided channels of the Yukon and its tributaries, with hardly a road in sight. Alaska is more than twice the size of Texas. On a clear day the white bulk of Denali, North America's highest peak at 6,190 m, may stand out far to the south.","w":"Dark forest, silvery lakes and braided rivers with almost no roads; snowy ranges on the southern horizon.","f":["The United States bought Alaska from Russia in 1867 for 7.2 million dollars.","Alaska has a longer coastline than all the other US states combined.","In Fairbanks, in the interior, midsummer days last almost 22 hours."],"q":{"q":"From which country did the US buy Alaska in 1867?","a":"Russia","x":["Britain","Denmark"]}},"Q5428":{"t":"Taiga from horizon to horizon, for hours on end","s":"You can fly for hours over Siberia and see little but taiga: dark larch and pine forest, winding rivers and, in winter, snow that turns the whole land white and blue. Here, on the Central Siberian Plateau, the Lena, the Lower Tunguska and their tributaries cut deep valleys. Siberia covers roughly three-quarters of Russia.","w":"Endless dark forest and looping rivers in deep valleys; in winter, white snow with grey larch woods showing through.","f":["Siberia covers about 13 million km², more than Canada or China.","Much of the taiga here is larch, which sheds its needles, so in winter the forest looks grey rather than green.","Lake Baikal, in southern Siberia, holds about a fifth of the world's unfrozen fresh surface water."],"q":{"q":"Which Siberian lake is the deepest on Earth?","a":"Baikal","x":["Ladoga","Taymyr"]}},"Q866405":{"t":"The bigger, older half of Antarctica's ice","s":"If you ever see this, it will be a white emptiness without edges: the East Antarctic ice sheet, more than 4 km thick in places and rising to over 4,000 m. Near here stands Russia's Vostok Station, where −89.2 °C was recorded in 1983, the lowest air temperature ever measured by a weather station.","w":"Flat white ice to every horizon, with no landmarks at all; blinding in the summer sun.","f":["About 4 km beneath Vostok Station lies Lake Vostok, a body of liquid water sealed under the ice.","The East Antarctic ice sheet holds enough water to raise world sea level by more than 50 metres.","This ice sheet has been building up for more than 30 million years."],"q":{"q":"What lies about 4 km beneath Vostok Station?","a":"A liquid lake","x":["An active volcano","A buried meteorite"]}},"Q530917":{"t":"An ice sheet resting on a floor below the sea","s":"West Antarctica looks like its eastern twin from above, white, silent and endless, but beneath the ice it is very different. Much of its bedrock lies far below sea level, so the ice sheet rests in a deep basin that the ocean could reach. Scientists watch its glaciers closely, because they are among the fastest-changing on Earth.","w":"An unbroken white plain; towards the coast, dark mountain ridges and crevassed glaciers break through the ice.","f":["Vinson Massif, Antarctica's highest peak at 4,892 m, rises in West Antarctica's Ellsworth Mountains.","If all its ice melted, world sea level would rise by several metres.","The Transantarctic Mountains separate it from East Antarctica."],"q":{"q":"What is Antarctica's highest peak?","a":"Vinson Massif","x":["Mount Erebus","Mount Kirkpatrick"]}},"Q76034":{"t":"Billions of years old, and speckled with lakes","s":"From the air the Canadian Shield looks as if someone had shattered a mirror over the land: thousands of lakes of every shape, bare pink-grey rock and dark patches of spruce or open tundra, all scraped and polished by ice-age glaciers. This is the ancient core of North America. Some of its rocks formed when Earth was barely half a billion years old.","w":"A jigsaw of countless lakes and bare rock with streaks of forest; in winter, white lakes framed by dark trees.","f":["The shield covers roughly half of Canada, wrapped around Hudson Bay like a horseshoe.","The Acasta Gneiss in the Northwest Territories, about 4 billion years old, is among the oldest known rocks on Earth.","Its rocks hold rich deposits of nickel, gold, copper and diamonds."],"q":{"q":"Which bay does the Canadian Shield wrap around?","a":"Hudson Bay","x":["Bay of Fundy","Gulf of St. Lawrence"]}},"Q147304":{"t":"Land built grain by grain by the Yellow River","s":"Below spreads one of the flattest and most crowded farmlands on Earth: a fine mosaic of fields, villages scattered like grains of rice, and often a grey-brown haze over it all. Most of the plain was laid down as silt by the Yellow River, which has shifted its course across it many times. In June, ripe winter wheat turns it gold.","w":"A vast flat patchwork of small fields and villages, often under haze; green in spring, gold in early summer.","f":["The Grand Canal, the longest artificial waterway in the world, crosses the plain between Beijing and Hangzhou.","In places the Yellow River flows higher than the fields around it, held between tall levees.","Around 1855 the Yellow River moved its mouth from south of the Shandong Peninsula to north of it."],"q":{"q":"Which river laid down most of the North China Plain?","a":"The Yellow River","x":["The Yangtze","The Pearl River"]}},"Q602925":{"t":"Grassland to every horizon, in the heart of Eurasia","s":"Here the land opens up completely: rolling grassland, green in May and straw-gold by midsummer, crossed by dirt tracks and dotted with salt lakes that flash white in the sun. The huge rectangular fields in the north date from the Soviet Virgin Lands campaign of the 1950s. In winter everything is white and scoured by wind.","w":"Flat to rolling tan grassland, straight dirt tracks, white salt pans and huge rectangular fields further north.","f":["In the Virgin Lands campaign, begun in 1954, millions of hectares of Kazakh steppe were ploughed for wheat.","The lakes of Korgalzhyn, west of Astana, hold one of the world's most northerly flamingo colonies.","Saiga antelope, with their swollen, trunk-like noses, still roam the Kazakh steppe."],"q":{"q":"Which antelope with a trunk-like nose roams the Kazakh Steppe?","a":"Saiga","x":["Oryx","Nilgai"]}},"Q560549":{"t":"Flat land from the North Sea all the way to the Urals","s":"You are over the north-eastern end of Europe's great lowland, which runs from the North Sea through Germany and Poland and across Russia to the Urals. Out here it is taiga and bog: dark forest, brown peatland glinting with pools, and rivers like the Pechora wandering in broad loops. In the west the same plain is a quilt of fields and towns.","w":"Dark taiga, brown bogs with glinting pools and looping rivers, stretching flat to the horizon.","f":["On average the plain lies less than 200 m above sea level.","Its gentle hills and chains of lakes in Germany and Poland are moraines left by Ice Age glaciers.","The Pechora, which drains its north-eastern part, flows into the Barents Sea."],"q":{"q":"Which mountains mark the plain's eastern end?","a":"The Urals","x":["The Carpathians","The Caucasus"]}},"Q12707":{"t":"Where the land is ruled into squares a mile wide","s":"Look for the grid: roads a mile apart, square fields in green, gold and brown, and here and there perfect circles where pivot sprinklers water the crops. The Great Plains roll from Canada to Texas, east of the Rockies. Once shortgrass prairie grazed by tens of millions of bison, they are now one of the great grain belts of the world.","w":"A ruler-straight grid of roads and fields; to the west, the crumpled badlands of the Little Missouri River.","f":["Much of the southern Plains is watered from the Ogallala Aquifer, one of the largest aquifers in the world.","The mile-square grid goes back to the Land Ordinance of 1785, which set out how US land was surveyed.","The Plains tilt gently east, losing well over a kilometre of height from the foot of the Rockies."],"q":{"q":"Why do many fields on the Great Plains look like perfect circles?","a":"Centre-pivot irrigation","x":["Ancient earthworks","Grazing patterns"]}},"Q200952":{"t":"A river that ties itself in loops","s":"Few rivers show their restlessness as plainly as the Ucayali. From above it is a brown ribbon coiling through the Peruvian rainforest, with a sandbar on every bend and abandoned loops lying beside it as crescent lakes. Its channel shifts year after year. Further north it meets the Marañón, and together they become the Amazon.","w":"Tight brown loops with pale sandbars and green crescent lakes beside them, in flat forest east of the Andes.","f":["The Amazon's most distant source, high in the Andes of southern Peru, drains towards the sea through the Ucayali.","Pucallpa, the main city on its banks, is linked to Lima by a road across the Andes.","Riverboats run from Pucallpa down to Iquitos, a city with no road link to the rest of Peru."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Ucayali meet to form the Amazon?","a":"Marañón","x":["Napo","Huallaga"]}},"Q46841":{"t":"A northern giant, braided around wooded islands","s":"Here, below Yakutsk, the Lena has already taken in the Aldan and the Vilyuy, and it spreads into a maze of channels and wooded islands, several kilometres from bank to bank. For about seven months a year it lies frozen, a white road through the taiga. In late May the ice breaks up with a roar and the spring flood covers the islands.","w":"A broad river split into channels around long wooded islands and sandbars; in winter, a white band through dark taiga.","f":["The Lena is more than 4,000 km long and flows entirely within Russia.","In winter lorries drive for hundreds of kilometres along the frozen river on an ice road.","Upstream of Yakutsk stand the Lena Pillars, rock towers along the bank listed by UNESCO in 2012."],"q":{"q":"Into which sea does the Lena flow?","a":"The Laptev Sea","x":["The Kara Sea","The East Siberian Sea"]}},"Q26076":{"t":"Myanmar's great river, braided with sandbanks","s":"Through the dry heart of Myanmar the Irrawaddy is a wide sheet of brown water braided around pale sandbanks that grow and vanish with the seasons. Near here it takes in its biggest tributary, the Chindwin. A little downstream, on the east bank, lies the plain of Bagan, where more than two thousand old temples and pagodas still stand.","w":"A broad river with pale sandbanks in a dry, brownish plain; a second large river joins it from the north-west.","f":["The Irrawaddy runs for more than 2,000 km, almost the whole length of Myanmar, to the Andaman Sea.","It gives its name to the Irrawaddy dolphin, a small, round-headed dolphin of rivers and coastal waters.","Its delta, laced with channels, is one of the great rice-growing regions of Asia."],"q":{"q":"Which old temple city lies on the Irrawaddy's east bank?","a":"Bagan","x":["Angkor","Ayutthaya"]}},"Q7821148":{"t":"The Yangtze in its youth, on the roof of the world","s":"Long before it becomes China's mightiest river, the Yangtze crosses the high Qinghai plateau as the Tongtian, 'the river that reaches to heaven'. From above it is a braided silver strand, spread in many channels over a broad gravel valley more than 4,000 m up. Around it lie brown-green pastures, snow-dusted hills and hardly any people.","w":"A silvery river split into many channels across a wide gravel valley, among bare rounded hills of a high plateau.","f":["In the classic novel 'Journey to the West', the monk Xuanzang crosses the Tongtian on the back of a giant turtle.","It lies in Sanjiangyuan, the 'Source of Three Rivers', where the Yangtze, the Yellow River and the Mekong all begin.","Below Yushu the Tongtian takes a new name: the Jinsha, the 'River of Golden Sand'."],"q":{"q":"The Tongtian is the upper course of which river?","a":"The Yangtze","x":["The Yellow River","The Mekong"]}},"Q1497":{"t":"A river of islands between tall wooded bluffs","s":"Here, on its upper course, the Mississippi is not one channel but a whole valley of water: islands, sloughs and backwaters between steep wooded bluffs on both sides. The bluffs stand tall because this corner of the Midwest, the Driftless Area, escaped the last ice sheets. Every few dozen kilometres a lock and dam holds the river in a chain of broad pools.","w":"A wide valley of braided water and islands between steep forested bluffs, running north–south through farmland.","f":["With the Missouri, the Mississippi drains about 40 percent of the contiguous United States.","Between Minneapolis and St. Louis, some 29 locks and dams turn the river into a navigable staircase.","Its source is Lake Itasca in Minnesota, where you can cross the young river on stepping stones."],"q":{"q":"In which state does the Mississippi begin?","a":"Minnesota","x":["Wisconsin","Iowa"]}},"Q1038418":{"t":"The 'River of Golden Sand' in its deep valleys","s":"Jinsha means 'golden sand', after the gold once panned from its gravels. This is the upper Yangtze, carving deep valleys through the mountains of Yunnan and Sichuan; from the air it is a green-brown thread at the bottom of steep, dry slopes. Today a chain of giant dams has turned long stretches of it into narrow, winding reservoirs.","w":"A winding river deep in steep, brownish mountain valleys, widening in places into long, snake-like reservoirs.","f":["Upstream, in Tiger Leaping Gorge, the mountainsides rise more than 3,000 m above the rushing water.","At the 'First Bend' near Shigu the river swings sharply from south to north-east.","The Baihetan Dam on the Jinsha is one of the most powerful hydroelectric plants in the world."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Jinsha mean?","a":"Golden sand","x":["Black water","Jade river"]}},"Q4814791":{"t":"A slow, pale river across the Sudanese plain","s":"The White Nile is in no hurry. On these flat Sudanese plains it has almost no slope left, so it spreads wide and lazy between low banks, its water pale with fine clay, hence the name. Irrigated fields and reed beds line the river; beyond them, dry savanna runs to the horizon.","w":"A broad, pale river with low banks and green irrigated strips, running north across a flat, dry plain.","f":["At Khartoum it meets the Blue Nile, and for a while their different colours run side by side.","Its steady flow comes mostly from Lake Victoria and other East African lakes, filtered through the Sudd swamps.","The Jebel Aulia Dam, south of Khartoum, was built across the White Nile in the 1930s."],"q":{"q":"What gives the White Nile its name?","a":"Pale clay in its water","x":["Snow-fed sources","White sandy banks"]}},"Q5413":{"t":"Asia's longest river, held back by a giant dam","s":"Around here the Yangtze no longer flows freely: this is the Three Gorges reservoir, a green-brown lake some 600 km long, winding between steep hills. Towns that stood on its banks were rebuilt higher up the slopes as the water rose. Downstream, the reservoir ends at the Three Gorges Dam, the largest power station in the world by capacity.","w":"A long, winding, lake-like river between steep green hills, with towns climbing its slopes.","f":["At about 6,300 km, the Yangtze is the longest river in Asia and the third longest in the world.","The reservoir behind the Three Gorges Dam reaches almost to Chongqing.","About a third of China's people live in the Yangtze basin."],"q":{"q":"Which dam holds back the Yangtze downstream of here?","a":"Three Gorges Dam","x":["Xiaolangdi Dam","Longtan Dam"]}},"Q2750140":{"t":"The river that feeds Lake Victoria, and so the Nile","s":"Look for a river winding through a wide valley of papyrus swamp, with lakes strung along it like beads. This is the Kagera, the largest river flowing into Lake Victoria, and its headwaters are often counted as the most distant source of the Nile. Along this stretch it forms Rwanda's eastern border, with Akagera National Park on its western bank.","w":"A winding river in a broad green swamp with a chain of lakes beside it, among rolling savanna hills.","f":["Its headwaters rise in Rwanda's Nyungwe forest and in the hills of Burundi.","Akagera National Park, named after the river, protects savanna, lakes and papyrus swamps along its banks.","The Kagera enters Lake Victoria in Tanzania, north of the town of Bukoba."],"q":{"q":"Which lake does the Kagera flow into?","a":"Lake Victoria","x":["Lake Tanganyika","Lake Kivu"]}},"Q5419":{"t":"The 'Big Muddy', tamed into smooth curves","s":"The Missouri earned its nickname, the Big Muddy, from the silt it once carried. Here, along the Kansas–Missouri line, it swings in wide, smooth curves between levees, a channel shaped by engineers for barges. Look beside it for the ghosts of older courses: crescent lakes and curving field boundaries in the flat floodplain.","w":"A brown river in smooth, regular bends across a flat floodplain of fields, with crescent lakes and old channel scars.","f":["At about 3,700 km, the Missouri is the longest river in the United States.","In 1804 Lewis and Clark set off up the Missouri on their expedition to the Pacific.","The Missouri joins the Mississippi just north of St. Louis."],"q":{"q":"What is the Missouri's nickname?","a":"The Big Muddy","x":["Old Man River","The Father of Waters"]}},"Q284780":{"t":"China's lowest, hottest hollow, full of vineyards","s":"Look for a dark green oasis at the foot of red, furrowed hills, beside a white salt flat. This is the Turpan Depression, where the ground sinks to about 154 m below sea level at Lake Aydingkol, the lowest point in China. Its summers are the hottest in the country, yet Turpan's vineyards thrive on meltwater from the Tian Shan.","w":"A green oasis and a white salt flat in a brown desert basin, south of the snowy Tian Shan.","f":["The Flaming Mountains, a bare red-sandstone ridge, run along its northern side and glow in the afternoon sun.","Karez, underground channels dug centuries ago, still carry mountain water to Turpan's fields.","Turpan is famous for grapes and raisins, dried in airy, open-walled brick houses."],"q":{"q":"About how far below sea level is the Turpan Depression's lowest point?","a":"About 150 m","x":["About 50 m","About 400 m"]}},"Q191333":{"t":"A gateway through the mountains, used for millennia","s":"West of Peshawar the green, crowded plain ends abruptly against bare brown ridges, and a narrow valley winds up into them: the Khyber Pass. For thousands of years traders, pilgrims and armies came this way between Central Asia and the Indian subcontinent. Its highest point is only about 1,070 m, but the slopes around it are steep and dry.","w":"Where the green Peshawar plain meets a wall of bare, brown, folded hills to the west.","f":["The Khyber is one of the main routes between Pakistan and Afghanistan, leading to the border crossing at Torkham.","A railway through the pass, with dozens of tunnels and bridges, was completed in the 1920s.","Gandhara, an ancient land of Buddhist art and learning, lay around Peshawar at the foot of the pass."],"q":{"q":"Which city lies at the eastern foot of the Khyber Pass?","a":"Peshawar","x":["Lahore","Quetta"]}},"Q118388":{"t":"North America's lowest, hottest, driest valley","s":"Look for a long white streak between dark mountains: the salt pan of Badwater Basin, at 86 m below sea level the lowest point in North America. Fans of gravel spill from every canyon onto the valley floor like poured sand. In 1913 a thermometer here read 56.7 °C, the highest air temperature ever officially recorded on Earth.","w":"A long white salt flat between steep, bare brown ranges, with fan-shaped gravel slopes below each canyon.","f":["Mount Whitney, the highest peak in the contiguous US at 4,421 m, stands less than 140 km away.","On Racetrack Playa, rocks slide across the mud when wind pushes thin sheets of ice, leaving long trails.","Death Valley is the largest national park in the contiguous United States."],"q":{"q":"How far below sea level is Badwater Basin?","a":"About 86 m","x":["About 28 m","About 154 m"]}},"Q534290":{"t":"Australia's food bowl, threaded by slow brown rivers","s":"From above, the Murray–Darling basin is a vast, flat plain in ochre and khaki, crossed by slow rivers traced in the dark green of river red gums. In the Riverina, near here, irrigation turns the plain into bright green squares of rice, cotton and orchards. The basin covers about a seventh of Australia.","w":"A flat ochre plain crossed by winding rivers lined with dark green trees; bright green irrigated fields near them.","f":["The Murray and the Darling together form Australia's longest river system, well over 3,000 km.","River red gums along its banks can live for several hundred years.","At dry Lake Mungo, in the west of the basin, people left traces of their lives more than 40,000 years ago."],"q":{"q":"Roughly what share of Australia does the Murray–Darling basin cover?","a":"About a seventh","x":["About a half","About a twentieth"]}},"Q191566":{"t":"Bog, taiga and gas flares on an endless flat","s":"Few places on Earth are as flat as this. Below you, rivers crawl across the West Siberian Plain in huge loops, and between them spread the bogs, a brown and olive patchwork of peat and countless small lakes. At night the gas flares and lights of the oil towns glow in the darkness.","w":"Flat olive-brown bogland spotted with small lakes, with looping rivers and dark ribbons of forest along them.","f":["The Vasyugan Swamp, in the south of the plain, is one of the largest bogs in the world.","Across the plain the Ob drops only a few centimetres per kilometre, so its floods spread wide and linger.","The plain holds some of Russia's biggest oil and gas fields, among them Samotlor near Nizhnevartovsk."],"q":{"q":"Which huge bog lies in the south of the West Siberian Plain?","a":"Vasyugan Swamp","x":["Pinsk Marshes","Meshchera Marshes"]}},"Q966943":{"t":"A land of ridges where no river reaches the sea","s":"Look down on Nevada and you see a rhythm: a long dark mountain range, a flat pale valley, another range, another valley, repeating to the horizon, all running north–south. This is the Great Basin, where no river finds its way to the ocean; water ends in salt lakes or simply evaporates. Many valley floors are dry lake beds, dazzling white.","w":"Parallel north–south mountain ridges separated by flat, pale valleys, some with white dry lake beds.","f":["Great Basin bristlecone pines, some almost 5,000 years old, are among the oldest living trees on Earth.","The Great Salt Lake in Utah is a remnant of a much larger Ice Age lake, Lake Bonneville.","Explorer John C. Frémont named the Great Basin in the 1840s, realising it had no outlet to the sea."],"q":{"q":"Where do the Great Basin's rivers end?","a":"In lakes and sinks inland","x":["In the Pacific Ocean","In the Gulf of California"]}},"Q656985":{"t":"A hidden sea of ancient water under the red outback","s":"Nothing on the surface gives it away: below you is the red and ochre outback, crossed by the braided dry channels of the Channel Country. But beneath it lies the Great Artesian Basin, one of the largest underground stores of fresh water on Earth. Some of its water fell as rain more than a million years ago.","w":"Rusty red plains laced with braided, mostly dry river channels that turn green after rare floods.","f":["The basin lies beneath about a fifth of Australia.","Water from some deep bores comes out hot, close to boiling point.","Where the water seeps out naturally, mound springs form small green oases in the desert."],"q":{"q":"How old is some of the Great Artesian Basin's water?","a":"Over a million years","x":["About 1,000 years","About 10,000 years"]}},"Q28809720":{"t":"Black-earth fields under a long white winter","s":"The Manchurian Plain is northern China's great granary: a flat expanse of huge fields laid out in long strips, with poplar windbreaks drawn across them like pencil lines. Its soil is a rich black earth, darker than almost anywhere else in China. From November to March it lies frozen and white, then turns green with maize and soybeans.","w":"Flat farmland in long strips divided by thin lines of trees; black in spring, green in summer, white in winter.","f":["It is one of the world's few great black-soil regions, along with the Ukrainian steppe and the US Corn Belt.","The Songhua drains the plain north to the Amur, while the Liao flows south to the Bohai Sea.","Harbin, on its northern edge, is famous for its winter festival of giant ice sculptures."],"q":{"q":"Which crop, with maize, dominates the Manchurian Plain?","a":"Soybeans","x":["Tea","Cotton"]}},"Q575516":{"t":"Two deserts, two rivers and a vanishing sea","s":"Two green threads cross a sea of sand here: the Amu Darya and the Syr Darya, winding through the Kyzylkum and Karakum deserts of the Turan lowland. Along them spread irrigated fields and oasis towns. Both rivers once filled the Aral Sea, which has shrunk dramatically since the 1960s as their water was drawn off for cotton.","w":"Ochre desert with long dune ridges, crossed by green ribbons of irrigated land along two big rivers.","f":["In Turkic languages Kyzylkum means 'red sand' and Karakum 'black sand'.","Khiva, whose walled inner town is a UNESCO site, stands in an oasis on the lower Amu Darya.","Much of the Aral Sea's exposed bed has become a new desert, the Aralkum."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Kyzylkum' mean?","a":"Red sand","x":["Black sand","White sand"]}},"Q23945221":{"t":"The shore of the Sahara, green for only a few months","s":"Sahel comes from the Arabic for 'shore': this is the coast of the Sahara, a belt of dry grass and thorny acacia running right across Africa from the Atlantic to the Red Sea. Its colours follow the rain. From July to September it turns green almost overnight; the rest of the year it fades to straw and dust, dotted with dark trees.","w":"Tan or green savanna dotted with dark trees, fading into bare sand to the north, crossed by dry riverbeds.","f":["The belt runs for roughly 5,000 km across Africa but is only a few hundred kilometres wide.","The Great Green Wall project aims to restore a band of land across the Sahel with trees and grasses.","Gum arabic, used in sweets and soft drinks, comes mostly from Sahel acacias, above all in Sudan."],"q":{"q":"What does the Arabic word 'sahel' mean?","a":"Shore","x":["Sand","Thirst"]}},"Q756832":{"t":"Flat pinewoods and farms between the hills and the sea","s":"Dark green pine forest, sandy fields in neat rectangles and slow rivers the colour of strong tea: this is the flat southern end of the Atlantic coastal plain. Once it was seabed, built of sand and clay laid down when the ocean reached further inland. Towards the coast it dissolves into salt marshes and a chain of barrier islands.","w":"Flat country of dark pinewoods and rectangular fields with winding tea-coloured rivers; marshes and islands near the coast.","f":["Richmond, Raleigh, Columbia and Augusta grew on the Fall Line, where rivers tumble off the hills onto the plain.","The Okefenokee Swamp, on the Georgia–Florida border, is one of North America's largest blackwater swamps.","Georgia grows more peanuts than any other US state, much of them on this plain."],"q":{"q":"Why are many rivers here the colour of tea?","a":"Tannins from swamp plants","x":["Red clay silt","Iron from springs"]}},"Q208347":{"t":"A hot, thorny forest-plain at the heart of South America","s":"Olive-green felt, flat to every horizon: from above, the dry thorn forest of the Gran Chaco looks almost uniform. Look closer and you see the wandering scars of rivers such as the Pilcomayo and the Bermejo, and huge rectangular clearings for cattle and soybeans, edged with strips of trees left standing. The Chaco spreads across Argentina, Paraguay and Bolivia.","w":"Flat olive-green scrub forest with winding pale river scars and big rectangular clearings with tree-lined edges.","f":["Its name is often traced to the Quechua 'chaku', a communal hunt or hunting ground.","It is the second-largest forest region in South America, after the Amazon.","The Chaco peccary was known only from fossils until living animals were identified here in the 1970s."],"q":{"q":"Which animal, once known only from fossils, was found alive in the Chaco?","a":"Chaco peccary","x":["Giant armadillo","Maned wolf"]}},"Q141501":{"t":"Plains that turn from dust to inland sea each year","s":"The Llanos swing between two faces. In the dry season, roughly December to April, this lowland along the Orinoco is golden grassland dotted with palms and crossed by dusty tracks. When the rains come, the rivers overflow and vast stretches become a shallow sheet of water shining in the sun. It is cowboy country, where llaneros still drive cattle across the plains.","w":"Flat grassland with lines of trees along winding rivers; in the rainy season, shining sheets of floodwater.","f":["The Llanos stretch across Venezuela and Colombia, between the Andes and the Orinoco.","They are home to capybaras, the world's largest rodents, and to Orinoco crocodiles.","Joropo, the fast harp-and-maracas music of the llaneros, is Venezuela's national dance."],"q":{"q":"What are the cowboys of the Llanos called?","a":"Llaneros","x":["Gauchos","Huasos"]}},"Q184382":{"t":"Flat as a table, and ruled into giant fields","s":"Pampa comes from a Quechua word for a plain, and few plains are flatter. From above you see a quilt of enormous rectangular fields, dead-straight roads, shallow lakes shining silver and dark clumps of trees marking each estancia. Once tall grassland ridden by gauchos, it is now one of the world's great farm belts for soy, wheat, maize and beef.","w":"An immense flat quilt of rectangular fields, straight roads and silvery shallow lakes, with dark tree clumps around farmsteads.","f":["Almost all the trees you see were planted: the natural pampas had grass but hardly any trees.","The deep, fertile soils of the Pampas are among the richest in the world.","Charles Darwin rode across the Pampas in the 1830s during the voyage of the Beagle."],"q":{"q":"What does the Quechua word 'pampa' mean?","a":"Plain","x":["Grass","Wind"]}},"Q192305":{"t":"A plain of Himalayan mud, home to hundreds of millions","s":"Here the Ganges, now heading for the Bay of Bengal as the Padma, swings in wide braided loops across a plain so flat that its rivers wander at will. Every field, village and pond below you sits on mud carried down from the Himalayas. The Indo-Gangetic Plain is among the most densely populated places on Earth, and at night it glitters with countless lights.","w":"A wide braided river with pale sandbars in a flat green patchwork of fields, ponds and villages.","f":["In places the river-laid sediments beneath the plain are more than a kilometre thick.","In winter a thick fog often settles over the plain, sometimes blanketing it from Pakistan to Bangladesh.","The plain runs for more than 2,500 km, from Pakistan across northern India to Bangladesh."],"q":{"q":"Which mountains supplied the mud that built this plain?","a":"The Himalayas","x":["The Western Ghats","The Vindhya Range"]}},"Q486324":{"t":"A green oval oasis walled in by snowy mountains","s":"Look for a green oval set among mountains: the Fergana Valley, about 300 km long, ringed by the Tian Shan to the north and the Alay range to the south. Rivers pouring down from the snow feed a dense patchwork of cotton fields, orchards, mulberry trees and towns. The Syr Darya is born here, where the Naryn and the Kara Darya meet.","w":"A green, densely farmed basin surrounded on almost all sides by high, often snow-capped mountains.","f":["The valley is shared by Uzbekistan, Kyrgyzstan and Tajikistan.","More than 2,000 years ago Chinese emperors sought the famous 'heavenly horses' of Fergana.","Margilan, in the valley, has been known for its silk weaving for centuries."],"q":{"q":"Which river is born in the Fergana Valley?","a":"Syr Darya","x":["Amu Darya","Zeravshan"]}},"Q51614":{"t":"A high plateau of wheat, salt and ancient towns","s":"The heart of Anatolia is a high, open plateau about a kilometre above the sea, where wheat fields turn from green in spring to gold in summer and bare ochre by autumn. Just to the south-west lies Lake Tuz, a vast salt lake that dries into a blinding white crust in summer. Further south rise the cones of old volcanoes such as Hasan and Erciyes.","w":"A wide ochre and golden plateau; look for the huge white patch of Lake Tuz and distant volcanic cones.","f":["The name comes from the Greek 'anatolē', meaning sunrise, and so the east.","Çatalhöyük, further south on the plateau, was a town of mud-brick houses more than 9,000 years ago.","Lake Tuz supplies a large share of Turkey's salt."],"q":{"q":"Which salt lake lies on the central Anatolian plateau?","a":"Lake Tuz","x":["Lake Iznik","Lake Urmia"]}},"Q1507":{"t":"Wind, steppe and sky at the end of the Americas","s":"Patagonia from the air is mostly emptiness: grey-brown steppe and flat-topped mesetas, cut by a few river valleys that run like green threads from the Andes to the Atlantic. To the west the snowy Andes rise above deep blue lakes. The wind blows hard and almost constantly from the west, bending the few trees there are.","w":"Vast grey-brown steppe with flat mesas and canyons; to the west, snowy Andes and long blue lakes.","f":["Welsh settlers reached the Chubut valley in 1865, and Welsh is still spoken in towns such as Gaiman.","Some of the largest known dinosaurs, including Argentinosaurus and Patagotitan, were found in Patagonia.","The name is usually linked to Magellan's crew, who in 1520 called the local people 'Patagones'."],"q":{"q":"Which European language is still spoken in Patagonia's Chubut valley?","a":"Welsh","x":["Irish","Basque"]}},"Q605779":{"t":"A vast slope of ice falling to the Southern Ocean","s":"From the coast of Wilkes Land the ice rises in a long, gentle slope towards the high plateau of East Antarctica, hundreds of kilometres inland. Near the sea, glaciers speed up and crack into crevasses, and their surface looks like crumpled paper. Beneath the ice lies the deep Aurora Subglacial Basin, much of it below sea level.","w":"A seamless white slope; near the coast, crevassed glaciers, ice cliffs and sea ice scattered on dark water.","f":["It is named after Charles Wilkes, who charted this coast in 1840 on a US Navy expedition.","The Totten Glacier, on its coast, drains a large part of the ice sheet and is closely watched by scientists.","Australia's Casey Station stands on its coast, in the Windmill Islands."],"q":{"q":"Who is Wilkes Land named after?","a":"An American naval officer","x":["A British whaling captain","A Norwegian king"]}},"Q1340442":{"t":"Ice over some of the oldest rocks on Earth","s":"Ice runs from horizon to horizon here, broken near the coast by dark ridges and nunataks, rocky peaks poking through the ice sheet like islands in a white sea. Some of those rocks are among the oldest on Earth. The coast was first sighted in 1831 by John Biscoe, a sealing and whaling captain sailing out of London.","w":"A white ice sheet with dark rocky peaks near the coast poking through it like islands.","f":["It takes its name from Enderby Brothers, the London whaling firm that sent Biscoe south.","Rocks of its Napier Complex are among the oldest in Antarctica, formed some 3.8 billion years ago.","Molodyozhnaya station, on its coast, was once the main Soviet base in Antarctica."],"q":{"q":"What kind of business gave Enderby Land its name?","a":"A whaling company","x":["A tea merchant","A coal company"]}},"Q189999":{"t":"Mountains drowning in ice, named after a queen","s":"Near the coast of Queen Maud Land, jagged mountain chains break through the ice sheet, their dark peaks and granite spires half-buried in white. Further south, where you are now, the ice rises into the high polar plateau and the mountains vanish beneath it. The land's name honours Queen Maud of Norway, wife of King Haakon VII.","w":"A white plateau inland; towards the coast, dark mountain ranges and needle-like peaks sticking out of the ice.","f":["It stretches for more than 2,000 km along the Antarctic coast.","Several countries run research stations here, among them Norway's Troll, Germany's Neumayer and India's Maitri.","Queen Maud was born a British princess, a daughter of King Edward VII."],"q":{"q":"Queen Maud, who gave this land its name, was queen of which country?","a":"Norway","x":["Denmark","Sweden"]}},"Q745767":{"t":"Volcanoes under ice, named after an explorer's wife","s":"Here and there the ice of Marie Byrd Land is pierced by the snowy cones of volcanoes, some rising more than 4 km high. Mount Sidley, about 4,285 m, is the highest volcano in Antarctica. Around them lie hundreds of kilometres of flat white ice. The American aviator Richard Byrd named this remote corner of West Antarctica after his wife, Marie, in 1929.","w":"A white expanse with isolated, broad volcanic cones rising above the ice towards the coast.","f":["No country has ever made a territorial claim to Marie Byrd Land.","In 1929 Byrd was among the first to fly over the South Pole.","Its coast faces the Amundsen and Ross seas, often choked with sea ice."],"q":{"q":"Who was Marie Byrd Land named after?","a":"An explorer's wife","x":["A British queen","A ship's captain"]}},"Q80299":{"t":"The world's highest waterfall, off a table mountain","s":"Angel Falls drops 979 m from the edge of Auyán-tepui, a flat-topped sandstone mountain rising sheer out of the Venezuelan rainforest. It is the highest uninterrupted waterfall in the world; in the dry season much of the water turns to mist before it reaches the bottom. Look for dark tabletop mountains with vertical walls, often wrapped in cloud.","w":"Dark, flat-topped mountains with sheer cliff walls rising from green forest, often half-hidden in cloud.","f":["It is named after Jimmie Angel, an American pilot who flew over it in 1933 and landed on the tepui in 1937.","In the local Pemón language it is called Kerepakupai Merú.","The falls lie in Canaima National Park, a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1994."],"q":{"q":"How high is Angel Falls?","a":"About 979 m","x":["About 450 m","About 1,500 m"]}},"Q599529":{"t":"The steppe where the horse was probably first tamed","s":"From the Danube to the Urals, the Pontic-Caspian steppe was once a sea of feather grass. Today, near the Volga, it is mostly ploughed into vast fields of black earth, crossed by shelter belts of trees and cut by branching ravines. In early summer the wheat is green; by August the whole land turns gold and dusty.","w":"Huge fields in green, gold or black, divided by thin lines of trees and cut by branching ravines; the Volga to the west.","f":["A genetic study published in 2021 traced modern domestic horses to this steppe, some 4,200 years ago.","Its black soils, chernozems, are among the most fertile on Earth.","For thousands of years nomads such as the Scythians and Sarmatians roamed this grassland."],"q":{"q":"Which animal was probably first domesticated on this steppe?","a":"The horse","x":["The camel","The yak"]}},"ne-rg-1730073989":{"t":"The Corn Belt's heartland, ironed flat by glaciers","s":"A checkerboard to the horizon: this is the heart of America's Corn Belt, land flattened and enriched by ice-age glaciers and wind-blown dust, then ruled into square-mile sections. In summer the squares are green with maize and soybeans; after harvest they turn brown and tan. Rivers such as the Mississippi wind through it in wooded valleys.","w":"A square-mile grid of fields and roads, green in summer and brown after harvest, cut by wooded river valleys.","f":["Iowa and Illinois, both in the Central Lowland, together grow roughly a third of the US maize crop.","Much of the soil formed on loess, fine dust blown off glacial outwash at the end of the Ice Age.","Before farming, most of this land was tallgrass prairie, with grasses taller than a person."],"q":{"q":"Which two crops fill the Corn Belt's summer checkerboard?","a":"Maize and soybeans","x":["Wheat and cotton","Rice and sugar beet"]}},"Q2016601":{"t":"The 'Big Land' tundra of lakes and reindeer","s":"East of the Pechora River spreads the Bolshezemelskaya Tundra, the 'Big Land' tundra: a treeless plain of moss, dwarf birch and low hills, glittering with thousands of lakes. For most of the year it is white; in summer it turns green and brown, and the lakes shine like scattered mirrors. Nenets herders still move their reindeer across it with the seasons.","w":"A flat, treeless plain spattered with countless lakes and low rounded hills; white for most of the year.","f":["Permafrost lies beneath the tundra, which is why meltwater gathers in so many shallow lakes.","Vorkuta, a coal-mining city, stands on its south-eastern edge.","Its name sets it against the smaller Malozemelskaya, or 'Small Land', Tundra west of the Pechora."],"q":{"q":"Which people herd reindeer on the Bolshezemelskaya Tundra?","a":"The Nenets","x":["The Sámi","The Yakuts"]}},"Q252609":{"t":"A slice of Antarctica first mapped from the air","s":"In 1938–39 a German expedition catapulted seaplanes off the ship Schwabenland and photographed this stretch of Queen Maud Land from the air, naming it New Swabia after the ship. Behind the flat coastal ice shelves the land climbs into dark mountain ranges, then vanishes under the polar plateau. Near the coast lies the Schirmacher Oasis, a rare strip of bare rock and lakes.","w":"Flat ice shelves at the coast, then dark mountain chains poking through the ice, and white plateau beyond.","f":["The Schirmacher Oasis, about 17 km long, holds dozens of lakes and two stations, Russia's Novolazarevskaya and India's Maitri.","The ship Schwabenland was named after Swabia, a region of south-western Germany.","Novolazarevskaya honours Mikhail Lazarev, who sailed with Bellingshausen when Antarctica was sighted in 1820."],"q":{"q":"What gave New Swabia its name?","a":"An expedition ship","x":["A research station","A mountain range"]}},"Q973":{"t":"A wandering giant across the Siberian bogs","s":"The Ob does not so much flow as wander: here, in the middle of the West Siberian Plain, it spreads into a broad floodplain of channels, oxbow lakes and willow-covered islands, in places tens of kilometres wide. In May and June the spring flood turns much of it into one shining sheet. Then the water slowly retreats to the main channel.","w":"A broad silvery floodplain with many channels, oxbow lakes and islands, winding through flat bog and taiga.","f":["With the Irtysh, the Ob forms one of Asia's longest river systems, about 5,400 km.","It ends in the Gulf of Ob, an estuary some 800 km long opening onto the Kara Sea.","The river lies under ice for around half the year."],"q":{"q":"Into which sea does the Gulf of Ob open?","a":"The Kara Sea","x":["The Laptev Sea","The Barents Sea"]}},"Q1538813":{"t":"The first thread of the Yangtze, below the glaciers","s":"This braided stream, shining in a wide stony valley more than 4,500 m up, is the Tuotuo, the headwater usually counted as the official source of the Yangtze. It is born in the glaciers of Geladaindong in the Tanggula Mountains. Near the tiny settlement of Tuotuohe, the Qinghai–Tibet railway and highway cross it on long bridges.","w":"A thin, braided silver stream spread across a broad grey-brown valley, with snowy peaks to the south-west.","f":["Geladaindong, the peak whose glaciers feed the Tuotuo, rises to about 6,600 m.","Winters here are long and bitter, and the river freezes for several months each year.","Some surveys find the Dangqu, another headstream, to be longer, so the Yangtze's true source is still debated."],"q":{"q":"Which great river begins with the Tuotuo?","a":"The Yangtze","x":["The Yellow River","The Salween"]}},"ne-rv-1159113693":{"t":"The Nile's western arm, and the stone that spoke","s":"Below Cairo the Nile splits in two, and this is its western arm, the Rosetta Branch, winding north through the delta to the Mediterranean. From above it is a dark thread through a dense green quilt of fields and villages, sharply edged by desert to the west. Near its mouth, at Rosetta, French soldiers found the Rosetta Stone in 1799.","w":"A winding dark river through a dense green delta of fields and villages, with desert close by to the west.","f":["The Rosetta Stone bears one decree in hieroglyphs, demotic and Greek, which helped scholars decipher hieroglyphs.","The town at its mouth is called Rashid in Arabic; Rosetta is its European name.","With the Damietta Branch, it frames the Nile Delta, one of the oldest farmed landscapes on Earth."],"q":{"q":"What famous object was found near the mouth of the Rosetta Branch?","a":"The Rosetta Stone","x":["The Narmer Palette","Tutankhamun's mask"]}},"ne-rv-1159113949":{"t":"The Nile's eastern arm through a crowded green delta","s":"Where the Nile fans out into its delta, the Damietta Branch takes the eastern route to the sea. From the air it is a dark, gently winding line through an intensely green and crowded land, where fields, canals and villages leave hardly a gap. At night the whole delta sparkles with lights, framed by the black of the desert.","w":"A dark winding river through an intensely green, crowded delta; at night, a web of lights edged by dark desert.","f":["The city of Mansoura stands on its banks, and Damietta lies near its mouth on the Mediterranean.","Damietta has long been Egypt's great centre of furniture-making.","Since the Aswan High Dam, the Nile's floods no longer reach the delta, and in places its coast is retreating."],"q":{"q":"Which of the Nile's two delta branches is the eastern one?","a":"Damietta","x":["Rosetta","Canopic"]}},"Q1653":{"t":"Europe's great river squeezed through the Iron Gates","s":"Here the Danube forces its way through the Carpathians in the Iron Gates, a chain of gorges on the border of Serbia and Romania. From above the wide river suddenly narrows between steep wooded cliffs, then broadens again into a long reservoir behind the Iron Gate dam. At its narrowest, in the Kazan gorge, it is only about 150 m wide.","w":"A broad river pinched between steep forested mountains into a narrow, winding gorge, with a large dam downstream.","f":["The Danube flows through or along ten countries, more than any other river in the world.","On a cliff near Orșova, the face of the Dacian king Decebalus is carved about 55 m high.","The river runs some 2,850 km, from Germany's Black Forest to the Black Sea."],"q":{"q":"How many countries does the Danube flow through or along?","a":"Ten","x":["Six","Eight"]}},"Q26301":{"t":"The far-flung Mongolian source of the Yenisey","s":"The Ider starts high in the Khangai Mountains of Mongolia and winds north-east through treeless steppe and valleys dotted with larch. From above it looks modest, a blue-grey thread with a thin green edge, but it is the most distant headstream of an enormous system: through the Selenga, Lake Baikal, the Angara and the Yenisey, its water reaches the Arctic Ocean.","w":"A thin winding river with a narrow green fringe in a broad valley of brownish steppe, dark larch on north slopes.","f":["Where the Ider meets the Delgermörön, the Selenga River begins.","Measured from the Ider's source, the Yenisey is one of the world's longest river systems, over 5,000 km.","The river lies frozen for about half the year."],"q":{"q":"Which lake does the Ider's water pass through on its way to the Arctic?","a":"Lake Baikal","x":["Lake Khövsgöl","Lake Balkhash"]}},"Q75745":{"t":"The river that fills Lake Baikal","s":"The Selenga gathers the rivers of northern Mongolia and carries them north into Russia and on to Lake Baikal. Here, near the border, it winds through a broad valley between steppe and pine-clad hills, splitting around islands and gravel bars. It brings in about half of all the water that flows into Baikal.","w":"A winding river with islands and gravel bars in a wide valley between steppe and dark pine-covered hills.","f":["Where it enters Baikal, the Selenga has built a large delta, a maze of channels, reeds and lakes.","It is the largest river in Mongolia by volume of water.","Ulan-Ude, the capital of Buryatia, stands on the Selenga."],"q":{"q":"About how much of Lake Baikal's inflow comes from the Selenga?","a":"About half","x":["About a tenth","Nearly all"]}},"Q15954437":{"t":"The Brahmaputra's first steps, at the 'Horse Spring'","s":"Far out in western Tibet, a young river wanders over a wide, windswept valley more than 4,500 m up, braided into silver channels between sand and gravel. This is the Maquan, the uppermost course of the Yarlung Tsangpo, which later becomes the Brahmaputra. Its source lies in glaciers south-east of sacred Mount Kailash and Lake Manasarovar.","w":"A braided silver river spread over a broad, dry, sandy valley, with snowy Himalayan peaks to the south.","f":["Maquan means 'Horse Spring', echoing its Tibetan name Tamchok Khabab, 'from the horse's mouth'.","In Tibetan tradition four great rivers rise near Kailash, from the mouths of a horse, a lion, an elephant and a peacock.","Below the town of Saga the river takes the name Yarlung Tsangpo."],"q":{"q":"Which great river begins as the Maquan?","a":"The Brahmaputra","x":["The Ganges","The Indus"]}},"Q162737":{"t":"The only river that escapes from Lake Baikal","s":"More than 300 rivers flow into Lake Baikal, but only one flows out: the Angara. Here, far downstream, it crosses dark taiga as a broad, lake-like reservoir, one step in a staircase of dams that turn its fall into power. Its water is famously clear, and below the dams it often steams in winter instead of freezing.","w":"A broad, lake-like reservoir with branching bays in dark taiga, narrowing to a river below the dams.","f":["The Angara is the largest tributary of the Yenisey, which it joins far to the west.","The Bratsk reservoir on the Angara is one of the largest artificial lakes in the world by volume.","A Buryat legend tells how Angara, Baikal's daughter, ran away to her beloved, the Yenisey."],"q":{"q":"How many rivers flow out of Lake Baikal?","a":"One","x":["Three","Twelve"]}},"Q2220":{"t":"A deep green valley through Canada's northern farmland","s":"Look for a surprisingly deep valley cut into flat farmland: the Peace River, winding in broad curves some 200 m below the prairie, its slopes dark with aspen and spruce. Around it lies the Peace Country, some of the northernmost farmland in North America, gold with grain in late summer. Downstream the river swings north towards Lake Athabasca and the Arctic.","w":"A deep, winding, forested valley slicing through flat patchwork farmland of gold and green fields.","f":["Its name recalls a peace made in the 18th century between the Cree and the Dane-zaa, or Beaver, people.","Near Lake Athabasca it feeds the Peace–Athabasca Delta, one of the largest inland freshwater deltas on Earth.","In British Columbia the W. A. C. Bennett Dam holds its water in Williston Lake, the province's largest lake."],"q":{"q":"Where does the Peace River's name come from?","a":"A peace between two peoples","x":["A missionary's motto","Its calm, slow current"]}},"Q3411":{"t":"Canada's longest river, on its way to the Arctic","s":"The Mackenzie flows north for more than 1,700 km from Great Slave Lake to the Beaufort Sea, and here it is a wide, grey-brown river between dark spruce forest and the ridges of the Franklin Mountains. Nearby the clear Great Bear River flows into it. In winter the Mackenzie freezes hard and becomes an ice road linking remote communities.","w":"A broad, muddy river winding north through dark spruce forest, with low mountain ridges along its eastern side.","f":["With its headwaters, the Mackenzie system runs about 4,200 km, the second longest in North America.","Its Dene name, Deh Cho, means 'big river'.","It is named after Alexander Mackenzie, who travelled down it to the Arctic Ocean in 1789."],"q":{"q":"Which lake does the Mackenzie flow out of?","a":"Great Slave Lake","x":["Great Bear Lake","Lake Athabasca"]}},"ne-rv-1159117999":{"t":"The Mekong as a mountain stream on the Tibetan Plateau","s":"It is hard to believe that this stream, winding through high grassland and rocky gorges in Qinghai, will one day become the mighty Mekong. The Za Qu, as the upper Mekong is called here, flows more than 4,000 m above the sea, past yak pastures and steep valley walls. It rises in small glaciers on the plateau and runs south-east towards Tibet.","w":"A winding river in a deep, narrow valley cut into high, bare grassland hills, often dusted with snow.","f":["At Qamdo in Tibet it joins the Ang Qu, and from there it is called the Lancang, China's name for the Mekong.","The Mekong then flows through six countries on its way to the South China Sea.","Its source, above 5,000 m, lies in Zadoi County, within the Sanjiangyuan 'Source of Three Rivers' area."],"q":{"q":"The Za Qu is the upper course of which river?","a":"The Mekong","x":["The Salween","The Yangtze"]}},"Q21847787":{"t":"South America's second great river, laced with islands","s":"After the Amazon, the Paraná is South America's mightiest river, draining a huge basin across Brazil, Paraguay and Argentina together with its great tributary, the Paraguay. From above it is a broad brown band split by islands and sandbars, with floodplain lakes and marshes glinting on either side. It ends in a vast delta near Buenos Aires, feeding the Río de la Plata.","w":"A broad brown river laced with islands and sandbars, with lagoons and marshes spreading across its floodplain.","f":["At about 4,900 km, it is the second-longest river in South America.","The Itaipu Dam on the Paraná, between Brazil and Paraguay, is one of the world's largest hydroelectric plants.","The Paraná Delta, just north of Buenos Aires, is a maze of islands and channels where houses stand on stilts."],"q":{"q":"Which estuary does the Paraná flow into?","a":"Río de la Plata","x":["Gulf of Paria","Bahía Blanca"]}},"Q836812":{"t":"The upper Congo, once thought to be the Nile","s":"The Lualaba is the Congo River in its youth, rising on the Katanga plateau and flowing north through savanna, gorges and a chain of marshy lakes. From the air it is a dark ribbon edged with swamp and pale floodplain grass. In the 1870s explorers still argued whether it fed the Nile or the Congo; Henry Morton Stanley settled it by following it to the Atlantic.","w":"A dark winding river edged by swamps and pale floodplains, with lakes strung along it, in green-brown savanna.","f":["David Livingstone believed the Lualaba might be the upper Nile.","At Boyoma Falls, near Kisangani, the Lualaba ends and the river is called the Congo.","Upemba National Park, along its upper course, protects a chain of lakes and wetlands."],"q":{"q":"Which explorer followed the Lualaba all the way to the Atlantic?","a":"Henry Morton Stanley","x":["David Livingstone","Richard Burton"]}},"Q2226":{"t":"A short, wide river where pelicans nest on the rapids","s":"The Slave River carries the water of the Peace River and Lake Athabasca north to Great Slave Lake, a wide brown river winding through spruce forest and wetlands. Near Fort Smith it tumbles over a series of rapids, the main barrier on the long waterway from Alberta to the Arctic. On rocky islands in those rapids, American white pelicans nest.","w":"A broad, muddy river meandering through green forest and wetland, with white rapids near the Alberta border.","f":["The pelicans at Fort Smith form the most northerly breeding colony of American white pelicans.","The river takes its name from 'Slavey', an old name for the Dene people living along it.","It flows along the edge of Wood Buffalo National Park, the largest national park in Canada."],"q":{"q":"Which bird nests on islands in the Slave River rapids?","a":"American white pelican","x":["Sandhill crane","Snow goose"]}},"Q41179":{"t":"Six countries, one river, starting in Yunnan's gorges","s":"In Yunnan the Mekong, known in China as the Lancang, runs south through deep, forested valleys, much of it now stepped into long, narrow reservoirs behind a chain of dams. From above it looks like a green-brown snake coiled between steep ridges. From here it still has thousands of kilometres to go, past Laos, Thailand, Cambodia and Vietnam to its delta.","w":"A long, narrow, winding river or reservoir deep between steep green ridges, running roughly north–south.","f":["The Mekong runs for more than 4,000 km, through or along six countries.","Its river fisheries are among the largest in the world and feed millions of people.","Each rainy season the Mekong's flood pushes water backwards up the Tonlé Sap river into Cambodia's great lake."],"q":{"q":"What is the Mekong called in China?","a":"Lancang","x":["Nu Jiang","Jinsha"]}},"Q2599627":{"t":"A great river flowing more than 3.5 km above the sea","s":"If you are landing at Lhasa, this is the river beside the runway: the Yarlung Tsangpo, braided across a broad valley floor some 3,600 m up, with golden sand dunes piled against the slopes. It flows east along the Himalayas, then bursts through them in one of the deepest canyons on Earth and becomes the Brahmaputra in India.","w":"A wide, braided river with pale sandbars and dunes in a broad valley between bare brown mountains.","f":["Its Great Canyon, around the peak Namcha Barwa, is often called the deepest in the world.","Much of its Tibetan course lies above 3,000 m, making it one of the highest major rivers on Earth.","The Lhasa River, which flows past the Tibetan capital, joins it just west of here."],"q":{"q":"Which city's airport lies in the Yarlung Tsangpo valley?","a":"Lhasa","x":["Kathmandu","Xining"]}},"Q128102":{"t":"A tributary longer than the river it joins","s":"The Irtysh rises in the Mongolian Altai, crosses China and Kazakhstan and enters Russia, winding north across the West Siberian Plain to meet the Ob. Here, below Omsk, it loops lazily through a green floodplain of meadows, oxbow lakes and birch groves, with fields beyond. Remarkably, this tributary is longer than the river it flows into.","w":"A winding river in wide, regular loops through a green floodplain of meadows, oxbow lakes and birch woods.","f":["At about 4,250 km, the Irtysh is longer than the Ob, into which it flows.","It meets the Ob near Khanty-Mansiysk.","Tobolsk, on the Irtysh, was the first capital of Siberia; its white stone kremlin stands above the river."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Irtysh flow into?","a":"The Ob","x":["The Yenisey","The Tobol"]}},"Q3503":{"t":"The deepest river on Earth, crossing the equator twice","s":"Around the equator the Congo spreads into a vast, slow river several kilometres wide, dotted with long forested islands and fringed by flooded forest. From above it looks like a brown lake threaded with green. It is the second-largest river on Earth by flow, and on its great arc through Central Africa it crosses the equator twice.","w":"A broad brown river full of long wooded islands, bordered by dark flooded forest on both banks.","f":["In its lower gorges the Congo is more than 200 m deep, the deepest river known.","It carries more water than any other river except the Amazon.","Kinshasa and Brazzaville face each other across it, the closest pair of capitals apart from Rome and Vatican City."],"q":{"q":"Which two capitals face each other across the Congo?","a":"Kinshasa and Brazzaville","x":["Kinshasa and Bangui","Brazzaville and Luanda"]}},"Q45403":{"t":"A river so wide it looks like a sea of sandbars","s":"In Assam the Brahmaputra is less a river than a braided plain of water and sand several kilometres wide, its channels shifting with every monsoon. From above, look for pale sandbars and green islands scattered across it, with tea gardens and rice fields on the higher ground beyond. Upstream lies Majuli, one of the largest river islands in the world.","w":"A very wide, braided river with pale sandbars and green islands, and the hills of Meghalaya to the south.","f":["Its name means 'son of Brahma'; it is one of the few Indian rivers with a male name.","Its islands and sandbars, called chars, are farmed and even lived on, though floods can wash them away.","Kaziranga National Park, on its south bank, holds about two-thirds of the world's greater one-horned rhinos."],"q":{"q":"Which animal is Kaziranga, on the Brahmaputra, famous for?","a":"One-horned rhinoceros","x":["Snow leopard","Asiatic lion"]}},"Q947477":{"t":"A trout river running from Yellowstone to the Missouri","s":"The Madison is born inside Yellowstone, where the Firehole and Gibbon rivers meet, and runs north through Montana in a broad, open valley between two mountain ranges. From above it is a bright, braided stream with a thin line of willows, crossing sage-grey ranchland. At Three Forks it joins the Jefferson and the Gallatin, and together they form the Missouri.","w":"A bright, winding stream in a wide sage-coloured valley between snowy ranges, lined with a thin green band.","f":["Lewis and Clark named it in 1805 after James Madison, then the US Secretary of State.","Anglers rank it among the finest trout rivers in North America.","The Firehole, one of its sources, is warmed by the hot springs and geysers of Yellowstone."],"q":{"q":"Which great river do the Madison, Jefferson and Gallatin form?","a":"The Missouri","x":["The Yellowstone","The Snake"]}},"Q619802":{"t":"The dustiest place on Earth, an ancient lake bed","s":"Look for pale, almost white ground streaked by dunes: the Bodélé Depression in Chad, the dried-up bed of an ancient lake. Its floor is diatomite, the fine skeletons of tiny algae, and winter winds funnelled between the Tibesti and Ennedi highlands lift it into vast plumes. Bodélé is the largest single source of dust on Earth; some of it crosses the Atlantic to the Amazon.","w":"A pale, whitish basin crossed by rows of crescent dunes; in winter, long plumes of dust streaming south-west.","f":["The basin is a remnant of Mega-Chad, a lake that once covered an area comparable to the Caspian Sea.","The strongest winds blow here from November to March, the season of the Harmattan.","Bodélé dust carries phosphorus and other nutrients that help fertilise the Amazon rainforest."],"q":{"q":"What is the pale floor of the Bodélé Depression made of?","a":"Remains of tiny algae","x":["Salt crystals","Volcanic ash"]}},"Q1914":{"t":"A land of lakes, all combed the same way","s":"Karelia looks as if a giant rake had been dragged across it: long lakes and ridges all run in the same direction, north-west to south-east, the way the ice sheet moved. Between them lie dark pine and spruce forest, bogs and bare granite. In winter the lakes become white brushstrokes on the dark forest.","w":"Countless long lakes and ridges lined up north-west to south-east among dark forest; Lake Onega to the east.","f":["The Republic of Karelia has around 60,000 lakes.","On Kizhi Island in Lake Onega stands the wooden, 22-domed Church of the Transfiguration, built in 1714.","Many of the songs that became the Finnish epic Kalevala were recorded from singers in Karelia."],"q":{"q":"In which direction do most Karelian lakes stretch?","a":"North-west to south-east","x":["East to west","North-east to south-west"]}},"Q137555":{"t":"A red-brown bushland plateau in the Horn of Africa","s":"The Ogaden is dry country: a red-brown plateau of thorn scrub and scattered acacias, sloping gently from the Ethiopian highlands towards Somalia. From above it looks like a rust-coloured carpet with a fine stipple of bushes, cut by pale, mostly dry riverbeds. After the short rains it flushes green for a few weeks.","w":"A rust-red plain with a fine speckle of bushes and pale dry riverbeds; green only for weeks after the rains.","f":["The Shebelle River, rising in the Ethiopian highlands, crosses the Ogaden on its way to Somalia.","Rain comes here in two short seasons, in spring and in autumn.","Most people here are Somali herders who keep camels, goats and sheep."],"q":{"q":"Which river crosses the Ogaden on its way to Somalia?","a":"The Shebelle","x":["The Awash","The Omo"]}},"Q181103":{"t":"Folded bare mountains in every shade of brown","s":"Balochistan is a wild tangle of bare mountains and dry basins, where rock is folded into long parallel ridges that curve like the grain of polished wood. There is little green: a few oases, dry riverbeds and salt flats in the hollows. The region spans parts of Pakistan, Iran and Afghanistan. To the north-west rises Taftan, a volcano almost 4,000 m high that still steams.","w":"Long, parallel, curving ridges of bare brown and grey rock, with pale dry riverbeds and salt flats between them.","f":["Balochistan province covers about 44 percent of Pakistan, more than any other province.","Mehrgarh, in Balochistan, is one of the oldest farming villages in South Asia, some 9,000 years old.","On the Makran coast, in the south, small mud volcanoes bubble close to the sea."],"q":{"q":"About what share of Pakistan's area is Balochistan province?","a":"About 44 percent","x":["About 10 percent","About 70 percent"]}},"Q5700":{"t":"Forest, fells and bogs under the northern lights","s":"Lapland from the air is dark forest, pale bogs and rounded, treeless fells, laced with rivers and lakes. North of the Arctic Circle the sun does not set for weeks around midsummer, and in midwinter it barely rises. On a clear winter night you may see the aurora from your window, a green veil moving over the snow below.","w":"Dark conifer forest, pale bogs and bare rounded hilltops with winding rivers; in winter, white with dark forest patches.","f":["Lapland spans the north of Norway, Sweden and Finland and Russia's Kola Peninsula, the homeland of the Sámi.","Reindeer roam freely across much of Finnish Lapland; herding them is a traditional Sámi livelihood.","Rovaniemi, the capital of Finnish Lapland, lies right on the Arctic Circle."],"q":{"q":"Which Indigenous people live in Lapland?","a":"The Sámi","x":["The Inuit","The Nenets"]}},"Q1528035":{"t":"A bird's foot of land reaching into the Gulf","s":"At the end of its long journey the Mississippi has built a strange shape: a bird's foot, with long thin fingers of marsh and levee stretching into the Gulf of Mexico. From above, look for brown water spreading from their tips into the blue sea, and a maze of green marsh, lakes and channels around them. The whole landscape is made of river mud.","w":"Long thin fingers of land reaching into the Gulf, with brown river water fanning into blue sea at their tips.","f":["Over the last 7,000 years the river has built southern Louisiana by switching between several delta lobes.","Coastal Louisiana has lost roughly 5,000 km² of land since the 1930s as the delta sinks and erodes.","Head of Passes, where the river splits into the bird's-foot channels, lies about 150 km below New Orleans."],"q":{"q":"What is the Mississippi's modern delta often compared to?","a":"A bird's foot","x":["A fan","A triangle"]}},"Q29412":{"t":"A maze of mangroves and creeks at the Niger's mouth","s":"Here the Niger breaks into countless creeks and channels before reaching the Atlantic, forming the largest delta in Africa. From above it is a dark green maze of mangrove and swamp forest, laced with brown waterways that glint in the sun. At night the orange points of gas flares dot the darkness.","w":"A dark green maze of mangroves and swamp forest threaded by winding brown creeks; at night, scattered orange flares.","f":["Nigeria's mangrove forests, most of them in this delta, are the largest in Africa.","The delta covers some 70,000 km² of wetlands, creeks and islands.","The Niger arrives here after flowing some 4,200 km from the highlands of Guinea."],"q":{"q":"Which river forms this delta?","a":"The Niger","x":["The Volta","The Senegal"]}},"Q1431507":{"t":"Europe's largest delta, where lotus blooms in August","s":"Before it reaches the Caspian, the Volga fans out into Europe's largest delta: hundreds of channels, reed beds and islands, green against the dry, pale steppe on either side. From above it looks like the branching veins of a leaf. In late summer, lotus flowers bloom across its shallow outer waters in whole pink fields.","w":"A huge green fan of branching channels and reed beds spreading into the Caspian, framed by pale, dry steppe.","f":["The delta is made up of some 500 branches, channels and small streams.","The Astrakhan Nature Reserve, founded here in 1919, is one of the oldest in Russia.","The Caspian lies about 28 m below sea level, so the delta itself lies below the level of the world ocean."],"q":{"q":"Which pink flower blooms across the outer Volga Delta in late summer?","a":"Lotus","x":["Tulip","Peony"]}},"Q184429":{"t":"Reed beds, pelicans and three arms to the sea","s":"Just before the Black Sea, the Danube splits into three arms and spreads into a vast wetland of reeds, lakes and winding channels, one of the largest reed beds on Earth. From above it is a green-gold mosaic stitched with silver water, with the ruler-straight Sulina channel cutting through the middle. In spring and summer it is home to huge colonies of pelicans.","w":"A green-gold mosaic of reeds, lakes and winding channels, cut by one ruler-straight channel, beside the Black Sea.","f":["The Danube Delta was listed as a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1991.","It is Europe's second-largest delta, after the Volga's.","Most of the delta lies in Romania; its northern edge belongs to Ukraine."],"q":{"q":"How many main arms does the Danube split into in its delta?","a":"Three","x":["Two","Seven"]}},"Q1052867":{"t":"A rice bowl of nine dragons and endless canals","s":"The Mekong ends here in a flat, vivid green world: rice paddies in endless rectangles, orchards, fish ponds and villages strung along canals, all laced by wide brown branches of the river. The Vietnamese call it Cửu Long, the 'Nine Dragons', after its many mouths. At harvest time whole districts turn from green to gold.","w":"Vivid green rectangles of rice paddies laced with brown river branches and straight canals, all perfectly flat.","f":["The delta grows about half of Vietnam's rice.","Most of it lies less than 2 m above sea level.","Floating markets, where traders sell straight from boats, still gather near Cần Thơ."],"q":{"q":"What does the Vietnamese name Cửu Long mean?","a":"Nine Dragons","x":["Great River","Golden Water"]}},"Q81591":{"t":"Where Africa is slowly tearing apart","s":"Here, in the Afar region, the Great Rift Valley opens into a scorched lowland of lava fields, salt flats and volcanoes, parts of it more than 100 m below sea level. This is where three plates of the Earth's crust are pulling apart, and one day the sea may flood in. From here the rift runs south for thousands of kilometres, marked by a chain of long lakes.","w":"Black lava fields, white salt pans and scattered volcanic cones in a hot lowland, walled by high escarpments to the west.","f":["In 1974 the fossil skeleton known as Lucy, about 3.2 million years old, was found at Hadar in Afar.","Erta Ale, a volcano in the Danakil Depression, held a lake of lava in its crater for much of the last century.","Dallol, in the north of the depression, has one of the highest average yearly temperatures on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Which famous fossil was found in the Afar region in 1974?","a":"Lucy","x":["Turkana Boy","Taung Child"]}},"Q4863367":{"t":"Treeless tundra where the caribou roam","s":"North of the treeline, Canada's forest gives way to the Barren Grounds: open tundra of moss, lichen and bare rock, scattered with more lakes than anyone has counted. Look for eskers, long snaking gravel ridges left by rivers that once flowed inside the ice sheet. Every year great herds of caribou migrate across this land.","w":"Treeless land speckled with countless lakes, crossed by long, winding gravel ridges; white for most of the year.","f":["The Thelon Wildlife Sanctuary, created in 1927, protects musk oxen and caribou in the heart of the Barren Grounds.","Nearby Baker Lake is Nunavut's only inland community.","Some eskers here run for hundreds of kilometres."],"q":{"q":"What are the long, winding gravel ridges of the Barren Grounds called?","a":"Eskers","x":["Drumlins","Moraines"]}},"Q381050":{"t":"No trees, one straight line, and cliffs above the sea","s":"Nullarbor is Latin for 'no tree', and from above the name fits: a vast, flat, olive-grey plain of saltbush and bluebush without a single tree in sight. It is the world's largest single slab of limestone, riddled with caves underneath. Along its southern edge the Bunda Cliffs drop sheer into the Great Australian Bight.","w":"A dead-flat grey-green plain without trees, one straight railway across it, and sheer cliffs along the southern sea.","f":["The Trans-Australian Railway crosses it on a dead-straight stretch of about 478 km, the longest in the world.","The limestone formed on an ancient seabed and covers some 200,000 km².","The Bunda Cliffs are up to about 120 m high and run for roughly 100 km."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Nullarbor' mean?","a":"No tree","x":["Red earth","Great plain"]}},"Q1807196":{"t":"Tundra of countless lakes, all pointing the same way","s":"Between the Brooks Range and the Arctic Ocean, the North Slope is a flat, treeless tundra covered in lakes, many of them oval and aligned in the same direction, as if combed by the wind. For about nine months the land is white; in summer it thaws into a glittering sheet of water and green. Beneath it lies permafrost hundreds of metres thick.","w":"Flat, treeless tundra with countless oval lakes all aligned the same way; the snowy Brooks Range to the south.","f":["Prudhoe Bay, on the North Slope, is the largest oilfield in North America.","In Utqiaġvik, the northernmost town in the US, the sun does not set for more than 80 days in summer.","The Trans-Alaska Pipeline carries oil about 1,300 km from here to the port of Valdez."],"q":{"q":"Which mountains form the southern edge of the North Slope?","a":"The Brooks Range","x":["The Alaska Range","The Chugach Mountains"]}},"Q223":{"t":"An island that is mostly one enormous dome of ice","s":"On flights between Europe and North America you often cross an island that is almost all ice. The ice sheet covers about four-fifths of Greenland and is up to 3 km thick, so its crest sits more than 3,000 m above the sea. At the edges, dark peaks and fjords break through, and in summer the ice is freckled with turquoise meltwater lakes.","w":"A vast featureless white plateau, edged by dark mountains, deep fjords and icebergs drifting off the coast.","f":["Greenland is the world's largest island, about 2.2 million km² — roughly four times the size of France.","If all its ice melted, the world's oceans would rise by about 7 metres.","Only about 56,000 people live here, nearly all on the ice-free coast."],"q":{"q":"What share of Greenland is covered by its ice sheet?","a":"About four-fifths","x":["About half","About a third"]}},"Q35942":{"t":"Thousands of kilometres of ocean, stitched with atolls","s":"Below you is mostly deep blue, broken now and then by a ring of turquoise — an atoll — or the green ridges of a volcanic island. Polynesia is a vast triangle with Hawaii, New Zealand and Easter Island at its corners. People settled all of it in canoes, reading stars, swells and birds, long before Europeans arrived.","w":"Scattered turquoise rings of reef with pale lagoons, and now and then a dark-green volcanic peak wrapped in cloud.","f":["The name comes from the Greek for 'many islands'.","New Zealand, the triangle's south-western corner, was settled only about 700 years ago — one of the last large lands people reached.","Polynesian languages are so close that 'moana', the ocean, is the same word in Hawaii and New Zealand."],"q":{"q":"Which island marks a corner of the Polynesian Triangle?","a":"Easter Island","x":["Fiji","Guam"]}},"Q669037":{"t":"Columbus's mistaken 'Indies', strung across turquoise seas","s":"The islands curve for thousands of kilometres from the Bahamas and Cuba to Trinidad, and from above the colours give them away: shallow banks glowing turquoise, white sand, green mountains. The name is an old mistake — Columbus believed he had reached the Indies of Asia when he landed here in 1492. The big islands lie to the west; a chain of small volcanic ones closes off the Caribbean to the east.","w":"Big mountainous islands to the west, a curving chain of small volcanic ones to the east, bright turquoise shallows in between.","f":["The highest peak, Pico Duarte on Hispaniola, rises to about 3,100 m.","The Caribbean Sea takes its name from the Caribs, a people of the Lesser Antilles."],"q":{"q":"On which island is the highest peak of the West Indies?","a":"Hispaniola","x":["Cuba","Jamaica"]}},"Q928":{"t":"More than 7,000 islands, reefs and volcano cones","s":"Green, mountainous islands break the sea into straits and bays, each coast fringed with a pale line of reef. There are more than 7,000 of them, though Luzon in the north and Mindanao in the south hold over two-thirds of the land. The archipelago sits on the Pacific Ring of Fire, so look for volcanic cones and crater lakes among the forested ridges.","w":"A maze of green islands and narrow straits, with pale reef edges along the coasts.","f":["The islands were named in the 1540s after Prince Philip of Spain, later King Philip II.","On Bohol more than 1,200 rounded hills turn brown in the dry season — the famous Chocolate Hills.","The Philippines lies in the Coral Triangle, the region with the richest variety of reef life on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Which Spanish king gave his name to the Philippines?","a":"Philip II","x":["Charles V","Ferdinand II"]}},"Q17":{"t":"A 3,000-km arc of mountains rising from the Pacific","s":"From cruise height Japan is a long, crumpled arc of forested mountains, with cities squeezed onto the narrow plains between the slopes and the sea. Four big islands carry almost everyone, and thousands of smaller ones are scattered around them. At night the Pacific coast from Tokyo to Osaka glows as one nearly unbroken ribbon of light.","w":"Rugged green mountains down the middle, crowded plains along the coast, and white-capped Fuji standing alone south-west of Tokyo.","f":["About three-quarters of Japan is mountainous, which is why its cities crowd onto the coastal plains.","The country has more than 100 active volcanoes.","A 2023 recount using digital maps found about 14,000 islands — twice the long-quoted figure of 6,852."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how much of Japan is mountainous?","a":"About three-quarters","x":["About a third","About half"]}},"Q85377":{"t":"A frozen jigsaw at the top of North America","s":"Seen from above, this is a jigsaw of brown and white land fitted together with channels of sea ice. Tens of thousands of islands spread north of the Canadian mainland towards Greenland, and for much of the year the sea between them is frozen solid. Explorers spent centuries looking for a way through; the Northwest Passage threads these channels.","w":"Big, flat islands split by straight channels, white with sea ice most of the year; glaciers and ice caps in the east.","f":["Three of the world's ten largest islands are here: Baffin, Victoria and Ellesmere.","Roald Amundsen was the first to sail the whole Northwest Passage, in 1903–1906.","More than 36,000 islands make up the archipelago."],"q":{"q":"Which sea route winds between these islands?","a":"The Northwest Passage","x":["The Northern Sea Route","The Drake Passage"]}},"Q37394":{"t":"Steep green islands where hundreds of languages are spoken","s":"Here the Pacific islands turn tall and green: steep volcanic ridges cloaked in rainforest and rimmed by reef, from New Guinea east to Fiji. Some volcanoes still smoke, so look for a grey plume drifting off a summit. Melanesia is also the most linguistically diverse corner of the planet — villages a few ridges apart can speak unrelated languages.","w":"Large, mountainous, forested islands with reef-fringed coasts; now and then a volcanic plume trailing downwind.","f":["Papua New Guinea alone has more than 800 living languages.","Vanuatu has about 110 languages for some 300,000 people — the densest mix of languages per person on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Papua New Guinea alone has roughly how many languages?","a":"More than 800","x":["About 100","About 300"]}},"Q3359409":{"t":"Thousands of tiny islands above the deepest sea","s":"Micronesia is mostly ocean: thousands of small islands, many of them low atolls whose rings of reef glow turquoise against deep blue. Many rise only a few metres above the waves. On its western edge lies the Mariana Trench, where the sea floor drops almost 11 km — about as far below the surface as you are above it.","w":"Small rings of reef with pale-green lagoons, scattered far apart across dark-blue ocean; occasionally a higher, forested island.","f":["The name means 'small islands' in Greek.","On Yap, huge stone discs called rai, some wider than a person is tall, served as money."],"q":{"q":"What does the name 'Micronesia' mean?","a":"Small islands","x":["Black islands","Many islands"]}},"Q208643":{"t":"The world's largest archipelago, where Asia meets Australia","s":"Between mainland Asia and Australia lies the largest group of islands on Earth — about 25,000 of them, from Sumatra to the Philippines. Look for chains of volcanic cones, rainforest cut into clearings, and shallow seas glowing green over coral. Alfred Russel Wallace travelled here for eight years and noticed that the animals on either side of a narrow strait belonged to different worlds.","w":"Big green islands and strings of volcanic peaks in warm, shallow seas; brown river plumes spreading into turquoise water.","f":["The Wallace Line runs between Bali and Lombok, dividing mostly Asian wildlife to the west from Australian-type wildlife to the east.","While travelling in these islands in 1858, Wallace hit on natural selection independently of Darwin."],"q":{"q":"Between which two islands does the Wallace Line run?","a":"Bali and Lombok","x":["Java and Sumatra","Borneo and Java"]}},"Q38272":{"t":"A green, cloud-dappled cluster off Europe's north-west edge","s":"Two large islands and thousands of small ones sit off the north-west coast of Europe, and on a clear day they look intensely green, patched with fields and fringed by rocky headlands. Cloud is common: Atlantic air brings frequent rain. Until roughly 8,000 years ago you could have walked here from the continent, across a plain now drowned beneath the North Sea.","w":"Great Britain's long north–south shape with Ireland to its west, a patchwork of green fields, and ragged island clusters in the north.","f":["The group has more than 6,000 islands, most of them small and uninhabited.","The drowned land that once joined Britain to Europe is called Doggerland, after the Dogger Bank.","Warm water carried by the Gulf Stream keeps winters here mild for such a northern latitude."],"q":{"q":"What is the drowned land between Britain and Europe called?","a":"Doggerland","x":["Lyonesse","Hyperborea"]}},"Q664":{"t":"One of the last large lands people reached","s":"Two long islands and hundreds of small ones lie alone in the South Pacific, about 2,000 km east of Australia. Look for the snowy spine of the Southern Alps in the south and volcanic cones in the north. Polynesian voyagers arrived only about 700 years ago, making this one of the last large lands on Earth to be settled.","w":"Snowy peaks in the south, glacier-blue lakes and fjords in the south-west, green farmland on the plains.","f":["Cook Strait, between the two main islands, is only about 22 km wide at its narrowest.","New Zealand sits on Zealandia, a sunken continent that is about 94% under the sea.","Aotearoa, the Māori name, is often translated as 'land of the long white cloud'."],"q":{"q":"What is the Māori name for New Zealand?","a":"Aotearoa","x":["Rapa Nui","Hawaiki"]}},"Q3740828":{"t":"Fire and ice on a rift in the Atlantic","s":"Iceland looks unfinished from above: black lava fields and grey outwash plains, white ice caps, and a thin green rim along the coast. The island sits right on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, where North America and Eurasia pull apart, which is why it has so many volcanoes. Glacial rivers spread across the sand in braids, like pale veins.","w":"Dazzling white ice caps inland, dark lava and sand plains, braided rivers and a narrow green strip by the sea.","f":["The two plates move apart by about 2 cm a year, and Iceland slowly widens with them.","Vatnajökull, the largest ice cap, covers about 8% of the country and hides active volcanoes beneath it.","Geologists everywhere use the Icelandic word 'sandur' for a glacial outwash plain."],"q":{"q":"Which two plates pull apart across Iceland?","a":"North American and Eurasian","x":["African and Eurasian","Pacific and North American"]}},"Q7463928":{"t":"The Red Island, where life went its own way","s":"Madagascar's nickname, the Red Island, makes sense from above: bare laterite soil shows through in rust and orange, and after rain the rivers pour red silt into the sea. It split from Africa, and later from India, tens of millions of years ago, and life here evolved in isolation. Look for green rainforest along the east coast and dry plains in the west.","w":"A huge island with a green eastern escarpment, rust-red highlands, and rivers bleeding red sediment into the sea.","f":["At about 587,000 km², it is the world's fourth-largest island.","Lemurs evolved only here, and about 90% of Madagascar's wildlife is found nowhere else.","Malagasy, the island's language, is closest to languages of southern Borneo, more than 6,000 km away."],"q":{"q":"Malagasy is most closely related to languages from which island?","a":"Borneo","x":["Sri Lanka","Zanzibar"]}},"Q710":{"t":"A country of atolls in all four hemispheres","s":"Kiribati is 33 tiny islands scattered across thousands of kilometres of ocean. From above, most are thin rings of land around pale lagoons, often only a few metres above the sea. It is the only country in all four hemispheres, and in 1995 it moved the date line so that all its islands share the same calendar day.","w":"Thin rings of land enclosing jade-green lagoons, tiny against the deep blue, spread far apart along the equator.","f":["'Kiribati' is pronounced 'Kiribas' — the local form of 'Gilberts', the islands' old British name.","About half of the country's people live on South Tarawa, the capital atoll."],"q":{"q":"'Kiribati' is the local form of which name?","a":"Gilbert Islands","x":["Caroline Islands","Marshall Islands"]}},"Q192626":{"t":"Volcanoes on a conveyor belt across the Pacific","s":"The islands line up from south-east to north-west, and their shapes tell the story. In the south-east the Big Island is broad and young, its volcanoes still active; further north-west the islands grow older, more eroded and more deeply cut by green valleys. Beyond them the chain dwindles into atolls and sunken seamounts. It formed as the Pacific Plate slid over a fixed hotspot.","w":"A diagonal line of islands: broad dark volcanoes to the south-east, older, green, deeply furrowed islands to the north-west.","f":["Hawaii is about 3,800 km from California, one of the most isolated island groups on Earth.","Kauai, near the north-western end of the main islands, is about 5 million years old; the Big Island is under 1 million.","A new volcano, Kamaʻehuakanaloa (formerly Lōʻihi), is growing on the sea floor south-east of the Big Island."],"q":{"q":"What created the Hawaiian Islands?","a":"A hotspot under the Pacific Plate","x":["A subduction zone","A mid-ocean ridge"]}},"Q234796":{"t":"Where the new year arrives before anywhere else","s":"A loose line of coral atolls runs across the equator in the middle of the Pacific, hundreds of kilometres apart. The largest, Kiritimati, is hard to miss: a huge ring of land with a maze of shallow, milky-blue lagoons inside. The islands that belong to Kiribati keep time 14 hours ahead of Greenwich, so they greet each new year first.","w":"Tiny, isolated atolls strung north to south; Kiritimati stands out as a big pale ring full of patchy lagoons.","f":["Kiritimati has the largest land area of any coral atoll, about 390 km².","Its name is the local spelling of 'Christmas': Captain Cook reached it on Christmas Eve 1777.","Of the 11 Line Islands, 8 belong to Kiribati; the rest are United States territories."],"q":{"q":"Which Line Island is the largest coral atoll by land area?","a":"Kiritimati","x":["Tabuaeran","Palmyra"]}},"Q118350":{"t":"Hundreds of atolls and a few high green islands","s":"The Carolines are scattered over some 3,000 km of the western Pacific, just north of the equator. Most are low atolls, rings of reef around turquoise lagoons, but Pohnpei, Kosrae and the peaks of Chuuk rise as green, cloud-capped islands. Their navigators crossed open ocean without instruments, and their knowledge helped revive Polynesian voyaging in the 1970s.","w":"Small turquoise-ringed atolls far apart; Chuuk shows as a huge reef ring with green peaks inside.","f":["The islands are named after King Charles II of Spain.","Nan Madol, a town of basalt built on about 100 artificial islets off Pohnpei, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Master navigator Mau Piailug of Satawal guided the canoe Hōkūleʻa from Hawaii to Tahiti in 1976."],"q":{"q":"Which king are the Caroline Islands named after?","a":"Charles II of Spain","x":["Charles I of England","Charles III of Spain"]}},"Q165481":{"t":"Four giant islands on a drowned edge of Asia","s":"Borneo, Sumatra, Java and Sulawesi make up the Greater Sunda Islands, and three of them rise from a shallow shelf that was dry land during the ice ages. The seas between them are rarely deeper than about 100 m, so the water often looks green rather than deep blue. Look for volcano chains along Sumatra and Java and dark rainforest on Borneo.","w":"Big green islands in shallow, greenish seas; lines of volcanic cones along Sumatra and Java.","f":["Java is the world's most populous island, home to more than 150 million people.","In the ice ages people and animals could walk from mainland Asia to Java, Sumatra and Borneo.","Sulawesi is the odd one out: it lies beyond the Wallace Line and has a strange mix of wildlife."],"q":{"q":"Which is the largest of the Greater Sunda Islands?","a":"Borneo","x":["Sumatra","Sulawesi"]}},"Q1042":{"t":"Granite islands left behind by a drifting continent","s":"Most islands in open ocean are volcanoes or coral, but the Seychelles' inner islands are granite — a sliver of an ancient continent, stranded when India drifted away to the north. From above, Mahé and Praslin show steep green hills and grey boulders tumbling into turquoise bays. Far to the south-west, the outer islands are flat coral atolls lost in the blue.","w":"Steep green granite islands with turquoise bays in the north-east; low, far-flung coral atolls to the south-west.","f":["The coco de mer palm, native to Praslin and Curieuse, bears the heaviest seed of any plant.","Aldabra atoll is home to roughly 100,000 giant tortoises.","The Seychelles has the smallest population of any African country."],"q":{"q":"What rock are Mahé and Praslin made of?","a":"Granite","x":["Basalt","Coral limestone"]}},"Q4526612":{"t":"A green teardrop off India's southern tip","s":"Sri Lanka hangs off the tip of India like a teardrop, its narrow northern end pointing at a chain of sandbanks that almost reaches the mainland. The south-central highlands rise as a dark-green knot, with tea estates climbing their slopes. Around them lie rice paddies, reservoirs built by ancient kings, and a golden rim of beaches.","w":"A teardrop-shaped island: mountains in the south-centre, and a thin chain of sandbanks reaching toward India from the north-west.","f":["The word 'serendipity' comes from Serendip, an old name for the island.","Adam's Bridge, a chain of shoals about 50 km long, almost links Sri Lanka to India.","A footprint-shaped hollow on top of Adam's Peak is revered by Buddhists, Hindus, Muslims and Christians alike."],"q":{"q":"Which English word comes from an old name for Sri Lanka?","a":"Serendipity","x":["Cinnamon","Paradise"]}},"Q3593416":{"t":"A heart-shaped island facing the Southern Ocean","s":"Tasmania is shaped a little like a heart, and from above its west is wild: dark forest, buttongrass plains, jagged mountains and lakes, with hardly a road. The east is gentler, with farmland and white-sand bays. The next land to the south is Antarctica, and the westerlies of the Roaring Forties arrive straight off thousands of kilometres of open ocean.","w":"A heart-shaped island south of Australia: rugged dark-green west, drier farmland and bright bays along the east coast.","f":["Abel Tasman sighted it in 1642, and for two centuries it was called Van Diemen's Land.","Air measured at Cape Grim, on the north-west tip, is among the cleanest on Earth.","The Tasmanian devil now survives in the wild only here."],"q":{"q":"What was Tasmania's old European name?","a":"Van Diemen's Land","x":["New Holland","Terra Australis"]}},"Q40285":{"t":"Rainforest, great rivers and ice beside the equator","s":"The world's second-largest island is a green giant with a mountain spine running its full length, usually wrapped in cloud. Rivers such as the Sepik and the Fly loop through swamp forest in wide brown meanders you can follow for hundreds of kilometres. In the west the peaks reach almost 4,900 m, high enough for small glaciers to have survived right beside the equator.","w":"A huge green island with a long, cloud-wrapped mountain spine and wide, snaking brown rivers through lowland swamp.","f":["Puncak Jaya, 4,884 m, is the highest mountain on any island in the world.","At about 786,000 km², New Guinea is second only to Greenland among the world's islands.","Most of the world's birds-of-paradise, famous for their dancing males, are found on New Guinea."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak on New Guinea?","a":"Puncak Jaya","x":["Mount Wilhelm","Mount Kinabalu"]}},"Q36117":{"t":"One island, three countries and a granite giant","s":"Borneo is the third-largest island on Earth, and from the air you can read its story in its textures. Deep, crinkled rainforest covers the interior, brown rivers coil towards the coast, and across many lowlands neat grids of oil-palm plantations have replaced the jungle. In the north-east, Mount Kinabalu's bare granite summit pokes above the clouds at 4,095 m.","w":"Dark rainforest, coiling brown rivers and geometric plantation grids; in the north-east, Kinabalu's rocky summit above the clouds.","f":["Borneo is shared by three countries: Indonesia, Malaysia and Brunei.","The Kapuas, about 1,140 km long, is Indonesia's longest river.","Wild orangutans live only on Borneo and Sumatra."],"q":{"q":"How many countries share the island of Borneo?","a":"Three","x":["Two","Four"]}},"Q3492":{"t":"Home of Toba, the world's largest volcanic lake","s":"Sumatra stretches almost 1,800 km across the equator, with a chain of volcanoes running down its western side. The unmissable landmark is Lake Toba: a blue oval about 100 km long, filling the crater of a supervolcano that erupted some 74,000 years ago. In its middle sits Samosir, nearly as big as Singapore — an island in a lake on an island.","w":"A long green island with a volcanic ridge along its west; look for Toba, a vast blue lake with a big island inside.","f":["It is the only place where tigers, rhinos, orangutans and elephants still live side by side in the wild.","At about 473,000 km², Sumatra is the world's sixth-largest island.","The equator crosses Sumatra, so the island lies in both hemispheres."],"q":{"q":"What is the island in the middle of Lake Toba called?","a":"Samosir","x":["Nias","Siberut"]}},"Q13989":{"t":"Fuji, the Japanese Alps and Tokyo on one long island","s":"Honshu is the long, bent heart of Japan, and from the window its variety is striking: snow on the Japanese Alps down the middle, rice plains and city sprawl along the coasts. On a clear day Fuji's lone white cone is unmistakable, south-west of Tokyo's vast grey-brown carpet. Tunnels and bridges tie Honshu to the other three main islands.","w":"A long curved island: snowy peaks down the centre, Fuji's solitary cone, and dense cities crowding the Pacific coast.","f":["About 100 million people live on Honshu — more than on any island except Java.","At about 228,000 km², it is the world's seventh-largest island.","The Seikan Tunnel, which runs under the sea to Hokkaido, is about 54 km long."],"q":{"q":"Which island has more people than Honshu?","a":"Java","x":["Great Britain","Luzon"]}},"Q22890":{"t":"The Emerald Isle lives up to its name","s":"On a clear day Ireland really is emerald: small fields in a hundred shades of green, stitched together by hedges and, in the west, by grey stone walls. The Atlantic coast is ragged with bays and headlands; the midlands are low, dotted with lakes and brown bog. Look for the River Shannon swelling into long lakes as it runs south towards the ocean.","w":"A green island west of Great Britain: ragged Atlantic coast, flat lake-dotted midlands and the long thread of the Shannon.","f":["The Shannon, about 360 km long, is the longest river in Ireland or Britain.","Ireland has no native snakes: after the Ice Age the sea cut it off before they could arrive.","Lough Neagh, in the north-east, is the largest lake in the British Isles."],"q":{"q":"What is the longest river in Ireland?","a":"The Shannon","x":["The Liffey","The Boyne"]}},"Q23666":{"t":"Europe's largest island, chalk cliffs to Highland lochs","s":"Great Britain runs almost 1,000 km from south to north, and flying along it you watch the land change. The south is a soft patchwork of fields and towns around London's huge sprawl; the Pennines form a brown spine through northern England; Scotland breaks up into mountains and long, narrow lochs. At night the cities blaze orange while the Highlands go almost dark.","w":"A long island narrowing northward: green farmland in the south, a brown upland spine, then Scotland's mountains and slender lochs.","f":["It is the ninth-largest island in the world and the largest in Europe.","Ben Nevis, its highest mountain at 1,345 m, is still almost 10 km below cruising height.","The Channel Tunnel, about 50 km long, links it to France beneath the Strait of Dover."],"q":{"q":"What is Great Britain's highest mountain?","a":"Ben Nevis","x":["Snowdon","Scafell Pike"]}},"Q186233":{"t":"A forested chain between the Bay of Bengal and Andaman Sea","s":"A narrow chain of islands, almost entirely cloaked in dark tropical forest, runs north to south between the Bay of Bengal and the Andaman Sea. It belongs to India but lies much closer to Myanmar. The water around it is exceptionally clear, so reefs show as pale turquoise halos. East of the main chain, tiny Barren Island is South Asia's only active volcano.","w":"A slim north–south chain of densely forested islands fringed by turquoise reef, with scattered small islets to the east.","f":["Barren Island, only about 3 km across, is the sole confirmed active volcano in South Asia.","North Sentinel Island is home to one of the last peoples on Earth living without contact with the outside world."],"q":{"q":"Which active volcano lies in the Andaman Islands?","a":"Barren Island","x":["Krakatoa","Mount Popa"]}},"Q157286":{"t":"Scattered green islands at the gateway to Malacca","s":"South of the Andamans, across the Ten Degree Channel, the Nicobars scatter in a loose line towards Sumatra. Some are flat coral islands ringed with palms; others, like Great Nicobar, are hilly and cloaked in rainforest. Most are closed to tourists to protect the Nicobarese and Shompen peoples, so the dark-green shapes and turquoise reefs below are seen by few outsiders.","w":"A loose chain of islands between the Andamans and Sumatra: flat, palm-fringed ones in the north, hilly rainforest ones in the south.","f":["The Ten Degree Channel, about 150 km wide, separates them from the Andamans along the 10th parallel.","The Nicobars lie far closer to Sumatra than to mainland India."],"q":{"q":"Which channel separates the Nicobars from the Andamans?","a":"Ten Degree Channel","x":["Six Degree Channel","Nine Degree Channel"]}},"Q3827":{"t":"The original Spice Islands","s":"For centuries these scattered islands were the world's only source of cloves and nutmeg, and ships sailed halfway round the globe to reach them. From above they are green cones and ridges rising steeply out of very deep blue water, their shores rimmed with coral. Ternate and Tidore, two volcanic cones facing each other across a narrow strait, are the classic view.","w":"Steep green volcanic islands and cones, rimmed with coral, rising from unusually dark-blue water.","f":["Under a 1667 treaty the Dutch kept Run, a tiny nutmeg island here, and the English kept Manhattan.","Nutmeg and mace both come from the same fruit, native to the Banda Islands.","The Banda Sea plunges to more than 7 km deep in the Weber Deep."],"q":{"q":"Which spice came originally only from the Banda Islands?","a":"Nutmeg","x":["Cinnamon","Black pepper"]}},"Q119285":{"t":"A 1,900-km arc of volcanoes reaching toward Asia","s":"Flights between North America and East Asia often skirt the Aleutians, a 1,900-km arc of volcanic islands trailing from Alaska towards Kamchatka. Fog and cloud hide them much of the time; when it clears, look for snow-streaked cones — Shishaldin on Unimak Island is one of the most symmetrical on Earth. North of the chain lies the Bering Sea, south of it the open Pacific.","w":"A long curving chain of rugged islands and snowy volcanic cones, often poking through a lid of fog.","f":["The chain crosses the 180th meridian, so Alaska reaches into the Eastern Hemisphere.","The arc has dozens of volcanoes, and some erupt every few years.","The Unangan (Aleut) people have lived on these islands for about 9,000 years."],"q":{"q":"Which sea lies north of the Aleutian Islands?","a":"The Bering Sea","x":["The Sea of Okhotsk","The Chukchi Sea"]}},"Q216533":{"t":"The top of Canada: ice caps, bare rock, almost no one","s":"At the northern edge of Canada lies a cluster of large islands with bare brown plateaus, glaciers spilling from ice caps, and channels choked with sea ice for most of the year. Only a few hundred people live here, in two small hamlets. The group was named in 1953 to mark the coronation of Queen Elizabeth II.","w":"Big, flat-topped islands of bare rock, white ice caps and glaciers, separated by channels of pack ice.","f":["Devon Island is the largest uninhabited island on Earth.","On Axel Heiberg Island, stumps of trees that grew about 45 million years ago still stand, preserved but not fossilised.","Scientists use Devon Island's Haughton impact crater to rehearse exploring Mars."],"q":{"q":"Which is the largest uninhabited island on Earth?","a":"Devon Island","x":["Ellesmere Island","Melville Island"]}},"Q68740":{"t":"Taller than Everest, if you start from the sea floor","s":"The Big Island is made of five volcanoes fused together, and from above you can see their broad, gentle domes and dark lava flows streaking down to the sea. Mauna Kea and Mauna Loa both top 4,100 m, and in winter their summits can be white with snow. Measured from its base on the ocean floor, Mauna Kea is over 10,000 m tall — more than Everest.","w":"Two huge, gentle domes, snow-dusted in winter, with black lava flows running to the coast and perhaps a volcanic plume.","f":["Mauna Loa is the largest active volcano on Earth by volume.","Lava from Kīlauea has added new land to the island's south-east coast within recent decades.","The Big Island is larger than all the other Hawaiian islands combined."],"q":{"q":"How many volcanoes make up the island of Hawaii?","a":"Five","x":["Two","Eight"]}},"Q120755":{"t":"Snowy Alps, turquoise lakes and fjords","s":"The Southern Alps run like a white wall down the length of the island, and on their dry eastern side glacial lakes glow an unreal milky turquoise — Tekapo and Pukaki are the brightest. To the west, glaciers tumble towards rainforest and the sea; in the south-west, Fiordland's drowned valleys cut deep into the mountains. East of the range, braided rivers fan across the Canterbury Plains.","w":"A white mountain wall down the island, vivid turquoise lakes on its eastern side, fjords slicing the south-west coast.","f":["Aoraki / Mount Cook, 3,724 m, is New Zealand's highest mountain.","The lakes' colour comes from 'rock flour', fine silt ground by glaciers and suspended in the water.","At about 150,000 km², it is the world's 12th-largest island."],"q":{"q":"What gives South Island's glacial lakes their turquoise colour?","a":"Rock flour from glaciers","x":["Algae blooms","Copper minerals"]}},"Q118863":{"t":"Volcanoes, a caldera lake and a perfect lone cone","s":"The centre of the North Island is a volcanic plateau, and it shows. Lake Taupō, the country's largest lake, fills a caldera left by enormous eruptions; south of it rise Ruapehu, Tongariro and the neat cone of Ngauruhoe, often snow-capped. Out on the west coast, Taranaki stands alone: a near-perfect cone ringed by a sharp circle of forest amid green farmland.","w":"A large lake in the centre with snowy volcanoes to its south; on the west coast, a lone cone inside a circle of forest.","f":["Lake Taupō, about 616 km², fills a volcanic caldera; its last great eruption was around 1,800 years ago.","About three-quarters of New Zealanders live on the North Island.","Auckland is built on a field of some 50 small volcanoes."],"q":{"q":"Which lake fills a volcanic caldera in the centre of the North Island?","a":"Lake Taupō","x":["Lake Wakatipu","Lake Tekapo"]}},"Q709":{"t":"Two chains of atolls, barely above the waves","s":"The Marshalls are two parallel chains of coral atolls — the 'sunrise' and 'sunset' chains — strung across the central Pacific. From above, each atoll is a necklace of narrow islets around a lagoon, bright turquoise inside and deep blue outside. The land averages only about 2 m above sea level, so the islands can look like lines painted on the water.","w":"Necklaces of thin islets around wide turquoise lagoons, lined up in two roughly parallel north-west to south-east chains.","f":["Marshallese navigators made stick charts — frames of sticks and shells mapping the ocean swells around the islands.","The country has 29 atolls and 5 single islands, with only about 180 km² of land in all."],"q":{"q":"What did Marshallese navigators map with sticks and shells?","a":"Ocean swells","x":["Star positions","Fish migrations"]}},"Q686":{"t":"A chain of volcanoes, some of them still glowing","s":"Vanuatu is a chain of about 80 islands running some 1,300 km north to south, and many of them are volcanoes. Steep green slopes drop to fringing reef, and in places bare ash plains and smoking craters break the forest. On Tanna, in the south, Mount Yasur has been erupting almost without pause for centuries; at night its crater glow can sometimes be seen from far away.","w":"A long chain of green volcanic islands with reef edges; look for grey ash slopes and drifting plumes, especially in the south.","f":["The land divers of Pentecost Island, who leap from wooden towers with vines tied to their ankles, inspired bungee jumping.","Vanuatu has an underwater post office, in the reef off Hideaway Island near Port Vila."],"q":{"q":"Which sport was inspired by Pentecost Island's land divers?","a":"Bungee jumping","x":["Cliff diving","Skydiving"]}},"Q80389":{"t":"A chain of volcanic cones stepping across the sea","s":"Between Kamchatka and Hokkaido the Kurils cross the sea like a line of stepping stones about 1,200 km long. Many are simply volcanoes poking out of the water, their slopes streaked with snow well into summer. Fog is the rule here, but on a clear day look for Alaid, a lone cone rising straight from the waves at the northern end of the chain.","w":"A line of small, steep volcanic islands and cones between two seas, often half hidden by fog.","f":["Alaid, on Atlasov Island, rises to about 2,300 m and is the highest volcano in the chain.","On Onekotan, a volcanic cone rises from a lake that fills an older, larger crater.","Just offshore, the Kuril–Kamchatka Trench plunges more than 9 km deep."],"q":{"q":"Which sea lies to the west of the Kuril Islands?","a":"The Sea of Okhotsk","x":["The Sea of Japan","The Bering Sea"]}},"Q1964385":{"t":"Volcanic islands that are, officially, part of Tokyo","s":"South of Tokyo Bay a thin line of volcanic islands runs more than 1,000 km out into the Pacific: first the Izu Islands, then the Ogasawara group, then the Volcano Islands. Many are single cones with steep, dark shores and a collar of white surf. However far they lie from the capital, all of them are governed by the Tokyo Metropolis.","w":"A long north–south dotted line of small, steep volcanic islands, some with craters, fringed by white surf.","f":["The Ogasawara Islands have never been joined to a continent, so many of their species live nowhere else; they are a UNESCO site.","Nishinoshima has grown many times over since eruptions began in 2013, swallowing the old island.","Ogasawara has no airport: the ferry from Tokyo takes about 24 hours."],"q":{"q":"Which city governs the Nanpō Islands?","a":"Tokyo","x":["Osaka","Yokohama"]}},"Q11832316":{"t":"Remote atolls of black pearls and empty lagoons","s":"Far north of Rarotonga, a handful of small atolls lie scattered across hundreds of kilometres of ocean. From above they are classic rings: a thin rim of coconut palms around a lagoon that glows every shade from jade to turquoise. A few hundred people at most live on each inhabited island, and cultured black pearls from Manihiki's lagoon are the local treasure.","w":"Isolated rings of land around jade and turquoise lagoons, widely scattered, with no high ground at all.","f":["New Zealander Tom Neale lived alone on Suwarrow for years and wrote 'An Island to Oneself' about it.","Suwarrow is now a national park with no permanent residents, only seasonal caretakers."],"q":{"q":"What is Manihiki atoll famous for?","a":"Black pearls","x":["Vanilla","Sea salt"]}},"Q1011":{"t":"Dry volcanic islands where Atlantic hurricanes are born","s":"Some 500 km off West Africa, ten volcanic islands rise from the Atlantic in shades of rust, ochre and grey — the Sahara's dry breath reaches this far. Sal and Boa Vista in the east are flat and sandy. In the south-west, Fogo is one enormous volcano, with a young black cone standing inside a broad, collapsed caldera.","w":"Brown, arid islands in deep-blue sea; Fogo is round, with a dark cone inside a caldera; the eastern islands are flat and sandy.","f":["The islands were uninhabited until the Portuguese arrived around 1460.","Pico do Fogo, 2,829 m, is the highest point; it last erupted in 2014–15.","Many of the strongest Atlantic hurricanes begin as storms near these islands."],"q":{"q":"Which Cape Verde island is a single volcano with a cone inside its caldera?","a":"Fogo","x":["Sal","Boa Vista"]}},"Q891593":{"t":"An island that was mapped as a peninsula until 1908","s":"At the mouth of the Khatanga Gulf, on the edge of the Laptev Sea, lies a low, roughly square island of tundra about 60 km across. For most of the year it is white and merges with the sea ice around it; in the short summer it turns brown-green and glitters with small lakes. Rivers run outwards from its central upland like the spokes of a wheel.","w":"A squarish, low tundra island at the mouth of a long gulf, lake-speckled in summer, often fused with sea ice.","f":["Until 1908 it was mapped as a peninsula; Nikifor Begichev showed it was an island, and it now bears his name.","Its highest point, on the central Kiryaka upland, is under 200 m.","Begichev had earlier sailed on Eduard Toll's Russian polar expedition of 1900–1902."],"q":{"q":"What was Bolshoy Begichev thought to be before 1908?","a":"A peninsula","x":["Two separate islands","A sandbank"]}},"Q219774":{"t":"India's southernmost tip, wrapped in rainforest","s":"Great Nicobar is the largest and wildest of the Nicobars: hilly, almost wholly covered in rainforest, its rivers winding out through mangroves to the sea. Its southern tip, Indira Point, is the southernmost point of India, and Indonesia's Sumatra lies less than 200 km beyond it. The Shompen, a small forest people, live in the interior.","w":"A large, hilly, dark-green island at the southern end of the Nicobar chain, with pale beaches and mangrove creeks.","f":["Much of the island is a biosphere reserve that includes two national parks.","Giant leatherback turtles come ashore on its beaches to nest."],"q":{"q":"What is Indira Point, on Great Nicobar, known for?","a":"India's southernmost point","x":["India's highest island peak","India's oldest lighthouse"]}},"Q1473243":{"t":"The Andamans' busy hub, ringed by reef and mangroves","s":"Most flights to the Andamans land here, on the island that holds the capital and most of the islands' people. From the air South Andaman is a ragged shape of forested hills and deep, winding creeks lined with mangroves. Off its south-west coast, a marine national park protects reefs and a scatter of small islands set in pale turquoise water.","w":"A ragged, hilly green island with long mangrove creeks; the town and airport on its east coast, small reef islands to the south-west.","f":["The Mahatma Gandhi Marine National Park, founded in 1983, covers some 15 small islands and their reefs.","Chidiya Tapu, at the island's southern tip, is known for its birdlife and sunsets over the sea."],"q":{"q":"Which marine national park lies off South Andaman?","a":"Mahatma Gandhi Marine National Park","x":["Gulf of Mannar National Park","Sundarbans National Park"]}},"Q222853":{"t":"The largest Andaman island, forest from shore to shore","s":"Middle Andaman is the largest island of the chain, and from above it is almost unbroken forest from coast to coast. Its eastern shore is cut by long, branching creeks fringed with mangroves; the western side, facing the open sea, is wilder still and part of the Jarawa people's reserve. Look for small islands and turquoise reef patches off the east coast.","w":"An elongated island of near-continuous dark forest, with mangrove-lined creeks on the east and small islets offshore.","f":["At about 1,500 km², it is the largest of the Andaman Islands.","Just to the south, on Baratang Island, small mud volcanoes bubble up through the forest."],"q":{"q":"What unusual feature is found on Baratang, just south of Middle Andaman?","a":"Mud volcanoes","x":["A salt lake","A crater lake"]}},"Q2000510":{"t":"The Andamans' highest peak rises from this forested isle","s":"At the northern end of the Great Andaman chain, this island rises higher than any other: Saddle Peak, 732 m, stands near its east coast in a national park of dense forest. Around it, creeks and mangroves eat into the shore, and reefs trace pale lines offshore. Beyond, open sea stretches north towards Myanmar's Coco Islands.","w":"The northernmost large island of the Andaman chain: forested hills rising to a high ridge near the east coast.","f":["Saddle Peak, 732 m, is the highest point of the Andaman and Nicobar Islands.","Off Diglipur, the little islands of Ross and Smith are joined by a narrow sandbar, making a pair of twins."],"q":{"q":"What is the highest point of the Andaman Islands?","a":"Saddle Peak","x":["Mount Harriet","Mount Thullier"]}},"Q82601":{"t":"Magellan's 'Land of Fire' at the bottom of the Americas","s":"At the very tip of South America the Andes bend east and crumble into islands, glaciers and fjords. The main island of Tierra del Fuego is two landscapes in one: flat, tawny steppe with pale lakes in the north, and snowy mountains with glaciers spilling towards the sea in the south. The Strait of Magellan cuts it off from the mainland.","w":"A big triangular island: yellow-brown plains in the north, snowy peaks and glaciers in the south, the Beagle Channel along its southern edge.","f":["Magellan named it in 1520 after the fires he saw burning along its shores.","Cape Horn lies on a small island at the archipelago's southern edge.","The Beagle Channel is named after HMS Beagle, the ship that later carried Charles Darwin here."],"q":{"q":"Which strait separates Tierra del Fuego from the mainland?","a":"The Strait of Magellan","x":["The Drake Passage","The Beagle Channel"]}},"Q1952":{"t":"Almost an island, hugged by the world's highest tides","s":"Nova Scotia is a long peninsula tied to the mainland only by a narrow neck of land, and nowhere in it is more than about 67 km from the sea. The Atlantic coast is a ragged fringe of coves, rocks and islets. On the north-west side, the Bay of Fundy has the highest tides in the world: at low water, look for vast red mudflats where there was sea a few hours before.","w":"A long peninsula parallel to the mainland, a deeply indented Atlantic coast, and reddish tidal flats along the Bay of Fundy.","f":["Fundy's tides can rise and fall by about 16 m, the greatest range on Earth.","The name is Latin for 'New Scotland'.","Sable Island, a thin crescent of sand far out in the Atlantic off Nova Scotia, is home to wild horses."],"q":{"q":"Which bay beside Nova Scotia has the world's highest tides?","a":"The Bay of Fundy","x":["Hudson Bay","Chesapeake Bay"]}},"Q170479":{"t":"A mountainous island between the ocean and the mainland","s":"Vancouver Island runs about 460 km along Canada's Pacific coast, its spine of snowy mountains dividing two worlds. The west side faces the open ocean with deep sounds, surf and temperate rainforest; the sheltered east side has farmland and towns along the Strait of Georgia. At its southern tip is Victoria, capital of British Columbia — the city of Vancouver is across the water on the mainland.","w":"A long mountainous island parallel to the coast: snowy peaks down the middle, fjord-like inlets on the ocean side.","f":["It is the largest island on the Pacific coast of North America.","Its highest peak, the Golden Hinde, reaches 2,195 m.","It is named after George Vancouver, who charted this coast in 1792."],"q":{"q":"Which capital city stands on Vancouver Island?","a":"Victoria","x":["Vancouver","Olympia"]}},"Q158129":{"t":"A jagged Arctic giant riddled with lakes","s":"Victoria Island is the eighth-largest island in the world, and one of the oddest-looking: its coast splays into long peninsulas and deep bays like a spilled blot of ink. Inland, the low tundra is spattered with countless lakes that stay frozen for much of the year. Only about 2,000 people live here, mostly in Cambridge Bay and Ulukhaktok.","w":"A huge, low, ragged island with spreading peninsulas, deep bays and tundra freckled with lakes, often under sea ice.","f":["At about 217,000 km², it is bigger than Great Britain.","The border between Nunavut and the Northwest Territories runs across it.","It holds the largest known island in a lake on an island in a lake on an island."],"q":{"q":"Victoria Island is larger than which of these islands?","a":"Great Britain","x":["Honshu","Sumatra"]}},"Q380307":{"t":"A wild, lake-spattered land where icebergs drift south","s":"Labrador is the wild mainland half of Newfoundland and Labrador: a rocky plateau scoured by ice-age glaciers and spattered with thousands of lakes. In the north the Torngat Mountains rise in sharp peaks straight from the sea, with small glaciers tucked into their hollows. Offshore, the cold Labrador Current carries icebergs south from Greenland in spring and early summer.","w":"Barren rocky upland dotted with countless lakes, a fjorded coast and sharp peaks in the north; icebergs offshore in spring.","f":["Mount Caubvick in the Torngats, 1,652 m, is the highest point of mainland Canada east of the Rockies.","The shimmering gemstone labradorite is named after this region.","Norse sagas mention a forested 'Markland', which many historians place here."],"q":{"q":"Which gemstone takes its name from Labrador?","a":"Labradorite","x":["Larimar","Amazonite"]}},"Q146841":{"t":"Canada's northern tip, under 800 km from the Pole","s":"Ellesmere is the northernmost part of Canada, and from above it looks as if the Ice Age never quite ended: ice caps and glaciers pour through dark mountains into fjords sealed with sea ice. Its northern tip, Cape Columbia, is less than 800 km from the North Pole. Only around 150 people live here, most of them in the Inuit hamlet of Grise Fiord.","w":"Dark mountains buried in ice caps, glaciers spilling into fjords, and frozen sea on every side.","f":["Alert, on its north-east coast, is the northernmost permanently staffed place on Earth.","Barbeau Peak, 2,616 m, is the highest point in Nunavut.","Fossils of Tiktaalik, a fish with limb-like fins, were found here in 2004."],"q":{"q":"What is the northernmost permanently staffed place on Earth?","a":"Alert","x":["Longyearbyen","Qaanaaq"]}},"Q81178":{"t":"The world's fifth-largest island, cut by fjords and ice","s":"On polar routes between Europe and western North America you may cross Baffin, Canada's biggest island. Its east coast is dramatic: mountains, ice caps and deep fjords walled with granite, often edged by sea ice or icebergs. The west is lower and flatter, dotted with lakes. Iqaluit, the capital of Nunavut, sits at the head of Frobisher Bay in the south-east.","w":"A huge island with a mountainous, fjord-slashed, ice-capped east coast and low, lake-dotted tundra to the west.","f":["At about 507,000 km², Baffin is Canada's largest island and the fifth-largest in the world.","Mount Thor's sheer granite face, about 1,250 m high, is the world's greatest vertical drop.","Norse sagas describe a stony 'Helluland', usually identified with Baffin."],"q":{"q":"Which territorial capital is on Baffin Island?","a":"Iqaluit","x":["Yellowknife","Whitehorse"]}},"Q48335":{"t":"The Rock, where Vikings landed a thousand years ago","s":"Transatlantic flights often make landfall over Newfoundland, a big, rugged island of rock, bog, forest and countless ponds. Its coast is a maze of long bays and headlands, and in spring icebergs from Greenland drift past. Near its northern tip, around the year 1000, Norse sailors built a small settlement at L'Anse aux Meadows — the only confirmed Viking site in North America outside Greenland.","w":"A big, rough-edged island of dark forest and bog, speckled with ponds, its coast a maze of deep bays.","f":["Newfoundland keeps its own time zone, half an hour ahead of Atlantic time.","In Gros Morne National Park, the Tablelands expose rock from the Earth's mantle.","Cape Spear, near St. John's, is the easternmost point of North America outside Greenland."],"q":{"q":"What is the Norse site near Newfoundland's northern tip called?","a":"L'Anse aux Meadows","x":["Port aux Basques","Trois-Rivières"]}},"Q3812":{"t":"The island shaped like a sprawling letter K","s":"Sulawesi is instantly recognisable on a map and from the air: four long, mountainous arms reach out from a central knot, so nowhere is very far from the sea. Deep gulfs lie between the arms, and inland the ridges hide clear, deep lakes. The island sits beyond the Wallace Line, and many of its animals, like the tusked babirusa pig, live nowhere else.","w":"Four long, narrow, mountainous peninsulas spreading from a centre like a starfish, with deep gulfs between them.","f":["Lake Matano, about 590 m deep, is the deepest lake in South-East Asia.","In the highlands of Tana Toraja, traditional houses carry huge upswept roofs shaped like boats.","At about 180,000 km², Sulawesi is the world's 11th-largest island."],"q":{"q":"Which letter is Sulawesi's outline often compared to?","a":"K","x":["S","Y"]}},"Q3757":{"t":"A string of volcanoes on the world's most crowded island","s":"Java is about 1,000 km long, and down its spine marches a line of volcanic cones, many of them active — on a clear day you can count them one after another. Between them, rice fields and towns fill every valley: more than 150 million people live here. In the east, look for the Tengger caldera, a grey 'sea of sand' with the smoking crater of Bromo inside.","w":"A long island with a row of volcanic cones along its length; in the east, a grey sandy caldera holding smaller cones.","f":["Semeru, 3,676 m, is Java's highest volcano and one of its most active.","At night Java glows almost from end to end with the lights of its towns and cities.","Borobudur, in central Java, is the largest Buddhist temple in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which is Java's highest volcano?","a":"Semeru","x":["Merapi","Bromo"]}},"Q124873":{"t":"Mount Apo, rare eagles and a 10-km-deep trench offshore","s":"Mindanao, the Philippines' big southern island, is a sprawling, many-armed shape of forested mountains, volcanoes and wide river plains. Near Davao, Mount Apo rises to 2,954 m, the highest point in the country. Just off the east coast the sea floor drops into the Philippine Trench, more than 10 km deep — nearly as far down as you are up.","w":"A large, many-lobed island of green mountains and plains, with a big lake on its north-central plateau and Mount Apo near Davao.","f":["Lake Lanao, on the north-central plateau, is the second-largest lake in the Philippines.","Mindanao's forests are the last stronghold of the Philippine eagle, one of the largest eagles in the world.","It is the second-largest island in the Philippines, after Luzon."],"q":{"q":"What is the highest mountain in the Philippines?","a":"Mount Apo","x":["Mayon Volcano","Mount Pulag"]}},"Q125384":{"t":"Mayon's perfect cone and a volcano inside a lake","s":"Luzon is the largest island of the Philippines: a long shape with a mountain spine in the north and a ragged tail curling to the south-east. Look south of Manila's sprawl for two lakes — broad Laguna de Bay, and Taal, with a volcanic island in its middle. Near the south-eastern tip stands Mayon, a cone so symmetrical it looks drawn with a ruler.","w":"Mountains in the north, Manila's sprawl around its bay, two big lakes to the south, and a lone perfect cone near the south-eastern tip.","f":["Mayon, 2,462 m, is famous as one of the most perfectly shaped volcanic cones on Earth.","Laguna de Bay, just south-east of Manila, is the largest lake in the Philippines.","The rice terraces of the Cordillera, in northern Luzon, are a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"Which lake near Manila has a volcanic island in its middle?","a":"Taal Lake","x":["Laguna de Bay","Lake Lanao"]}},"Q22502":{"t":"Mountains higher than Fuji on a leaf-shaped island","s":"Taiwan is shaped like a leaf, and its two sides could hardly be more different. The east is a wall of mountains dropping almost straight into the Pacific, with more than 200 peaks above 3,000 m. The west slopes gently down to a crowded plain of cities and fields that blazes with light at night. The Tropic of Cancer runs across the middle.","w":"A leaf-shaped island: steep forested mountains packed along the east, a busy flat plain along the west coast.","f":["Yushan, or Jade Mountain, reaches 3,952 m — higher than Mount Fuji.","Portuguese sailors called it Ilha Formosa, 'beautiful island'.","Sun Moon Lake, in the central mountains, is the island's largest body of water."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Formosa', an old name for Taiwan, mean?","a":"Beautiful island","x":["Green island","Mountain island"]}},"Q13987":{"t":"Where volcanoes smoke above the city streets","s":"Kyushu, the southernmost of Japan's main islands, is volcano country. In its centre, Aso's caldera is so vast that towns, farms and a railway fit inside it. In the south, Sakurajima rises from Kagoshima Bay just across the water from the city, often trailing a plume of ash. The coast is ragged with bays and islands, especially towards Nagasaki in the west.","w":"A mountainous island with a huge round caldera in its middle and a smoking volcano in a southern bay.","f":["The name Kyushu means 'nine provinces', after its old divisions.","During Japan's long isolation, the islet of Dejima in Nagasaki harbour was its only window for European traders.","Aso's caldera measures roughly 25 by 18 km."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Kyushu mean?","a":"Nine provinces","x":["Southern island","Land of fire"]}},"Q13991":{"t":"Japan's quiet island of 88 temples","s":"Shikoku is the smallest and least crowded of Japan's four main islands, mostly steep, forested mountains with towns strung along the coast. To the north lies the Seto Inland Sea, calm water studded with islands and crossed by long bridges. In the Naruto Strait, on the east side, the turning tide spins whirlpools that can reach about 20 m across.","w":"A compact mountainous island south of the island-dotted Seto Inland Sea, linked to Honshu by long bridges.","f":["Pilgrims walk a circuit of 88 Buddhist temples around the island, about 1,200 km in all.","Mount Ishizuchi, 1,982 m, is the highest peak in western Japan.","Three bridge routes link Shikoku to Honshu across the Seto Inland Sea."],"q":{"q":"How many temples are on Shikoku's famous pilgrimage?","a":"88","x":["33","108"]}},"Q35581":{"t":"Japan's snowy north, where sea ice reaches the shore","s":"Hokkaido feels different from the rest of Japan: wider fields, bigger forests, fewer people and far more snow. From above, look for round caldera lakes — Shikotsu, Tōya, Kussharo — set among volcanic hills. In late winter, drift ice from the Sea of Okhotsk packs against the north-east coast and the Shiretoko Peninsula, turning the sea white.","w":"A big island of broad farmland, forested mountains and round blue caldera lakes; white in winter, with sea ice along the north-east.","f":["The Shiretoko Peninsula in the north-east has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2005.","It covers over a fifth of Japan's land but holds only about 4% of its people.","Sapporo hosted the Winter Olympics in 1972."],"q":{"q":"Which indigenous people have their homeland on Hokkaido?","a":"The Ainu","x":["The Ryukyuans","The Nivkh"]}},"Q7792":{"t":"A long, thin island almost touching the mainland","s":"Sakhalin stretches nearly 950 km from north to south but is never more than about 160 km wide — from the air it looks like a long green-and-brown fish. Two mountain ranges run along it with a valley in between. In the north-west, the Nevelskoy Strait narrows to about 7 km from the mainland, and in winter it freezes right across.","w":"A very long, narrow island parallel to the coast, with two mountain ridges and a central valley; icy seas in winter.","f":["It is Russia's largest island, at about 76,000 km².","Anton Chekhov travelled here in 1890 and wrote the book 'Sakhalin Island'.","At its southern tip, La Pérouse Strait separates Sakhalin from Hokkaido by about 40 km."],"q":{"q":"Which writer travelled to Sakhalin in 1890 and wrote a book about it?","a":"Anton Chekhov","x":["Leo Tolstoy","Fyodor Dostoevsky"]}},"Q1462":{"t":"Emerald coves and 7,000 ancient stone towers","s":"Sardinia is the second-largest island in the Mediterranean, a rugged block of hills and plateaus with surprisingly few towns. Watch the coast: in the north-east, the Costa Smeralda's granite coves glow emerald and turquoise, while long white beaches line the south. Just to the north, across a narrow strait, Corsica rises much higher.","w":"A large, roughly rectangular island with a rugged interior; bright turquoise coves in the north-east and Corsica just to the north.","f":["Some 7,000 nuraghi — Bronze Age stone towers — are scattered across the island.","The mountain villages of Ogliastra are famous for an unusually high number of people living past 100.","The Strait of Bonifacio, only about 11 km wide, separates Sardinia from Corsica."],"q":{"q":"What are Sardinia's ancient stone towers called?","a":"Nuraghi","x":["Trulli","Talayots"]}},"Q14112":{"t":"A mountain range rising straight from the sea","s":"The French call Corsica 'the mountain in the sea', and from above you see why: a jagged granite range fills almost the whole island, streaked with snow well into spring and rising to 2,706 m. The west coast is a run of deep gulfs and red cliffs; the east is a flatter strip of lagoons. At the northern tip, the thin finger of Cap Corse points towards Genoa.","w":"A mountainous island with a finger-like peninsula pointing north; deep gulfs on the west coast, flat lagoons on the east.","f":["Napoleon Bonaparte was born in Ajaccio, on the west coast, in 1769.","The GR20 trail along Corsica's mountain spine is often called Europe's toughest long-distance hike.","The red cliffs of Scandola, on the Gulf of Porto, are a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"Which emperor was born on Corsica?","a":"Napoleon Bonaparte","x":["Charlemagne","Napoleon III"]}},"Q4951156":{"t":"A triangular island crowned by smoking Etna","s":"Sicily is the largest island in the Mediterranean, a rough triangle that earned it the ancient name Trinacria. Its landmark is unmissable: Etna, a broad, dark volcano on the east coast, snow-capped in winter and often trailing a plume. To the north, look for the Aeolian Islands, where Stromboli has been throwing up small bursts of lava for thousands of years.","w":"A triangular island; on its east coast, Etna's huge dark cone, often with a plume; small volcanic islands to the north.","f":["Etna, about 3,300 m, is the tallest active volcano in Europe, and its height changes with its eruptions.","The Strait of Messina separates Sicily from mainland Italy by only about 3 km.","Stromboli's regular glowing bursts earned it the nickname 'Lighthouse of the Mediterranean'."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient name for Sicily refers to its shape?","a":"Trinacria","x":["Hesperia","Taprobane"]}},"Q337524":{"t":"Vanuatu's biggest island, once taken for a continent","s":"Espiritu Santo is the largest island of Vanuatu: a rugged, forested west side rising to the country's highest peaks, and a lower east side of plantations, reefs and white beaches. In 1606 the navigator Pedro Fernández de Quirós landed here and believed he had found the great southern continent. Look for turquoise reef flats along its eastern shores.","w":"A large green island with a high mountain wall along its west side and flatter, reef-fringed shores to the east.","f":["Mount Tabwemasana, 1,879 m, is the highest point in Vanuatu.","Spring-fed 'blue holes' in its eastern forest are pools of startlingly clear, bright-blue water."],"q":{"q":"What did Quirós believe Espiritu Santo was in 1606?","a":"The great southern continent","x":["The coast of Asia","Part of New Guinea"]}},"Q148966":{"t":"A double chain of jungle islands and vast lagoons","s":"The Solomons form two long, roughly parallel chains of high, jungle-covered islands with a sheltered sea between them. Their coasts are fringed by reefs and mangroves, and in the west the Marovo Lagoon spreads as a maze of islets and turquoise channels. Far to the south, Rennell is a raised coral atoll with a huge lake where its lagoon used to be.","w":"Two parallel lines of mountainous, dark-green islands, fringed by reef, with a turquoise maze of lagoon in the west.","f":["The Spanish explorer Álvaro de Mendaña named the islands in 1568, hoping they held King Solomon's riches.","Lake Tegano on Rennell Island is the largest lake in the insular Pacific.","On Malaita, strings of shell money are still used for bride price and ceremonies."],"q":{"q":"Whose legendary wealth inspired the name of the Solomon Islands?","a":"King Solomon's","x":["King Midas's","The Queen of Sheba's"]}},"Q10905515":{"t":"A 1,000-km arc of coral seas from Kyushu to Taiwan","s":"South-west of Kyushu, the Ryukyus trail away in a long arc towards Taiwan, each island ringed by coral reef and water in a dozen shades of blue. Okinawa, the largest, is long and narrow, with busy towns in the south and green hills in the north. For more than four centuries these islands were the independent Ryukyu Kingdom, trading between Japan, China and South-East Asia.","w":"A long chain of small islands wrapped in bright turquoise reef, stretching south-west from Kyushu towards Taiwan.","f":["Karate grew up in Okinawa, from local martial arts blended with Chinese influences.","The Iriomote cat, one of the rarest wild cats on Earth, lives on a single island here.","From Yonaguni, the westernmost island, Taiwan is only about 110 km away."],"q":{"q":"Which martial art originated in Okinawa?","a":"Karate","x":["Judo","Aikido"]}},"Q183963":{"t":"The world's largest chain of atolls","s":"The Tuamotus scatter nearly 80 coral atolls across a vast stretch of the South Pacific — the largest group of atolls on Earth. From above, each is a thin, broken ring of palm-topped land around a lagoon of luminous turquoise, with deep blue all around. The biggest, Rangiroa, is so wide that from one shore of its lagoon you cannot see the other.","w":"Dozens of thin, broken rings of land around glowing turquoise lagoons, strewn across dark-blue ocean.","f":["Early navigators called them the 'Dangerous Archipelago', because the low atolls were so hard to spot.","Rangiroa's lagoon covers about 1,450 km², one of the largest in the world.","Much of French Polynesia's famous black-pearl farming takes place in Tuamotu lagoons."],"q":{"q":"What did early European navigators call the Tuamotus?","a":"The Dangerous Archipelago","x":["The Friendly Islands","The Isles of Thieves"]}},"Q154628":{"t":"A broken ring of volcanic islands off New Guinea","s":"The Bismarck Archipelago curves around the Bismarck Sea like a broken ring. New Britain is the big crescent, mountainous and smoking in places; New Ireland is its long, skinny neighbour; Manus and the Admiralty Islands lie to the north-west. At New Britain's north-eastern tip, look for Rabaul, a town built around a flooded volcanic caldera.","w":"A big crescent island with volcanic peaks, a long thin island to its north-east, and scattered smaller islands.","f":["The islands were named in 1884 after the German chancellor Otto von Bismarck.","New Ireland is about 350 km long but in places less than 10 km wide.","Rabaul's harbour fills a caldera whose volcanoes, such as Tavurvur, still erupt."],"q":{"q":"Who is the Bismarck Archipelago named after?","a":"Otto von Bismarck","x":["Kaiser Wilhelm I","Alfred von Tirpitz"]}},"Q201766":{"t":"Smoking volcanoes on an explorer's namesake island","s":"Bougainville is a big, mountainous island draped in rainforest, with a chain of volcanoes down its spine. Mount Balbi is the highest, at about 2,700 m, while its neighbour Bagana is one of the most active volcanoes in the region, often trailing a plume of steam and ash. The coasts are lined with reef, black-sand beaches and mangroves.","w":"A long mountainous island of dark rainforest with volcanic peaks down the middle, often with a plume drifting from one of them.","f":["It is named after the French explorer Louis-Antoine de Bougainville, who sailed past in 1768.","The bright bougainvillea flower is named after the same explorer.","Geographically, it is the largest island of the Solomon Islands chain."],"q":{"q":"Which garden plant shares its name with this island's explorer?","a":"Bougainvillea","x":["Magnolia","Fuchsia"]}},"Q192767":{"t":"Jungle peaks on one side, grassy plains on the other","s":"Guadalcanal is the largest island of the Solomon Islands, and strikingly lopsided. A wall of jungle-covered mountains, rising to more than 2,300 m, runs along its southern side, where the rainy 'Weather Coast' drops steeply to the sea. The north is gentler, with grassy plains, plantations and the national capital, Honiara.","w":"A long island with steep, cloud-capped mountains along the south coast and pale grassy plains along the north.","f":["It is named after Guadalcanal in Andalusia, the home town of a member of Mendaña's Spanish expedition of 1568.","The south coast is called the Weather Coast for its heavy rain and rough seas."],"q":{"q":"Which city on Guadalcanal is the capital of Solomon Islands?","a":"Honiara","x":["Gizo","Auki"]}},"Q83639":{"t":"Six volcanoes fused into a seahorse on the equator","s":"Isabela, the largest of the Galápagos, is made of six shield volcanoes that have grown together, and from above its outline looks like a seahorse. Broad, dark lava flows streak their flanks, and several summits hold vast calderas. The equator crosses its northern end, yet Galápagos penguins fish in the cold currents along its shores.","w":"A seahorse-shaped island of broad, dark volcanoes with huge summit craters and black lava flows running down to the sea.","f":["Wolf volcano, 1,707 m, is the highest point in the Galápagos.","The pink iguana lives on the slopes of Wolf volcano and nowhere else on Earth.","Sierra Negra's summit caldera measures about 7 by 10 km."],"q":{"q":"What shape is Isabela Island often compared to?","a":"A seahorse","x":["A crescent moon","A boot"]}},"Q42000":{"t":"A figure eight of green peaks inside a reef","s":"Tahiti is two old volcanoes joined by a narrow neck, so from above it looks like a figure eight: big, round Tahiti Nui and smaller Tahiti Iti. Their slopes are sliced into deep green valleys that run from cloud-wrapped peaks down to beaches of black sand. A reef encircles much of the island, holding a pale turquoise lagoon inside.","w":"A large round island joined to a smaller one by a thin isthmus: knife-edge green ridges, a turquoise lagoon and a white line of surf.","f":["Mount Orohena, 2,241 m, is the highest point in French Polynesia.","Captain Cook came here in 1769 to observe Venus crossing the face of the Sun.","The surf break at Teahupo'o hosted the Olympic surfing contest in 2024."],"q":{"q":"What did Captain Cook come to Tahiti to observe in 1769?","a":"The transit of Venus","x":["A total solar eclipse","Halley's Comet"]}},"Q188705":{"t":"The Valley Isle, crowned by Haleakalā's vast crater","s":"Maui is two volcanoes joined by a low isthmus, which earned it the name the Valley Isle. The big eastern one, Haleakalā, rises to 3,055 m, and its summit holds an enormous depression dotted with rust- and ochre-coloured cinder cones, easy to pick out on a clear day. The older West Maui Mountains are carved into sharp green ridges and valleys.","w":"Two lumps joined by a flat neck: the huge, often cloud-ringed dome of Haleakalā to the east, sharp green ridges to the west.","f":["Haleakalā's summit depression is about 11 km long and 800 m deep.","In winter, humpback whales gather to breed in the channels between Maui, Lānaʻi and Molokaʻi.","Haleakalā means 'house of the sun' in Hawaiian."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Haleakalā' mean in Hawaiian?","a":"House of the sun","x":["Mountain of fire","White mountain"]}},"Q208198":{"t":"Fiji's main island: lush in the east, golden in the west","s":"Viti Levu is the largest island of Fiji, roughly round, with rugged forested mountains in the middle. The weather splits it in two: the south-east, around Suva, catches the trade-wind rain and stays dark green, while the drier north-west is a patchwork of sugar-cane fields and pale grassland. Coral reefs and lagoons fringe much of the coast.","w":"A roundish, mountainous island: dark rainforest in the south-east, paler golden cane country in the north-west, reefs along the shore.","f":["Mount Tomanivi, 1,324 m, is the highest point in Fiji.","The Sigatoka Sand Dunes on the south coast became Fiji's first national park.","Most international flights to Fiji land at Nadi, on the island's dry western side."],"q":{"q":"Which city on Viti Levu is Fiji's capital?","a":"Suva","x":["Nadi","Lautoka"]}},"Q205695":{"t":"Home of the dodo, and an 'underwater waterfall'","s":"Three volcanic islands lie in a line east of Madagascar: Réunion, Mauritius and little Rodrigues. Réunion is the tallest and wildest, its highest peak topping 3,000 m, with one of the world's most active volcanoes on its south-east side. Mauritius is lower and ringed by a turquoise lagoon; off its south-west corner, sand pouring down an undersea slope looks from the air like a waterfall.","w":"Réunion's high, round, cloud-capped peaks; lower Mauritius to the north-east, wrapped in a wide turquoise lagoon.","f":["The islands are named after the Portuguese navigator Pedro Mascarenhas.","The dodo, a flightless pigeon, lived only on Mauritius and was extinct by the late 1600s.","Piton des Neiges on Réunion, 3,069 m, is the highest point in the Indian Ocean."],"q":{"q":"On which island did the dodo live?","a":"Mauritius","x":["Réunion","Madagascar"]}},"Q192281":{"t":"The last large land to be put on the world map","s":"Severnaya Zemlya was the last sizeable land on Earth to be discovered: a Russian expedition sighted it in 1913, and it was not properly mapped until 1930–32. From above it is a cluster of big islands where smooth ice caps cover about half the land, their edges calving into the sea, and the rest is bare brown polar desert. Sea ice locks it in for most of the year.","w":"Big islands under smooth white ice domes, bare brown ground at their edges and frozen straits between, north of the Taymyr Peninsula.","f":["The Academy of Sciences Ice Cap on Komsomolets Island is the largest ice cap in Russia.","Georgy Ushakov and Nikolay Urvantsev mapped the archipelago by dog sled in 1930–32.","Cape Arktichesky, its northern tip, is a common starting point for North Pole expeditions."],"q":{"q":"In which year was Severnaya Zemlya discovered?","a":"1913","x":["1878","1596"]}},"Q1249802":{"t":"Windswept, treeless islands full of penguins","s":"Two main islands and hundreds of small ones lie in the South Atlantic east of Patagonia: low, treeless and brown-green under a big sky. The coast is torn into long inlets and sounds. Look for pale streaks on the hillsides — these are stone runs, rivers of boulders left by ice-age frost. Along the shores live huge colonies of penguins and albatrosses.","w":"Two large, low, treeless islands with deeply indented coasts and pale streaks of boulder fields on the hills.","f":["Five species of penguin breed here, including king, gentoo and rockhopper penguins.","About 70% of the world's black-browed albatrosses nest on these islands.","There are no native trees; the land is covered in grass, heath and peat."],"q":{"q":"What are the Falklands' 'stone runs'?","a":"Rivers of boulders","x":["Ancient stone walls","Old lava flows"]}},"Q25231":{"t":"Pointed mountains, flowing glaciers and a vault of seeds","s":"Svalbard is where the Arctic looks most dramatic from the air: dark, sharp-crested mountains, glaciers flowing between them like frozen rivers, and ice fronts calving into fjords. Around 60% of the land lies under ice. Willem Barentsz named the main island Spitsbergen — 'pointed mountains' — when he sighted it in 1596.","w":"Jagged dark peaks separated by broad white glaciers, fjords ending in ice cliffs, and sea ice to the north and east.","f":["Longyearbyen is the northernmost town on Earth with more than 1,000 inhabitants.","The Global Seed Vault, dug into a mountain near Longyearbyen, stores seeds from all over the world.","The midnight sun shines here from about late April to late August."],"q":{"q":"What does the name 'Spitsbergen' mean?","a":"Pointed mountains","x":["White coast","Ice fjords"]}},"Q185018":{"t":"Europe's northernmost islands, locked in pack ice","s":"Franz Josef Land is a scatter of nearly 200 islands high in the Arctic, most of them buried under ice caps, with dark basalt cliffs showing at their edges. From above the ice domes look smooth and rounded, and the channels between them stay frozen for much of the year. Cape Fligely on Rudolf Island is the northernmost point of Europe, about 900 km from the Pole.","w":"Dozens of islands under smooth, rounded ice caps, with dark cliffs at their edges and frozen channels between them.","f":["An Austro-Hungarian expedition discovered the islands in 1873 and named them after Emperor Franz Joseph.","Fridtjof Nansen and Hjalmar Johansen spent the winter of 1895–96 here in a stone hut.","Champ Island is famous for its strange, almost perfectly round stone balls."],"q":{"q":"Which emperor gave his name to Franz Josef Land?","a":"Franz Joseph I of Austria","x":["Wilhelm II of Germany","Alexander II of Russia"]}},"Q80413":{"t":"A 1,000-km wall of ice and rock between two seas","s":"Novaya Zemlya is a long, curved barrier, nearly 1,000 km from end to end, dividing the Barents Sea from the Kara Sea — the Ural Mountains carried on out into the Arctic. Its two main islands are separated by the narrow Matochkin Shar strait. The southern one is tundra and bare hills; the northern one is largely buried under a vast ice cap whose glaciers break off into the sea.","w":"A very long, narrow, curving pair of islands: brown tundra in the south, a continuous white ice cap in the north.","f":["The northern island's ice cap, about 20,000 km², is one of the largest in Europe.","Willem Barentsz and his crew spent the winter of 1596–97 near its northern tip, in a hut of driftwood.","Matochkin Shar, the strait between the two main islands, is about 100 km long."],"q":{"q":"Which two seas does Novaya Zemlya separate?","a":"The Barents and Kara seas","x":["The Kara and Laptev seas","The Barents and White seas"]}},"Q3803":{"t":"Volcano after volcano, from Bali to Timor","s":"East of Java the islands grow smaller and drier — Bali, Lombok, Sumbawa, Flores and on towards Timor — and nearly each is crowned by volcanoes. Some peaks hold striking crater lakes: Rinjani on Lombok cradles a blue crescent of water with a young cone rising inside it. Farther east, green gives way to brown savanna, where Komodo dragons still roam a few islands.","w":"A west–east chain of volcanic islands, greener in the west and browner in the east; look for crater lakes and calderas.","f":["On Flores, the three crater lakes of Kelimutu each have a different colour, and the colours shift over time.","Tambora, on Sumbawa, was left with a caldera about 6 km wide by its great eruption of 1815.","Remains of Homo floresiensis, a tiny extinct human nicknamed the 'hobbit', were found on Flores in 2003."],"q":{"q":"Which Lesser Sunda island has three crater lakes of different colours?","a":"Flores","x":["Lombok","Sumba"]}},"Q215664":{"t":"Hundreds of reefs scattered across the South China Sea","s":"Across the southern South China Sea lie well over a hundred reefs, atolls and sandy cays, many of them awash at high tide. From above they appear as faint turquoise smudges and pale rings on deep blue, with only a few specks of real land among them. Old British charts marked this whole area with a blunt warning: 'Dangerous Ground'.","w":"Faint turquoise rings and patches of reef strewn across dark-blue sea, with very little dry land.","f":["The islands are named after Richard Spratly, a British whaling captain who sighted one of them in 1843.","All their natural dry land put together covers only about 2 km²."],"q":{"q":"How did old British charts label the Spratly area?","a":"Dangerous Ground","x":["Treacherous Shoals","Coral Maze"]}},"Q201012":{"t":"Arctic islands made of ice, sand and mammoth bones","s":"The New Siberian Islands lie low and flat between the Laptev and East Siberian seas, often impossible to tell apart from the sea ice around them. In summer they show as brown tundra and grey sand, with lakes and crumbling cliffs of ancient ground ice. The frozen soil holds so many mammoth bones and tusks that ivory hunters have come here for more than two centuries.","w":"Low, flat, brown and grey islands with many lakes, blending into the surrounding sea ice for much of the year.","f":["Bunge Land, between Kotelny and Faddeyevsky islands, is a low sandy plain that floods during storms.","In 1811 Yakov Sannikov reported a mysterious land to the north; 'Sannikov Land' was never found.","Kotelny is the largest island of the group."],"q":{"q":"Which phantom land was reported north of the New Siberian Islands?","a":"Sannikov Land","x":["Crocker Land","Bunge Land"]}},"Q128011":{"t":"The Caribbean's big islands, from Cuba to Puerto Rico","s":"The Greater Antilles are the heavyweights of the Caribbean: Cuba, Hispaniola, Jamaica and Puerto Rico, lined up from west to east. From above they are large, green and mountainous, edged by pale sand and turquoise shallows, while the Atlantic to the north falls away to deep, dark blue. Cuba, the largest, stretches some 1,200 km from end to end.","w":"A west–east line of big green islands: long, narrow Cuba, then mountainous Hispaniola, with Jamaica to the south and Puerto Rico last.","f":["Cuba is the largest island in the Caribbean, at about 105,000 km².","North of Puerto Rico, the Puerto Rico Trench plunges to about 8,400 m, the deepest point of the Atlantic.","Jamaica's Blue Mountains, rising to about 2,250 m, gave their name to a famous coffee."],"q":{"q":"Which is the largest island in the Caribbean?","a":"Cuba","x":["Hispaniola","Jamaica"]}},"Q21713615":{"t":"Turquoise banks so bright they seem lit from below","s":"In the Bahamas the water steals the show. The islands sit on vast limestone banks only a few metres deep, which glow an almost unreal turquoise, rippled with sand patterns. Cutting into the Great Bahama Bank is the Tongue of the Ocean, a deep trench whose ink-blue water ends abruptly against the pale shallows — one of the sharpest colour lines you will see from a plane.","w":"Huge areas of glowing pale-turquoise shallows with sandy ripples, low islands along their edges, and sharp lines where deep blue begins.","f":["Columbus made his first landfall in the Americas here, in October 1492.","The country counts about 700 islands and more than 2,000 small cays.","Dean's Blue Hole, off Long Island, drops about 200 m, one of the deepest known."],"q":{"q":"In which year did Columbus make his first landfall in the Bahamas?","a":"1492","x":["1498","1502"]}},"Q93259":{"t":"A curved necklace of islands closing off the Caribbean","s":"The Lesser Antilles curve for some 800 km from the Virgin Islands down to Grenada, dividing the Caribbean from the Atlantic. Most are steep, green volcanic islands, each a peak with a skirt of reef; Guadeloupe is shaped like a butterfly. On a clear day, look on Saint Lucia's west coast for the Pitons, two sharp volcanic spires standing side by side at the water's edge.","w":"A curving chain of small, steep, green volcanic islands running north to south, with the open Atlantic to the east.","f":["The inner islands are volcanic; outer ones such as Antigua and Barbados are flatter and made of limestone.","Kick 'em Jenny, north of Grenada, is an active volcano still growing beneath the sea.","The Pitons of Saint Lucia are a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"Which island is famous for two volcanic spires called the Pitons?","a":"Saint Lucia","x":["Dominica","Grenada"]}},"Q59771":{"t":"A crescent of rainforest and steep volcanic cones","s":"New Britain is a long, curving crescent about 500 km from end to end, the largest island of the Bismarck Archipelago. Along its north coast stands a line of volcanoes; the most striking is Ulawun, a steep, symmetrical cone that is also one of the most active in Papua New Guinea. Inland, the limestone Nakanai Mountains hide giant sinkholes and underground rivers.","w":"A long crescent-shaped island of dark rainforest with volcanic cones along its north coast, one of them tall and symmetrical.","f":["Ulawun, about 2,300 m, is one of the Decade Volcanoes chosen for special study.","It is Papua New Guinea's largest island after New Guinea itself.","Some sinkholes in the Nakanai Mountains are hundreds of metres deep."],"q":{"q":"Which active volcano stands on New Britain's north coast?","a":"Ulawun","x":["Bagana","Manam"]}},"Q17044696":{"t":"The country that gave the world the word 'atoll'","s":"The Maldives look like turquoise beads scattered on dark-blue velvet: 26 atolls in a double chain some 870 km long, each a ring of reefs and tiny islands around a pale lagoon. Many islands are just a strip of white sand and palms holding a single resort or village. The capital, Malé, fills its small island edge to edge with buildings.","w":"Rings of pale-turquoise reef and dots of white sand on deep-blue ocean, arranged in a long north–south double chain.","f":["'Atoll' comes from 'atholhu', a word in Dhivehi, the Maldivian language.","The Maldives is the world's lowest-lying country: its natural ground rises only about 2.4 m at most.","The country has about 1,200 coral islands, fewer than 200 of them inhabited."],"q":{"q":"Which English word comes from the Maldivian language?","a":"Atoll","x":["Lagoon","Reef"]}},"Q25263":{"t":"Green volcanic islands in the middle of the Atlantic","s":"Nine volcanic islands lie scattered across the mid-Atlantic, about 1,500 km west of Lisbon. From above they are intensely green, patched with small fields, with lakes lying in old craters. The unmistakable one is Pico: a near-perfect cone 2,351 m high, Portugal's tallest mountain, whose summit often pokes out above a sea of cloud.","w":"Small green islands in open ocean; a single tall dark cone on Pico, and round crater lakes on São Miguel.","f":["The Azores sit where the Eurasian, North American and African plates meet.","In the Sete Cidades caldera on São Miguel, two joined lakes show different colours — one blue, one green.","The Azores High, a zone of high pressure named after the islands, shapes much of Europe's summer weather."],"q":{"q":"What is the highest mountain in Portugal?","a":"Pico","x":["Torre, Serra da Estrela","Pico Ruivo"]}},"Q6980542":{"t":"An island you can barely tell from the ice around it","s":"Along the coast of West Antarctica, the Getz Ice Shelf holds a string of islands locked into its floating ice. Wright Island, about 65 km long, is one of them. Completely buried under ice, it shows only as a broad, gentle swell above the flat shelf, easiest to spot when a low sun casts shadows. To the north lies the Amundsen Sea, often choked with sea ice.","w":"A long, smooth white swell rising slightly above the flat ice shelf, with ice-covered sea to the north.","f":["It was first mapped from air photos taken during the US Navy's Operation Highjump in January 1947.","It is named after Admiral Jerauld Wright, in overall command of Operation Deep Freeze in 1957–58.","The Getz Ice Shelf, which holds the island, stretches for more than 500 km along the coast."],"q":{"q":"Which ice shelf holds Wright Island?","a":"The Getz Ice Shelf","x":["The Ross Ice Shelf","The Ronne Ice Shelf"]}},"Q1543171":{"t":"An ice island found by an icebreaker in 1962","s":"Grant Island is one of several islands frozen into the Getz Ice Shelf off Marie Byrd Land, in West Antarctica. About 37 km long and wholly ice-covered, it is hemmed in by the shelf on every side except the north, where a strait opens towards the sea. From above it is a smooth white hump, its outline given away mainly by shading along its slopes.","w":"A smooth white hump rising from the flat ice shelf, with a strait of open water or sea ice along its northern side.","f":["Sailors aboard the icebreaker USS Glacier discovered and charted it on 4 February 1962.","It is named after Commander E. G. Grant, the ship's captain at the time.","The smaller Shepard Island lies only about 9 km to the west."],"q":{"q":"Which ship's crew discovered Grant Island in 1962?","a":"USS Glacier","x":["USS Burton Island","HMS Endurance"]}},"Q6272638":{"t":"A lonely ice island off the Ruppert Coast","s":"On the Ruppert Coast of Marie Byrd Land, far from any regular flight path, the Nickerson Ice Shelf holds a handful of ice-covered islands. Newman Island is about 28 km long and completely buried. From above it looks less like an island than a low, rounded bulge in the ice, with glaciers flowing down from the mainland to the south.","w":"A low, rounded white bulge within a flat ice shelf, with glaciers descending from the land to the south.","f":["It was mapped from US surveys and Navy air photographs taken between 1959 and 1965.","It is named after Commander J. F. Newman of the US Navy, who served on Operation Deep Freeze in 1966."],"q":{"q":"Which ice shelf holds Newman Island?","a":"The Nickerson Ice Shelf","x":["The Getz Ice Shelf","The Ross Ice Shelf"]}},"Q559842":{"t":"An ice-bound island in the shadow of Mount Siple","s":"Dean Island lies in the middle of the Getz Ice Shelf, off the coast of Marie Byrd Land, between Grant Island and Siple Island. It is about 37 km long and 19 km wide, and so thoroughly covered in ice that from above it merges almost seamlessly with the shelf. The landmark nearby is Mount Siple, a huge volcanic dome on the neighbouring island, rising above 3,000 m.","w":"A broad, low white rise within the flat ice shelf; to the north-east, the tall snowy dome of Mount Siple.","f":["It is one of several islands embedded in the Getz Ice Shelf, which runs for more than 500 km along the coast.","Mount Siple, on neighbouring Siple Island, is a shield volcano about 3,100 m high."],"q":{"q":"Which two islands does Dean Island lie between?","a":"Grant and Siple","x":["Shepard and Grant","Carney and Wright"]}},"Q33788":{"t":"A long mountain island inside a vast turquoise lagoon","s":"Grande Terre, New Caledonia's main island, is about 400 km long and only some 50 km wide: a mountainous spine marked by red scars where nickel has been mined. Around it spreads one of the largest lagoons on Earth, bright turquoise and closed off by a barrier reef that traces a thin white line of surf far offshore.","w":"A long, narrow, mountainous island with reddish bare patches, inside a wide turquoise lagoon edged by a distant line of surf.","f":["Captain Cook named it in 1774 because its hills reminded him of Scotland.","Its lagoons and reefs have been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2008.","The kagu, a grey, nearly flightless bird that barks like a dog, lives only here."],"q":{"q":"Which metal is mined in New Caledonia's red hills?","a":"Nickel","x":["Copper","Gold"]}},"Q712":{"t":"More than 300 islands astride the 180th meridian","s":"Fiji is a scatter of more than 300 islands, from two big, mountainous ones to countless specks ringed by reef. From above the colours are the show: deep-blue ocean, turquoise lagoons and white lines of surf where the reefs break the swell. The 180th meridian passes through the islands, but the date line bends around them so the whole country keeps the same day.","w":"Two large green islands and a scatter of small ones, all fringed by turquoise lagoons and white lines of breaking surf.","f":["Only about a third of the islands are inhabited.","The Great Sea Reef off Vanua Levu is one of the longest barrier reefs in the world.","Viti Levu and Vanua Levu together hold most of the land and nearly all of the people."],"q":{"q":"Which is Fiji's largest island?","a":"Viti Levu","x":["Vanua Levu","Taveuni"]}},"ne-rg-1730072401":{"t":"The Friendly Islands, beside one of the deepest trenches","s":"Tonga is a double line of islands running north to south. On the east lie low coral islands such as flat Tongatapu, ringed by reef; on the west runs a string of volcanoes, some of them little more than peaks breaking the surface. Captain Cook called them the Friendly Islands. East of the chain, the sea floor plunges into the Tonga Trench, one of the deepest places on Earth.","w":"Flat, reef-ringed coral islands in the east, a parallel line of small volcanic islands to the west, and deep ocean beyond.","f":["The Tonga Trench reaches about 10,800 m at the Horizon Deep, the second-deepest point in the oceans.","Tonga is the only kingdom in the Pacific, ruled by its own monarchs right through the colonial era.","In the Vava'u group, humpback whales come to give birth between July and October."],"q":{"q":"What did Captain Cook call Tonga?","a":"The Friendly Islands","x":["The Society Islands","The Navigator Islands"]}},"Q700048":{"t":"The 'sunrise' chain, home of the Marshalls' capital","s":"Ratak means 'sunrise', and this is the eastern of the Marshall Islands' two chains: a line of coral atolls stretching for several hundred kilometres across the ocean. From above each is a narrow necklace of islets around a turquoise lagoon. Majuro, the national capital, lies near the southern end — a thin, crowded ribbon of land curling around a lagoon many kilometres across.","w":"A line of thin rings of land around turquoise lagoons, strung north to south across deep-blue ocean.","f":["On Majuro, the strip of inhabited land is often only a few hundred metres wide.","Bokak, the northernmost atoll, is dry and uninhabited, a haven for seabirds."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Ratak' mean?","a":"Sunrise","x":["Sunset","Big lagoon"]}},"Q697840":{"t":"The 'sunset' chain and its giant lagoons","s":"Ralik means 'sunset', and this is the western of the Marshall Islands' two parallel chains of atolls. Its best-known member is Kwajalein, whose nearly 100 islets enclose one of the largest lagoons in the world, over 2,000 km² of calm turquoise water. From above, the islets look like a broken bracelet dropped on the ocean.","w":"Big rings of tiny islets around vast turquoise lagoons, scattered along a line west of the Ratak chain.","f":["Kwajalein's lagoon covers about 2,170 km².","The bikini swimsuit, launched in 1946, took its name from Bikini Atoll in this chain."],"q":{"q":"Which garment is named after an atoll in the Ralik Chain?","a":"The bikini","x":["The sarong","The muumuu"]}},"Q11826289":{"t":"Rarotonga's green peaks and Aitutaki's glowing lagoon","s":"The southern Cooks hold most of the country's people and its most famous views. Rarotonga is a high volcanic island of jagged green peaks, ringed by a reef and by a coastal road only about 32 km long. To the north, Aitutaki's lagoon is a vast triangle of luminous turquoise dotted with tiny islets; to the south-east, Mangaia is rimmed by cliffs of ancient raised coral.","w":"Rarotonga: a small round island of sharp green peaks inside a reef; to the north, Aitutaki's huge triangular turquoise lagoon.","f":["Mangaia, formed about 18 million years ago, is one of the oldest islands in the Pacific.","Cook Islanders are also citizens of New Zealand."],"q":{"q":"Which southern Cook island is famous for its turquoise lagoon?","a":"Aitutaki","x":["Mangaia","Atiu"]}},"Q244978":{"t":"Perfumed volcanic islands between Africa and Madagascar","s":"The Comoros lie at the northern end of the Mozambique Channel: four volcanic islands that get older from west to east. The biggest, Grande Comore, is dominated by Karthala, a broad active volcano with a huge crater at its summit and dark lava flows on its flanks. At the eastern end Mayotte, the oldest, is wrapped in a wide lagoon behind a barrier reef.","w":"Four dark-green volcanic islands in a row: the western one topped by a vast crater, the eastern one inside a turquoise lagoon.","f":["Karthala, about 2,360 m, has erupted more than 20 times since the 19th century.","Coelacanths, fish once thought to have died out with the dinosaurs, live in the deep water off these islands.","The islands are one of the world's main sources of ylang-ylang oil, used in perfumes."],"q":{"q":"Which 'living fossil' fish lives in deep water off the Comoros?","a":"The coelacanth","x":["The lungfish","The sturgeon"]}},"ne-rg-1730073253":{"t":"800 forested islands where sea nomads still sail","s":"Off Myanmar's southern coast some 800 islands scatter across the Andaman Sea, most of them hilly, cloaked in forest and edged with white beaches and mangroves. There are few towns and little development, so from above you see mostly green islands, pale sand and clear water in every shade of blue. The Moken, sea nomads who spend much of the year in boats, have lived among them for centuries.","w":"Hundreds of steep, forested islands with white beaches, scattered along the coast of southern Myanmar.","f":["The archipelago was largely closed to foreign visitors until the late 1990s.","Lampi Island lies at the heart of Myanmar's only marine national park."],"q":{"q":"What are the Moken of the Mergui Archipelago known as?","a":"Sea nomads","x":["Mountain herders","Salt traders"]}},"Q5765":{"t":"Mountains, coves and water so clear the seabed shows","s":"The Balearics lie off eastern Spain: big Mallorca in the middle, gentler Menorca to the north-east, Ibiza and tiny Formentera to the south-west. Mallorca's north-west coast is a wall of limestone mountains, the Serra de Tramuntana, above deep-blue sea. Around Formentera the water is so clear that underwater meadows of seagrass show as dark patches in the turquoise shallows.","w":"A large island with a mountain ridge along its north-west coast, a flatter one to the north-east, two more to the south-west.","f":["Mallorca's Serra de Tramuntana is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","A seagrass meadow off Formentera may be one of the oldest living things on Earth, possibly tens of thousands of years old.","The whole of Menorca is a UNESCO biosphere reserve."],"q":{"q":"Which is the largest of the Balearic Islands?","a":"Mallorca","x":["Menorca","Ibiza"]}},"ne-rg-1730073309":{"t":"Bare Arctic islands along the Northwest Passage","s":"The Parry Islands form a broad band of Arctic islands along the northern side of the Northwest Passage, from Cornwallis in the east to Prince Patrick in the west. From above they are brown and grey, flat-topped and bare, scored by dry valleys and fringed with sea ice for most of the year. In 1819 William Parry's ships sailed farther west through these channels than anyone before.","w":"Big, bare, brown-grey islands with flat uplands and dry valleys, surrounded by sea ice most of the year.","f":["Parry's ships spent the winter of 1819–20 frozen in at Winter Harbour, on Melville Island.","By sailing past 110° west, Parry's crews won a £5,000 prize offered by the British Parliament.","Resolute, on Cornwallis Island, is one of the northernmost communities in Canada."],"q":{"q":"Who are the Parry Islands named after?","a":"William Edward Parry","x":["John Franklin","John Ross"]}},"Q741243":{"t":"An island the size of Denmark, with no sea in sight","s":"Berkner Island is an island mostly on the map: it is surrounded not by open water but by the floating Filchner–Ronne Ice Shelf, and buried under ice itself. From above it appears as a vast, gentle dome rising from the flat shelf, some 320 km long and less than a kilometre high. It splits the shelf in two, with the Ronne to the west and the Filchner to the east.","w":"A huge, gently rounded ice dome rising above the perfectly flat ice shelf that surrounds it on every side.","f":["At about 44,000 km², it is roughly the size of Denmark.","It was discovered in 1957–58 by a US party from Ellsworth Station, led by Finn Ronne.","It is named after the physicist Lloyd Berkner, a radio engineer on Byrd's 1928–30 Antarctic expedition."],"q":{"q":"Which two ice shelves does Berkner Island separate?","a":"Ronne and Filchner","x":["Ross and Ronne","Filchner and Brunt"]}},"Q65":{"t":"A city poured into a basin between peaks and the Pacific","s":"Los Angeles doesn't so much end as run out of room: its sprawl fills a broad basin from the Pacific shore to the foot of the San Gabriel Mountains, then spills into the valleys beyond. In winter those peaks can wear snow above a city of palm trees. It all began in 1781 as a small Spanish farming pueblo.","w":"A vast grey-beige sprawl between mountains and Santa Monica Bay; the long harbour breakwaters of Long Beach to the south.","f":["The Los Angeles River runs mostly in a concrete channel, lined from the 1930s onward to tame its floods.","The neighbouring ports of Los Angeles and Long Beach together form the busiest container port complex in the US.","The highest of the San Gabriels, Mount San Antonio (about 3,070 m), is known locally as Mount Baldy."],"q":{"q":"Which mountain range walls in Los Angeles to the north?","a":"The San Gabriel Mountains","x":["The Sierra Nevada","The Cascade Range"]}},"Q61":{"t":"A capital drawn on paper first, then built on the Potomac","s":"Washington was planned before it existed: in 1791 Pierre L'Enfant laid broad diagonal avenues over a street grid on farmland beside the Potomac. The district began as a square ten miles a side, set on its corner like a diamond. Virginia took back its share in 1846, so today the river bites off the south-west corner.","w":"Where the Anacostia meets the broad Potomac; a low city without a skyscraper cluster, Reagan National airport right on the riverbank.","f":["Forty stone markers were set along the original diamond-shaped boundary in 1791–92; many still stand.","Congress first met in Washington in 1800.","A law of 1910 caps building heights, which is why the city looks so strikingly low."],"q":{"q":"Which river forms Washington's south-western edge?","a":"The Potomac","x":["The Delaware","The Hudson"]}},"Q60":{"t":"Look for the green rectangle in the middle of the island","s":"Find Manhattan — a long, thin island between the Hudson and the East River — and in its middle a neat green rectangle: Central Park, about four kilometres long. Around it the street grid laid out in 1811 runs block after block up the island. The Dutch called this port New Amsterdam until the English took it in 1664 and renamed it after the Duke of York.","w":"A narrow island pointing south into a wide harbour, a green rectangle at its heart, long bridges fanning out to the east.","f":["Central Park measures about 4 km by 0.8 km — big enough to pick out from cruise on a clear day.","The Verrazzano-Narrows Bridge at the harbour mouth had the world's longest suspension span when it opened in 1964.","Manhattan's street grid was fixed by a plan of 1811, long before most of the island was built up."],"q":{"q":"What was New York called before 1664?","a":"New Amsterdam","x":["New Rotterdam","Fort Orange"]}},"Q1489":{"t":"A megacity on a lake that isn't there any more","s":"The grey-brown sea of roofs below fills an old lake bed 2,240 m above sea level. The Aztecs founded Tenochtitlan on an island in Lake Texcoco around 1325; the Spanish later drained most of the water. The clay beneath is still settling, and parts of the city sink every year. On a clear day, look south-east for two snow-capped volcanoes.","w":"A vast city filling a flat high valley ringed by mountains; Popocatépetl and Iztaccíhuatl rise to the south-east.","f":["Popocatépetl, about 5,400 m high, is an active volcano and often trails a plume of steam.","In places the city sinks by more than 20 cm a year as the old lake clay dries out.","Iztaccíhuatl means 'white woman' in Nahuatl, and its long ridge is said to look like a sleeping figure."],"q":{"q":"What did Mexico City's centre stand on in Aztec times?","a":"An island in a lake","x":["A volcanic crater","A river delta"]}},"Q2841":{"t":"A capital at 2,640 m, pressed against a green mountain wall","s":"Bogotá lies on a high, flat savanna in the Andes, about 2,640 m up, with a steep ridge of hills along its eastern edge. The city fills the plain westward from that wall. Look around it for glinting white patches: greenhouses of the flower farms that make Colombia one of the world's great exporters of cut flowers.","w":"A large city on a flat green plateau, hard against a dark ridge to the east; white greenhouse roofs scattered across the fields.","f":["The Spanish conquistador Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada founded the city in 1538.","The legend of El Dorado grew from a Muisca ritual at Lake Guatavita, about 50 km north-east of the city.","Monserrate, the hill above the old town, rises to about 3,150 m."],"q":{"q":"At which lake did the El Dorado legend begin?","a":"Lake Guatavita","x":["Lake Titicaca","Lake Tota"]}},"Q2887":{"t":"A city in a bowl, with the Andes rising straight behind","s":"Santiago fills a broad valley between the low Coastal Range and the Andes, which climb to snowy peaks over 5,000 m just east of the suburbs. In winter a haze often pools in the bowl while the summits shine above it. Pedro de Valdivia founded the city in 1541 beside a rocky hill on the Mapocho River.","w":"A large city on a flat valley floor at the foot of the Andes' snowy wall; the thin line of the Mapocho River across it.","f":["Aconcagua, at 6,961 m the highest peak outside Asia, stands about 100 km north-east of the city.","Cerro Santa Lucía, the hill where the city was founded, is now a park in the very centre.","The border with Argentina follows the crest of the Andes less than 100 km from the city."],"q":{"q":"Who founded Santiago in 1541?","a":"Pedro de Valdivia","x":["Francisco Pizarro","Diego de Almagro"]}},"Q8678":{"t":"Green granite domes rising straight out of the sea","s":"Few cities are so tangled up with rock: steep, forested granite domes rise between the neighbourhoods and straight out of Guanabara Bay. Portuguese sailors arrived in January 1502 and took the bay for a river mouth — hence 'River of January'. From 1808 to 1821 the Portuguese court lived here, a European monarchy ruling its empire from the Americas.","w":"A wide bay with a narrow mouth, green domes of rock among the buildings, and long pale beaches facing the open Atlantic.","f":["The Rio–Niterói Bridge crosses Guanabara Bay for about 13 km; from cruise it's a fine line over the water.","Much of the Tijuca Forest above the city was replanted by hand from the 1860s on old coffee land.","Sugarloaf Mountain rises about 396 m straight from the water at the mouth of the bay."],"q":{"q":"Which bay does the Rio–Niterói Bridge cross?","a":"Guanabara Bay","x":["All Saints Bay","Paranaguá Bay"]}},"Q174":{"t":"The Southern Hemisphere's biggest city, up on a plateau","s":"From the window São Paulo looks endless: grey and white rooftops to the horizon, stitched together by rivers in straight concrete channels. It sits on a plateau about 760 m up, only some 70 km from the Atlantic, behind the steep forested wall of the Serra do Mar. Jesuit missionaries founded it as a small mission in 1554.","w":"A boundless grey city; large dark reservoirs on its southern edge, the green escarpment and the coast beyond to the south-east.","f":["The Tietê River rises near the coast yet flows inland, over 1,000 km, to join the Paraná.","The Billings and Guarapiranga reservoirs on the southern edge are easy to spot from cruise.","The coffee trade of the 1800s turned a modest hill town into Brazil's economic engine."],"q":{"q":"Which way does the Tietê River flow from São Paulo?","a":"Inland, towards the Paraná","x":["East, to the Atlantic at Santos","North, to the Amazon"]}},"Q62":{"t":"A hilly peninsula tip where fog pours through the Gate","s":"San Francisco fills the tip of a peninsula, with the Pacific on one side and a great bay on the other, joined by the narrow Golden Gate. On summer afternoons you may see fog pouring through that gap like a slow white river. The gold rush of 1849 turned a village of about a thousand people into a boom town within a couple of years.","w":"A peninsula tip between ocean and bay; thin bridge lines across the water; bright red and green salt ponds far to the south.","f":["The salt ponds of the south bay glow red, orange and green, coloured by salt-loving microorganisms.","The Golden Gate strait was named in 1846, almost a century before the famous bridge was built.","Mission San Francisco de Asís, the city's Spanish seed, was founded in 1776."],"q":{"q":"What colours the salt ponds of south San Francisco Bay?","a":"Salt-loving microorganisms","x":["Iron-rich clay","Copper minerals"]}},"Q16554":{"t":"Where the Great Plains run into the Rockies","s":"Denver marks the line where the flat country stops: to the east the plains roll away for a thousand kilometres, to the west the Front Range rises in a wall of peaks over 4,000 m. The city grew from a gold-rush camp of 1858 and sits about a mile above sea level — hence its nickname, the Mile High City.","w":"A city on flat tan plains at the very foot of the Rockies; its huge airport stands alone far out to the north-east.","f":["Denver International is the largest airport in North America by land area, about 135 km².","The city was named after James W. Denver, governor of Kansas Territory, which then included the site.","Longs Peak, about 4,350 m high, is the prominent summit on the north-western skyline."],"q":{"q":"What brought the first settlers to Denver in 1858?","a":"A gold rush","x":["A new railway line","A silver strike"]}},"Q16555":{"t":"Bayous, refineries and a ship channel on a flat plain","s":"Houston spreads across a coastal plain so flat that its slow brown bayous wander in loops through the suburbs. Look south-east for the Ship Channel, a dredged waterway of about 80 km lined with refineries and tanks, running out to Galveston Bay. Founded in 1836, the city was named after Sam Houston, hero of Texan independence.","w":"Endless flat sprawl ringed by freeway loops; to the south-east, a waterway lined with industry leading to a wide bay.","f":["Houston is famous as the largest US city without a formal zoning code.","NASA's Mission Control for crewed spaceflight is in the city's south-eastern suburbs.","At night the refineries along the Ship Channel glitter as a long, dense band of light."],"q":{"q":"Who is Houston named after?","a":"Sam Houston","x":["Stephen F. Austin","Davy Crockett"]}},"Q8652":{"t":"A thin city between the Everglades and turquoise water","s":"Miami is a long, narrow city pinned between two very different edges: the pale green marshes of the Everglades to the west and the turquoise shallows of Biscayne Bay to the east. Beyond the bay runs the thin barrier island of Miami Beach. The city was incorporated only in 1896, just after Henry Flagler's railway arrived.","w":"A strip of city along the coast; a straight line where streets stop and marsh begins; a bright ribbon of island offshore.","f":["Much of Miami lies only a couple of metres above sea level, on porous limestone.","Miami Beach was largely mangrove swamp before it was cleared and built up in the 1910s and 1920s.","The chain of small Venetian Islands across Biscayne Bay is largely man-made."],"q":{"q":"Which wetland lies directly west of Miami?","a":"The Everglades","x":["The Okefenokee Swamp","The Atchafalaya Basin"]}},"Q23556":{"t":"Towers in a forest, beside a giant of an airport","s":"Atlanta looks surprisingly green from above: a thick canopy of trees hides most of its suburbs, with clusters of towers rising out of it. South of downtown, look for the long parallel runways of Hartsfield–Jackson, for many years the world's busiest airport by passengers. The city began in 1837 as the end point of a railway, simply named Terminus.","w":"Clusters of towers in dense green forest; a huge airport with parallel east–west runways to the south; a pale granite dome to the east.","f":["Stone Mountain, a bare granite dome east of the city, rises about 250 m above the surrounding forest.","The Eastern Continental Divide crosses Atlanta: rain on one side flows to the Atlantic, on the other to the Gulf.","The name Atlanta was adopted in 1845, after a brief spell as Marthasville."],"q":{"q":"What was Atlanta called when it began in 1837?","a":"Terminus","x":["Peachtree","Stone Mountain"]}},"Q1297":{"t":"A perfect grid ruled against a lake like an inland sea","s":"Chicago's edge is one long, straight line: Lake Michigan, so wide that from the ground its far shore lies beyond the horizon. Inland the streets run in a strict grid, and at night it glows orange, block by block, far out across the prairie. The city exists because of a short portage here between the Great Lakes and the Mississippi basin.","w":"A huge city on the south-west shore of Lake Michigan; a ruler-straight lakefront, an even street grid, O'Hare to the north-west.","f":["In 1900 engineers reversed the Chicago River, sending it away from the lake towards the Mississippi.","The name comes from a local Native American word for a wild onion that grew along the river.","Most of Chicago's lakefront is kept as public parks and beaches rather than private land."],"q":{"q":"What did engineers do to the Chicago River in 1900?","a":"Reversed its flow","x":["Filled it in","Dammed it into a lake"]}},"Q1533":{"t":"A valley city, walled off from the sea by one mountain","s":"Caracas stretches for some 20 km along a narrow valley about 900 m up, parallel to the Caribbean coast yet hidden from it. Between city and sea rises the green ridge of El Ávila, over 2,700 m at its highest. So the airport sits on the coast at Maiquetía, on the far side of the mountain. The city was founded in 1567.","w":"A long, narrow city in an east–west valley; a steep green ridge to the north, the blue sea just beyond it.","f":["Simón Bolívar, who led much of South America to independence, was born in Caracas in 1783.","The city's full founding name was Santiago de León de Caracas."],"q":{"q":"What separates Caracas from the Caribbean Sea?","a":"The Ávila mountain ridge","x":["A wide coastal lagoon","A desert plain"]}},"Q2868":{"t":"A capital in the desert, under a lid of grey sea mist","s":"Lima is one of the driest capitals on Earth: a brown city on bare desert that gets barely a couple of centimetres of rain a year. Yet for months it hides under a low, grey sea fog, the garúa. Look for the cliffs where the city drops to the Pacific. Francisco Pizarro founded it in 1535 as the City of Kings.","w":"A vast tan city on a desert coast, edged by sea cliffs; bare brown hills inland and the green thread of the Rímac valley.","f":["For more than two centuries Lima was the capital of a Spanish viceroyalty covering most of Spanish South America.","The port of Callao and the international airport lie just west of the centre, on the same coastal plain.","In winter the garúa can keep the sky grey for weeks while barely wetting the ground."],"q":{"q":"What did Pizarro name Lima when he founded it in 1535?","a":"City of Kings","x":["City of the Sun","New Toledo"]}},"Q81033":{"t":"An industrial city in a ring of jagged grey mountains","s":"Monterrey sits in a valley of the Sierra Madre Oriental, and the mountains crowd right up to its suburbs. The most famous is Cerro de la Silla, whose twin-humped ridge really does look like a saddle. Founded in 1596, the city became Mexico's industrial powerhouse: in 1900 the first large steelworks in Latin America was founded here.","w":"A dense city in a valley hemmed by steep, folded grey ridges; a saddle-shaped mountain on its eastern edge.","f":["Cerro de la Silla rises to about 1,800 m, well over a kilometre above the city.","The Santa Catarina River that crosses the city is dry for most of the year.","The Huasteca Canyon on the western edge cuts deep into folded limestone ridges."],"q":{"q":"Which saddle-shaped mountain is the symbol of Monterrey?","a":"Cerro de la Silla","x":["Pico de Orizaba","Nevado de Toluca"]}},"Q24639":{"t":"A green peninsula city between the inlet and the peaks","s":"Vancouver is all edges: sea on three sides, and snowy Coast Mountains rising almost straight from the northern shore. Look for the dark green thumb of Stanley Park pointing into Burrard Inlet, and the flat, chequered farmland of the Fraser delta to the south, where the airport sits on an island. The city was incorporated in 1886.","w":"A city on a peninsula between two waters; a dark green wooded tip, snowy peaks to the north, flat delta to the south.","f":["Stanley Park covers about 400 hectares — more than New York's Central Park.","The inlet was charted in 1792 by Captain George Vancouver, whose name the city took.","The Canadian Pacific Railway arrived in 1887, making the city Canada's gateway to the Pacific."],"q":{"q":"Which river's delta lies on Vancouver's southern side?","a":"The Fraser","x":["The Columbia","The Skeena"]}},"Q172":{"t":"A lakefront city with islands cupping its harbour","s":"Toronto runs along the north shore of Lake Ontario, and the easiest way to spot it is a string of low green islands just offshore, curling round the harbour. Until a storm broke through in 1858 they were a sandy peninsula. Inland, look for deep wooded ravines snaking through the street grid. Until 1834 the town was called York.","w":"A dense city on the north shore of Lake Ontario; a curl of low islands off downtown, green ravines inland.","f":["One of the islands carries a small city airport, only a stone's throw from downtown.","The name is often traced to a Mohawk word for a place where trees stand in the water.","Toronto's network of wooded ravines is among the most extensive of any city in the world."],"q":{"q":"What was Toronto called before 1834?","a":"York","x":["Kingston","Newark"]}},"Q1486":{"t":"A grid of lights on a brown river as wide as a sea","s":"Buenos Aires faces the Río de la Plata, an estuary so broad that the Uruguayan shore lies roughly 50 km away, out of sight from the waterfront. The water is a milky coffee-brown, stained by silt from the Paraná and Uruguay rivers. Behind the shore, the city's square blocks march out across the flat pampas without a hill in sight.","w":"A vast grid city on the south bank of a wide brown estuary; the green maze of the Paraná Delta to the north-west.","f":["The city was founded twice: first in 1536, then for good by Juan de Garay in 1580.","The Río de la Plata is formed where the Paraná and Uruguay rivers meet.","At night the regular blocks glow like an orange chessboard reaching far inland."],"q":{"q":"Who refounded Buenos Aires for good in 1580?","a":"Juan de Garay","x":["Pedro de Mendoza","Juan Díaz de Solís"]}},"Q36091":{"t":"A city of lakes, split by the young Mississippi","s":"Minneapolis is dotted with lakes, several of them strung together inside the city like blue beads. Through the middle runs the Mississippi, still a modest river here, dropping over St. Anthony Falls, the only natural major waterfall on its whole course. The falls powered the flour mills that, around 1900, made this the milling capital of the world.","w":"A flat city freckled with round lakes; the Mississippi winding through its centre and meeting the Minnesota River by the airport.","f":["Minnesota has more than 11,000 lakes larger than 4 hectares — its '10,000 lakes' slogan undersells it.","St. Paul, the twin city and state capital, lies just downstream on the same river.","The airport sits right beside the confluence of the Mississippi and Minnesota rivers."],"q":{"q":"Which industry made Minneapolis famous around 1900?","a":"Flour milling","x":["Car making","Steelmaking"]}},"Q18094":{"t":"A tropical city between a crater and green fluted ridges","s":"Honolulu is squeezed along Oʻahu's south shore, between the reef-fringed sea and the steep, fluted green ridges of the Koʻolau Range. At its eastern end rises Diamond Head, a crater so round it looks stamped into the coast. The nearest point of mainland North America is roughly 3,800 km away, which makes this one of the remotest big cities on Earth.","w":"A strip of city along an island's south coast; a round crater at the east end, a long runway on the water to the west.","f":["Diamond Head is a volcanic tuff cone; sailors in the 1800s mistook glittering calcite crystals on its slopes for diamonds.","One of the airport's main runways, the Reef Runway, was built on landfill over a coral reef.","The name Honolulu is usually translated from Hawaiian as 'sheltered bay'."],"q":{"q":"What is Diamond Head in Honolulu?","a":"An extinct volcanic crater","x":["A coral island","A sea cliff"]}},"Q5083":{"t":"A city on a narrow neck of land, a giant volcano beyond","s":"Seattle sits on a slim strip of hilly land between the salt water of Puget Sound and the long, freshwater Lake Washington. On a clear day the view is stolen by Mount Rainier, a glacier-covered volcano of about 4,390 m some 90 km to the south-east. The city is named after Si'ahl, a leader of the local Duwamish and Suquamish peoples.","w":"A city between a fjord-like sound and a long lake; thin bridges floating across the lake; Rainier's white cone to the south-east.","f":["The bridges over Lake Washington float on concrete pontoons; one is the world's longest floating bridge, about 2.3 km.","The Klondike gold rush of 1897 made Seattle the main outfitting port for prospectors heading north.","Mount Rainier carries more glacier ice than any other peak in the contiguous United States."],"q":{"q":"Which volcano looms south-east of Seattle?","a":"Mount Rainier","x":["Mount Hood","Mount Shasta"]}},"Q16556":{"t":"A mile-square grid stamped across the Sonoran Desert","s":"Phoenix is geometry on sand: a vast grid of arterial roads a mile apart, stamped across a desert valley and broken by bare rocky mountains. Watch for canals and green squares of irrigated land. Long before the city, the Hohokam people dug hundreds of kilometres of canals here, and settlers of the 1860s named their new town after the bird that rises from its ashes.","w":"A huge, pale, regular grid in brown desert, with dark rocky peaks poking through; thin canals and green irrigated patches.","f":["The Hohokam canals, dug from about the 600s to the 1400s, formed one of the largest irrigation networks in pre-Columbian North America.","Summer afternoons here typically climb above 40 °C.","Camelback Mountain, surrounded by suburbs, is named for its hump-backed profile."],"q":{"q":"Why was Phoenix given its name?","a":"It rose on the ruins of ancient canals","x":["A bird-shaped mountain stands nearby","Its founder was called Phoenix"]}},"Q16552":{"t":"A deep blue bay, a sandy spit and Mexico next door","s":"San Diego wraps around a long, sheltered bay, closed off from the Pacific by the flat Coronado peninsula and the high ridge of Point Loma. Planes land almost among the downtown towers, at an airport right on the water. Spanish friars founded California's first mission here in 1769, and the Mexican border is only about 25 km to the south.","w":"A curved bay behind a pale sandy spit and a long ridge; the city spreads over canyon-cut mesas, merging with Tijuana to the south.","f":["In 1542 Juan Rodríguez Cabrillo sailed into this bay, the first European known to reach the US West Coast.","Coronado is not quite an island: a thin sand strip, the Silver Strand, ties it to the mainland."],"q":{"q":"What did Spanish friars found at San Diego in 1769?","a":"California's first mission","x":["California's first university","A silver mine"]}},"Q38022":{"t":"Where the muddy Missouri pours into the Mississippi","s":"Just north of St. Louis two of America's great rivers meet, and from above they often keep their colours for a while: the silt-laden Missouri alongside the darker Mississippi. French fur traders founded the town in 1764 and named it after King Louis IX. In 1804 Lewis and Clark set out from near the confluence to cross the continent.","w":"A city on the west bank of the Mississippi; to the north, two big brown rivers meet in a maze of channels and islands.","f":["The Eads Bridge of 1874 was one of the first major bridges built with steel as its main material.","A little further upstream the Illinois River joins the Mississippi too.","The Gateway Arch on the riverfront is 192 m tall but far too slender to pick out from cruise."],"q":{"q":"Which river joins the Mississippi just north of St. Louis?","a":"The Missouri","x":["The Ohio","The Arkansas"]}},"Q34404":{"t":"The Crescent City, cradled in a bend of the Mississippi","s":"New Orleans grew inside a tight curve of the Mississippi, which is why it's called the Crescent City. To its north spreads the broad, round Lake Pontchartrain, crossed by a bridge nearly 40 km long — a hairline over the water. The French founded the city in 1718; much of it now lies below sea level, ringed by levees.","w":"A city in a sharp bend of a wide brown river; a big round lake to the north crossed by a hairline bridge.","f":["The Lake Pontchartrain Causeway, about 38 km long, is one of the longest bridges over water in the world.","The city is named after Philippe, Duke of Orléans, who ruled France as regent when it was founded.","In 1803 the United States bought New Orleans as part of the vast Louisiana Purchase."],"q":{"q":"Why is New Orleans called the Crescent City?","a":"It sits in a bend of the Mississippi","x":["Its harbour is moon-shaped","Its main square is crescent-shaped"]}},"Q16557":{"t":"A prairie metropolis with an airport bigger than Manhattan","s":"Dallas rises from flat, open prairie, with no coast or mountain to give it shape — just freeways fanning out and a scatter of reservoirs. The Trinity River crosses it in a wide green floodway between levees. Towards Fort Worth, look for the runways of DFW, an airport that covers more ground than Manhattan. It all began with a trading post by a river ford in 1841.","w":"Flat sprawl without natural edges; a broad green floodway along the Trinity; a giant airport with long parallel runways to the north-west.","f":["Dallas–Fort Worth International covers about 70 km², more than the island of Manhattan.","Founder John Neely Bryan picked the spot for its natural ford across the Trinity River.","Almost all the lakes around Dallas are man-made reservoirs."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Dallas?","a":"The Trinity","x":["The Brazos","The Red River"]}},"Q171632":{"t":"On the neck of a vast lake studded with oil wells","s":"Maracaibo sits on the narrow strait where Lake Maracaibo, one of South America's biggest bodies of water, opens to the sea. Out on the lake stand countless oil wells, and at night their lights speckle the dark water. Look for the thin line of the bridge across the strait, over 8 km long. In 1499 an expedition sailing for Spain saw stilt houses here and called the land 'Little Venice' — Venezuela.","w":"A city on the west shore of a narrow strait; a long bridge across it; a vast lake opening to the south.","f":["Over the south-west of the lake, near the mouth of the Catatumbo River, lightning flickers on well over a hundred nights a year.","The General Rafael Urdaneta Bridge across the strait is about 8.7 km long and opened in 1962.","The lake is connected to the sea, so its water is slightly salty."],"q":{"q":"What gave Venezuela its name?","a":"Stilt villages on the lake","x":["A Venetian-born explorer","Canals in its capital"]}},"Q100":{"t":"A city that built its own ground out into the harbour","s":"Boston's outline is largely man-made. The Puritans who arrived in 1630 settled a small, hilly peninsula joined to the mainland by a thin neck; in the 1800s the mudflats around it were filled in to create whole districts. Look for the Charles River basin, the island-dotted harbour and the runways stretching into the sea just across from downtown.","w":"A compact city on a harbour full of islands; a wide river basin to the west; runways reaching into the water to the east.","f":["The Back Bay district stands on tidal flats that were filled in with gravel in the 1800s.","Harvard, founded in 1636 across the Charles River, is the oldest university in the United States.","Logan Airport was also built largely on land reclaimed from the harbour."],"q":{"q":"What was Boston's Back Bay before the 1800s?","a":"Tidal mudflats","x":["Farmland","Forest"]}},"Q49255":{"t":"A city at the head of a bay stitched with causeways","s":"Tampa sits at the top of a big, branching bay, and from above the whole area looks stitched together: long causeways and bridges run straight across the shallow water to St. Petersburg on the peninsula opposite. In 1886 cigar makers moved their factories here, and hand-rolled cigars made Tampa famous. Look for the dredged shipping channel running out towards the Gulf.","w":"A city at the northern head of a wide, branching bay; several thin causeways across the water; a peninsula city to the west.","f":["Tampa Bay is Florida's largest open-water estuary, covering about 1,000 km².","Ybor City, the old cigar district, once turned out hundreds of millions of cigars a year."],"q":{"q":"Which city faces Tampa across the bay?","a":"St. Petersburg","x":["Orlando","Jacksonville"]}},"Q1345":{"t":"Penn's grid, laid out between two rivers in 1682","s":"Philadelphia began as a plan on paper: in 1682 William Penn drew a neat grid between the Delaware and Schuylkill rivers, with a central square and four smaller ones. That rectangle is still the heart of the city. The Declaration of Independence was adopted here in 1776, and for a decade from 1790 Philadelphia served as the nation's capital.","w":"A dense grid between two rivers; the wide Delaware to the east with the airport on its bank; a long green park along the Schuylkill.","f":["Four of Penn's five original squares are still parks; the fifth holds City Hall.","The name is Greek for 'brotherly love'.","Fairmount Park, along the Schuylkill, is one of the largest urban park systems in the US."],"q":{"q":"Between which two rivers did Penn lay out Philadelphia?","a":"The Delaware and the Schuylkill","x":["The Hudson and the Harlem","The Potomac and the Anacostia"]}},"Q12439":{"t":"The US city with Canada to its south","s":"Detroit comes with a quirk of the map: across the Detroit River, due south of downtown, lies Canada. The river is really a strait linking Lake St. Clair to Lake Erie, and the French word for strait, détroit, gave the city its name. Antoine de la Mothe Cadillac founded a fort here in 1701. Two centuries later carmakers turned it into the Motor City.","w":"A city along a broad river channel between two lakes; Windsor on the opposite bank; a long wooded island in the river.","f":["Belle Isle, the island park in the Detroit River, is larger than New York's Central Park.","The Ambassador Bridge to Windsor carries more trade than any other US–Canada border crossing."],"q":{"q":"In which direction from downtown Detroit does Canada lie?","a":"South","x":["North","West"]}},"Q39450":{"t":"A frontier city between mudflats and the Chugach peaks","s":"Anchorage fills a wedge of flat land between two long arms of Cook Inlet, with the Chugach Mountains rising right behind it. At low tide the inlet drains to glistening grey mudflats: the tides here can rise and fall by more than 9 m. On a clear day, look north for Denali, about 200 km away. The city began in 1914 as a tent camp for railway builders.","w":"A grid city on a lowland wedge between two grey-brown inlets, backed by snowy mountains; a lake full of floatplanes beside the airport.","f":["Lake Hood, next to the international airport, is one of the world's busiest seaplane bases.","Turnagain Arm got its name in 1778, when James Cook's ships had to turn back there.","In Turnagain Arm the incoming tide sometimes arrives as a bore — a single travelling wave."],"q":{"q":"How did Turnagain Arm near Anchorage get its name?","a":"Cook's ships had to turn back there","x":["It bends back on itself","Whalers used it as a turning point"]}},"Q9022":{"t":"Between a deep canyon and Mexico's largest lake","s":"Guadalajara spreads across a high, open valley about 1,560 m above sea level. Its northern edge ends abruptly at a deep canyon cut by the Río Grande de Santiago, and to the south lies Lake Chapala, Mexico's largest freshwater lake. West of the city, look for blue-grey rows of agave: this is the country around the town of Tequila.","w":"A large city on a flat high valley; a dark canyon along its northern edge; a big lake to the south.","f":["The canyon at the northern edge, the Barranca de Huentitán, is roughly 600 m deep.","The city was moved several times before settling on its present site in 1542.","Blue agave for tequila covers the hills around the town of Tequila, about 60 km north-west."],"q":{"q":"Which lake lies just south of Guadalajara?","a":"Lake Chapala","x":["Lake Pátzcuaro","Lake Texcoco"]}},"Q125293":{"t":"A colonial grid ringed by great volcanoes","s":"Few cities have a volcanic horizon like Puebla's. To the west stand snowy Popocatépetl and Iztaccíhuatl, to the north-east the lone cone of La Malinche, and far to the east the white peak of Orizaba, Mexico's highest mountain. The Spanish founded Puebla in 1531 as a brand-new planned town, and its old centre is still a tidy grid.","w":"A large city on a high plain with big volcanoes on the horizon; La Malinche's isolated cone close by to the north-east.","f":["Next door in Cholula stands the largest pyramid in the world by volume, so overgrown it looks like a hill.","Pico de Orizaba, visible to the east on clear days, rises to about 5,600 m.","Puebla is famous for Talavera, a glazed pottery tradition brought from Spain in the 1500s."],"q":{"q":"Which nearby town has the largest pyramid in the world by volume?","a":"Cholula","x":["Teotihuacan","Tula"]}},"Q43509":{"t":"A tidal river port among mangroves and shrimp ponds","s":"Guayaquil spreads along the west bank of the broad, brown Guayas River, where the Pacific tide still reaches. Downstream the estuary dissolves into a maze of mangrove channels and neat grids of rectangular shrimp ponds. Ecuador's biggest city and main port, it was founded on this spot in 1538, after earlier attempts nearby.","w":"A large city beside a wide brown river; rows of rectangular ponds and dark mangroves along the estuary to the south.","f":["The ponds around the Gulf of Guayaquil help make Ecuador one of the world's biggest shrimp exporters.","Rafts of floating water hyacinth drift up and down the Guayas with every tide.","Francisco de Orellana, later the first European to sail down the Amazon, is credited with the 1538 founding."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past Guayaquil?","a":"The Guayas","x":["The Napo","The Esmeraldas"]}},"Q48278":{"t":"A city filling a green Andean valley from wall to wall","s":"Medellín is poured into the Aburrá Valley, a narrow trough in the Andes about 1,500 m up, and its red-brick neighbourhoods climb high up both slopes. Down the middle runs the Medellín River, heading north. At this height and so close to the equator the air stays mild all year, which is why locals call it the City of Eternal Spring.","w":"A long city in a narrow north–south valley, climbing its green walls; the main airport on a plateau to the east.","f":["The main airport stands on a plateau near Rionegro, at about 2,100 m — higher than the city itself.","The average temperature stays around 22 °C in every month of the year.","Medellín was granted the status of a town in 1675."],"q":{"q":"What is Medellín's nickname, thanks to its climate?","a":"City of Eternal Spring","x":["City of the Winds","City of Mists"]}},"Q51103":{"t":"Salsa city on a plain of sugar cane","s":"Cali sits where the flat, fertile Cauca Valley meets the steep green slopes of the Western Andes. Look out across the valley floor: a patchwork of sugar cane fields in every shade of green stretches north and south for well over a hundred kilometres. Sebastián de Belalcázar founded the city in 1536; today it proudly calls itself the world capital of salsa.","w":"A large city at the foot of a mountain wall to the west; a flat valley of striped green cane fields to the east.","f":["The Cauca Valley is the heart of Colombia's sugar industry.","The Cauca River, one of Colombia's longest, winds along the city's eastern edge.","At about 1,000 m, Cali is lower and warmer than Colombia's highland cities."],"q":{"q":"Which crop blankets the Cauca Valley around Cali?","a":"Sugar cane","x":["Wheat","Tea"]}},"Q1563":{"t":"A pocket harbour behind a narrow, fortified mouth","s":"Havana grew around a bag-shaped bay entered through a single narrow channel, easy to defend and safe from storms. That made it the gathering point for Spain's treasure fleets, which assembled here before crossing the Atlantic. The city moved to this shore in 1519. Look for the harbour's slim neck and the long seafront curving west from it.","w":"A city on Cuba's north coast around a pouch-shaped bay with a slender entrance; the old town on its western side.","f":["Old Havana and its fortifications have been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1982.","Key West, in Florida, lies about 170 km to the north across the Straits of Florida.","The Morro and La Cabaña fortresses guard the harbour mouth from its eastern shore."],"q":{"q":"What gathered in Havana harbour before crossing to Spain?","a":"Spanish treasure fleets","x":["Whaling fleets","Pirate squadrons"]}},"Q34820":{"t":"The Americas' oldest European city, at a river mouth","s":"Santo Domingo lies where the Ozama River meets the Caribbean, and its colonial quarter sits on the west bank right at the river mouth. Founded in 1496, it is the oldest continuously inhabited European-founded city in the Americas. Look for the long, straight seafront running west from the river, and the airport out on the coast to the east.","w":"A large city along a straight stretch of Caribbean coast, split by a river mouth; the airport on the coast to the east.","f":["In 1502 the city moved from the east bank of the Ozama to the west bank.","Its cathedral, completed in the 1540s, is the oldest in the Americas."],"q":{"q":"Which river splits Santo Domingo into west and east?","a":"The Ozama","x":["The Yaque del Norte","The Artibonite"]}},"Q12829733":{"t":"A river port at the gates of the Amazon","s":"Belém faces a vast expanse of muddy water — the Pará River and Guajará Bay, part of the Amazon's maze of mouths. Across it lies Marajó, an island roughly the size of Switzerland. The Portuguese built a fort here in 1616 to guard the way into the Amazon, and the rubber boom of the late 1800s made the city rich.","w":"A dense city on a point between brown waterways, forest and islands all around; the huge, low island of Marajó to the north-west.","f":["Marajó covers about 40,000 km², one of the largest islands ringed by fresh and brackish water.","Belém lies less than 200 km south of the equator, and afternoon downpours are almost daily.","The Ver-o-Peso waterfront market has traded Amazon goods since the 1600s."],"q":{"q":"Which huge island lies across the water from Belém?","a":"Marajó","x":["Bananal","Ilha Grande"]}},"Q2844":{"t":"A capital drawn in the shape of an aeroplane","s":"Brasília is one of the few cities whose design you can read from the window: two curved residential wings crossed by a straight monumental axis, like an aeroplane or a bird. Lúcio Costa drew the plan, and the city was built on almost empty savanna and inaugurated in 1960. An artificial lake wraps around it on the east.","w":"A cross-shaped city — a long straight axis across a curved arc — beside a branching man-made lake on the high savanna.","f":["Lake Paranoá was created by damming a river when the city was built.","The whole Pilot Plan has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1987.","The city stands on a plateau at about 1,100 m, amid Brazil's reddish cerrado savanna."],"q":{"q":"What does Brasília's original plan resemble?","a":"An aeroplane","x":["A star","A spiral"]}},"Q40269":{"t":"A city on a river that might be a lake","s":"Porto Alegre lines the eastern shore of the Guaíba, a broad body of water formed where several rivers meet in a green delta of islands. Locals still argue whether it counts as a river or a lake. To the south it opens into the Lagoa dos Patos, a coastal lagoon more than 200 km long. Settlers from the Azores helped found the town in the 1700s.","w":"A city on the east bank of wide brown water; a delta of green islands to the north, a vast lagoon to the south.","f":["The Lagoa dos Patos covers about 10,000 km², making it Brazil's largest lagoon.","The official founding date is 1772, though Azorean families had settled here some twenty years earlier."],"q":{"q":"Which lagoon stretches away south of Porto Alegre?","a":"Lagoa dos Patos","x":["Lagoa Rodrigo de Freitas","Lagoa da Conceição"]}},"Q4361":{"t":"A cool plateau city among candelabra pines","s":"Curitiba sits on a high plateau about 900 m up, just inland from the forested Serra do Mar, and is one of Brazil's coolest big cities, with frosty winter mornings. Its name is usually traced to a Tupi phrase for 'many pines', after the araucaria trees whose flat, candelabra crowns still dot the countryside. Look for the lakes in its many parks.","w":"A large city on a green plateau dotted with park lakes; the dark, forested Serra do Mar rising to the east.","f":["Many of the city's park lakes were created as flood-control reservoirs.","A railway opened in 1885 still winds down the Serra do Mar from Curitiba to the port of Paranaguá.","In the 1970s Curitiba pioneered express bus lanes that cities around the world later copied."],"q":{"q":"Which tree gave Curitiba its name?","a":"The araucaria, or Paraná pine","x":["The Brazil nut tree","The rubber tree"]}},"Q43463":{"t":"A sunny fortress city on Brazil's north-east shoulder","s":"Fortaleza faces north onto a warm, turquoise Atlantic, its high-rise seafront curving along a coast of white dunes. The steady trade winds that fill the sails of local jangada rafts also turn rows of wind turbines along the shore. The name simply means 'fortress': the city grew around a fort the Dutch built here in 1649, later taken over by the Portuguese.","w":"A large city on a north-facing coast; long pale beaches and dunes on either side; breakwaters and a port at its eastern end.","f":["Ceará, the state Fortaleza is capital of, abolished slavery in 1884, four years before the rest of Brazil.","The coast here lies only about 4° south of the equator, so days and nights stay nearly equal all year."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Fortaleza mean?","a":"Fortress","x":["Lighthouse","Harbour"]}},"Q36947":{"t":"Brazil's first capital, on a cliff above All Saints Bay","s":"Salvador occupies a blunt peninsula between the open Atlantic and the huge, calm Bay of All Saints. Its old town was built on top of a steep escarpment, with the port crowded at the foot — a city on two levels. Founded in 1549, it served as the capital of Portuguese Brazil for more than two centuries, until 1763.","w":"A big city on a triangular peninsula; a wide, island-studded bay to the west, ocean beaches to the south and east.","f":["The bay was named in 1501 by a Portuguese expedition that arrived on All Saints' Day.","Since 1873 a public lift, the Lacerda Elevator, has carried people between the upper and lower towns.","The bay covers more than 1,000 km² and holds dozens of islands."],"q":{"q":"Which bay did Salvador grow up beside?","a":"The Bay of All Saints","x":["Guanabara Bay","Paranaguá Bay"]}},"Q2096":{"t":"A green river gorge winding through a northern city","s":"Edmonton is cut in two by the North Saskatchewan River, which loops through a deep, wooded valley kept almost entirely as parkland. From above it's a dark green ribbon across the grid of streets. Around the city, flat farmland stretches to the horizon. This is the northernmost metropolis of more than a million people in North America.","w":"A large grid city on flat farmland, split by a deep, winding, tree-lined river valley; refineries on the eastern edge.","f":["The river valley parks form one of the longest continuous stretches of urban parkland in North America.","The Hudson's Bay Company built Fort Edmonton on the river in 1795.","Edmonton is Alberta's capital, though Calgary to the south is the larger city."],"q":{"q":"Which river winds through Edmonton?","a":"The North Saskatchewan","x":["The Athabasca","The Red Deer"]}},"Q340":{"t":"An island city with a mountain at its heart","s":"Montreal fills most of an island in the St. Lawrence, and in its middle rises a dark, wooded hill: Mount Royal, which gave the city its name. Jacques Cartier climbed it in 1535, and in 1642 French settlers founded Ville-Marie below. Just upstream the Lachine Rapids stopped ocean-going ships, so the city grew into a great river port.","w":"A large island city in a broad river, with a wooded hill in the middle; long bridges to the south shore.","f":["The island of Montreal, about 50 km long, is the largest of more than 200 islands in the Hochelaga Archipelago.","Ships now bypass the Lachine Rapids through the St. Lawrence Seaway along the south shore.","Montreal is one of the largest French-speaking cities in the world."],"q":{"q":"What is Montreal named after?","a":"The hill in its centre","x":["A French king","The St. Lawrence River"]}},"Q83189":{"t":"A 1930s planned capital on the red cerrado plateau","s":"Goiânia was drawn on a drawing board and built from 1933 to replace the old colonial town of Goiás as state capital. Its central avenues radiate from a civic square like spokes, a plan a generation older than Brasília's. Around it the cerrado savanna has become a patchwork of soy fields and pasture, with reddish soil showing between them.","w":"A large city on a gently rolling plateau; green and red-brown squares of farmland all round; Brasília about 200 km to the north-east.","f":["The old capital, the town of Goiás, is a UNESCO World Heritage site for its colonial centre.","Many of Goiânia's early public buildings were built in Art Deco style."],"q":{"q":"Which town did Goiânia replace as state capital?","a":"The old town of Goiás","x":["Anápolis","Pirenópolis"]}},"Q48344":{"t":"A city of rivers and bridges behind a natural reef","s":"Recife is threaded with water: the Capibaribe and Beberibe rivers split into channels around islands before reaching the sea, and bridges stitch the districts together. Offshore, a long, straight line of rock runs parallel to the beach and shelters the harbour — the reef that gave the city its name. From 1630 to 1654 this was the capital of Dutch Brazil.","w":"A dense coastal city criss-crossed by river channels; a thin line of reef just offshore; a long beach to the south.","f":["The first synagogue in the Americas, Kahal Zur Israel, opened here in the 1630s under Dutch rule.","Under Johan Maurits of Nassau the Dutch built a new town, Mauritsstad, on one of the islands."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Recife mean?","a":"Reef","x":["Bridge","Island"]}},"Q49219":{"t":"A city planned from scratch on an old cattle ranch","s":"Irvine was drawn up almost from nothing in the 1960s on the Irvine Ranch, once a huge cattle and farming estate stretching from the coast to the mountains. Architect William Pereira laid it out around a new University of California campus, in tidy villages divided by green belts. To the north-east, look for Saddleback, the twin-peaked ridge that shapes the skyline.","w":"Tidy curving suburbs with green belts, between low coastal hills and the Santa Ana Mountains; John Wayne Airport on the western edge.","f":["Saddleback is named for the dip between its two summits; the higher one, Santiago Peak, is about 1,700 m.","Departures from John Wayne Airport climb steeply and throttle back to spare the houses below.","Upper Newport Bay, just to the south-west, is one of southern California's few surviving coastal estuaries."],"q":{"q":"What was the land under Irvine before the city was planned?","a":"A vast private ranch","x":["A national forest","A salt lake"]}},"Q42800":{"t":"A planned capital walled in by iron-rich mountains","s":"Belo Horizonte was built from scratch and inaugurated in 1897 as the new capital of Minas Gerais, replacing the old gold town of Ouro Preto. Its planners overlaid a street grid with diagonal avenues, all wrapped in a ring road. Along the southern edge rises the Serra do Curral, and beyond it lie the red-brown scars of iron-ore mines. The name means 'beautiful horizon'.","w":"A big, hilly city under a long ridge to the south; reddish mine pits in the hills beyond; a lake on the northern side.","f":["The surrounding 'Iron Quadrangle' is one of the world's great iron-ore regions.","Lake Pampulha, an artificial lake in the north, is ringed by 1940s buildings by Oscar Niemeyer, now UNESCO-listed."],"q":{"q":"Which old gold town did Belo Horizonte replace as capital in 1897?","a":"Ouro Preto","x":["Diamantina","Mariana"]}},"Q1335":{"t":"The southernmost capital in the Americas, by a lone hill","s":"Montevideo sits on a rocky peninsula on the north shore of the Río de la Plata, just where the brown estuary begins to turn into the blue Atlantic. Across its bay rises a lone, rounded hill, the Cerro, which gave the city its name. Spain founded Montevideo in the 1720s as a fortress to hold the estuary's north shore against Portugal.","w":"A city on a peninsula and a long curving shore; a round hill across the bay to the west; brown water shading to blue eastward.","f":["The Cerro de Montevideo is only about 134 m high, but it was the landmark sailors steered by.","Montevideo is the southernmost national capital in the Americas.","A seaside promenade, the Rambla, follows the shore for more than 20 km."],"q":{"q":"What gave Montevideo its name?","a":"A hill across the bay","x":["A river mouth","An island in the harbour"]}},"Q38733":{"t":"A gold-rush capital at the edge of the Rockies","s":"Helena began in 1864, when four worn-out prospectors tried one last creek and struck gold; the town's main street still follows that 'Last Chance Gulch'. Today it's a small state capital where forested mountains meet the flat, open Helena Valley. Look for the Missouri River's reservoirs glinting to the north-east and east.","w":"A small town tucked against wooded hills; a broad, flat valley to the north-east, with the Missouri's blue reservoirs beyond.","f":["In 1805 Meriwether Lewis named a narrow canyon of the Missouri nearby the 'Gates of the Rocky Mountains'.","Helena became Montana's capital in 1875, while Montana was still a territory."],"q":{"q":"What was the gulch called where Helena's gold was found?","a":"Last Chance Gulch","x":["Lucky Strike Gulch","Golden Creek Gulch"]}},"Q37066":{"t":"A prairie capital named to charm German investors","s":"Bismarck lies on the east bank of the Missouri, here a wide river winding through open, rolling plains. The town began in 1872 as Edwinton, a railway crossing; a year later it was renamed after Germany's chancellor, Otto von Bismarck, in the hope of drawing German money and settlers to the Northern Pacific Railway. Its twin town, Mandan, faces it across the river.","w":"A modest city on a broad, winding river with sandbars; a smaller town across the water; wide tan prairie all around.","f":["Lewis and Clark spent the winter of 1804–05 at Fort Mandan, about 60 km up the river.","North Dakota's capitol is a 19-storey tower, often called the 'Skyscraper on the Prairie'."],"q":{"q":"Who is Bismarck named after?","a":"Chancellor Otto von Bismarck","x":["A railway engineer","A Sioux chief"]}},"Q35775":{"t":"A green river of trees across the sagebrush","s":"From above, Boise's origin story makes sense: a ribbon of cottonwoods follows the Boise River out of the mountains and across a dry, sage-grey plain. French-Canadian fur trappers are said to have greeted the trees with 'Les bois!', and the name stuck. Today the city spreads from the foothills onto the irrigated patchwork of the Snake River Plain.","w":"A city at the foot of bare brown foothills; a green, tree-lined river running west through it onto a patchwork of farmland.","f":["Idaho's capitol is the only US state capitol heated by water from underground hot springs.","Boise has one of the largest Basque communities in the United States."],"q":{"q":"Where does the name Boise come from?","a":"The French word for 'woods'","x":["A Shoshone chief's name","A Spanish saint's name"]}},"Q16553":{"t":"The flat heart of Silicon Valley, once all orchards","s":"San Jose spreads across the flat floor of the Santa Clara Valley, between the wooded Santa Cruz Mountains to the west and the bare, rounded Diablo Range to the east. Founded in 1777, it was the first Spanish civilian town in California. Until the mid-1900s the valley was a sea of fruit orchards; now it's the hub of Silicon Valley.","w":"A vast, low city on a flat valley floor at the southern tip of San Francisco Bay; mountains on both sides.","f":["San Jose was California's first state capital, from 1849 to 1851.","On Mount Hamilton to the east, Lick Observatory opened in 1888, one of the first mountaintop observatories."],"q":{"q":"What was the Santa Clara Valley known for before computers?","a":"Fruit orchards","x":["Oil wells","Gold mines"]}},"Q18013":{"t":"A gold-rush capital where two rivers meet","s":"Sacramento sits where the American River flows into the Sacramento, in the middle of California's flat Central Valley. In 1848 gold was found at a sawmill up the American River, and this riverside settlement became the supply town for the rush. Look west for a wide, straight belt of fields between levees: the Yolo Bypass, which floods on purpose in wet winters.","w":"A tree-filled city at a river junction on a flat valley floor; rice paddies around; a broad, leveed flood channel to the west.","f":["In spring the flooded rice fields around the city can shine like mirrors.","The Central Pacific began building the first transcontinental railway eastward from Sacramento in 1863.","Sacramento has been California's capital since 1854."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows into the Sacramento River in the city?","a":"The American River","x":["The Colorado River","The Klamath River"]}},"Q23768":{"t":"A blaze of light in a black desert","s":"At night Las Vegas is unmistakable: a dense pool of white and gold light in a desert that's otherwise almost completely dark, with one brilliant stripe running north–south through it. By day the valley shows its bones — bare mountains all round, snow on Mount Charleston in winter. The name is Spanish for 'the meadows', after springs that once watered grass here.","w":"A sharply bounded city in a desert basin ringed by bare mountains; the blue of Lake Mead about 40 km to the east.","f":["The city was founded in 1905 with a railway company's auction of town lots.","Lake Mead, formed by the Hoover Dam, is the largest reservoir in the US by capacity.","Mount Charleston, west of the city, rises to about 3,630 m."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Las Vegas' mean in Spanish?","a":"The meadows","x":["The lights","The sands"]}},"Q38555":{"t":"America's oldest and highest state capital","s":"Santa Fe is easy to miss: a low, earth-coloured town of adobe walls that blends into the piñon-dotted hills at the foot of the Sangre de Cristo Mountains. Spanish colonists founded it around 1610, making it the oldest state capital in the US, and at about 2,100 m it is also the highest. The broad Rio Grande valley opens to the west.","w":"A small, low, tan town at the southern tip of a snowy range; dark green juniper hills around; a wide valley to the west.","f":["The Santa Fe Trail, opened in 1821, linked the town to Missouri by a trade route of well over 1,000 km.","The name Sangre de Cristo ('Blood of Christ') is often linked to the mountains' red glow at sunset.","The Palace of the Governors, built in the 1610s, is among the oldest public buildings in the US."],"q":{"q":"When was Santa Fe founded?","a":"Around 1610","x":["In 1776","In 1848"]}},"Q6106":{"t":"A city of bridges under the white cone of Mount Hood","s":"Portland straddles the Willamette River just before it joins the broad Columbia, and a string of bridges stitches its two halves together. On a clear day Mount Hood's snowy cone floats on the eastern horizon. The name was settled in 1845 by a coin toss: one founder wanted Boston, the other Portland, after his home town in Maine.","w":"A city on both banks of a river just above a much wider one; a dark wooded ridge to the west; Mount Hood east.","f":["Forest Park, on the ridge above the city, is one of the largest urban forests in the United States.","Mount Hood, about 3,430 m high, is a volcano and Oregon's highest peak.","The Columbia River here forms the border between Oregon and Washington."],"q":{"q":"How was Portland's name chosen in 1845?","a":"By tossing a coin","x":["By a vote of settlers","By a railway company"]}},"Q23337":{"t":"A grid city between a mountain wall and a salty inland sea","s":"Salt Lake City sits on a flat bench between the abrupt wall of the Wasatch Range and the shallow, glittering Great Salt Lake. Look at the lower mountain slopes for faint horizontal lines: old shorelines of Lake Bonneville, the vast ancient lake of which the Great Salt Lake is a remnant. Mormon pioneers founded the city in 1847 and laid out its grid of very wide streets.","w":"A city at the foot of a steep mountain wall; a vast pale lake to the north-west, with colourful evaporation ponds along its shore.","f":["A railway causeway splits the lake, and the saltier northern part often looks pinkish-red from above.","Street addresses count outward from a single central point, Temple Square.","In places the Great Salt Lake is several times saltier than the ocean."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient lake left terraces on the mountains around Salt Lake City?","a":"Lake Bonneville","x":["Lake Lahontan","Lake Agassiz"]}},"Q39042":{"t":"A railway town that sprang up on the windy high plains","s":"Cheyenne stands alone on open high plains about 1,850 m up, with the low, pine-dark Laramie Range to the west. It was born in 1867, when the Union Pacific tracks arrived, and grew so fast it was nicknamed the Magic City of the Plains. Look for the railway running east–west through town: the reason it exists at all.","w":"A compact town on bare, rolling grassland; a railway line straight through it; low, dark hills to the west.","f":["In 1869 the territorial legislature in Cheyenne gave women the vote — a first in the United States.","Cheyenne Frontier Days, held every July since 1897, is one of the world's largest outdoor rodeos."],"q":{"q":"What did Wyoming's legislature in Cheyenne grant first in 1869?","a":"Votes for women","x":["An eight-hour working day","Free railway travel"]}},"Q39709":{"t":"A capital at a river fork in a sea of corn","s":"Des Moines grew where the Raccoon River flows into the Des Moines, and from above it sits like a knot in a vast chequerboard: Iowa's fields, laid out in one-mile squares of corn and soybeans, run to every horizon. The US Army founded Fort Des Moines at the fork in 1843. Look north for the long reservoir of Saylorville Lake.","w":"A mid-sized city at a river junction, surrounded by a perfect grid of square fields; a long, narrow lake to the north.","f":["The name comes from the river's French name, often translated as 'River of the Monks', though its origin is debated.","Iowa was surveyed into square miles in the 1800s, and its country roads still follow that grid."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers meet at Des Moines?","a":"The Des Moines and the Raccoon","x":["The Iowa and the Cedar","The Missouri and the Platte"]}},"Q43199":{"t":"A river city where the Missouri once switched sides","s":"Omaha lines the west bank of the Missouri, a brown river looping across a wide floodplain, with Council Bluffs in Iowa on the other side. Look by the airport for a horseshoe-shaped lake: it's an old river bend, cut off when the Missouri changed course in 1877. The town beside it stayed in Iowa, stranded on the Nebraska bank.","w":"A city on bluffs above a winding brown river; a horseshoe lake and the airport on the floodplain; Council Bluffs across the water.","f":["In the 1860s the Union Pacific began building the first transcontinental railway westward from Omaha.","The city, founded in 1854, is named after the Omaha people.","Carter Lake is the only Iowa town west of the Missouri River."],"q":{"q":"Why is Carter Lake, Iowa, on Omaha's side of the Missouri?","a":"The river changed course","x":["Iowa bought it from Nebraska","A treaty swapped it"]}},"Q34863":{"t":"A city that appeared in a single afternoon in 1889","s":"Oklahoma City has an unusual birthday. At noon on 22 April 1889 a gunshot opened the Land Run, and settlers raced in to claim plots; by nightfall around 10,000 people were camped on what had been open prairie. Today the city sprawls across flat, reddish plains, with round reservoirs on its edges and the Oklahoma River running through.","w":"A very spread-out city on flat red-brown plains; round blue reservoirs to the north-west; a river channel just south of downtown.","f":["Oil wells have pumped on the grounds of the State Capitol — a sign of the oil field beneath the city.","Oklahoma City covers about 1,600 km², making it one of the largest US cities by land area."],"q":{"q":"How did Oklahoma City come into being in 1889?","a":"In a single-day land run","x":["As a mining camp","As an army fort"]}},"Q40747":{"t":"A tiny capital below a dam and a 370-km lake","s":"Pierre, pronounced 'peer', is one of the smallest state capitals in the US, a quiet town on the Missouri amid treeless, rolling plains. Just upstream the river is blocked by Oahe Dam, a huge earthen embankment, and behind it Lake Oahe winds north for some 370 km, almost to Bismarck, the capital of North Dakota.","w":"A small town on a river just below a massive earth dam; a long, winding blue lake stretching north through bare brown hills.","f":["In 1743 French explorers buried a lead plate claiming the land for France on a hill across the river; children found it in 1913.","The town is named after Pierre Chouteau Jr., a fur trader based in St. Louis."],"q":{"q":"Which huge reservoir begins just north of Pierre?","a":"Lake Oahe","x":["Lake Sakakawea","Lake Mead"]}},"Q975":{"t":"Where the Texas Hill Country drops to the plains","s":"San Antonio sits right on the Balcones Escarpment, and from above you can see the change: rumpled, wooded hills to the north-west, flat, open farmland to the south-east. Spanish missionaries and soldiers founded the settlement in 1718 by the springs of the San Antonio River. A chain of five missions along the river, the Alamo among them, is now a World Heritage site.","w":"A big, sprawling city where dark green hills to the north-west meet flat plains to the south-east; freeway rings around the centre.","f":["In 2015 the San Antonio Missions became the first UNESCO World Heritage site in Texas.","Much of the city's water comes from the Edwards Aquifer, a vast limestone reservoir beneath the escarpment."],"q":{"q":"When was San Antonio founded?","a":"In 1718","x":["In 1836","In 1781"]}},"Q820235":{"t":"An Andean terrace city near the Colombian border","s":"San Cristóbal sits on a series of terraces above the Torbes River, about 800 m up, in a green valley where Venezuela's Andes begin. The mountains here form a knot between the Colombian ranges to the south-west and the high Cordillera de Mérida to the north-east. Captain Juan Maldonado founded the city in 1561; Venezuelans know it as the City of Courtesy.","w":"A city spread over stepped terraces in a green mountain valley; forested ridges all round; a river winding along the valley floor.","f":["The border with Colombia runs only a few dozen kilometres to the west, near the city of Cúcuta.","To the north-east the Andes climb to Pico Bolívar, Venezuela's highest peak, at about 4,980 m."],"q":{"q":"What do Venezuelans call San Cristóbal?","a":"The City of Courtesy","x":["The City of Eternal Spring","The City of Bridges"]}},"Q54880":{"t":"An industrial city beside a lake with no way to the sea","s":"Valencia spreads over a green valley beside Lake Valencia, a broad lake ringed by mountains with no outlet to the sea. When Alexander von Humboldt came here in 1800, he noticed that the water had been falling and blamed the clearing of forests around it — one of the earliest scientific warnings about human impact on nature. The city itself was founded in 1555.","w":"A large city in a flat valley west of a wide lake; the forested Coastal Range to the north, the sea just beyond it.","f":["Lake Valencia is Venezuela's largest natural freshwater lake.","The port of Puerto Cabello lies about 35 km to the north, on the far side of the coastal mountains."],"q":{"q":"Who studied Lake Valencia's falling water level in 1800?","a":"Alexander von Humboldt","x":["Charles Darwin","Charles Marie de La Condamine"]}},"Q28198":{"t":"A capital on the Pearl River, above a buried volcano","s":"Jackson lies on gently rolling, wooded land beside the Pearl River, with the broad Ross Barnett Reservoir glinting to the north-east. Nothing at the surface hints at it, but about 900 m underground lies an extinct volcano, long buried by sediment. The site began as a trading post called LeFleur's Bluff and was chosen as the state capital in 1821, named after Andrew Jackson.","w":"A mid-sized city in green, rolling woodland; a winding river on its east side; a large reservoir to the north-east.","f":["The buried Jackson Volcano was last active tens of millions of years ago, in the age of the dinosaurs.","The Natchez Trace, an old trail of traders and travellers, passes nearby as a scenic parkway."],"q":{"q":"What lies buried deep beneath Jackson?","a":"An extinct volcano","x":["A meteorite crater","A gold vein"]}},"Q41087":{"t":"The City of Oaks, a capital planned in a forest","s":"Raleigh hides under trees: from above, rooftops and roads peek through an almost continuous canopy of oak and pine. It was planned from scratch in 1792, when North Carolina bought land in the woods for a new capital, and named after Sir Walter Raleigh, sponsor of the Roanoke colony. Look for the branching reservoirs of Falls Lake and Jordan Lake nearby.","w":"A city deep in green forest; a compact downtown; branching reservoirs to the north and west; the airport among trees to the north-west.","f":["Raleigh, Durham and Chapel Hill form the 'Research Triangle', with a large research park between them.","The 1792 plan put the State House on a central square, with four smaller squares around it."],"q":{"q":"Who is Raleigh named after?","a":"Sir Walter Raleigh","x":["Sir Francis Drake","John White"]}},"Q37320":{"t":"A lake port where a crooked river meets Lake Erie","s":"Cleveland spreads along the south shore of Lake Erie, and its heart is where the Cuyahoga River winds through a valley of old industry before slipping into the lake. The name Cuyahoga is often translated as 'crooked river', and from above it's easy to see why. Surveyor Moses Cleaveland laid out the town in 1796; the city later dropped the first 'a'.","w":"A city along a straight lakeshore; a tightly winding river through the centre; a small airport right on the waterfront.","f":["Cuyahoga Valley, just south of the city, is Ohio's only national park.","Burke Lakefront Airport sits on land reclaimed from Lake Erie, next to downtown."],"q":{"q":"Who laid out Cleveland in 1796?","a":"Moses Cleaveland","x":["Grover Cleveland","Anthony Wayne"]}},"Q43196":{"t":"A hilly river city whose airport is in another state","s":"Cincinnati fills a bowl of hills on the north bank of the Ohio River, which swings past in broad curves; across the water is Kentucky. The city's main airport is on the Kentucky side too. Founded in 1788, it was named after the Society of the Cincinnati, which honoured Cincinnatus, the Roman statesman who went back to his plough after saving Rome.","w":"A city in green hills along a curving river; towns on the opposite, Kentucky bank; a big airport on the plateau to the south-west.","f":["The Roebling Suspension Bridge, finished in 1866, was a forerunner of the same engineer's Brooklyn Bridge.","In the 1800s the city packed so much pork that it was nicknamed Porkopolis."],"q":{"q":"In which state is Cincinnati's main airport?","a":"Kentucky","x":["Ohio","Indiana"]}},"Q23197":{"t":"Music City in the loops of the Cumberland River","s":"Nashville grew on the bluffs of the Cumberland River, which coils through the city in wide, lazy loops before heading north. Settlers built Fort Nashborough here in 1779. Look for the reservoirs east of town, Percy Priest and Old Hickory, their arms reaching into wooded hills. The nickname Music City comes from its country-music studios and radio stages.","w":"A city in rolling green country, with a winding river making big loops through it; branching lakes to the east and north-east.","f":["The Grand Ole Opry has broadcast from Nashville every week since 1925, the longest-running radio show in the US.","The city was named after Francis Nash, a general from North Carolina."],"q":{"q":"Which river loops through Nashville?","a":"The Cumberland","x":["The Tennessee","The Ohio"]}},"Q16563":{"t":"A bluff-top river city named after Egypt's ancient capital","s":"Memphis sits on bluffs on the east bank of the Mississippi, above the river's floods; across the water the Arkansas side is flat floodplain scattered with curved oxbow lakes. Its founders in 1819 likened the river to the Nile and named the town after the ancient Egyptian capital. At night its airport is famously busy: it's the main hub of a giant cargo airline.","w":"A city on high ground on the east bank of a broad, winding brown river; flat farmland and oxbow lakes on the Arkansas side.","f":["For years Memphis was the world's busiest cargo airport, thanks to FedEx's overnight hub.","The two steel arches of the Hernando de Soto Bridge over the river form the letter 'M'."],"q":{"q":"Which state lies across the Mississippi from Memphis?","a":"Arkansas","x":["Missouri","Louisiana"]}},"Q49231":{"t":"A harbour city at the mouth of Chesapeake Bay","s":"Norfolk sits on Hampton Roads, a great natural anchorage where several rivers pour into the mouth of Chesapeake Bay. Look east across the bay entrance for a thin line that suddenly vanishes: the Chesapeake Bay Bridge–Tunnel, which dives under the shipping lanes between man-made islands. South of the city stretches the dark forest of the Great Dismal Swamp.","w":"A low, watery city of peninsulas and inlets on a vast harbour; a long causeway crossing the bay mouth to the north-east.","f":["The Bridge–Tunnel, opened in 1964, runs about 28 km from shore to shore, with two tunnels under the channels.","Lake Drummond, in the middle of the Great Dismal Swamp, is one of only two natural lakes in Virginia.","Hampton Roads is one of the largest natural harbours in the world."],"q":{"q":"What links the two shores at the mouth of Chesapeake Bay?","a":"A bridge–tunnel about 28 km long","x":["A 28-km suspension bridge","Only a car ferry"]}},"Q37836":{"t":"Three rivers, three rival towns and a Great Lake","s":"Milwaukee sits where three rivers join and flow into Lake Michigan behind a long breakwater. Each river bank once belonged to a rival settlement, and when the three merged in 1846 their street grids didn't match — which is why several bridges still cross the river at an angle. German immigrants later made the city famous for its beer.","w":"A city on the western shore of Lake Michigan, where three rivers meet in a harbour sheltered by long breakwaters.","f":["In the late 1800s some of America's biggest breweries were based in Milwaukee.","The opposite, Michigan shore of the lake lies about 130 km away."],"q":{"q":"How many rival settlements merged to form Milwaukee in 1846?","a":"Three","x":["Two","Five"]}},"Q40435":{"t":"Where Lake Erie pours into the Niagara River","s":"Buffalo stands at the eastern tip of Lake Erie, right where the lake drains into the Niagara River and starts its run north to the falls, about 30 km away. In 1825 the Erie Canal linked this spot to the Hudson and New York, and Buffalo boomed as the gateway to the Great Lakes. In winter, look for bands of lake-effect snow.","w":"A city at the eastern corner of Lake Erie; a river flowing north from it along the Canadian border towards Niagara Falls.","f":["The world's first steam-powered grain elevator was built here in 1842.","Wind blowing over the unfrozen lake can dump snow on the city in narrow, very intense bands.","The Peace Bridge links Buffalo with Fort Erie, in Canada, at the head of the river."],"q":{"q":"Which canal ended at Buffalo when it opened in 1825?","a":"The Erie Canal","x":["The Welland Canal","The Ohio and Erie Canal"]}},"Q1342":{"t":"Where two rivers meet to begin the Ohio","s":"Pittsburgh's downtown is a sharp triangle of land where the Allegheny and Monongahela rivers meet to form the Ohio, and from above that point stands out clearly. All around, the rivers run through steep, wooded valleys crossed by bridge after bridge. In 1758 the British named the spot after the statesman William Pitt and soon built Fort Pitt at the point.","w":"A pointed wedge of towers where two rivers merge into one; hills and deep river valleys all around, crossed by many bridges.","f":["Pittsburgh has around 450 bridges, often said to be more than any other city in the world.","For much of the 20th century the city was the heart of American steelmaking."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers meet at Pittsburgh to form the Ohio?","a":"The Allegheny and the Monongahela","x":["The Susquehanna and the Juniata","The Kanawha and the Elk"]}},"Q507080":{"t":"A planned city where dark water meets muddy water","s":"Look where two great rivers meet: the Caroní, dark as strong tea, flows into the broad, muddy Orinoco, and the two colours run side by side before blending. Ciudad Guayana spreads over both banks of the Caroní, a city planned in 1961 by merging two older towns, Puerto Ordaz and San Félix. Its steel and aluminium plants run on power from dams upstream.","w":"A city on both sides of a dark river where it joins a much wider, pale brown one; industrial plants along the shore.","f":["The Caroní's dark colour comes from tannins washed out of forest soils.","The Guri Dam, upstream on the Caroní, is one of the largest hydroelectric plants in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers meet at Ciudad Guayana?","a":"The Orinoco and the Caroní","x":["The Orinoco and the Apure","The Amazon and the Negro"]}},"Q79985":{"t":"Russian America's first capital, on a big green island","s":"Kodiak town lies on the north-east corner of Kodiak Island, the second-largest island in the United States, a land of steep green mountains and deep, fjord-like bays. Russian fur traders made their first permanent Alaskan settlement on the island in 1784, and until 1808 this harbour was the capital of Russian America. Today it's one of the busiest fishing ports in the US.","w":"A small harbour town on a green, mountainous island; bays cutting deep into the land; smaller wooded islands just offshore.","f":["Kodiak Island covers about 9,300 km²; in the US only Hawaii's Big Island is larger.","A warehouse built by the Russians around 1808 still stands in town, now the Baranov Museum.","Kodiak bears, a local subspecies of brown bear, are among the largest bears on Earth."],"q":{"q":"What was Kodiak until 1808?","a":"The capital of Russian America","x":["A Hudson's Bay Company fort","A Spanish mission"]}},"Q79523":{"t":"A tiny village with a runway for Pacific emergencies","s":"Cold Bay is barely a village, near the stormy tip of the Alaska Peninsula, yet it has one of the longest runways in Alaska, over 3 km. Airliners crossing the North Pacific keep it on their list of diversion airports. Around it lie treeless tundra, volcanic peaks and Izembek Lagoon, where vast eelgrass beds feed migrating geese every autumn.","w":"A thin neck of land near the peninsula's tip; a long runway beside a wide bay; snow-streaked volcanic cones nearby.","f":["Nearly all the world's Pacific brant geese stop at Izembek Lagoon on their autumn migration.","Frosty Peak, a volcano about 1,900 m high, rises just south-west of the village.","No roads link Cold Bay to the rest of Alaska; everything arrives by sea or air."],"q":{"q":"What does tiny Cold Bay have that transpacific flights value?","a":"A runway over 3 km long","x":["A large passenger terminal","An oil refinery"]}},"Q80022":{"t":"A tundra town in a delta of a thousand lakes","s":"Bethel sits on the Kuskokwim River in one of the flattest landscapes on the continent: the Yukon–Kuskokwim Delta, a green-brown tundra so full of ponds and lakes that from above it looks like lace. No road connects the town to the rest of Alaska. Moravian missionaries founded it in 1885 beside a Yup'ik village.","w":"A small town on a broad, winding river in flat tundra shattered into countless small lakes.","f":["In winter the frozen Kuskokwim becomes an ice road linking Bethel with villages up and down the river.","The Yukon–Kuskokwim Delta is one of the world's largest river deltas and a huge nesting ground for waterbirds."],"q":{"q":"How do people drive between Bethel and nearby villages in winter?","a":"On the frozen river","x":["On a paved highway","Through a tunnel"]}},"Q79813":{"t":"A gravel finger pointing into the Chukchi Sea","s":"Point Hope sits near the end of a long, low gravel spit that juts westward into the Chukchi Sea — its Iñupiaq name, Tikiġaq, means 'index finger'. It's one of the oldest continuously inhabited places in North America: the nearby Ipiutak site holds the remains of hundreds of houses about 2,000 years old. Bowhead whaling is still at the heart of life here.","w":"A thin, pale triangular spit jutting west into grey sea, with lagoons behind it; flat tundra and low hills inland.","f":["In the 1970s the village moved a few kilometres east because the sea was eroding the tip of the spit.","For much of the year the point is surrounded by sea ice."],"q":{"q":"What does Point Hope's Iñupiaq name, Tikiġaq, mean?","a":"Index finger","x":["Whale's tail","Northern lights"]}},"Q79809":{"t":"The northernmost town in the United States","s":"Utqiaġvik, long known as Barrow, clings to the edge of the Arctic Ocean on flat tundra dotted with lakes. Just to the north-east, Point Barrow is the northernmost tip of the United States, where the Chukchi and Beaufort seas meet. Here the sun stays below the horizon for about two months in winter, and in summer it doesn't set for nearly three months.","w":"A small town on a flat, lake-spotted coastal plain at the Arctic shore; a thin gravel spit curving north-east to the point.","f":["The polar night here lasts from mid-November to late January.","The town was named Barrow after Sir John Barrow of the British Admiralty; in 2016 it officially took back its Iñupiaq name."],"q":{"q":"How long does the polar night last in Utqiaġvik?","a":"About two months","x":["About two weeks","About five months"]}},"Q79996":{"t":"A gold-rush town where the gold lay in the beach","s":"Nome faces the grey waters of Norton Sound from the edge of the treeless Seward Peninsula. In 1899 prospectors discovered gold in the sand of the beach itself, and within a year a tent city of some 20,000 people crowded the shore. No road links Nome to the rest of Alaska, yet every March it is the finish of the Iditarod sled-dog race.","w":"A small grid town on a straight coast of Norton Sound; bare tundra hills and snowy mountains inland.","f":["People still pan and dredge for gold along Nome's beaches in summer.","The Iditarod trail runs more than 1,500 km from near Anchorage to Nome."],"q":{"q":"Where was the gold of Nome's 1899–1900 rush famously found?","a":"In the beach sand","x":["Deep in a mountain mine","Under a glacier"]}},"Q80037":{"t":"A fjord port where the Alaska pipeline meets the sea","s":"Valdez sits at the head of a narrow fjord off Prince William Sound, boxed in by steep, glacier-hung peaks of the Chugach Mountains. Across the water lies the terminal of the Trans-Alaska Pipeline, which carries oil some 1,300 km from Prudhoe Bay on the Arctic coast to this harbour, ice-free all year. Winters here bring several metres of snow.","w":"A small town at the head of a long, steep-walled fjord; glaciers on the mountains; a tank farm on the opposite shore.","f":["A Spanish expedition named the place in 1790 after Antonio Valdés, Spain's naval minister.","The pipeline was completed in 1977 and crosses three mountain ranges on its way south."],"q":{"q":"Who named Valdez in 1790?","a":"A Spanish naval expedition","x":["Russian fur traders","Captain James Cook"]}},"Q29445":{"t":"A capital between an icefield and the sea, with no road in","s":"Juneau is squeezed onto a thin shelf between Gastineau Channel and mountains that rise almost straight from the rooftops. Behind them spreads the Juneau Icefield, feeding dozens of glaciers; one, the Mendenhall, flows down nearly to the suburbs by the airport. No road connects the city to the rest of North America. It all began with a gold strike in 1880.","w":"A narrow town at the foot of steep mountains on a sea channel; a broad glacier tongue reaching down to a lake near the airport.","f":["Juneau's municipal area is larger than the whole state of Rhode Island.","The 1880 gold find was made by Joe Juneau and Richard Harris, guided by the Tlingit leader Kowee."],"q":{"q":"How do you get to Juneau from the rest of the US?","a":"Only by air or sea","x":["By highway from Anchorage","By railway from Canada"]}},"Q79638":{"t":"A river town under the aurora, in the heart of Alaska","s":"Fairbanks lies deep in interior Alaska, where the Chena River meets the broad, braided Tanana, surrounded by spruce forest and low hills. It began as a trading post in 1901, and a gold strike the next year turned it into a boom town. Its climate is extreme: winters can drop below −40 °C, while summer nights barely get dark.","w":"A spread-out town on flat forested lowland beside a wide, braided river; the white wall of the Alaska Range far to the south.","f":["Since 1906 a Midnight Sun baseball game has been played here around the solstice without floodlights.","In deep winter, cold still air can trap a layer of ice fog over the city.","The town was named after Charles Fairbanks, a senator who later became US vice-president."],"q":{"q":"What is played in Fairbanks near midnight each June without floodlights?","a":"A baseball game","x":["A football match","A golf tournament"]}},"Q1013046":{"t":"An oil field on Arctic tundra, where the pipeline starts","s":"Prudhoe Bay is less a town than a work camp on the flat Arctic coast beside the Beaufort Sea. Beneath it lies the largest oil field in North America, found in 1968, and from here the Trans-Alaska Pipeline begins its journey of about 1,300 km south. Around it the tundra is speckled with thousands of lakes, many stretched in the same direction.","w":"Flat tundra peppered with elongated lakes; a web of gravel roads and pads; sea ice or grey sea to the north.","f":["Sir John Franklin named the bay in 1826 after his friend Algernon Percy, Lord Prudhoe.","The Dalton Highway, one of the most remote roads in the US, ends here at Deadhorse."],"q":{"q":"Who named Prudhoe Bay in 1826?","a":"Sir John Franklin","x":["James Cook","Vitus Bering"]}},"Q3238":{"t":"A capital in a bowl of pine-covered mountains","s":"Tegucigalpa fills a tight mountain basin about 1,000 m up, its houses spilling up the slopes from the Choluteca River. Across the river lies its twin, Comayagüela; together they form the capital. The town grew from a silver-mining settlement founded in 1578 and became Honduras's capital in 1880. Pine-covered ridges ring the whole bowl.","w":"A dense city in a small mountain bowl, climbing the surrounding slopes; a river through the middle; the airport in a valley to the south.","f":["Toncontín airport lies among hills, and pilots must make a tight, low turn on the approach.","The capital moved here from the colonial city of Comayagua, about 80 km to the north-west."],"q":{"q":"Which city faces Tegucigalpa across the Choluteca River?","a":"Comayagüela","x":["Comayagua","San Pedro Sula"]}},"Q10717":{"t":"A Dutch-drained capital hiding behind a seawall","s":"Georgetown lies at the mouth of the Demerara River on a coast so low that much of the city sits below the level of high tide. Dutch colonists drained it with canals and sluices, and a long seawall still holds back the Atlantic. The sea here is brown, stained by silt carried along the coast. The city took its present name in 1812, after King George III.","w":"A flat city at the mouth of a brown river on a straight, muddy coast; a long floating bridge across the river just upstream.","f":["The Demerara Harbour Bridge, nearly 2 km long, rests on floating pontoons.","St. George's Cathedral is one of the tallest wooden churches in the world."],"q":{"q":"What protects low-lying Georgetown from the Atlantic?","a":"A long seawall","x":["Coral reefs","High sand dunes"]}},"Q34261":{"t":"A capital at the head of a vast horseshoe bay","s":"Port-au-Prince sits at the innermost point of the Gulf of Gonâve, a great horseshoe of sea between Haiti's two long peninsulas. The city climbs from the waterfront up steep slopes towards the mountains of the Massif de la Selle. Out in the gulf lies the long, flat island of Gonâve. The French founded the city in 1749; Haiti won its independence in 1804.","w":"A city at the head of a wide bay, rising up mountain slopes to the south-east; a long island in the gulf to the west.","f":["The bay is said to take its name from a French ship, Le Prince, that anchored here in 1706.","Pic la Selle, Haiti's highest peak at about 2,680 m, stands in the mountains south-east of the city."],"q":{"q":"Which island lies out in the bay off Port-au-Prince?","a":"Gonâve","x":["Tortuga","Île-à-Vache"]}},"Q3001":{"t":"A wooden Dutch colonial capital on a jungle river","s":"Paramaribo lines the west bank of the wide, brown Suriname River, some 15 km from the Atlantic. Its old centre of white wooden houses and churches is a UNESCO World Heritage site. In 1667, under the Treaty of Breda, the Dutch kept this colony while England kept New Amsterdam — today's New York. Beyond the city, rainforest covers most of the country.","w":"A low city on the west bank of a broad brown river near the coast; a long bridge across it; unbroken green forest inland.","f":["The Jules Wijdenbosch Bridge across the Suriname River is about 1.5 km long.","Forest covers more than 90 per cent of Suriname, one of the highest shares of any country."],"q":{"q":"Under the 1667 treaty the Dutch kept Suriname. What did England keep?","a":"New Amsterdam (New York)","x":["Jamaica","Barbados"]}},"Q5582862":{"t":"The Inca capital, 3,400 m up in an Andean valley","s":"Cuzco fills a narrow valley high in the Andes, about 3,400 m above sea level, a sea of terracotta roofs between bare, brown-green slopes. This was the capital of the Inca Empire, which stretched along the Andes from today's Ecuador to Chile. The Spanish arrived in 1533 and built their churches on Inca stone walls. Machu Picchu lies about 75 km to the north-west.","w":"A city of red-brown roofs filling a high, narrow valley; bare mountains around; a runway squeezed along the valley floor.","f":["Tradition says the Inca city was laid out in the shape of a puma.","The historic centre has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1983.","The snowy peak of Ausangate, over 6,300 m high, rises about 100 km to the south-east."],"q":{"q":"Which empire had its capital at Cuzco?","a":"The Inca Empire","x":["The Aztec Empire","The Maya"]}},"Q272795":{"t":"A green oasis at Peru's southern tip","s":"Tacna is a patch of green in one of the driest places on Earth, the northern edge of the Atacama Desert, where rain may add up to only a few millimetres a year. The city lives off the thin Caplina River coming down from the Andes, whose snowy peaks line the eastern horizon. The border with Chile runs only about 35 km to the south.","w":"A mid-sized city in otherwise bare, beige desert; green irrigated fields along a narrow valley; the Andes rising to the east.","f":["Tacna is the southernmost city in Peru.","Olive groves around the city make the region one of Peru's main olive producers."],"q":{"q":"Which country's border lies just south of Tacna?","a":"Chile","x":["Bolivia","Argentina"]}},"Q214173":{"t":"An oval colonial core beside a vast mud-brick ruin","s":"Trujillo sits on the desert coast of northern Peru, its old centre neatly enclosed by an oval avenue that follows the line of the colonial city wall. Just west, towards the sea, sprawl the eroded mud-brick ruins of Chan Chan, capital of the Chimú kingdom and the largest adobe city of the pre-Columbian Americas. The Spanish founded Trujillo in 1534.","w":"A city on a desert coastal plain between the sea and bare mountains; green irrigated valley fields around it.","f":["Chan Chan's ruins cover about 20 km² and have been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1986.","The city is named after Trujillo in Spain, home town of Francisco Pizarro.","Fishermen at nearby Huanchaco still head out on small reed boats called caballitos de totora."],"q":{"q":"Which people built Chan Chan, near Trujillo?","a":"The Chimú","x":["The Inca","The Nazca"]}},"Q840712":{"t":"Vineyards and giant dunes in the Peruvian desert","s":"Ica is a green oasis in the coastal desert of southern Peru, watered by a river from the Andes and surrounded by huge sand dunes. Its irrigated valley is the heart of Peru's grape growing and the home of pisco, the local grape brandy. Just west of the city, the tiny lagoon of Huacachina sits in a hollow ringed by towering dunes.","w":"A city in a green valley strip cutting through pale desert; big, sharp-crested dunes to the west.","f":["The famous Nazca Lines lie roughly 100 km to the south-east, though they are too fine to see from cruise height.","Rain is so rare here that the valley depends almost entirely on water from the Andes."],"q":{"q":"What is Huacachina, near Ica?","a":"A small lagoon among dunes","x":["A volcanic crater lake","A salt flat"]}},"Q2933":{"t":"The 'Mother of Cities' on a bend of the Paraguay River","s":"Asunción spreads along the east bank of the Paraguay River where it curves around a wide bay. Across the water begins the Chaco, a flat, green-brown plain of marshes and scrub running west for hundreds of kilometres. Founded in 1537, it is one of the oldest cities in South America, and its settlers went on to found many others, including Buenos Aires for good in 1580.","w":"A city on the outside of a big river bend, beside a rounded bay; flat, wet, scrubby plains across the river to the west.","f":["The Pilcomayo River joins the Paraguay just downstream of the city.","Santa Cruz de la Sierra, now in Bolivia, was also founded by an expedition from Asunción, in 1561."],"q":{"q":"Why is Asunción called the 'Mother of Cities'?","a":"Its settlers founded many other cities","x":["It is the oldest city in the Americas","It has the oldest cathedral"]}},"Q3274":{"t":"A lakeside capital dotted with round crater lakes","s":"Managua stretches along the southern shore of Lake Managua, and within the city itself you can spot small, round crater lakes — old volcanic vents now filled with water. At the far north-western end of the lake rises the near-perfect cone of Momotombo. The city became Nicaragua's capital in 1852, a compromise between the rival cities of León and Granada.","w":"A low, sprawling city on a big lake's south shore; round blue crater lakes in and around it; a volcanic cone to the north-west.","f":["Laguna de Asososca, one of the crater lakes, supplies part of the city's drinking water.","Lake Nicaragua, the largest lake in Central America, lies about 30 km to the south-east."],"q":{"q":"Why was Managua made Nicaragua's capital in 1852?","a":"As a compromise between rival cities","x":["It had the best harbour","It was the largest city"]}},"Q3306":{"t":"Where ships queue for the Panama Canal","s":"Look out to sea off Panama City and you'll often see dozens of ships at anchor, waiting their turn for the canal. The city's towers line a curving bay right beside the canal's Pacific entrance. Founded in 1519, the original town was the first European city on the Pacific coast of the Americas. The canal itself opened in 1914 and cuts about 80 km across the isthmus.","w":"A high-rise city along a curving bay; ships dotted offshore; a canal channel and a bridge just west of the city.","f":["Oddly, the canal's Pacific end lies further east than its Atlantic end.","The Amador Causeway, linking small islands at the canal mouth, was built with rock dug out for the canal.","Locks lift ships about 26 m to Gatún Lake in the middle of the isthmus."],"q":{"q":"Which end of the Panama Canal lies further east?","a":"The Pacific end","x":["The Atlantic end","Both lie on the same longitude"]}},"Q124739":{"t":"A city pressed right up against the US border","s":"Tijuana fills a jumble of mesas and canyons in Mexico's far north-west corner, and its northern edge is a hard, straight line: the border with the United States, with San Diego beyond. The airport's runway runs just south of that line, so close that a footbridge lets passengers walk straight in from the US side. The city was officially founded in 1889.","w":"A dense city on hilly, canyon-cut ground, cut off to the north by a sharp straight line; the Pacific to the west.","f":["The San Ysidro crossing between Tijuana and San Diego is one of the busiest land border crossings in the world.","The Tijuana River flows north across the border and reaches the Pacific in the US."],"q":{"q":"What runs right alongside Tijuana airport's runway?","a":"The US–Mexico border","x":["The Pacific shore","An irrigation canal"]}},"Q61302":{"t":"A high-desert capital, gateway to the Copper Canyon","s":"Chihuahua sits on a wide, dry plateau about 1,400 m up, ringed by bare, rocky ranges; it is the capital of Mexico's largest state. It was founded in 1709 as a silver-mining town. From here the Chihuahua–Pacific railway heads south-west across the Sierra Madre and through the Copper Canyon, a system of gorges that is deeper in places than the Grand Canyon.","w":"A city on a flat, tan high-desert basin, encircled by bare rocky ranges; the dark Sierra Madre far to the west.","f":["The Chihuahua–Pacific railway, completed in 1961, crosses dozens of bridges and runs through dozens of tunnels.","The tiny dog breed takes its name from the state of Chihuahua."],"q":{"q":"What sets out from Chihuahua through the Copper Canyon?","a":"A railway to the Pacific coast","x":["A navigable river","A cable car line"]}},"Q40194":{"t":"A Pacific port on a peninsula, just south of the tropic","s":"Mazatlán sits on a rocky peninsula on Mexico's Pacific coast, with a long seafront curving north along the beach and three small islands just offshore. The Tropic of Cancer passes only about 25 km to the north, so at the June solstice the midday sun here is almost directly overhead. The name comes from Nahuatl and means 'place of deer'.","w":"A city on a knobbly peninsula and along a long, straight beach; small islands offshore; green hills and the Sierra Madre inland.","f":["The lighthouse on Cerro del Crestón shines from about 157 m above the sea, one of the highest in the world.","Ferries cross the Gulf of California from here to La Paz, in Baja California."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Mazatlán' mean in Nahuatl?","a":"Place of deer","x":["Place of fish","Place of the sun"]}},"Q273014":{"t":"An oil port among lagoons at the mouth of the Pánuco","s":"Tampico lies a few kilometres up the Pánuco River from the Gulf of Mexico, and water is everywhere: lagoons within the city, marshes around it, the wide brown river on its southern side. In the early 1900s oil struck nearby turned it into one of the busiest oil ports in the world. Look for the long cable-stayed bridge across the river.","w":"A city between lagoons and a wide river near the coast; marshes all around; a long bridge across the river to the south.","f":["The modern city was refounded in 1823 near the site of an older colonial settlement.","Laguna del Carpintero, a lagoon right in the city, is home to crocodiles."],"q":{"q":"What made Tampico boom in the early 1900s?","a":"Oil","x":["Silver","Sugar"]}},"Q81398":{"t":"A horseshoe bay ringed by a hillside amphitheatre","s":"Acapulco wraps around one of the most perfectly shaped bays on the Pacific, a deep blue horseshoe with the city climbing the steep hills on all sides like the seats of an amphitheatre. For some 250 years, from 1565, Spanish galleons sailed from here to Manila, carrying silver west and bringing back silk and porcelain from Asia.","w":"A deep, rounded bay almost closed by headlands; white buildings climbing the green hills around it; long beaches to the east.","f":["Cliff divers at La Quebrada plunge about 35 m into a narrow rocky inlet.","The star-shaped Fort of San Diego was built in the 1600s to guard the galleon harbour."],"q":{"q":"Which Asian port did Acapulco's galleons sail to?","a":"Manila","x":["Macau","Nagasaki"]}},"Q84":{"t":"A dark river coiling through a sea of lights","s":"Find the Thames and London falls into place: a brown ribbon looping east through an enormous grey-green sprawl, then widening towards the North Sea. The M25 motorway draws a rough ring around it all. The Romans bridged the river here almost two thousand years ago, and the city has spread outward from that crossing ever since.","w":"The looping Thames running east to its estuary; square reservoirs glint beside Heathrow on the western edge.","f":["The M25 ring motorway is about 188 km long; at night it traces a faint loop of lights around the city.","The Thames is tidal right through London: in the centre the water rises and falls by several metres twice a day.","The Romans founded Londinium on the north bank of the Thames around AD 50."],"q":{"q":"What was London called in Roman times?","a":"Londinium","x":["Lutetia","Eboracum"]}},"Q406":{"t":"One city, two continents, one blue thread between them","s":"Look for a thin blue line cutting a vast city in two: the Bosphorus, with Europe on one bank and Asia on the other. The Golden Horn curls into the old town on the European side. As Constantinople, this was an imperial capital for more than a thousand years.","w":"A narrow strait running north–south between the Black Sea and the Sea of Marmara.","f":["The Bosphorus is about 30 km long and well under a kilometre wide at its narrowest.","Three suspension bridges cross the strait; from cruise they show as fine pale lines.","At night the two shores glow as one ribbon of light along the water."],"q":{"q":"Which strait splits Istanbul between Europe and Asia?","a":"The Bosphorus","x":["The Dardanelles","The Strait of Messina"]}},"Q3692":{"t":"A grid of lights dropped into the Arabian desert","s":"Riyadh sits close to the middle of the Arabian Peninsula, hundreds of kilometres from any sea. From above it is a vast beige grid of broad avenues, edged on the west by the green thread of Wadi Hanifa. Its name comes from the Arabic for 'gardens' — the oases this valley once watered.","w":"A huge rectangular street grid in brown desert, with the long Tuwaiq escarpment running north–south to the west.","f":["The Tuwaiq escarpment west of the city curves for roughly 800 km across central Arabia.","On Riyadh's north-western edge, the mud-brick At-Turaif district of Diriyah is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","The city stands about 600 metres above sea level on the Najd plateau."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Riyadh mean in Arabic?","a":"Gardens","x":["Sands","Stars"]}},"Q5465":{"t":"A flat-topped mountain near the tip of Africa","s":"Look for a mountain sliced flat like a table, with the city curled around its feet and the sea on both sides. South of it, a rocky finger of land runs down to the Cape of Good Hope. On many summer days a white cloud spills over the summit like a tablecloth, then melts away on the way down.","w":"A flat grey mountain block above a curved bay, a rocky peninsula running south, and a low island out in the bay.","f":["Table Mountain's flat summit reaches about 1,085 metres — roughly a tenth of your cruising height.","The low island in Table Bay is Robben Island, a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1999.","Cape Town is South Africa's oldest city, founded in 1652 as a supply station for Dutch ships."],"q":{"q":"In which year did the Dutch East India Company found Cape Town?","a":"1652","x":["1588","1714"]}},"Q649":{"t":"Rings within rings, like a cut tree trunk","s":"Moscow is laid out like a tree trunk seen in cross-section: ring roads nested one inside another around the old centre, with radial highways running out like spokes. At night the whole pattern glows amber and white. Through it winds the Moskva River in big, lazy loops. The city first appears in a chronicle in 1147.","w":"Concentric road rings with straight spokes radiating out; the winding Moskva River crosses the centre from north-west to south-east.","f":["The MKAD, Moscow's outer ring motorway, is about 109 km long.","The inner Garden Ring runs for about 16 km around the historic centre.","The city and its river share one name: in Russian, both are called Moskva."],"q":{"q":"In which year is Moscow first mentioned in a chronicle?","a":"1147","x":["1240","1380"]}},"Q8673":{"t":"A megacity stitched together across a lagoon","s":"Lagos is as much water as land. Look for a broad lagoon behind a long, straight Atlantic beach, with the city spilling over the islands and shores all around it. A thin bridge strides across the water for kilometres. Portuguese sailors named the place after these lakes, and today it is one of Africa's largest cities.","w":"A wide brown-grey lagoon behind a ruler-straight surf coast, dense city on its islands and a long bridge across the water.","f":["The Third Mainland Bridge runs about 11.8 km across Lagos Lagoon, linking the islands to the mainland.","Lagos was Nigeria's capital until 1991, when the government moved to Abuja.","In Yoruba, the city's own name is Eko."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Lagos mean in Portuguese?","a":"Lakes","x":["Harbours","Palms"]}},"Q220":{"t":"The Eternal City, framed by old volcanoes","s":"Rome lies about 25 km inland on the Tiber, which snakes through the centre in tight bends. A motorway ring, the Grande Raccordo Anulare, loops around the city. Look beyond it: the round lakes to the north-west and south-east fill the craters of extinct volcanoes. Tradition dates the city's founding to 753 BC.","w":"A sprawl on a winding river, framed by the round crater lakes of Bracciano to the north-west and the Alban Hills to the south-east.","f":["The Grande Raccordo Anulare ring road is about 68 km long.","Vatican City, the world's smallest country, lies entirely inside Rome.","Lake Bracciano, north-west of the city, fills an old volcanic basin about 9 km across."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Rome?","a":"The Tiber","x":["The Arno","The Po"]}},"Q3870":{"t":"A highland capital with lions on its doorstep","s":"Nairobi sits on a high plateau at about 1,800 metres, just east of the Great Rift Valley. It began in 1899 as a depot on the railway from Mombasa to Lake Victoria, and its name comes from a Maasai phrase for 'cool water'. On its southern edge, open grassland is a national park where lions and giraffes roam within sight of town.","w":"A dense city with an open tawny plain — the national park — on its southern side; the Ngong Hills rise to the south-west.","f":["Nairobi National Park begins only about 7 km from the city centre.","West of the city the land drops sharply into the Great Rift Valley.","The city lies less than 150 km south of the equator, yet its altitude keeps nights cool."],"q":{"q":"What does the Maasai name behind 'Nairobi' mean?","a":"Cool water","x":["Red earth","High hill"]}},"Q85":{"t":"Where the green Nile valley opens into the delta","s":"Cairo marks the hinge of Egypt. South of the city the Nile runs in a narrow green strip through desert; just to the north it fans out into the delta. The city fills both banks, with the Giza plateau and its pyramids on the western edge, where the houses stop and the Sahara begins.","w":"A huge grey-beige city on the Nile, exactly where the thin green valley widens into the triangle of the delta.","f":["Cairo was founded as al-Qahira, 'the Victorious', by the Fatimid dynasty in 969.","The line between irrigated fields and bare desert around Cairo is razor-sharp, even from cruise height.","The Great Pyramid of Giza was the tallest human-made structure on Earth for more than 3,800 years."],"q":{"q":"Which dynasty founded Cairo in 969?","a":"The Fatimids","x":["The Ottomans","The Mamluks"]}},"Q90":{"t":"An oval city inside a ring, with a river in loops","s":"Paris proper is compact: a neat oval held inside the Boulevard Périphérique, with suburbs sprawling far beyond it. The Seine enters from the south-east, curves through the centre past two small islands, then leaves in a series of great loops towards the Channel. The Romans called the settlement Lutetia.","w":"An oval ring road enclosing the dense centre, with the Seine snaking through it and meandering west in big loops.","f":["The Boulevard Périphérique is about 35 km long and follows the line of the city's 19th-century fortifications.","Inside the ring, Paris covers only about 105 km².","The city began on the Île de la Cité, the larger of the two islands in the Seine."],"q":{"q":"About how long is the Boulevard Périphérique around Paris?","a":"About 35 km","x":["About 70 km","About 12 km"]}},"Q1899":{"t":"Green hills on one bank, flat plain on the other","s":"The Dnipro splits Kyiv in two, and its banks could hardly differ more: steep wooded hills on the west, where the old city grew, and low flat land to the east. Sandy islands and side channels lie between them. Upstream, the river swells into a long reservoir. A thousand years ago this was the capital of Kyivan Rus.","w":"A broad river with sandy islands running north–south; the hilly old city on the west bank, newer districts on the flat east bank.","f":["Just north of the city the Dnipro widens into the Kyiv Reservoir, more than 100 km long.","The Kyiv Pechersk Lavra, a cave monastery founded in 1051, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Large wooded islands in the Dnipro, such as Trukhaniv, sit right opposite the city centre."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Kyiv?","a":"The Dnipro","x":["The Dniester","The Southern Bug"]}},"Q612":{"t":"A palm tree drawn on the sea in sand","s":"Few cities are so easy to spot. Off Dubai's coast, a palm tree has been drawn on the sea in sand, its fronds ringed by a crescent breakwater. Further out, a scatter of islets imitates a map of the world. Onshore, towers line the coast for kilometres, and the old town hugs a winding creek.","w":"The palm-shaped island off a straight desert coast, with the cluster of 'World' islands further out to sea.","f":["Palm Jumeirah was built from sand dredged from the sea floor and is roughly 5 km across.","The World islands are an archipelago of about 300 artificial islands laid out like a world map.","Dubai Creek, a natural inlet, was the heart of the old pearling and trading town."],"q":{"q":"What shape is Dubai's best-known artificial island?","a":"A palm tree","x":["A seashell","A falcon"]}},"Q2807":{"t":"A capital on a high plateau, with snowy peaks behind","s":"Madrid lies almost at the centre of Spain, on a dry plateau more than 600 metres above the sea. Look for ring motorways drawing ever wider loops around it, and for the Sierra de Guadarrama to the north-west, often white-capped in winter. Philip II moved his royal court to this small town in 1561.","w":"A dense city on a tan plateau ringed by motorways, with the Guadarrama mountains rising to the north-west.","f":["At about 650 metres, Madrid is the highest capital city in the European Union.","The Puerta del Sol in the centre is Kilometre Zero for Spain's radial national roads.","The city's own river, the Manzanares, is so small it barely shows from the air."],"q":{"q":"Which mountains rise north-west of Madrid?","a":"Sierra de Guadarrama","x":["Sierra Nevada","Picos de Europa"]}},"Q71":{"t":"Where the Rhône slips out of a crescent lake","s":"Geneva sits at the south-western tip of Lake Geneva, where the Rhône leaves the lake and heads for France. The city is almost surrounded by French territory. On a clear day look south-east to the snowy Mont Blanc massif, and north-west to the long, dark wall of the Jura.","w":"The narrow pointed end of a large crescent-shaped lake, with a river leaving it; the Jura ridge lies to the north-west.","f":["The canton of Geneva shares about 100 km of border with France and only a few kilometres with the rest of Switzerland.","Underground, just west of the city, CERN's particle accelerator runs in a 27 km ring beneath the French border.","Lake Geneva is about 73 km long, curving like a crescent between Switzerland and France."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows out of Lake Geneva at Geneva?","a":"The Rhône","x":["The Rhine","The Aare"]}},"Q1754":{"t":"A city on islands where a lake meets the sea","s":"Stockholm is spread over fourteen islands at the point where Lake Mälaren drains into the Baltic. From above it is a jigsaw of water, granite and dark pine forest. To the east the archipelago takes over: tens of thousands of islands and skerries scattered towards the open sea. The city is first recorded in 1252.","w":"A jigsaw of islands and channels between a big lake to the west and a vast archipelago spreading east to the Baltic.","f":["The Stockholm archipelago contains roughly 30,000 islands, islets and rocks.","Lake Mälaren sits slightly higher than the Baltic, and its water drains out through the city's channels.","The land here is still rising after the last Ice Age, by around half a centimetre a year."],"q":{"q":"About how many islands make up the Stockholm archipelago?","a":"About 30,000","x":["About 300","About 3,000"]}},"Q3718":{"t":"The westernmost city of mainland Africa","s":"Dakar crowns the Cap-Vert peninsula, a hook of land pushing out into the Atlantic, the farthest west that mainland Africa reaches. From above, the city fills the whole triangle, with sea on three sides and the ochre Sahel behind. Just offshore lies the small island of Gorée, a UNESCO site and memorial to the Atlantic slave trade.","w":"A triangular peninsula jutting west into the Atlantic, built over almost entirely, with a small island off its south-eastern shore.","f":["Pointe des Almadies, on Dakar's peninsula, is the westernmost point of mainland Africa.","Two low volcanic hills, the Mamelles, rise near the western shore; one carries a lighthouse.","Gorée Island became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1978."],"q":{"q":"Dakar stands on which peninsula?","a":"Cap-Vert","x":["Cap Bon","Sinai"]}},"Q34647":{"t":"A city built on gold, its old mine dumps in view","s":"Johannesburg exists because of gold. It sprang up in 1886, after a rich reef was found along the Witwatersrand, a long east–west ridge on the high veld. Look along the city's southern side for flat-topped yellowish mounds — old mine dumps — strung out in a line that traces the reef buried below.","w":"A sprawling city on a high grassy plateau; a line of pale yellow mine dumps runs east–west along its south.","f":["Johannesburg lies about 1,750 metres above sea level.","Witwatersrand means 'ridge of white waters' in Afrikaans.","Some Witwatersrand gold mines reach about 4 km below the surface, among the deepest in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which discovery led to the founding of Johannesburg in 1886?","a":"Gold","x":["Diamonds","Coal"]}},"Q727":{"t":"A canal city on land won from the water","s":"Amsterdam grew from a 13th-century dam on the river Amstel, and water still shapes everything you see: the broad IJ channel along its north side, the grey expanse of the Markermeer to the east, and a flat patchwork of polders all around. Many of those green rectangles lie below sea level.","w":"Flat green polders cut by straight ditches, the city on a broad channel, and a large grey lake to its east.","f":["Schiphol airport lies about 3 metres below sea level, on the bed of a lake drained in the mid-1800s.","The ring of 17th-century canals became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2010.","The North Sea Canal, opened in 1876, links the city's harbour with the sea about 25 km to the west."],"q":{"q":"Which river gave Amsterdam its name?","a":"The Amstel","x":["The Maas","The IJssel"]}},"Q7903":{"t":"A white city strung along the Atlantic surf","s":"Casablanca stretches along a flat Atlantic shore, a pale spread of buildings pressed right up to the surf line. Look for the long breakwaters of its port, one of the largest in Africa, curving out to sea. The name is Spanish for 'white house', echoing the Arabic Dar al-Bayda, and from the air the city does look mostly white.","w":"A pale city along a straight Atlantic coast, with a big harbour enclosed by long breakwaters.","f":["The minaret of the Hassan II Mosque, on the seafront, is about 210 metres tall.","Part of the Hassan II Mosque stands on a platform built out over the Atlantic.","Casablanca is Morocco's largest city, though the capital is Rabat, about 90 km up the coast."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Casablanca mean?","a":"White house","x":["White sands","Blue harbour"]}},"Q64":{"t":"A flat city of lakes, forests and one big green void","s":"Berlin spreads across a flat, sandy plain left behind by Ice Age glaciers, and water is everywhere: the Spree winding through the centre, a chain of Havel lakes in the west, the broad Müggelsee in the east. Dark pine forests reach deep into the city. Look too for a vast open rectangle south of the centre — Tempelhof, an airport turned park.","w":"A huge flat city scattered with lakes and dark forest; a large open green rectangle lies just south of the centre.","f":["The A10 Berliner Ring motorway around the city is about 196 km long, one of Europe's longest ring roads.","Tempelhofer Feld, the old Tempelhof airport, is now an open park of about 300 hectares.","Berlin covers about 890 km², more than eight times the area of Paris inside its ring road."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through the centre of Berlin?","a":"The Spree","x":["The Elbe","The Oder"]}},"Q3838":{"t":"Two capitals facing each other across a great river","s":"Kinshasa lies on the south bank of the Congo, where the river spreads into a vast, lake-like pool dotted with sandy islands. On the far bank is Brazzaville, the capital of another country. Just downstream, the river leaves the pool and plunges into a long run of rapids. Henry Morton Stanley set up a trading post here in 1881 and named it Léopoldville.","w":"A sprawling city on the south shore of a broad, island-studded widening of the Congo River, with another city on the opposite bank.","f":["Apart from Rome and the Vatican, Kinshasa and Brazzaville are the world's closest pair of capital cities.","Pool Malebo, the river's widening here, is about 35 km long and more than 20 km wide.","Below the city, the Livingstone Falls — a chain of rapids — lower the Congo by about 270 metres."],"q":{"q":"Which capital faces Kinshasa across the Congo River?","a":"Brazzaville","x":["Bangui","Luanda"]}},"Q1524":{"t":"A white city poured into a basin down to the sea","s":"Athens pours across a basin ringed by bare mountains, spilling down to the Saronic Gulf at the port of Piraeus. From above it is an almost unbroken white-and-beige carpet. Look at the mountain to the north-east: pale scars on its flanks are marble quarries, worked since antiquity, when they supplied the stone for the Parthenon.","w":"A dense white city in a mountain-rimmed basin opening south-west onto the sea, with ship wakes fanning out from Piraeus.","f":["The Parthenon's marble came from Mount Penteli, about 15 km north-east of the Acropolis.","Piraeus has served as the port of Athens since the 5th century BC, when the Long Walls linked the two.","The city takes its name from the goddess Athena, its patron in Greek myth."],"q":{"q":"Which mountain supplied the marble for the Parthenon?","a":"Mount Penteli","x":["Mount Olympus","Mount Parnassus"]}},"Q1530":{"t":"A city threaded on the loops of the Tigris","s":"Baghdad is threaded on the Tigris, which swings through it in great looping bends, with dark palm groves along both banks. To the west the Euphrates comes within a few dozen kilometres: the two rivers of Mesopotamia draw closer here than almost anywhere else. The Abbasid caliph al-Mansur founded the city in 762 as a perfectly round walled town.","w":"A flat, dust-coloured city on a wide, deeply looping river, with dark green palm groves along its banks.","f":["Al-Mansur named his new round city Madinat al-Salam, the City of Peace.","Near Baghdad the Tigris and the Euphrates run less than 50 km apart.","In the 9th century Baghdad's House of Wisdom drew scholars who translated Greek science into Arabic."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Baghdad?","a":"The Tigris","x":["The Euphrates","The Orontes"]}},"Q3624":{"t":"A capital in the clouds, ringed by eucalyptus hills","s":"Addis Ababa sits on the Ethiopian Highlands at about 2,350 metres, one of the highest capitals in the world. The hills to its north are dark with eucalyptus, a tree brought in during the 1890s to give the young city firewood. Emperor Menelik II and Empress Taytu founded the city in 1886, near a group of hot springs.","w":"A sprawling city on a high green plateau, with dark eucalyptus-covered hills rising along its northern edge.","f":["The African Union has its headquarters in Addis Ababa.","The eucalyptus around the city came from Australia and now covers much of the Entoto hills.","At this altitude water boils at about 92 °C rather than 100 °C."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Addis Ababa mean in Amharic?","a":"New flower","x":["High city","Spring water"]}},"Q3616":{"t":"A city tilting down from a wall of mountains","s":"Tehran lies at the foot of the Alborz, a steep mountain wall that shuts the city off from the Caspian Sea to the north. The whole city tilts: its northern districts sit hundreds of metres higher than the southern ones, which fade into dry plain. To the north-east, look for a lone white cone above the range — Damavand, the highest peak in Iran.","w":"A huge grey city pressed against a high mountain wall to its north; a lone snowy volcanic cone stands out to the north-east.","f":["Mount Damavand, about 70 km north-east of the city, reaches roughly 5,600 metres.","Tehran became the capital in 1786 under Agha Mohammad Khan, founder of the Qajar dynasty.","Damavand is a volcano, and the highest in Asia."],"q":{"q":"Which volcano rises north-east of Tehran?","a":"Damavand","x":["Ararat","Sabalan"]}},"Q1741":{"t":"Where the Danube runs in straight parallel lines","s":"Vienna's river is easy to find but strange to look at: the Danube passes the city in straight parallel channels with a long, thin island between them. That is the work of engineers; the old river once wandered freely across its floodplain. To the west, the wooded hills of the Vienna Woods are the last foothills of the Alps.","w":"The Danube as two straight parallel channels with a slim island between them, north-east of the centre; forested hills to the west.","f":["Danube Island, the thin strip between the channels, is about 21 km long and was built for flood protection.","Vienna has working vineyards inside its city limits, and wine-making here goes back centuries.","For centuries Vienna was the main seat of the Habsburg dynasty."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Vienna?","a":"The Danube","x":["The Elbe","The Vltava"]}},"Q3640":{"t":"A capital on the bare steppe of Anatolia","s":"Ankara spreads over a bowl of hills in the middle of the Anatolian plateau, nearly 900 metres up and ringed by dry, tawny steppe. Its old name, Angora, lives on in the soft wool of goats, cats and rabbits once bred here. Far to the south you may catch a gleam of white: Lake Tuz, a vast, shallow salt lake.","w":"A large city in a bowl of low hills on bare, tan steppe; far to the south, the white sheet of a salt lake.","f":["Ankara replaced Istanbul as Turkey's capital in 1923.","In Roman times the city was Ancyra, capital of the province of Galatia.","Lake Tuz is so shallow that in summer much of it dries into a crust of white salt."],"q":{"q":"Which old name of Ankara was given to a soft wool?","a":"Angora","x":["Merino","Cashmere"]}},"Q1781":{"t":"Buda's hills and Pest's plain, stitched by bridges","s":"The Danube runs straight north to south through Budapest and splits it into two characters: the wooded hills of Buda on the west bank and the flat, dense grid of Pest on the east. A slim, leafy island, Margaret Island, sits in mid-river near the centre. The towns on the two banks were only merged into one city in 1873.","w":"A broad river flowing south, green hills on its west bank, flat city to the east, and a narrow island in mid-stream.","f":["Budapest sits on a geological fault that feeds more than a hundred thermal springs.","South of the city the Danube splits around Csepel Island, which is nearly 50 km long.","Margaret Island is about 2.5 km long and once held a medieval convent."],"q":{"q":"In which year were Buda, Óbuda and Pest united?","a":"1873","x":["1848","1896"]}},"Q2471":{"t":"An ancient capital on a high mountain plateau","s":"Sanaa lies in a high basin among the mountains of Yemen, at about 2,250 metres. From above it is an earth-coloured patch between bare, folded ridges. Its old city, packed with tall tower houses of mud brick and stone trimmed in white, has been inhabited for more than 2,500 years.","w":"An earth-toned city in a broad upland basin, hemmed in by bare, rugged mountains on its east and west.","f":["The Old City of Sanaa has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1986.","West of the city rises Jabal an-Nabi Shu'ayb, at about 3,660 m the highest peak in the Arabian Peninsula.","Sanaa's tower houses are several storeys tall, with white gypsum friezes framing their windows."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how high above sea level is Sanaa?","a":"About 2,250 m","x":["About 750 m","About 4,000 m"]}},"Q1492":{"t":"A perfect grid between the hills and the sea","s":"Barcelona fills a coastal plain boxed in by two rivers and a line of wooded hills. Its heart is unmistakable: the Eixample, a vast chessboard of near-identical blocks laid out in the 1860s. Look for one broad avenue slicing across it at an angle, and for the hill of Montjuïc rising straight up from the port.","w":"A grid of square blocks between green hills and the Mediterranean, crossed by a long diagonal avenue, with a rounded hill by the port.","f":["The Eixample was planned in 1859 by the engineer Ildefons Cerdà.","Each Eixample block is about 113 metres across, with its corners cut off at an angle.","The Avinguda Diagonal cuts across the grid for about 11 km."],"q":{"q":"Which hill rises beside Barcelona's port?","a":"Montjuïc","x":["Tibidabo","Montserrat"]}},"Q19660":{"t":"A flat city with a string of lakes along its north","s":"Bucharest lies on the flat Wallachian plain, about 60 km north of the Danube. Look along its northern side for a chain of lakes strung along the little Colentina river, like beads on a thread. The first document to mention the city was signed here in 1459 by Vlad III — better known as Vlad the Impaler.","w":"A large, flat city on an open plain, with a curving chain of small lakes along its northern edge.","f":["Its elegant boulevards once earned Bucharest the nickname 'Little Paris'.","Bucharest became the capital of Wallachia in 1659.","The Dâmbovița river crosses the centre in a straight, walled channel."],"q":{"q":"Which ruler signed the first document naming Bucharest, in 1459?","a":"Vlad the Impaler","x":["Stephen the Great","Michael the Brave"]}},"Q41183":{"t":"One of the world's oldest cities, on a limestone plain","s":"Aleppo is among the oldest continuously inhabited cities on Earth, a stop on caravan routes between the Mediterranean and Mesopotamia for thousands of years. From above it is a large pale city on a rolling limestone plain. Its old centre grew up around a citadel on an ancient mound. South-east of the city lies a shallow salt lake, Jabbul.","w":"A large pale city on a dry, rolling plain; a shallow salt lake glints to the south-east.","f":["The Ancient City of Aleppo has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1986.","The city has long been famous for soap made with olive and laurel oil, known worldwide as Aleppo soap.","Aleppo lies roughly 100 km inland from the Mediterranean."],"q":{"q":"Which salt lake lies south-east of Aleppo?","a":"Lake Jabbul","x":["Lake Tuz","Lake Urmia"]}},"Q3766":{"t":"An ancient oasis city beneath a bare mountain","s":"Damascus owes its existence to one small river. The Barada flows down from the Anti-Lebanon mountains and spreads into the Ghouta, an oasis of orchards that has fed the city for thousands of years. The city lies at the foot of bare Mount Qasioun, and in winter the snowy ridge of Mount Hermon stands out to the south-west.","w":"A large city between green orchards and desert, backed by a bare mountain on its north-west side.","f":["From 661 to 750 Damascus was the capital of the Umayyad Caliphate, which stretched from Spain to Central Asia.","Mount Hermon, south-west of the city, rises to about 2,800 metres and often keeps snow into spring.","The Ancient City of Damascus has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1979."],"q":{"q":"Which fabric takes its name from Damascus?","a":"Damask","x":["Muslin","Calico"]}},"Q72":{"t":"A city at the end of a long, slender lake","s":"Zürich sits at the northern tip of Lake Zurich, a slim lake that curves about 40 km south-east towards the Alps. The river Limmat drains out of it straight through the centre. On a clear day, look past the lake to the snowy wall of the Glarus Alps. The Romans knew the settlement as Turicum.","w":"The northern end of a long, narrow lake curving south-east, a river leaving it through the city, and the Alps beyond.","f":["Lake Zurich is about 40 km long but nowhere more than about 4 km wide.","The Uetliberg, the wooded ridge on the city's western edge, rises to about 870 metres.","Zürich is Switzerland's largest city, although the federal government sits in Bern."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows out of Lake Zurich through the city?","a":"The Limmat","x":["The Aare","The Reuss"]}},"Q597":{"t":"Mainland Europe's westernmost capital, on a wide estuary","s":"Lisbon climbs its hills on the north shore of the Tagus, just where the river's broad inland lake narrows into a channel to the Atlantic. Two long bridges cross the water: a red suspension bridge at the narrows and, upstream, a thin line of a bridge that runs on for kilometres. No capital in mainland Europe lies further west.","w":"A hilly city on the north shore of a broad estuary, with a long bridge across its upper reaches and another at the narrows.","f":["The Vasco da Gama Bridge across the Tagus is about 12 km long, one of the longest in Europe.","The inland part of the estuary is so wide that locals call it the Mar da Palha, the 'Sea of Straw'.","Cabo da Roca, west of Lisbon, is the westernmost point of mainland Europe."],"q":{"q":"Which river forms the broad estuary at Lisbon?","a":"The Tagus","x":["The Douro","The Guadiana"]}},"Q1963":{"t":"Where the Blue Nile and the White Nile become one","s":"Khartoum stands at one of the great river meetings of the world. The Blue Nile, coming down from the Ethiopian Highlands, joins the White Nile, which has travelled all the way from Lake Victoria. Their waters can run side by side in two shades for a while before they mix. Around the confluence, three cities face each other across the rivers.","w":"Two large rivers meeting in a Y around a green island, with a city on each of the three banks.","f":["Tuti Island sits right at the confluence of the two Niles.","Most of the water in the Nile below Khartoum comes from the Blue Nile, especially in the flood season.","The three cities around the confluence are Khartoum, Omdurman and Khartoum North."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers meet at Khartoum?","a":"The Blue Nile and White Nile","x":["The Nile and the Atbara","The White Nile and the Sobat"]}},"Q374365":{"t":"The Red Sea gateway, fringed by turquoise reefs","s":"Jeddah stretches for kilometres along the Red Sea, a long, narrow city pressed between desert and a band of turquoise shallows. Those pale greens and blues offshore are coral reefs. For centuries pilgrims bound for Mecca, about 70 km inland, have landed here, and the old port town grew rich on their arrival.","w":"A long city along a straight Red Sea coast, edged by bright turquoise reefs, with brown desert and hills behind.","f":["Historic Jeddah, an old quarter of houses built from coral stone, became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2014.","King Fahd's Fountain, off the city's seafront, throws water more than 250 metres into the air.","The reefs along this coast are part of one of the warmest and saltiest seas on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Which sea does Jeddah face?","a":"The Red Sea","x":["The Arabian Sea","The Persian Gulf"]}},"Q5806":{"t":"A holy city in a valley of dark, bare mountains","s":"Mecca lies in narrow valleys among dark, jagged mountains, about 70 km inland from the Red Sea. For Muslims it is the holiest city of Islam: they turn towards it in prayer, and millions come each year on pilgrimage. East of the city, look for the Mina valley, carpeted with rows of white tents that house pilgrims during the Hajj.","w":"A city squeezed into winding valleys between bare black hills, with a valley of white tents stretching away to the east.","f":["The Prophet Muhammad was born in Mecca around AD 570.","The tent city in Mina holds tens of thousands of fireproof tents in neat rows.","The plain of Arafat, a key stop of the pilgrimage, lies about 20 km south-east of the city."],"q":{"q":"What covers the valley of Mina, east of Mecca?","a":"Rows of white tents","x":["Solar panels","Date palm groves"]}},"Q585":{"t":"A capital at the end of a long, island-dotted fjord","s":"Oslo sits at the head of the Oslofjord, where a long arm of the sea finally runs out between forested hills. The city fills a green amphitheatre, and dark woods scattered with lakes begin right at its edge. For three centuries it was called Christiania, after the Danish-Norwegian king Christian IV, who rebuilt it in 1624.","w":"The inner end of a long fjord scattered with small islands, with the city in a bowl of dark, forested hills.","f":["The Oslofjord runs about 100 km from the city south to the Skagerrak.","The city took back its medieval name, Oslo, in 1925.","Much of the municipality is protected forest, the Marka, laced with lakes and ski trails."],"q":{"q":"What was Oslo called from 1624 until 1925?","a":"Christiania","x":["Nidaros","Bjørgvin"]}},"Q5468":{"t":"A warm harbour city on the Indian Ocean","s":"Durban wraps around a big natural harbour on South Africa's east coast, sheltered by a long, wooded ridge called the Bluff. North of the port, a straight line of beaches faces the Indian Ocean, where the warm Agulhas Current keeps the water mild all year. Inland, rolling green hills are patched with sugar-cane fields.","w":"A near-enclosed bay with a narrow entrance, a green ridge along its south side, and a long straight beach to the north.","f":["The city was named in 1835 after Sir Benjamin D'Urban, then governor of the Cape Colony.","Durban's harbour is one of the busiest ports in Africa.","The warm Agulhas Current flows south-west along this coast, one of the fastest ocean currents on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Which ocean does Durban face?","a":"The Indian Ocean","x":["The Atlantic Ocean","The Southern Ocean"]}},"Q656":{"t":"Peter's city on the islands of the Neva delta","s":"Saint Petersburg spreads over the islands of the Neva delta at the eastern tip of the Gulf of Finland. Look for a thin line crossing the gulf to the west: a barrier about 25 km long, running through Kotlin Island, that shields the city from storm surges. Peter the Great founded the city on this marshy ground in 1703.","w":"A big city on a branching river delta at the head of a gulf, with a long dam crossing the water through an island.","f":["The Neva is only about 74 km long, yet it drains Lake Ladoga, the largest lake in Europe.","The city is built on dozens of islands, divided by rivers and canals.","Around midsummer, the 'white nights' keep the sky light even at midnight."],"q":{"q":"In which year did Peter the Great found Saint Petersburg?","a":"1703","x":["1682","1721"]}},"Q182984":{"t":"An ancient Sahel trading city on the savanna","s":"Kano spreads across the flat, dusty savanna of northern Nigeria, a vast carpet of low, earth-coloured roofs. For a thousand years it has been a trading city, a southern end of the caravan routes across the Sahara. In its old walled heart, cloth is still dyed in pits of indigo that have been in use since the 15th century.","w":"A huge low city of sandy browns on flat, dry savanna, with green fields and reservoirs scattered around it.","f":["The Kofar Mata dye pits have been worked for more than 500 years.","Kano's Kurmi Market dates back to the 15th century.","The rainy season here lasts only about four months, from June to September."],"q":{"q":"Which dye colours cloth in Kano's old dye pits?","a":"Indigo blue","x":["Saffron yellow","Madder red"]}},"Q270":{"t":"A capital on a river still left half wild","s":"Warsaw stands on the Vistula, and the river is the surprise: while most capitals have tamed theirs, here broad sandbanks, willow thickets and beaches line the water right through the city. The centre sits on a low escarpment on the west bank; the Praga district faces it across the river. The royal court moved here from Kraków around 1596.","w":"A wide river with pale sandbanks and green, wooded banks winding north through a large city on a flat plain.","f":["The Vistula, Poland's longest river, runs just over 1,000 km from the Carpathians to the Baltic.","Frédéric Chopin was born in 1810 at Żelazowa Wola, about 50 km west of Warsaw.","Warsaw's historic Old Town is a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"From which city did Poland's royal court move to Warsaw around 1596?","a":"Kraków","x":["Gdańsk","Poznań"]}},"Q1960":{"t":"A 'haven of peace' on a sheltered harbour","s":"Dar es Salaam curls around a narrow, sheltered harbour on the Indian Ocean, with coral islets and turquoise reefs scattered just offshore. Sultan Majid of Zanzibar founded the town in the 1860s and gave it an Arabic name meaning 'haven of peace'. Today it is Tanzania's largest city, sprawling far inland across the coastal plain.","w":"A sprawling coastal city around a narrow-mouthed harbour, with small green islands and reefs just offshore to the north.","f":["Tanzania's official capital is Dodoma, but Dar es Salaam remains its largest city and main port.","Zanzibar Town lies about 70 km to the north, across a channel of the Indian Ocean.","The islands just north of the city, such as Bongoyo and Mbudya, are part of a marine reserve."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Dar es Salaam mean?","a":"Haven of peace","x":["Gate of the sea","House of spices"]}},"Q1761":{"t":"A river city between a round bay and green mountains","s":"Dublin rings the inner curve of Dublin Bay, with the Liffey running straight east through its heart into the sea. The rocky peninsula of Howth closes the bay to the north, and the Wicklow Mountains rise dark and green just to the south. Vikings set up a fortified harbour here in 841, beside a dark tidal pool — the dubh linn.","w":"A city around a rounded bay, a river running east through its centre, a knobbly peninsula to the north and hills to the south.","f":["Phoenix Park, on the city's western side, covers about 700 hectares — one of Europe's largest enclosed city parks.","The city's Irish name, Baile Átha Cliath, means 'town of the hurdled ford'.","Two long sea walls reach into the bay from the river mouth; the southern one was finished in the 1790s."],"q":{"q":"What does 'dubh linn', the source of Dublin's name, mean?","a":"Black pool","x":["Green hill","Fair harbour"]}},"Q3748":{"t":"A rain-soaked capital on a green cape","s":"Monrovia stretches along Cape Mesurado, a narrow peninsula between the Atlantic and a maze of lagoons and mangrove creeks. It is one of the rainiest capitals on Earth, and in the wet season the land around it glows a deep green. The city was founded in 1822 by freed African Americans and named after US president James Monroe.","w":"A narrow cape between the ocean and a mangrove-fringed river mouth, with the city running along its spine.","f":["Monrovia gets more than 4,500 mm of rain a year, several times as much as London.","The first settlers landed in 1822 on Providence Island, in the mouth of the Mesurado River.","The city's port lies on Bushrod Island, wrapped by the Saint Paul and Mesurado rivers."],"q":{"q":"Which US president was Monrovia named after?","a":"James Monroe","x":["Thomas Jefferson","James Madison"]}},"Q2634":{"t":"A city on a famous bay, beneath a volcano","s":"Naples curves around one of the most famous bays in the world, and its landmark is impossible to miss: the cone of Vesuvius, cradled in the broken rim of an older volcano just south-east of the city. To the west lies another volcanic area, the Phlegraean Fields, pitted with craters. Out in the bay float Capri and Ischia.","w":"A broad bay with a big volcanic cone on its south-eastern side, islands at its mouth, and the city along the north shore.","f":["Vesuvius rises to about 1,280 metres straight from the shore of the bay.","The Phlegraean Fields west of the city form a large volcanic caldera, partly under the sea.","Greek settlers named the city Neapolis, and its historic centre is now a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"What does the Greek name Neapolis, Naples' origin, mean?","a":"New city","x":["Sea city","Holy city"]}},"Q490":{"t":"A flat city on the Po plain, the Alps on its horizon","s":"Milan lies in the flat heart of the Po plain, a city of rings around a medieval core, with motorways and railways fanning out in every direction. Look north on a clear day: the Alps rise like a white wall, and at their feet glint the great lakes, Como and Maggiore. In late Roman times, as Mediolanum, this was an imperial capital.","w":"A dense, roughly round city on a perfectly flat plain, with the Alps and long glacial lakes to the north.","f":["Milan Cathedral was begun in 1386 and took nearly six centuries to finish.","With no big river of its own, Milan dug canals, the Navigli, from the 12th century to reach the Ticino and Adda.","On clear days Monte Rosa, more than 4,600 metres high, can be seen from the city."],"q":{"q":"What was Milan called in Roman times?","a":"Mediolanum","x":["Augusta Taurinorum","Placentia"]}},"Q87":{"t":"A long ribbon of a city between sea and lake","s":"Alexandria stretches for kilometres along the Mediterranean, a thin ribbon of pale buildings squeezed between the sea and the shallow Lake Mariout behind it. Look for the curve of its eastern harbour. Alexander the Great founded the city in 331 BC, and its lighthouse, the Pharos, was one of the Seven Wonders of the ancient world.","w":"A narrow city strung along a straight coast, with a curved harbour and a flat lake and marshes just inland.","f":["The city runs more than 30 km along the coast but is only a few kilometres deep.","The ancient Library of Alexandria was one of the great centres of learning of the classical world.","The Pharos stood on an island at the harbour mouth for well over 1,500 years."],"q":{"q":"Who founded Alexandria in 331 BC?","a":"Alexander the Great","x":["Ptolemy I","Julius Caesar"]}},"Q1794":{"t":"A city on the Main, its airport carved from forest","s":"Frankfurt lies on the Main about 30 km before it joins the Rhine, with the dark, wooded Taunus hills rising to the north-west. South of the river a great forest spreads out, and cut into it is one of Europe's busiest airports, its runways pale stripes among the trees. For centuries this was where German kings were elected.","w":"A compact centre on a river running east–west, with a big forest to the south cut by long, straight airport runways.","f":["From 1356 the Holy Roman Emperors were elected in Frankfurt, and from 1562 they were crowned here too.","Johann Wolfgang von Goethe was born in Frankfurt in 1749.","The Taunus range to the north-west rises to about 880 metres at the Großer Feldberg."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Frankfurt?","a":"The Main","x":["The Neckar","The Mosel"]}},"Q1055":{"t":"A great port far up the Elbe, with a lake at its heart","s":"Hamburg lies about 100 km up the Elbe from the North Sea, yet it is one of Europe's largest ports. From above, look for the maze of docks and container terminals on the south bank, and for a bright lake right in the city centre: the Alster, a small river dammed centuries ago. Hamburg was a leading city of the Hanseatic League.","w":"A wide river with a maze of harbour basins on its south bank and a broad lake in the city centre just north of it.","f":["The Alster lakes were created in the Middle Ages by damming a small river to drive a mill.","The Speicherstadt, a warehouse district built on oak piles, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","The tide reaches Hamburg: here the Elbe rises and falls by more than three metres."],"q":{"q":"Which lake lies in the centre of Hamburg?","a":"The Alster","x":["The Müritz","The Steinhuder Meer"]}},"Q1726":{"t":"A Bavarian city with the Alps on its doorstep","s":"Munich spreads across a flat gravel plain on the green Isar, with the wall of the Alps only about 60 km to the south. On clear days, especially when the warm, dry Föhn wind blows, the peaks look close enough to touch. South-west of the city, two big lakes, Starnberg and Ammersee, glint among fields and woods.","w":"A large city on a flat plain split by a green river valley, with lakes to the south-west and the Alps beyond.","f":["Munich's name comes from the monks, 'Mönche', who lived here before Henry the Lion founded the town in 1158.","The first Oktoberfest was held in 1810 to celebrate a royal wedding.","At about 520 metres, Munich is one of the highest large cities in Germany."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Munich?","a":"The Isar","x":["The Inn","The Lech"]}},"Q1085":{"t":"A river bending in S-curves through a hundred spires","s":"The Vltava gives Prague its shape. From above, the river swings through the city in a broad S, with the wooded rise of Petřín and the castle ridge on the west bank and the dense old town on the east. Green islands sit in the stream. About 30 km further north, the Vltava ends its journey in the Elbe.","w":"A river making big S-shaped bends through a dense city, wooded hills on its west bank and small islands mid-stream.","f":["Prague Castle is often listed as the largest ancient castle complex in the world.","Charles Bridge was begun in 1357 under Emperor Charles IV.","The historic centre of Prague has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1992."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Prague?","a":"The Vltava","x":["The Elbe","The Morava"]}},"Q35178":{"t":"A desert city curled around a wide, shallow bay","s":"Kuwait City wraps around the southern shore of Kuwait Bay, a broad inlet at the north-western corner of the Persian Gulf. Around it lies flat, almost featureless desert, crossed by ring roads that curve out from the old town in wide arcs. The name comes from the Arabic for 'little fort', and the town grew on pearling and seafaring.","w":"A city on the south shore of a wide bay, with semicircular ring roads arcing out into flat desert.","f":["The city's ring roads, numbered First to Seventh, curve out from the old centre in concentric arcs.","On Failaka Island, at the mouth of the bay, stand the remains of a Greek temple from the 3rd century BC.","Before oil, pearl diving was the backbone of Kuwait's economy."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Kuwait mean?","a":"Little fort","x":["Pearl bay","Sweet water"]}},"Q3761":{"t":"A capital right next door to the zero meridian","s":"Accra spreads along a flat stretch of the Gulf of Guinea, its low roofs running inland over a grassy coastal plain. Just to the east, at the port of Tema, the Greenwich meridian comes ashore, so you are very close to zero longitude — and only about 600 km north of the point where it crosses the equator.","w":"A broad, low city on a flat, gently curving coast, with a long ridge of hills rising inland to the north.","f":["The Greenwich meridian passes through Tema, about 25 km east of central Accra.","Accra's name is thought to come from 'nkran', an Akan word for ants.","Accra became the capital of the British Gold Coast colony in 1877."],"q":{"q":"Which port near Accra lies on the Greenwich meridian?","a":"Tema","x":["Takoradi","Lomé"]}},"Q3579":{"t":"An old Mediterranean port between sea and desert","s":"Tripoli spreads along the Libyan coast, a pale city on a low shore where a belt of gardens and palm groves gives way inland to desert. Phoenicians founded it as Oea in the 7th century BC. Its Greek name means 'three cities', after Oea and its neighbours Sabratha and Leptis Magna, both now famous Roman ruins.","w":"A pale city on a straight Mediterranean shore, a harbour at its old centre, green farmland fading into desert behind.","f":["The Arch of Marcus Aurelius, built around AD 165, still stands in Tripoli's old town.","Leptis Magna, some 120 km east of the city, is one of the best-preserved Roman cities in the Mediterranean.","Tripoli's walled old town sits right beside the harbour."],"q":{"q":"What does the Greek name Tripoli mean?","a":"Three cities","x":["Three hills","Sea gate"]}},"Q33935":{"t":"A white city on the dunes, an ancient port at its tip","s":"Tel Aviv lines a straight, sandy stretch of the Mediterranean, a dense band of pale buildings pressed right up to the beach. At its southern end, on a low rocky headland, is Jaffa, a port used for thousands of years. Tel Aviv itself is young: it was founded on the dunes north of Jaffa in 1909.","w":"A pale, dense city along a ruler-straight sandy coast, a small headland at its southern end, a river mouth to the north.","f":["Tel Aviv's 'White City' holds thousands of Bauhaus-style buildings and has been a UNESCO site since 2003.","The name Tel Aviv means 'spring hill' — spring as in the season.","The Yarkon River reaches the sea at the city's northern edge."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient port forms Tel Aviv's southern end?","a":"Jaffa","x":["Acre","Caesarea"]}},"Q1757":{"t":"A granite peninsula city fringed with islands","s":"Helsinki sits on a ragged granite peninsula poking into the Gulf of Finland, surrounded by bays, inlets and more than three hundred islands. At the harbour mouth, a cluster of islets carries the old sea fortress of Suomenlinna. In a cold winter the sea around the city freezes, turning the whole archipelago white. Tallinn lies only about 80 km across the gulf.","w":"A rocky peninsula ragged with bays and islands, facing south across the gulf, with forest and lakes inland.","f":["Helsinki became Finland's capital in 1812, taking over from Turku.","The Suomenlinna sea fortress, begun in 1748, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Helsinki is the northernmost capital in the European Union."],"q":{"q":"Which king founded Helsinki in 1550?","a":"Gustav I of Sweden","x":["Christian IV of Denmark","Peter the Great"]}},"Q121157":{"t":"A pilgrimage city in a valley between two ranges","s":"Mashhad fills a broad valley in north-eastern Iran, with the Binalud mountains to the south-west and the Hezar Masjed range to the north-east. It grew around the shrine of Imam Reza, and its name means 'place of martyrdom'. Millions of pilgrims arrive every year, making it one of the great pilgrimage centres of the Muslim world.","w":"A large city in a wide valley, between a high, often snowy range to the south-west and bare mountains to the north-east.","f":["The tomb of Ferdowsi, author of the Persian epic Shahnameh, stands at Tus, about 25 km from the city.","Mashhad is Iran's second-largest city after Tehran.","The Binalud range south-west of the city rises above 3,000 metres."],"q":{"q":"Which poet's tomb stands at Tus, near Mashhad?","a":"Ferdowsi","x":["Hafez","Saadi"]}},"Q1748":{"t":"Where a bridge dives into a tunnel under the sea","s":"Copenhagen faces the Øresund, the strait between Denmark and Sweden. Look south of the city for one of Europe's great engineering lines: a bridge runs out from Sweden, lands on an artificial island and then vanishes into a tunnel under the sea to reach the Danish shore. The city itself spreads over the edge of Zealand and the flat island of Amager.","w":"A long bridge crossing the strait to an artificial island, where it disappears; the city and airport lie on the Danish side.","f":["The Øresund Bridge is about 8 km long, and the link continues in a 4 km tunnel under the sea.","Peberholm, where the bridge meets the tunnel, is an artificial island built for the crossing.","Bishop Absalon built a castle here around 1167, traditionally taken as the city's founding."],"q":{"q":"What does the Danish name København mean?","a":"Merchants' harbour","x":["King's castle","Salt bay"]}},"Q1515":{"t":"A lagoon city with rainforest inside its limits","s":"Abidjan wraps around the Ébrié Lagoon, a long body of calm water behind a narrow sand bar on the Gulf of Guinea. The city's districts sit on peninsulas and islands, joined by bridges. Look for the straight cut through the sand bar — the Vridi Canal — that lets ships into the lagoon. Within the city, a block of dark green is protected rainforest.","w":"A city spread around a branching lagoon behind a straight sandy coast, cut through by a narrow canal to the sea.","f":["The Vridi Canal, opened in 1950, turned Abidjan into one of West Africa's main ports.","Banco National Park, a remnant of rainforest, lies inside the city.","Abidjan is Côte d'Ivoire's largest city, though the official capital is Yamoussoukro."],"q":{"q":"Which lagoon does Abidjan surround?","a":"The Ébrié Lagoon","x":["The Aby Lagoon","Lagos Lagoon"]}},"Q239":{"t":"A city whose river vanished beneath its streets","s":"Brussels spreads over gentle hills in the middle of Belgium, ringed by a motorway and fading into farmland. Don't look for its river: in the 19th century the Senne was vaulted over and hidden beneath the boulevards. What you can see is the Sonian Forest on the south-eastern edge, a large dark block of old beech woods.","w":"A large city in rolling farmland, circled by a ring motorway, with a big dark forest on its south-eastern edge.","f":["The Sonian Forest covers more than 4,000 hectares, and parts of it are UNESCO-listed for their ancient beeches.","The Senne was covered over in the 1860s and 1870s and still flows in tunnels under the city.","The Grand-Place, the old market square, is a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"What does Bruocsella, the old name of Brussels, mean?","a":"Home in the marsh","x":["Bridge town","Hill fort"]}},"Q3897":{"t":"A bay city sheltered by a long finger of sand","s":"Luanda curves around a bay on Angola's Atlantic coast, sheltered by the Ilha, a long, thin sand spit lined with beaches. Further south, another spit, the Mussulo peninsula, encloses a calm turquoise lagoon for kilometres. Portuguese settlers founded the city in 1576, making it one of the oldest European-founded cities south of the Sahara.","w":"A city curving around a bay closed by a thin sandy spit, with a longer spit and lagoon further south.","f":["The Mussulo peninsula south of the city is a sand spit nearly 30 km long.","The cold Benguela Current keeps this coast surprisingly dry for the tropics.","The Kwanza River, which gave Angola's currency its name, reaches the sea some 70 km to the south."],"q":{"q":"Which European country founded Luanda in 1576?","a":"Portugal","x":["The Netherlands","Spain"]}},"Q3561":{"t":"A white city stacked on the hills above a bay","s":"Algiers rises from a wide, curving bay in white tiers, climbing the steep hills behind the port; the French called it Alger la Blanche, 'Algiers the White'. Its Arabic name refers to small islets offshore, long ago joined to the mainland to form the harbour. The old Casbah clings to the slope above the sea.","w":"A white city climbing steep green hills around a broad, curving bay, with a long harbour breakwater.","f":["The Casbah of Algiers has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1992.","The town was refounded in the 10th century by the Berber ruler Buluggin ibn Ziri, on the site of ancient Icosium.","Algeria is Africa's largest country by area, and Algiers is both its capital and largest city."],"q":{"q":"What does Algiers' Arabic name, al-Jazair, mean?","a":"The islands","x":["The fortress","The harbour"]}},"Q3792":{"t":"A capital pressed right against a border","s":"Lomé lies on the Gulf of Guinea at the western end of Togo's short coastline, so close to Ghana that the city's edge runs straight into the border. Behind the straight, surf-washed beach, a chain of lagoons runs parallel to the coast. The town became the capital of German Togoland in 1897, and later of the independent country.","w":"A low city on a straight sandy coast with lagoons behind it and harbour breakwaters jutting out to sea.","f":["Togo's entire coastline is only just over 50 km long.","Lomé's western districts run right up to the border with Ghana.","The city's Grand Marché is famous for its cloth traders, especially of printed wax fabrics."],"q":{"q":"Which country's border runs along Lomé's western edge?","a":"Ghana","x":["Benin","Nigeria"]}},"Q3572":{"t":"A city between two lakes, with Carthage next door","s":"Tunis lies on a narrow neck of land between two shallow lakes, where the Gulf of Tunis cuts deep into North Africa. East of the old town, the Lake of Tunis is split by a straight channel that lets ships through to the city's port. On the gulf's shore just to the north-east lie the ruins of Carthage, once Rome's great rival.","w":"A city between a shallow lagoon crossed by a straight channel to the east and a salt flat to the south-west.","f":["The ruins of Carthage, some 15 km from the centre, have been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1979.","The Medina of Tunis, a maze of souks and alleys, is UNESCO-listed too.","The Lake of Tunis is only about a metre deep in places, and flamingos wade in its shallows."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient city's ruins lie beside Tunis?","a":"Carthage","x":["Leptis Magna","Cyrene"]}},"Q7525":{"t":"A city of deep bays near the tip of Crimea","s":"Sevastopol lies near the south-western tip of the Crimean peninsula, on a long, deep bay that cuts several kilometres inland, with smaller bays branching off it like fingers. Beyond the city, the land rises towards the Crimean Mountains. On a headland to the west are the ruins of Chersonesus, a Greek colony founded some 2,400 years ago.","w":"A long, narrow bay reaching inland from the Black Sea, with smaller side bays branching off and the city between them.","f":["The main bay reaches about 7 km inland and is one of the best natural harbours on the Black Sea.","Chersonesus was founded by Greek colonists in the 5th century BC.","The modern city was founded in 1783."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient Greek city's ruins lie at Sevastopol?","a":"Chersonesus","x":["Olbia","Tanais"]}},"Q1519":{"t":"An island city in a maze of mangroves and shallows","s":"Abu Dhabi stands on an island just off the Gulf coast, separated from the mainland by a narrow channel and joined to it by bridges. Around it, the sea is a puzzle of turquoise shallows, sandbanks, dark mangroves and low islands. The city's central grid ends abruptly at the water, with a long corniche curving along its north-western shore.","w":"A grid-planned island city ringed by turquoise shallows, with dark mangroves and many low islands to its east.","f":["Qasr al-Hosn, a fort that began as an 18th-century watchtower, is the oldest stone building in the city.","The emirate of Abu Dhabi covers more than four-fifths of the UAE's territory.","The mangroves east of the island are protected as a national park."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Abu Dhabi mean?","a":"Father of the gazelle","x":["Father of pearls","Gate of the sun"]}},"Q23438":{"t":"A white capital between mountains and the Karakum","s":"Ashgabat lies on a narrow strip of green between two worlds: the bare Kopet Dag range rising to the south, and the Karakum Desert stretching away to the north. A thin line of water, the Karakum Canal, curves along the desert's edge, carrying water from the Amu Darya far to the east. The city is known for its white marble facades.","w":"A pale city at the foot of a bare mountain range, with sand desert to the north and a long canal curving past.","f":["The Karakum Canal runs for more than 1,000 km across the desert, one of the longest irrigation canals in the world.","The ruins of Nisa, an early capital of the Parthians, lie just west of the city and are UNESCO-listed.","The Kopet Dag, south of the city, forms Turkmenistan's border with Iran."],"q":{"q":"Which desert stretches north of Ashgabat?","a":"The Karakum","x":["The Kyzylkum","The Gobi"]}},"Q3881":{"t":"A plateau capital that began at a railway siding","s":"Lusaka lies on a broad, flat plateau in the middle of Zambia, about 1,280 metres up, where the air is fresher than the latitude suggests. It grew from a small siding on the railway line from the south, built in the early 1900s. In 1935 it became the colonial capital, chosen for its central position and healthy altitude.","w":"A spreading low city on a flat, lightly wooded plateau, with farmland and red-brown earth all around.","f":["The city takes its name from Lusaaka, a local headman whose village stood here.","The Kafue River passes some 50 km south of the city.","Thanks to the altitude, winters here are mild and dry, with cool nights from May to August."],"q":{"q":"What made Lusaka grow in the early 1900s?","a":"A railway siding","x":["A gold mine","A river port"]}},"Q3921":{"t":"A highveld capital among granite hills and jacarandas","s":"Harare spreads over the high grassland of northern Zimbabwe, about 1,500 metres above the sea, where rounded granite hills poke out of the plateau. South-west of the city, look for a winding lake: Lake Chivero, a reservoir on the Manyame River. In October, thousands of jacaranda trees along the avenues burst into purple flower.","w":"A sprawling city on a flat grassy plateau dotted with granite outcrops; a winding reservoir lies to the south-west.","f":["Harare lies at about 1,490 metres, so its summers are warm rather than hot.","Lake Chivero, about 30 km south-west of the centre, supplies much of the city's water.","The first settlement grew beside the Kopje, a granite hill next to today's city centre."],"q":{"q":"What was Harare called until 1982?","a":"Salisbury","x":["Fort Victoria","Umtali"]}},"Q193250":{"t":"A city of wide streets near a land of granite domes","s":"Bulawayo sits on the dry, open plateau of south-western Zimbabwe, a neat grid of unusually broad streets. They were laid out wide enough, it is said, for a wagon pulled by a full span of oxen to turn around. About 35 km south rise the Matobo Hills, a wild landscape of bare granite domes and precariously balanced boulders.","w":"A grid-planned city on a dry, flat plateau, with rough country of bare granite hills to the south.","f":["The Matobo Hills have been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2003, noted for their ancient rock paintings.","Bulawayo was the royal town of the Ndebele king Lobengula before the colonial city was laid out in 1894.","Bulawayo is Zimbabwe's second-largest city."],"q":{"q":"Which granite hills rise south of Bulawayo?","a":"The Matobo Hills","x":["The Chimanimani Mountains","The Drakensberg"]}},"Q1764":{"t":"The world's northernmost capital, named after steam","s":"Reykjavík lies on a low peninsula on the south shore of Faxaflói bay, with the long, flat-topped ridge of Mount Esja across the water. To the south-west, the Reykjanes peninsula is a dark sweep of lava fields, some of the youngest land in Europe. The first settler, Ingólfur Arnarson, is said to have arrived around 874 and named the bay after steam rising from hot springs.","w":"A low, compact city on a peninsula, a flat-topped mountain across the bay, and dark lava fields to the south-west.","f":["Reykjavík is the northernmost capital of a sovereign country.","Nearly all homes in the city are heated with hot water from geothermal sources.","Mount Esja, across the bay, rises to about 900 metres."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Reykjavík mean?","a":"Smoky bay","x":["Ice harbour","Whale fjord"]}},"Q4093":{"t":"Scotland's biggest city, on the shipbuilding Clyde","s":"Glasgow spreads along the Clyde, a river that once built a large share of the world's ships. Downstream, the river widens into the long Firth of Clyde, scattered with islands. To the north the low, green Campsie Fells rise, and beyond them, some 30 km away, lies Loch Lomond, the largest lake by area in Great Britain.","w":"A large city on a river flowing west to a long, island-studded firth, with green hills and a big loch to the north-west.","f":["Loch Lomond is the largest lake in Great Britain by surface area.","Glasgow's name is often said to come from Gaelic words meaning 'dear green place'.","Glasgow is Scotland's largest city, bigger than the capital, Edinburgh."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Glasgow?","a":"The Clyde","x":["The Forth","The Tay"]}},"Q3894":{"t":"A city of hills on the shore of Africa's largest lake","s":"Kampala is built across a cluster of green hills just north of Lake Victoria, with the city filling the valleys in between. Its name comes from an old Luganda phrase for 'hill of the impala'. You are almost exactly on the equator here: the line runs only about 35 km south of the centre, across the lake.","w":"A green, hilly city spilling down to a ragged bay of a vast lake, with the Entebbe peninsula to the south-west.","f":["Lake Victoria is the largest lake in Africa and the main source of the White Nile.","The Nile leaves Lake Victoria at Jinja, about 80 km east of Kampala.","The Kasubi Tombs, burial place of the kings of Buganda, are a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"Which lake lies just south of Kampala?","a":"Lake Victoria","x":["Lake Albert","Lake Kyoga"]}},"Q131694":{"t":"A port city built inside an old volcano","s":"Aden grew on a rugged volcanic peninsula on the Gulf of Aden, and its oldest district really does sit in a crater, ringed by the jagged walls of an extinct volcano. Across the harbour, the Little Aden peninsula is another old volcano. The deep, sheltered water between them made Aden one of the great ports on the sea route between Europe and India.","w":"Two dark volcanic peninsulas enclosing a wide natural harbour, with the city tucked into their bays and craters.","f":["The ancient Tawila cisterns, cut into the crater wall, were built to catch the area's rare rain.","In 1839 Aden became a British coaling station for steamships sailing to India.","Jebel Shamsan, the highest point of the crater rim, rises to about 550 metres."],"q":{"q":"What is Aden's old town, Crater, built inside?","a":"An extinct volcano","x":["A meteorite crater","A dried-up lake"]}},"Q8717":{"t":"The inland port that once ruled trade with the Americas","s":"Seville lies on the Guadalquivir some 80 km upriver from the sea, on a flat, sun-bleached plain of olive groves and farmland. Ocean-going ships once sailed right up to the city, and from 1503 it held the royal monopoly on trade with the Americas. Downstream, the river winds through the vast Doñana marshes towards the Atlantic.","w":"A large city on a flat plain beside a river, with a straight, canal-like channel and farmland stretching in every direction.","f":["Seville has Spain's only inland river port.","Magellan's expedition, the first to sail around the world, set out from Seville in 1519.","Seville Cathedral is often called the largest Gothic cathedral in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Seville?","a":"The Guadalquivir","x":["The Guadiana","The Ebro"]}},"Q204365":{"t":"An old sultanate town among Sahel granite","s":"Zinder spreads across the dry Sahel of southern Niger, a low, sand-coloured town scattered among outcrops of bare granite. It was the seat of the Sultanate of Damagaram, which grew rich on caravans crossing the Sahara between Kano and the north. Its old walled quarter, the Birni, still surrounds the sultan's palace.","w":"A low, sandy town among bare rocky outcrops on flat, dry Sahel plains, with scattered fields around it.","f":["The Sultanate of Damagaram made Zinder its capital in the 18th century.","The Nigerian city of Kano lies about 200 km to the south.","Zinder served as the capital of the French colony of Niger until 1926."],"q":{"q":"Which city replaced Zinder as Niger's capital in 1926?","a":"Niamey","x":["Agadez","Maradi"]}},"Q3674":{"t":"A Sahel capital on the great river Niger","s":"Niamey spreads along the left bank of the Niger, a broad brown river bordered by green strips of rice fields and gardens in an otherwise dusty land. Only a small village in the late 1800s, it became the colonial capital in 1926 and has grown fast ever since. East of the city, the scrubland is home to the last wild herd of West African giraffes.","w":"A city on a wide brown river flowing south-east, with green ribbons of fields along its banks and dry plateaus beyond.","f":["The last wild West African giraffes live around Kouré, about 60 km south-east of Niamey.","The Niger, Africa's third-longest river, loops through the Sahel past the city.","Downstream lies W National Park, named after a W-shaped bend of the Niger."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past Niamey?","a":"The Niger","x":["The Senegal","The Volta"]}},"Q208718":{"t":"A young Red Sea harbour with coral reefs offshore","s":"Port Sudan lies on a flat, hot strip of Red Sea coast, where a deep, coral-fringed inlet makes a natural harbour. It is a young city, built in the early 1900s to replace the old port of Suakin, whose channel was too narrow for modern ships. Offshore, look for turquoise patches in the deep blue: reefs and atolls.","w":"A low city around a sheltered inlet on a straight desert coast, with turquoise reefs offshore and dry hills inland.","f":["Sanganeb, an atoll about 25 km offshore, is a UNESCO-listed marine national park.","The Red Sea Hills behind the coastal plain rise to more than 2,000 metres.","Work on the new port began in 1905."],"q":{"q":"Which old port did Port Sudan replace as the main harbour?","a":"Suakin","x":["Massawa","Berbera"]}},"Q3780":{"t":"A city between green mountains and a great harbour","s":"Freetown clings to the northern tip of a mountainous peninsula, wedged between forested hills and the wide estuary of the Sierra Leone River. That estuary is one of the largest natural harbours in the world. Portuguese sailors named the peaks 'Serra Leoa', the lion mountains. The city was founded in 1792 as a home for freed Africans.","w":"A city squeezed between green, mountainous ridges and a very wide river estuary opening onto the Atlantic.","f":["The highest hills of the Freetown Peninsula rise to nearly 900 metres.","Settlers from Nova Scotia, formerly enslaved Africans, founded the city in 1792.","The peninsula's rainforest is protected as the Western Area Peninsula National Park."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Sierra Leone mean?","a":"Lion mountains","x":["Green coast","River of gold"]}},"Q320049":{"t":"A mud-brick gateway to the Sahara","s":"Agadez is a sand-coloured oasis town at the southern edge of the Aïr Mountains, where the Sahel gives way to the Sahara. For centuries it was a crossroads for Tuareg caravans carrying salt and goods across the desert. Its skyline is marked by a mud-brick minaret bristling with wooden beams, first raised in the early 16th century.","w":"A small, earth-coloured town on flat sand, with the dark, rugged Aïr Mountains rising to the north and east.","f":["The minaret of the Grand Mosque is about 27 metres tall, one of the tallest mud-brick structures in the world.","The historic centre of Agadez became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2013.","The Aïr Mountains north of the town rise to just over 2,000 metres."],"q":{"q":"What is Agadez's famous minaret built of?","a":"Mud brick","x":["White marble","Coral stone"]}},"Q851576":{"t":"A savanna city below a volcanic massif","s":"Nyala lies in the rolling savanna of southern Darfur, beside a wide, sandy wadi that runs with water only in the rainy season. From above, the land is dotted with acacia and cultivated patches that turn green after the summer rains. To the north-west rise the Marra Mountains, a volcanic massif crowned by a great caldera.","w":"A spread-out town on flat, dotted savanna beside a broad sandy riverbed, with dark volcanic highlands far to the north-west.","f":["Jebel Marra rises to about 3,000 metres, far above the surrounding plains.","Nyala is the largest city in the Darfur region.","Rain falls here mostly between June and September; the rest of the year is dry."],"q":{"q":"What lies inside the summit caldera of Jebel Marra, near Nyala?","a":"Two crater lakes","x":["A glacier","A salt flat"]}},"Q276534":{"t":"A river town on the red earth of the ironstone plateau","s":"Wau sits on the Jur River in the north-west of South Sudan, where wooded savanna on reddish ironstone soils stretches in every direction. In the rainy season the whole region turns a vivid green and the river swells. Its water flows on towards the Sudd, one of the largest wetlands in the world, before reaching the White Nile.","w":"A modest town on a winding river amid flat, wooded savanna, with patches of red earth showing through.","f":["Wau is one of the largest cities in South Sudan.","The Jur feeds the Bahr el Ghazal, which meets the White Nile at Lake No.","The reddish soils around Wau belong to the ironstone plateau that covers much of western South Sudan."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past Wau?","a":"The Jur","x":["The Sobat","The Blue Nile"]}},"Q821321":{"t":"A thin green thread of the Nile through the desert","s":"Dongola sits on the west bank of the Nile in northern Sudan, where the river's strip of date palms and fields is only a few kilometres wide and yellow desert presses in on both sides. From above it is the clearest picture of how the Nile makes life possible. Upstream, Old Dongola was once a medieval Nubian royal capital.","w":"A narrow ribbon of dark green along the Nile, with a small town on its west bank and bare desert on both sides.","f":["Kerma, about 50 km downstream, was the centre of one of Africa's earliest kingdoms more than 4,000 years ago.","Between Khartoum and Egypt the Nile makes a huge S-shaped bend, and Dongola lies on its north-flowing stretch.","Rain is so rare here that some years pass with almost none."],"q":{"q":"Old Dongola was the capital of which medieval kingdom?","a":"Makuria","x":["Aksum","Kanem"]}},"Q686813":{"t":"A town at the foot of sudden granite towers","s":"Kassala lies on the flat plain of eastern Sudan, right at the foot of the Taka Mountains, a cluster of bare granite domes that shoot up abruptly from the land and are unmistakable from the air. The seasonal Gash River flows past the town and spreads into an inland delta of farms to the north. The Eritrean border lies just to the east.","w":"A cluster of steep, bare rock domes rising straight out of a flat plain, with a town and green orchards at their base.","f":["The Taka Mountains rise several hundred metres straight up from the surrounding plain.","The Gash River flows only in the rainy season and fans out into an inland delta north of the town.","Orchards watered by the Gash make Kassala known across Sudan for its fruit."],"q":{"q":"What rises abruptly beside Kassala?","a":"Bare granite mountains","x":["Sand dunes","A volcanic cone"]}},"Q26087":{"t":"An Arctic island city under the northern lights","s":"Tromsø sits mainly on a small island between steep, snow-streaked mountains and deep sounds, about 350 km north of the Arctic Circle. Bridges and tunnels tie it to the mainland. In midwinter the sun doesn't rise for weeks and the northern lights often dance overhead; in midsummer it doesn't set. Polar expeditions long set out from here.","w":"A small city on a low island in a narrow sound, ringed by steep, white-topped mountains and fjords.","f":["The midnight sun shines here from about late May to late July.","During the polar night, from late November to mid-January, the sun stays below the horizon.","Tromsø lies under the auroral oval, one of the best places on Earth to see the northern lights."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how far north of the Arctic Circle is Tromsø?","a":"About 350 km","x":["About 50 km","About 1,000 km"]}},"Q25804":{"t":"A Viking-age town cradled in a loop of river","s":"Trondheim sits on the south shore of the long Trondheimsfjord, where the river Nidelva curls around the old town in a tight loop, almost turning it into an island. King Olav Tryggvason founded the town in 997, and for centuries pilgrims came here to the shrine of Saint Olav. Look for the fjord's broad arm reaching far inland.","w":"A city on a long fjord, with a river curling round the compact old centre in a near-complete loop.","f":["Nidaros Cathedral, built over Saint Olav's grave, is the northernmost medieval cathedral in the world.","The Trondheimsfjord is about 130 km long, one of Norway's longest fjords.","Warm Atlantic water keeps the fjord largely free of ice through the winter."],"q":{"q":"What was Trondheim's medieval name?","a":"Nidaros","x":["Bjørgvin","Christiania"]}},"Q26793":{"t":"A rainy port ringed by seven mountains","s":"Bergen sits on Norway's west coast between steep mountains and a maze of islands and fjords, so it is no surprise that it is one of Europe's rainiest cities. Look for a small harbour bay cutting into the centre, framed by mountains. For centuries this was a key Hanseatic trading port, shipping dried cod from the north across Europe.","w":"A city squeezed around a narrow harbour between steep mountains, with countless islands and fjords spreading out to sea.","f":["Bergen gets roughly 2,250 mm of rain in an average year.","Bryggen, the old Hanseatic wharf, has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1979.","The Sognefjord, Norway's longest and deepest fjord, opens to the sea north of the city."],"q":{"q":"Which Hanseatic wharf is Bergen famous for?","a":"Bryggen","x":["Speicherstadt","Nyhavn"]}},"Q990080":{"t":"A deep-water bay on Mozambique's northern coast","s":"Nacala lies on the shore of a long, deep bay on Mozambique's northern coast, one of the finest natural harbours in East Africa. The water is deep enough for big ships to come close in without dredging. From above, look for a narrow-mouthed inlet reaching inland between green hills, with coral reefs along the ocean shore outside.","w":"A long, deep inlet reaching inland through a narrow mouth, with a town on its shore and green hills around.","f":["Nacala's bay is often described as the deepest natural harbour on Africa's east coast.","A railway links Nacala's port with landlocked Malawi.","Coral reefs fringe the coast outside the bay."],"q":{"q":"Which UNESCO-listed island lies about 60 km south of Nacala?","a":"Mozambique Island","x":["Zanzibar","Lamu"]}},"Q37701":{"t":"The judicial capital on the open Free State plains","s":"Bloemfontein sits in the middle of South Africa's high, flat grasslands, about 1,400 metres up, where a few rocky, flat-topped hills — koppies — break the horizon. It is one of the country's three capitals, home to the Supreme Court of Appeal. It is also where J. R. R. Tolkien was born, in 1892.","w":"A compact city on wide, flat, tawny grassland, with a few small flat-topped hills around its centre.","f":["Naval Hill, in the middle of the city, is a small nature reserve with giraffes.","South Africa has three capitals: Pretoria, Cape Town and Bloemfontein.","The city was founded in 1846 as a British outpost beside a spring."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Bloemfontein mean?","a":"Fountain of flowers","x":["Flower hill","Blooming valley"]}},"Q3926":{"t":"The jacaranda city between long rocky ridges","s":"Pretoria lies in valleys between long, parallel ridges running east–west, part of the Magaliesberg range, about 50 km north of Johannesburg. It is South Africa's executive capital. In October and November tens of thousands of jacaranda trees bloom along its streets, washing whole suburbs in purple. The city was founded in 1855 and named after Andries Pretorius.","w":"A city spread along valleys between long, straight east–west ridges, merging southwards into the Johannesburg sprawl.","f":["Tswaing, a meteorite crater about 1.1 km across, lies some 40 km north-west of the city.","The rocks of the Magaliesberg are more than two billion years old.","Pretoria's jacarandas are not native: they were brought from South America."],"q":{"q":"Which tree turns Pretoria purple in spring?","a":"Jacaranda","x":["Flame tree","Mimosa"]}},"Q80985":{"t":"A medieval maze still living inside its walls","s":"Fez lies in a green valley between the Middle Atlas and the Rif, and its old medina, Fes el-Bali, is one of the largest car-free urban areas in the world. From above, it is a dense, pale mass in a bowl of hills, with the newer city laid out on the plain beside it. Its university, al-Qarawiyyin, dates back to 859.","w":"A dense, pale old city packed into a bowl of hills, with a grid-planned new town spreading beside it on the plain.","f":["Al-Qarawiyyin, founded in 859, is often cited as the oldest continuously operating university in the world.","The Medina of Fez has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1981.","Fez's tanneries still dye leather in stone vats, much as they did centuries ago."],"q":{"q":"Who founded al-Qarawiyyin in Fez in 859?","a":"Fatima al-Fihri","x":["Idris I","Ibn Battuta"]}},"Q3551":{"t":"A capital facing its twin across a river mouth","s":"Rabat sits on the Atlantic where the Bou Regreg river reaches the sea, facing its older twin, Salé, across the estuary. On the clifftop at the river mouth stands the walled Kasbah of the Udayas. A little upstream, a 12th-century caliph began a vast mosque whose unfinished minaret, the Hassan Tower, still overlooks the water.","w":"Two cities facing each other across a river mouth on a straight Atlantic coast, with a walled fortress on the southern point.","f":["The Hassan Tower was left unfinished when its builder, the Almohad caliph Yaqub al-Mansur, died in 1199.","Rabat's historic and modern city became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2012.","In the 17th century Salé was a base for Atlantic corsairs, the 'Sallee Rovers'."],"q":{"q":"Which city faces Rabat across the Bou Regreg river?","a":"Salé","x":["Kenitra","Témara"]}},"Q101625":{"t":"A red city with snowy peaks on the horizon","s":"Marrakesh spreads across the flat Haouz plain, a city of ochre-red walls and palm groves. The view south is the show: the High Atlas rises like a wall some 50 km away, its peaks often white from winter into spring. Look for the dark green of the palm grove north of the city and the neat rectangles of old garden pools.","w":"A reddish city on a flat, dry plain, with a dark palm grove to the north and the snow-capped High Atlas to the south.","f":["Toubkal, North Africa's highest peak at about 4,170 metres, lies some 60 km south of the city.","The Almoravid dynasty founded Marrakesh around 1070.","The Medina of Marrakesh has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1985."],"q":{"q":"Which mountain range rises south of Marrakesh?","a":"The High Atlas","x":["The Rif","The Middle Atlas"]}},"Q21197":{"t":"A green hill city above endless wine cellars","s":"Chișinău spreads over gentle hills in the middle of Moldova, with an unusual amount of green: parks, lakes and tree-lined avenues show up clearly from above. Around it, rolling farmland is striped with vineyards. Beneath the countryside just north and south of the city run old limestone quarries, many kilometres long, now filled with wine.","w":"A leafy city on rolling hills, dotted with small lakes and parks and surrounded by striped fields and vineyards.","f":["The Mileștii Mici cellars south of the city hold what Guinness World Records listed as the largest wine collection in the world.","The Cricova cellars, north of the city, have about 120 km of underground streets.","Chișinău is first mentioned in a document of 1436."],"q":{"q":"What fills the old limestone tunnels near Chișinău?","a":"Wine cellars","x":["Salt mines","Mushroom farms"]}},"Q211563":{"t":"A low port city on a muddy estuary with big tides","s":"Beira lies on flat, low ground where the Pungwe River meets the Indian Ocean, surrounded by mangroves, mudflats and wide, sandy shallows. The tides here are strong, exposing broad banks of sand twice a day. Founded by the Portuguese in 1887, the city grew as the sea gateway for landlocked lands inland.","w":"A flat coastal city at a wide, muddy estuary, fringed by dark mangroves and pale sandbanks exposed at low tide.","f":["The Beira Corridor links the port with Zimbabwe by road, railway and oil pipeline.","At spring tides the sea here rises and falls by several metres.","The Zambezi delta, Mozambique's largest, lies about 200 km up the coast to the north-east."],"q":{"q":"At the mouth of which river does Beira stand?","a":"The Pungwe","x":["The Zambezi","The Limpopo"]}},"Q125434":{"t":"A windy bay city with elephants just inland","s":"Port Elizabeth curves along the western shore of Algoa Bay, a broad sweep of the Indian Ocean on South Africa's southern coast. It is nicknamed the Windy City, and from above you may spot whitecaps racing across the bay. About 40 km inland, Addo Elephant National Park protects hundreds of elephants in dense bush. The city is now officially called Gqeberha.","w":"A city along the western curve of a wide, open bay, with a small cape at its southern end and dry bush inland.","f":["British settlers who arrived in 1820 laid the foundations of the town.","Fort Frederick, a stone fort built by the British in 1799, overlooks the harbour.","St Croix Island in Algoa Bay has one of the world's largest African penguin colonies."],"q":{"q":"Which bay does Port Elizabeth face?","a":"Algoa Bay","x":["Table Bay","False Bay"]}},"Q3889":{"t":"A capital on a wide bay, a bridge across its estuary","s":"Maputo sits on the western shore of Maputo Bay, a broad inlet of the Indian Ocean, on low red cliffs above the water. Out in the bay, the green island of Inhaca guards the entrance. A tall suspension bridge now crosses the estuary just south of the centre. Until 1976 the city was called Lourenço Marques, after a Portuguese trader who explored the bay in the 1540s.","w":"A city on red cliffs along a wide bay, with a suspension bridge across the estuary and a large island near the bay's mouth.","f":["The Maputo–Katembe Bridge, opened in 2018, has a main span of about 680 metres.","Inhaca Island, at the mouth of the bay, is ringed by coral reefs and seagrass meadows.","Maputo lies less than 100 km from the borders of both South Africa and Eswatini."],"q":{"q":"What was Maputo called until 1976?","a":"Lourenço Marques","x":["Sofala","Vila Pery"]}},"Q1763":{"t":"The largest city beyond the Arctic Circle","s":"Murmansk stretches along the eastern shore of the Kola Bay, a long, fjord-like inlet running north to the Barents Sea. It is the largest city in the world north of the Arctic Circle. Thanks to the last warm reach of the North Atlantic Current, its port stays free of ice all winter, while the lakes and tundra around it freeze solid.","w":"A long city on the hilly east shore of a narrow, fjord-like bay, surrounded by low tundra dotted with lakes.","f":["The polar night here lasts about 40 days, from early December to mid-January.","The midnight sun shines over Murmansk from late May to late July.","Murmansk was founded in 1916."],"q":{"q":"What keeps Murmansk's port free of ice in winter?","a":"Warm North Atlantic water","x":["Salt from nearby mines","Hot volcanic springs"]}},"ne-pp-1159150693":{"t":"Russia's first seaport, on a great northern delta","s":"Arkhangelsk lies where the Northern Dvina fans out into a broad delta of channels and islands before meeting the White Sea. Endless dark taiga surrounds it. Founded in 1584 around the monastery of the Archangel Michael, it was for over a century Russia's main gateway to the sea, until Saint Petersburg took over. In winter the frozen river becomes a white highway.","w":"A long city along a wide river splitting into many channels just before a large bay, surrounded by dark forest.","f":["The scientist Mikhail Lomonosov was born in 1711 near Kholmogory, upstream on the Dvina.","Ice covers the sea near the city for roughly half the year.","The Solovetsky Islands, a UNESCO site, lie in the White Sea about 230 km to the west."],"q":{"q":"Which river forms a delta at Arkhangelsk?","a":"The Northern Dvina","x":["The Pechora","The Onega"]}},"Q891":{"t":"Where the Oka pours into the Volga","s":"Nizhny Novgorod stands at one of Russia's great river meetings: the Oka flows into the Volga right at the city's heart. The old town sits high on the steep right bank, looking across the water to a low, forested plain on the far side. The city was founded in 1221 to guard this junction of river routes.","w":"Two wide rivers meeting at a sharp point, with the city on high wooded bluffs and flat forest across the Volga.","f":["A cable car across the Volga links the city with the town of Bor, about 3.6 km away.","From 1817 the city hosted a famous summer trade fair, one of the largest in the Russian Empire.","The Volga, which flows past the city, is Europe's longest river at about 3,500 km."],"q":{"q":"Which river joins the Volga at Nizhny Novgorod?","a":"The Oka","x":["The Kama","The Sheksna"]}},"Q914":{"t":"A city stretched for dozens of kilometres along the Volga","s":"Volgograd is one of the longest cities in Europe: a narrow ribbon along the high western bank of the Volga, running for more than 60 km. Across the river lies a completely different world, the green Volga–Akhtuba floodplain, laced with channels, lakes and woods. At the city's southern end, the Volga–Don Canal sets off west towards the Don.","w":"A long, narrow city on the west bank of the broad Volga, facing a green, channel-laced floodplain on the east.","f":["The Volga–Don Canal, about 100 km long, links the Volga with the Don and so with the Black Sea.","A dam just north of the city holds back the Volgograd Reservoir, which is about 540 km long.","The Volga–Akhtuba floodplain opposite the city is protected as a nature park."],"q":{"q":"What was Volgograd's original name?","a":"Tsaritsyn","x":["Simbirsk","Kuybyshev"]}},"Q911":{"t":"A city on a ridge between two winding rivers","s":"Ufa stretches along a long, high ridge between two rivers, the Belaya and the Ufa, which wind below it in wide loops before they meet. To the east the land rises gently towards the Southern Urals. The city is the capital of Bashkortostan, a region known for its forested hills and its honey from wild bees.","w":"A long city on a high ridge squeezed between two meandering rivers, with forested hills to the east.","f":["The Belaya, Ufa's main river, is the largest tributary of the Kama.","In Bashkortostan's forests, honey is still gathered from wild bees nesting in hollow trees.","The city grew from a Russian fortress founded in 1574."],"q":{"q":"Ufa stands where the river Ufa meets which other river?","a":"The Belaya","x":["The Chusovaya","The Vyatka"]}},"Q894":{"t":"Where the Volga swings round a loop of wooded hills","s":"Samara lies on the left bank of the Volga beside one of the river's most dramatic features, the Samara Bend: the Volga swings east in a huge loop around the forested Zhiguli Mountains. From above, the loop and its dark wooded hills are unmistakable. The city stretches for kilometres along the river, which is more than a kilometre wide here.","w":"A long city on the east bank of the broad Volga, facing the dark, forested hills inside the river's great loop.","f":["The Zhiguli Mountains inside the loop are protected as a nature reserve and a national park.","Samara was founded in 1586 as a fortress guarding the Volga trade route.","Upstream, the Volga widens into the Kuybyshev Reservoir, the largest reservoir in Europe."],"q":{"q":"Which hills does the Volga loop around near Samara?","a":"The Zhiguli Mountains","x":["The Valdai Hills","The Khibiny"]}},"Q900":{"t":"A city where the Volga spreads wide as a sea","s":"Kazan stands on the left bank of the Volga where the little Kazanka river flows in, and here the Volga, swollen by a reservoir downstream, is several kilometres wide. The white-walled kremlin sits on a hill above the confluence. For centuries Russian and Tatar, Christian and Muslim cultures have met here, and it is the capital of Tatarstan.","w":"A large city on the east bank of a very wide stretch of the Volga, split by a smaller river flowing in from the north-east.","f":["The Kazan Kremlin has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2000.","Some 70 km downstream, the Kama, the Volga's largest tributary, joins it.","Kazan celebrated its 1,000th anniversary in 2005."],"q":{"q":"Which small river flows into the Volga at Kazan's kremlin?","a":"The Kazanka","x":["The Oka","The Sura"]}},"Q2449":{"t":"An old Indian Ocean trading port among white dunes","s":"Mogadishu lines the Indian Ocean coast of the Horn of Africa, a pale city backed by sand dunes, with reefs just offshore. For a thousand years it was a trading port linking Africa with Arabia, Persia and India. When the traveller Ibn Battuta visited in 1331, he described a very large and wealthy town whose cloth was sent as far as Egypt.","w":"A pale city along a straight surf coast, backed by sandy dunes, with the green valley of the Shabelle inland to the west.","f":["Mogadishu lies only about 230 km north of the equator.","The Shabelle River passes within about 30 km of the city but never reaches the sea.","In the Middle Ages the Sultanate of Mogadishu minted its own coins."],"q":{"q":"Which famous traveller visited Mogadishu in 1331?","a":"Ibn Battuta","x":["Marco Polo","Vasco da Gama"]}},"Q3826":{"t":"A city threaded between dark mountains and the sea","s":"Muscat doesn't sprawl like other Gulf capitals: it is threaded through a string of coves and valleys between the rugged Hajar Mountains and the Gulf of Oman. The dark brown rock of those mountains is ancient ocean floor, pushed up onto land. In the old harbour, two Portuguese forts from the 16th century still guard the entrance.","w":"A long string of white districts in valleys and coves between dark, jagged mountains and a blue sea.","f":["The Semail ophiolite, the dark rock around Muscat, is one of the largest exposed slabs of oceanic crust on Earth.","Forts Jalali and Mirani were built by the Portuguese in the 1580s.","For centuries Muscat's sheltered cove served ships trading between Arabia, India and East Africa."],"q":{"q":"What are Muscat's dark mountains largely made of?","a":"Ancient ocean floor","x":["Volcanic ash","Coral limestone"]}},"Q465022":{"t":"Nigeria's coal city below a green escarpment","s":"Enugu lies at the foot of the Udi Hills, a steep, green escarpment rising on its western side in south-eastern Nigeria. Coal was found in those hills in 1909, and the town grew up around the mines, earning the nickname 'Coal City'. From above, look for the city nestled against the ridge, with rolling, wooded farmland all around.","w":"A city at the foot of a steep, green escarpment on its west side, amid rolling, wooded hills.","f":["Coal was discovered in the Udi Hills in 1909, and mining began a few years later.","The Udi Plateau rises a few hundred metres above the plain around the city."],"q":{"q":"What does the Igbo name Enugu mean?","a":"Top of the hill","x":["River bend","Market town"]}},"Q336350":{"t":"Capital of a 19th-century caliphate, on a Sahel river","s":"Sokoto lies in the far north-west of Nigeria, where the Sokoto and Rima rivers meet on a broad, flat floodplain. In the rainy season the valley floor turns green with rice and vegetables; the rest of the year, dusty savanna stretches to the horizon. The city was founded in 1809 as the capital of the Sokoto Caliphate, one of Africa's largest 19th-century states.","w":"A low, dusty city on a flat plain beside a broad river valley, with green fields along the water.","f":["The Sokoto Caliphate stretched from present-day Burkina Faso to Cameroon.","Sokoto is one of Nigeria's hottest cities, with April afternoons often above 40 °C.","The Sultan of Sokoto is still regarded as a spiritual leader by many Nigerian Muslims."],"q":{"q":"Who founded the Sokoto Caliphate?","a":"Usman dan Fodio","x":["Mansa Musa","Sundiata Keita"]}},"Q915":{"t":"The city that gave its name to a geological age","s":"Perm stretches for kilometres along the Kama, a broad river that widens into a reservoir just upstream. Endless taiga surrounds it, and to the east the first low ridges of the Urals begin. In 1841 the Scottish geologist Roderick Murchison studied the rocks of this region and named a whole period of Earth's history after it: the Permian.","w":"A long city on both banks of a wide river, with a reservoir upstream and dark forest in every direction.","f":["The Permian period, roughly 299 to 252 million years ago, ended with the largest mass extinction in Earth's history.","Perm began in 1723 as a copper-smelting works.","The city stretches for about 70 km along the Kama."],"q":{"q":"Which geological period is named after the Perm region?","a":"The Permian","x":["The Devonian","The Jurassic"]}},"Q335548":{"t":"A highland town near a giant iron meteorite","s":"Mbeya sits in a high valley in Tanzania's Southern Highlands, about 1,700 metres up, with the steep Mbeya Range rising right behind it. The land around is intensely green, a patchwork of tea, coffee and maize on rich volcanic soils. South-west of the town, a lump of iron from space weighing around 16 tonnes still rests where it fell.","w":"A town in a green valley at the foot of a steep mountain range, surrounded by a patchwork of highland farms.","f":["The Mbozi meteorite, some 50 km south-west of Mbeya, is one of the largest meteorites ever found.","Mbeya Peak, the highest point of the range above the town, rises to about 2,800 metres.","Further east, the Kitulo Plateau is so rich in wild orchids that it is called the 'Garden of God'."],"q":{"q":"What giant object lies at Mbozi, near Mbeya?","a":"An iron meteorite","x":["A dinosaur skeleton","A fallen satellite"]}},"Q3935":{"t":"A highland capital in the heart of a dry land","s":"Windhoek lies in a basin in Namibia's central highlands, about 1,650 metres up, framed by the Auas Mountains to the south and the Eros hills to the north. Around it stretches dry, thorny savanna, brown for much of the year. Water is so precious here that since 1968 the city has turned wastewater directly into drinking water, a world first.","w":"A compact city in a bowl of low, rugged hills on dry, brown highland savanna.","f":["Namibia's Atlantic coast lies about 300 km to the west.","The Auas Mountains south of the city rise to about 2,480 metres, among the highest in Namibia.","Hot springs once bubbled up in the valley, drawing the first settlers."],"q":{"q":"What has Windhoek done with its wastewater since 1968?","a":"Turned it into drinking water","x":["Piped it into the Atlantic","Used it to cool a power plant"]}},"Q986336":{"t":"A quiet farm town beside the heaviest meteorite on Earth","s":"Grootfontein is a small town in the bushland of northern Namibia, named after a large spring that drew settlers and cattle herders. The surprise lies about 20 km west, on a farm: the Hoba meteorite, a slab of iron weighing around 60 tonnes, the largest known on Earth. It is so heavy that it has never been moved from where it landed.","w":"A small town in green, bushy savanna, with low dolomite hills and farmland around it.","f":["The Hoba meteorite fell less than 80,000 years ago and was found by a farmer in 1920.","Grootfontein means 'big spring' in Afrikaans.","The dolomite country around the town hides sinkhole lakes, such as Otjikoto near Tsumeb."],"q":{"q":"What record-breaking object lies near Grootfontein?","a":"The largest known meteorite","x":["The oldest known baobab","Africa's largest crater"]}},"Q2222874":{"t":"A spice-island port of coral stone and turquoise water","s":"Zanzibar City lies on the west coast of Unguja, the main island of the Zanzibar archipelago, facing the African mainland across a channel of turquoise shallows and reefs. Its old heart, Stone Town, is a maze of coral-stone houses. In 1840 the Sultan of Oman made it his capital, and cloves grown on the island were shipped around the world.","w":"A town on the west coast of a flat green island, fringed by turquoise reefs and sandbanks, with the mainland to the west.","f":["Stone Town has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2000.","Freddie Mercury was born in Stone Town in 1946.","In the 19th century Zanzibar was the world's leading producer of cloves."],"q":{"q":"Which spice made Zanzibar famous in the 19th century?","a":"Cloves","x":["Saffron","Vanilla"]}},"Q8818":{"t":"A green river of parkland through an orange-grove city","s":"Valencia lies on a flat coastal plain by the Mediterranean, surrounded by the huerta, a patchwork of irrigated orchards and fields. Look for a long green ribbon curving through the city: the old bed of the river Turia, turned into a park after the river was diverted to the south. Just south of the city glints the Albufera, a lagoon ringed by rice paddies.","w":"A large city on a flat coast, with a green park snaking through it and a big shallow lagoon and rice fields to the south.","f":["The Turia Gardens, laid out in the old riverbed, run for about 9 km through the city.","The rice fields around the Albufera lagoon are the home of paella.","The Romans founded Valencia in 138 BC."],"q":{"q":"Which dish was born in the rice fields around Valencia?","a":"Paella","x":["Gazpacho","Tortilla"]}},"Q3787":{"t":"A planned capital among giant granite domes","s":"Abuja was built from scratch near the geographic centre of Nigeria and became the capital in 1991. From above, look for its broad avenues fanning out below Aso Rock, a bare granite dome about 400 metres high on the city's eastern edge. The hilly savanna around it is studded with more of these great rounded rocks.","w":"A spacious planned city of wide avenues at the foot of a big bare granite dome, in hilly green savanna.","f":["Zuma Rock, a huge granite monolith about 40 km west of the city, appears on Nigeria's 100-naira note.","The city's master plan was drawn up in the late 1970s.","Abuja lies in the Federal Capital Territory, carved out so the capital would belong to no single state."],"q":{"q":"Which city did Abuja replace as Nigeria's capital in 1991?","a":"Lagos","x":["Ibadan","Kano"]}},"Q3739":{"t":"A river-mouth capital facing a maze of islands","s":"Bissau lies on the wide estuary of the Geba River, where muddy water, mangroves and tidal flats blur the line between land and sea. Offshore spreads the Bijagós archipelago, a scatter of dozens of low, green islands and sandbanks. The tides are strong, and at low water vast banks of mud and sand appear. The Portuguese built a fort here in 1687.","w":"A city on a broad, muddy estuary lined with mangroves, with a scatter of low green islands out to sea.","f":["The Bijagós archipelago has about 88 islands and islets, only around 20 of them permanently inhabited.","On the Bijagós, hippos swim in salt water between the islands.","The Geba estuary is so broad at Bissau that it looks more like an arm of the sea than a river."],"q":{"q":"Which archipelago lies off the coast at Bissau?","a":"The Bijagós","x":["The Los Islands","The Banana Islands"]}},"Q2656":{"t":"A city in a golden shell between mountains and sea","s":"Palermo fills a broad bay on Sicily's north coast, set in a crescent of bare mountains. The plain it stands on is called the Conca d'Oro, the 'golden shell', after the citrus groves that once covered it. To the north, the limestone headland of Monte Pellegrino juts out into the sea. Phoenicians founded the city in the 8th century BC.","w":"A city on a plain curving around a bay, ringed by bare mountains, with a big rocky headland on its northern side.","f":["Goethe called Monte Pellegrino the most beautiful promontory in the world.","Palermo's Arab-Norman buildings became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2015.","The Phoenicians called the city Ziz, usually translated as 'flower'."],"q":{"q":"What is the plain around Palermo called?","a":"The Conca d'Oro","x":["The Campania Felix","The Tavoliere"]}},"Q3805":{"t":"A white-stone city spilling over many hills","s":"Amman spreads over a tangle of steep hills on the edge of the desert plateau, and from above it looks almost entirely white, since most buildings are faced with pale limestone. To the west the land drops away into the Jordan Valley and the Dead Sea, some 40 km off. In Greco-Roman times the city was called Philadelphia.","w":"A pale, almost white city draped over steep hills, with the deep trench of the Jordan Valley and the Dead Sea to the west.","f":["The shore of the Dead Sea, about 430 metres below sea level, is the lowest land on Earth.","Amman's Roman theatre, cut into a hillside, seated about 6,000 people.","The city grew on the site of Rabbath Ammon, capital of the ancient Ammonites."],"q":{"q":"What was Amman called in Greco-Roman times?","a":"Philadelphia","x":["Gerasa","Petra"]}},"Q216":{"t":"A baroque city among forested hills and river bends","s":"Vilnius lies where the little Vilnia joins the winding Neris, among wooded hills that give the city a surprisingly green look from above. Its old town, one of the largest in Eastern Europe, is packed with baroque church towers. The city is first mentioned in 1323, in letters of Grand Duke Gediminas inviting merchants and craftsmen to settle here.","w":"A green, hilly city on a looping river, with forests reaching right into town and many lakes to the west.","f":["The Old Town of Vilnius has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1994.","In 1989 French geographers placed the geographic centre of Europe about 26 km north of Vilnius.","Trakai, with its island castle on a lake, lies less than 30 km to the west."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Vilnia flow into at Vilnius?","a":"The Neris","x":["The Nemunas","The Daugava"]}},"Q1773":{"t":"A Hanseatic port where a great river meets the gulf","s":"Riga stands on the Daugava a few kilometres before it flows into the Gulf of Riga, a broad, shallow bay of the Baltic. From above, the wide river divides the compact old town on the east bank from newer districts on the west, and pine forests and bogs close in all around. Bishop Albert founded the city in 1201, and it became a leading Hanseatic port.","w":"A city on a wide river just before it enters a broad bay, with sandy coast and dark pine forest along the shore.","f":["About a third of the buildings in central Riga are Art Nouveau, one of the densest collections anywhere.","The historic centre of Riga has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1997.","West of the city, the resort of Jūrmala stretches for more than 30 km along a sandy beach."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Riga?","a":"The Daugava","x":["The Neman","The Gauja"]}},"Q225641":{"t":"An island port wrapped in two deep creeks","s":"Mombasa's old heart is an island, a low coral block wedged between two deep inlets that reach into the Kenyan coast. On one side, Kilindini Harbour shelters one of East Africa's biggest ports; on the other, the old town faces Tudor Creek. Offshore, a line of white surf marks the fringing reef. For centuries this was a key port of the Swahili coast.","w":"A small island tucked between two winding creeks on a green coast, with white surf breaking on the reef offshore.","f":["Fort Jesus, begun in 1593, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Vasco da Gama called at Mombasa in 1498 on his way to India.","The island is joined to the mainland by causeways and bridges, and to the south coast by ferry."],"q":{"q":"Who built Fort Jesus in Mombasa in the 1590s?","a":"The Portuguese","x":["The Omanis","The British"]}},"Q3909":{"t":"The lowland capital of a kingdom in the sky","s":"Maseru sits on the Caledon River, right on Lesotho's western border, and even this 'lowland' capital is about 1,600 metres above the sea. Lesotho is completely surrounded by South Africa. From above, look east: the land climbs into the Maloti Mountains, where most of the country lies above 1,800 metres, often dusted with snow in winter.","w":"A town beside a narrow river border, with flat farmland to the west and folded, bare mountains rising to the east.","f":["Lesotho's lowest point, about 1,400 metres, is the highest 'lowest point' of any country.","Maseru's name means 'red sandstones' in Sesotho.","Thaba Bosiu, the flat-topped mountain stronghold of King Moshoeshoe I, lies about 25 km to the east."],"q":{"q":"Which country completely surrounds Lesotho?","a":"South Africa","x":["Botswana","Eswatini"]}},"Q3915":{"t":"A hill city among rice paddies on the Great Red Island","s":"Antananarivo sprawls over a long granite ridge and the hills around it in the middle of Madagascar, about 1,300 metres up. Between the hills, bright green rice paddies still fill the valleys right inside the city, and the soil around is a striking red. The name means 'city of the thousand', said to recall a thousand warriors who guarded it.","w":"A hilly city with a long ridge at its centre, bright green rice paddies in the valleys and red earth all around.","f":["The Rova, the old royal palace compound, crowns the highest hill in the city.","Lake Anosy in the city centre is ringed by jacaranda trees that bloom purple in spring.","Madagascar's red laterite soils earned it the nickname 'the Great Red Island'."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Antananarivo mean?","a":"City of the thousand","x":["City of rice","Red hill"]}},"Q456":{"t":"Two rivers meeting at the tip of a narrow peninsula","s":"Lyon is built around a meeting of rivers. The broad, fast Rhône, coming from the Alps, and the calmer Saône, from the north, run side by side through the city, enclosing a long, narrow peninsula before they join at its southern tip. West of the Saône rises the hill of Fourvière. The Romans founded Lugdunum here in 43 BC.","w":"Two rivers running side by side through a big city, a long thin strip of land between them, joining at a point to the south.","f":["Lugdunum was a capital of Roman Gaul, and the emperor Claudius was born here.","The historic centre of Lyon has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1998.","From the 16th to the 19th century Lyon was Europe's great silk-weaving city."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers meet at Lyon?","a":"The Rhône and the Saône","x":["The Loire and the Allier","The Seine and the Marne"]}},"Q1004822":{"t":"A river port near the top of the Congo's great arc","s":"Bumba sits on the north bank of the Congo, near the top of the great arc the river draws across Central Africa. Here the Congo is a braided giant, several kilometres wide and dotted with long wooded islands, flowing through unbroken rainforest. From above, the brown river and the dark green canopy stretch to every horizon.","w":"A small town on the north bank of a huge, braided brown river full of wooded islands, amid endless rainforest.","f":["The Congo crosses the equator twice on its long arc through Central Africa.","Only the Amazon carries more water to the sea than the Congo.","Around Bumba the river reaches the northernmost part of its course."],"q":{"q":"How many times does the Congo River cross the equator?","a":"Twice","x":["Once","Three times"]}},"Q3659":{"t":"A Sahel capital where two rivers meet on a border","s":"N'Djamena stands where the Logone flows into the Chari, on a flat plain at the southern edge of the Sahel. Across the Logone lies Cameroon, so close that the two countries face each other over the water. The combined river flows on north-west to Lake Chad, a shallow lake that swells and shrinks dramatically with the seasons and the years.","w":"A flat city at a river confluence, with a smaller town across the water and floodplains and dry savanna around.","f":["The Chari river system supplies about 90 percent of the water reaching Lake Chad.","The city was founded in 1900 as Fort-Lamy.","The southern shore of Lake Chad lies roughly 100 km north-west of the city."],"q":{"q":"Which lake does the Chari feed, north-west of N'Djamena?","a":"Lake Chad","x":["Lake Victoria","Lake Volta"]}},"Q242587":{"t":"An old sultanate capital on a caravan road","s":"Abéché lies on the dry plateau of eastern Chad, a low, sand-coloured town among scattered rocky hills and seasonal riverbeds. From 1850 it was the capital of the Sultanate of Wadai, a powerful Sahel state. Caravans once set out from here across the Sahara to the Mediterranean coast at Benghazi, some 2,000 km away.","w":"A small, dusty town among low rocky hills and dry, sandy riverbeds on a flat, brown plateau.","f":["The earlier Wadai capital, Ouara, lies in ruins some 50 km to the north.","Most of the year's rain falls in July and August, briefly turning the wadis into rivers."],"q":{"q":"Abéché was the capital of which sultanate?","a":"Wadai","x":["Bornu","Darfur"]}},"Q3818":{"t":"An island capital beneath a 3,000-metre volcano","s":"Malabo lies on the north coast of Bioko, a lush volcanic island in the Gulf of Guinea, separated from the rest of its country on the mainland. Behind it rises Pico Basilé, a forested volcano topping 3,000 metres and often capped in cloud. The city's harbour curves inside the rim of an old volcanic crater, partly flooded by the sea.","w":"A small city on the north shore of a green volcanic island, with a huge forested peak rising behind it.","f":["Pico Basilé, at about 3,010 metres, is the highest point of Equatorial Guinea.","Bioko lies less than 40 km off the coast of Cameroon.","Malabo is one of the few capitals set on an island apart from its country's mainland."],"q":{"q":"What was Malabo called when the British founded it in 1827?","a":"Port Clarence","x":["Fernando Po","Bathurst"]}},"Q130514":{"t":"Ancient Thebes, split by the Nile between life and death","s":"Luxor straddles the Nile in a narrow green valley between bare desert cliffs. On the east bank, where the sun rises, lie the town and the vast temples of Karnak and Luxor. On the west bank, beyond the fields, the land rises to dry limestone hills hiding the Valley of the Kings, where pharaohs of the New Kingdom were buried.","w":"A green strip on both banks of the Nile, a town on the east side and pale desert cliffs rising sharply on the west.","f":["As Thebes, this was Egypt's capital for much of the New Kingdom, more than 3,000 years ago.","The Karnak temple complex is one of the largest religious sites ever built.","Ancient Thebes and its necropolis became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1979."],"q":{"q":"What did the ancient Greeks call the city at Luxor?","a":"Thebes","x":["Memphis","Heliopolis"]}},"Q3642":{"t":"A modernist city on the rim of a great escarpment","s":"Asmara sits on the Eritrean highlands at about 2,300 metres, near the edge of a dramatic escarpment. Just to the east, the land plunges more than 2,000 metres towards the hot Red Sea coast in only a few dozen kilometres. The city's streets are lined with early 20th-century modernist buildings, and in 2017 UNESCO listed it as a modernist African city.","w":"A compact city on a high plateau, beside the edge of a steep, deeply cut escarpment falling east towards the Red Sea.","f":["A narrow-gauge railway winds down the escarpment from Asmara to the port of Massawa.","Thanks to its altitude, Asmara has mild temperatures all year despite lying in the tropics.","Asmara's name is often explained as 'they united them', after four villages that joined together."],"q":{"q":"For which style of architecture is Asmara UNESCO-listed?","a":"Modernist","x":["Gothic","Baroque"]}},"Q1435":{"t":"A city between a wooded mountain and the Sava","s":"Zagreb spreads south from the slopes of Medvednica, a forested mountain rising just north of the city, down to the broad plain of the Sava. The historic core sits on the lower hills; newer districts stretch across the flat land beyond the river. The two medieval towns of Gradec and Kaptol were united as one city in 1850.","w":"A city between a dark, forested mountain to the north and a river crossing the flat plain to the south.","f":["Sljeme, the summit of Medvednica, rises to about 1,030 metres.","In the Lower Town, a U-shaped chain of parks and squares is known as the 'Green Horseshoe'.","The Sava, flowing past the city, joins the Danube at Belgrade."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Zagreb?","a":"The Sava","x":["The Drava","The Danube"]}},"Q1770":{"t":"A medieval Hanseatic town on a limestone bay","s":"Tallinn sits on a wide bay of the Gulf of Finland, with wooded islands out in the water and Helsinki about 80 km across the sea. Its medieval old town, one of the best preserved in Northern Europe, clusters around Toompea, a limestone hill that belongs to the Baltic Klint, a long coastal escarpment. As Reval, it was a prosperous Hanseatic port.","w":"A compact city curving around a broad bay with a few wooded islands, and flat, forested coast stretching east and west.","f":["The historic centre of Tallinn has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1997.","The Baltic Klint, a limestone escarpment, runs for more than 1,000 km from Sweden into Russia.","Tallinn's name is often explained as 'Danish town', recalling Danish rule in the 13th century."],"q":{"q":"What was Tallinn's old Hanseatic name?","a":"Reval","x":["Dorpat","Memel"]}},"Q261887":{"t":"A river town where grassy plateaus meet forested valleys","s":"Kikwit lies on the Kwilu River in south-western Congo, a busy regional town in a landscape of grassy plateaus sliced by deep, forested valleys. From above, look for the striking pattern: pale savanna on the high ground and dark green ribbons of gallery forest along every stream. The Kwilu flows north towards the Kasai and, eventually, the Congo.","w":"A town on a winding river, with pale grassy plateaus and dark green strips of forest lining the valleys.","f":["The Kwilu joins the Kwango, which feeds the Kasai, one of the Congo's largest tributaries.","The country around Kikwit has long been known for its oil palms."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Kikwit?","a":"The Kwilu","x":["The Kwango","The Lomami"]}},"Q211073":{"t":"A rocky river port where the ocean ships must stop","s":"Matadi clings to steep, rocky hills on the left bank of the Congo, about 150 km from the Atlantic. It is as far as ocean-going ships can sail: just upstream, the river squeezes into gorges and rapids. In the 1890s a railway was built to carry goods around them to Kinshasa. The town's name means 'stones' in Kikongo.","w":"A hilly, rocky town on a narrow, fast stretch of the Congo, crossed by a single long suspension bridge.","f":["The Matadi–Kinshasa railway, built between 1890 and 1898, bypasses the Livingstone Falls.","The Matadi Bridge over the Congo has a main span of about 520 metres.","Upstream at Yelala Falls, the Portuguese navigator Diogo Cão left an inscription on the rocks in 1485."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Matadi mean in Kikongo?","a":"Stones","x":["Harbour","Sandbank"]}},"Q271404":{"t":"A mining town ringed by giant open pits","s":"Kolwezi sits on a high plateau in the south of the Congo, on one of the richest mineral belts on Earth. From above, the land around the town is scarred by huge open-pit mines: terraced craters, grey spoil heaps and pale tailings ponds. The ores here carry copper and cobalt, a metal now in demand for rechargeable batteries.","w":"A town surrounded by vast terraced open-pit mines, grey spoil heaps and flat tailings ponds on a high plateau.","f":["The Democratic Republic of the Congo produces more than half of the world's mined cobalt, much of it around Kolwezi.","The Central African Copperbelt stretches from Kolwezi across the border into Zambia.","Kolwezi lies about 1,450 metres above sea level."],"q":{"q":"Which battery metal is mined in large amounts around Kolwezi?","a":"Cobalt","x":["Lithium","Nickel"]}},"Q187593":{"t":"Copper capital with a black mountain of slag","s":"Lubumbashi spreads across the high, flat plateau of the Copperbelt in the far south-east of the Congo, only a few dozen kilometres from Zambia. Look for a dark hill near the city centre: a huge heap of slag left by a century of copper smelting, beside the smelter's tall chimney. The city was founded in 1910 as Élisabethville.","w":"A grid-planned city on a flat plateau, with a dark, flat-topped slag heap near its centre and dry woodland around.","f":["Lubumbashi is one of the largest cities in the Democratic Republic of the Congo.","The Zambian border lies only some 30 km away.","Miombo woodland, a dry forest of broad-leaved trees, covers much of the plateau around the city."],"q":{"q":"What was Lubumbashi called when it was founded in 1910?","a":"Élisabethville","x":["Léopoldville","Stanleyville"]}},"Q3876":{"t":"A garden capital spread across the central plateau","s":"Lilongwe lies on Malawi's central plateau, about 1,050 metres up, a spread-out city of separate districts divided by green belts and the winding Lilongwe River. Around it, red soil and farmland stretch in every direction, much of it planted with maize and tobacco. About 100 km to the east, the land drops to the shore of Lake Malawi.","w":"A loose, spread-out city of separate districts among trees and red farmland, crossed by a small winding river.","f":["Lilongwe replaced Zomba as Malawi's capital in 1975.","Lake Malawi is home to more species of fish than any other lake on Earth.","Many of Lilongwe's neighbourhoods have numbers instead of names, such as Area 3 or Area 47."],"q":{"q":"Which lake lies about 100 km east of Lilongwe?","a":"Lake Malawi","x":["Lake Tanganyika","Lake Kariba"]}},"Q3825":{"t":"A 'free town' on an estuary just north of the equator","s":"Libreville lies on the north shore of the Komo estuary, where the river opens into the Atlantic, only about 40 km north of the equator. Rainforest reaches almost to the city's edge, and across the estuary the mangroves and beaches of Pongara National Park stay wild. The city was founded in 1849 by freed Africans rescued from a slave ship.","w":"A city along the north shore of a wide estuary opening to the sea, with dense green forest and mangroves on the far side.","f":["The beaches of Pongara, across the estuary, are a nesting ground for leatherback turtles.","Close to 90 percent of Gabon is covered by forest.","Libreville is Gabon's capital and by far its largest city."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Libreville mean?","a":"Free town","x":["New town","River town"]}},"Q501715":{"t":"An island oil town on the edge of a great delta","s":"Port-Gentil stands near the tip of Cape Lopez on Mandji Island, a flat, sandy island at the mouth of the Ogooué, Gabon's great river. Behind it spreads the river's delta, a maze of channels, lagoons and mangroves. The town is the centre of Gabon's oil industry, and at night you may spot the lights of offshore platforms out in the Atlantic.","w":"A small town on the northern tip of a flat, sandy island, with a vast delta of channels and mangroves to the south-east.","f":["The Ogooué is Gabon's longest river, draining most of the country.","Cape Lopez is named after the Portuguese navigator Lopo Gonçalves, who sailed past it in the 1470s."],"q":{"q":"At the mouth of which river does Port-Gentil stand?","a":"The Ogooué","x":["The Congo","The Sanaga"]}},"Q9427":{"t":"The fabled desert city, just beyond the Niger","s":"Timbuktu stands where desert meets river: the Sahara's sand reaches its northern edge, while the Niger flows about 15 km to the south. From above, it is a small, sand-coloured town that could be anywhere. Yet from the 14th to the 16th centuries it was a great centre of trade and Islamic scholarship, its libraries filled with manuscripts.","w":"A small, sand-coloured town on the edge of dunes, with the green floodplain of the Niger a short way to the south.","f":["The Djinguereyber Mosque, built around 1327, is made largely of mud and straw.","Families in Timbuktu have kept tens of thousands of old manuscripts on law, astronomy and medicine.","Timbuktu has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1988."],"q":{"q":"Which Mali emperor had Timbuktu's Djinguereyber Mosque built?","a":"Mansa Musa","x":["Sundiata Keita","Askia Muhammad"]}},"Q3688":{"t":"A desert capital built on the dunes by the Atlantic","s":"Nouakchott lies where the Sahara meets the Atlantic, a low, sprawling city on flat sand between the ocean and a sea of dunes. It was little more than a village when work began in 1958 on a new capital, planned for about 15,000 people. Today it is home to around a million, and the desert still presses against its edges.","w":"A low, sprawling city on flat sand, a straight Atlantic beach to the west and orange dunes pressing in from the east.","f":["Banc d'Arguin National Park, a UNESCO site up the coast, hosts millions of wintering shorebirds.","Much of the city lies only a few metres above sea level.","The name Nouakchott is often translated as 'place of the winds'."],"q":{"q":"In which year did work begin on Nouakchott as a new capital?","a":"1958","x":["1901","1985"]}},"Q3703":{"t":"A capital on the Niger beneath a sandstone escarpment","s":"Bamako spreads along both banks of the Niger, a wide, shallow river braided with sandbanks and small rocky islands. Behind the city to the north rises the edge of the Manding Plateau, a line of sandstone cliffs and hills. Bridges tie the two halves of the city together. The name is often said to mean 'crocodile river' in Bambara.","w":"A broad city on both banks of a wide river with sandbanks and bridges, backed by sandstone hills to the north.","f":["Just downstream of the centre, the Sotuba rapids break the flow of the Niger.","Bamako is one of the fastest-growing cities in Africa.","The Niger rises only about 250 km from the Atlantic, yet flows inland, away from it, past Bamako."],"q":{"q":"What is Bamako's name often said to mean?","a":"Crocodile river","x":["Hippo pool","Red cliff"]}},"Q735645":{"t":"An oasis gateway to the Adrar and the Eye of the Sahara","s":"Atar is an oasis town of date palms in a valley of the Adrar plateau, in the heart of the Mauritanian Sahara. Dark sandstone cliffs and orange dunes surround it. Some 180 km to the north-east lies one of the most famous sights seen from the air: the Richat Structure, or Eye of the Sahara, a vast set of concentric rock rings.","w":"A green strip of palm groves in a dry valley between dark plateau cliffs, with sand dunes spreading around.","f":["The Richat Structure is about 40 km across, big enough to be picked out by astronauts.","Chinguetti, an old caravan town about 80 km to the east, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Every summer the date harvest, the guetna, draws families to the oases of the Adrar."],"q":{"q":"What is the Eye of the Sahara, north-east of Atar?","a":"An eroded dome of rock layers","x":["A meteorite crater","A dried-up salt lake"]}},"Q213507":{"t":"A mud-brick town that becomes an island in the flood","s":"Djenné stands in the inland delta of the Niger, on a patch of higher ground that the Bani River's floods turn into an island each rainy season. Its Great Mosque is the largest mud-brick building in the world, and every year the townspeople replaster it together in a festival. From above, look for a compact brown town ringed by water or green floodplain.","w":"A compact, earth-brown town on a low island in a wide, flat floodplain that turns to water in the wet season.","f":["The present Great Mosque dates from 1907, rebuilt on the site of a much older mosque.","Nearby Djenné-Djeno, settled around 250 BC, is one of the oldest known towns in sub-Saharan Africa.","The old towns of Djenné have been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1988."],"q":{"q":"What do Djenné's people replaster together every year?","a":"The Great Mosque","x":["The city walls","The market hall"]}},"Q208389":{"t":"A Sahara oasis city with green circles in the sand","s":"Sabha lies deep in the Libyan Sahara, in the Fezzan, a region of sand seas, stony plateaus and strings of oases. From above, look for perfect green circles in the desert around it: fields watered by rotating irrigation arms that draw on ancient groundwater. For centuries the town was a crossroads for caravans between the Mediterranean and the Sahel.","w":"A town on a flat desert plain, with scattered green circular fields and dark rocky plateaus and sand seas around.","f":["The groundwater pumped around Sabha fell as rain thousands of years ago, when the Sahara was greener.","About 150 km to the west, Germa was the capital of the Garamantes, a desert kingdom of Roman times.","Many years pass in the Fezzan with almost no rain at all."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient people ruled a desert kingdom west of Sabha?","a":"The Garamantes","x":["The Nabataeans","The Phoenicians"]}},"Q40816":{"t":"A Greek-founded port on the Gulf of Sidra","s":"Benghazi spreads along a low coast at the north-eastern corner of the Gulf of Sidra, where salt marshes and lagoons edge the sea. Inland to the east rise the green uplands of the Jebel Akhdar, one of the wettest parts of Libya. The city began as the Greek colony of Euesperides and was later renamed Berenice, after a Ptolemaic queen.","w":"A flat coastal city with lagoons and salt flats along the shore, and a green, wooded upland rising to the north-east.","f":["Greek settlers founded Euesperides here in the 6th century BC.","The Jebel Akhdar, the 'Green Mountain', gets far more rain than almost anywhere else in Libya.","Benghazi is Libya's second-largest city."],"q":{"q":"What was Benghazi called in Ptolemaic times?","a":"Berenice","x":["Cyrene","Apollonia"]}},"Q17151":{"t":"A port city facing Mount Olympus across the gulf","s":"Thessaloniki curves along the head of the Thermaic Gulf, a long waterfront backed by hills. On a clear day, look south-west across the water: the massif of Mount Olympus, home of the Greek gods, rises to nearly 3,000 metres, often capped with snow. West of the city, the Axios River builds a green delta of wetlands and rice fields.","w":"A city along the curving head of a gulf, backed by hills, with a river delta to the west and Olympus across the water.","f":["King Cassander founded the city in 315 BC and named it after his wife Thessalonike, half-sister of Alexander the Great.","Mount Olympus reaches about 2,918 metres, the highest point in Greece.","The city's early Christian and Byzantine monuments are a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"Thessaloniki is named after the half-sister of which ruler?","a":"Alexander the Great","x":["Julius Caesar","Ptolemy I"]}},"Q3820":{"t":"A city on a cape, with snowy mountains at its back","s":"Beirut sits on a triangular cape pushing into the Mediterranean, with the slopes of Mount Lebanon rising steeply just behind it. In winter the peaks, well over 2,000 metres high, turn white with snow while the sea below stays blue. Off the western tip stand two great sea arches, the Pigeon Rocks. As Roman Berytus, the city was famous for its school of law.","w":"A dense city covering a triangular cape, with steep mountain slopes rising immediately inland to the east.","f":["Beirut has been inhabited for around 5,000 years.","The cedar is Lebanon's emblem, and old cedar groves survive high on Mount Lebanon.","In late winter it is possible to ski on Mount Lebanon and swim in the sea on the same day."],"q":{"q":"What kind of school made Roman Berytus famous?","a":"A school of law","x":["A school of medicine","A school of philosophy"]}},"Q994":{"t":"A city named for its warm springs, in a river gorge","s":"Tbilisi winds along the valley of the Kura, which cuts through the city in a narrow gorge between steep hills. The old town clusters beneath a fortress on a ridge, where hot sulphur springs still feed domed bathhouses. The name comes from the Georgian word for 'warm'. On clear days the snowy wall of the Greater Caucasus stands along the northern horizon.","w":"A long city in a winding river valley between bare hills, with the snowy Greater Caucasus range far to the north.","f":["King Vakhtang Gorgasali is traditionally credited with founding the city in the 5th century.","The Tbilisi Sea, a reservoir on the city's north-eastern edge, is about 9 km long.","The Kura, called the Mtkvari in Georgian, flows on to the Caspian Sea through Azerbaijan."],"q":{"q":"What gave Tbilisi its name?","a":"Its warm springs","x":["Its vineyards","Its river bend"]}},"Q218861":{"t":"A castle-studded royal city above Lake Tana","s":"Gondar sits on the northern Ethiopian Highlands at over 2,100 metres, on hills overlooking the plain that runs down to Lake Tana, some 35 km to the south. In 1636 Emperor Fasilides made it his capital and built a stone castle; his successors added more, forming a walled royal enclosure. To the north-east rise the jagged Simien Mountains.","w":"A hilly town on the highlands with a large lake to the south and a range of jagged peaks far to the north-east.","f":["The royal enclosure of Fasil Ghebbi has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1979.","Gondar remained Ethiopia's capital for more than two centuries.","The Simien Mountains include Ras Dashen, at about 4,550 metres Ethiopia's highest peak."],"q":{"q":"Which lake, source of the Blue Nile, lies south of Gondar?","a":"Lake Tana","x":["Lake Turkana","Lake Abaya"]}},"Q956":{"t":"An imperial grid framed by mountains on two sides","s":"Beijing is laid out like a chessboard: straight avenues, a strict north–south axis and ring roads nested one inside another. Mountains curl around it on the west and north, while the plain opens towards the sea in the south-east. Its name means “northern capital”, and emperors ruled from here for most of the time since the 13th century.","w":"A vast grid on a flat plain, hemmed in by dark mountains to the north and west; at night, rectangular rings of light.","f":["The 2nd Ring Road traces the line of the old city walls, which were largely pulled down in the 20th century.","The Grand Canal, almost 1,800 km long, reaches its northern end on Beijing's eastern side.","The Forbidden City, finished in 1420, sits exactly on the city's north–south axis."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Beijing” mean?","a":"Northern capital","x":["Southern capital","Western capital"]}},"Q3630":{"t":"Java's great sprawl, with a scatter of islands offshore","s":"Jakarta spreads over a flat, hazy plain where Java meets the shallow Java Sea, and the city seems to have no edge — it simply fades into its neighbours. Offshore, the Thousand Islands dot the water like crumbs. On a clear day, volcanic cones rise to the south. The Dutch called it Batavia and ran their East Indies trade from here.","w":"A huge grey sprawl on Java's north coast, a bay with small islands to the north, volcanoes on the southern horizon.","f":["Despite their name, the Thousand Islands off Jakarta number only a little over a hundred.","The Dutch East India Company founded Batavia here in 1619, on the site of the port of Jayakarta.","The old harbour of Sunda Kelapa still hosts wooden pinisi schooners."],"q":{"q":"What was Jakarta called under Dutch rule?","a":"Batavia","x":["Buitenzorg","Nieuw Amsterdam"]}},"Q8686":{"t":"Where the muddy Yangtze finally meets the sea","s":"Look for brown water turning blue: the Yangtze pours its silt into the East China Sea right beside Shanghai. The city lies flat on the south bank, cut in two by the winding Huangpu River. Out to sea, a hair-thin line runs across the water — a bridge more than 30 km long to an island container port.","w":"A giant flat city on the south side of the brown Yangtze estuary, with a long, low island in the river mouth.","f":["Chongming Island in the Yangtze mouth is built entirely of river silt and is about 80 km long.","Shanghai has been the world's busiest container port since 2010.","The name is usually read as “on the sea” or “upon the sea”."],"q":{"q":"Which river winds through the centre of Shanghai?","a":"The Huangpu","x":["The Pearl River","The Qiantang"]}},"Q1490":{"t":"A city that fills an entire plain, wrapped round a bay","s":"Tokyo does not so much end as run out of flat land: buildings cover the Kanto Plain, Japan's largest, from the mountains to the sea. The city wraps around Tokyo Bay, much of whose shore is reclaimed land. On a clear day Fuji's cone stands about 100 km to the south-west. Until 1868 this was Edo, the shoguns' city.","w":"Continuous city around a wide bay; Fuji's lone cone to the south-west; at night an unbroken carpet of lights.","f":["Odaiba is named after cannon batteries built in the bay in 1853–54, after Commodore Perry's ships arrived.","The name Tokyo means “eastern capital”; the emperor moved here from Kyoto in 1868–69.","The Aqua-Line runs out into Tokyo Bay and seems to stop at an island — the rest of the road is a tunnel under the seabed."],"q":{"q":"What was Tokyo called before 1868?","a":"Edo","x":["Naniwa","Heian-kyō"]}},"Q1156":{"t":"Seven islands stitched into one long peninsula","s":"Mumbai points south into the Arabian Sea like a thumb. It was once seven separate islands, joined from the 18th century onwards by causeways and landfill into one crowded strip. Harbour water lies to the east, open sea to the west. After dark, look for the bright curve of lights along Back Bay — locals call it the Queen's Necklace.","w":"A narrow finger of land jutting south into the sea, a wide harbour on its east side, a green forest patch in the north.","f":["Portugal handed Bombay to England in 1661 as part of Catherine of Braganza's dowry on her marriage to Charles II.","Sanjay Gandhi National Park, a forest with wild leopards, lies inside the city limits.","A sea bridge about 22 km long, opened in 2024, links the city with the mainland across the harbour."],"q":{"q":"Mumbai was formed by joining how many islands?","a":"Seven","x":["Three","Twelve"]}},"Q1348":{"t":"Former capital of British India on a delta river","s":"Kolkata stretches along the east bank of the Hooghly, a working arm of the Ganges, with Howrah facing it across the water. Beyond its eastern edge, a patchwork of ponds and marshes quietly cleans the city's wastewater and raises fish. Further south the land dissolves into the channels and mangroves of the Sundarbans. Until 1911, this was the capital of British India.","w":"A long city on a brown river running north–south; square fish ponds to the east, delta channels to the south.","f":["The East Kolkata Wetlands use the city's sewage to feed fish ponds and are a protected Ramsar site.","Kolkata's port lies on the river, well over 100 km from the open sea.","The Sundarbans to the south are the world's largest mangrove forest, shared with Bangladesh."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Kolkata?","a":"The Hooghly","x":["The Brahmaputra","The Mahanadi"]}},"Q3130":{"t":"A drowned valley of blue fingers, open to the Pacific","s":"Sydney's harbour is a drowned river valley, and from above it looks like a hand of blue fingers reaching deep into the city. Sandstone headlands guard its narrow mouth to the Pacific. South lies Botany Bay, where the airport's runways run out into the water. To the west, the Blue Mountains rise in a haze that really does look blue.","w":"A deep, branching harbour entering the coast between two headlands; a round bay with runways to the south.","f":["The blue haze over the Blue Mountains comes from oils evaporating from eucalyptus leaves.","James Cook landed at Botany Bay in 1770; the First Fleet settled at Sydney Cove in 1788.","The Harbour Bridge, opened in 1932, is nicknamed the Coathanger for the shape of its arch."],"q":{"q":"Where did James Cook land in 1770, on Sydney's south side?","a":"Botany Bay","x":["Moreton Bay","Port Phillip Bay"]}},"Q334":{"t":"An island nation ringed by hundreds of waiting ships","s":"Before the city, you notice the sea: hundreds of ships at anchor in the Singapore Strait, scattered like grains of rice. The diamond-shaped island at the tip of the Malay Peninsula has grown by roughly a quarter through land reclamation, and its straight new shorelines are easy to spot. The equator lies less than 150 km to the south.","w":"A diamond-shaped island just off the Malay Peninsula, joined by a causeway, with anchored ships speckling the strait.","f":["Stamford Raffles set up a British trading post here in 1819.","The name comes from the Malay Singapura, “lion city”.","Jurong Island, the refining hub in the south-west, was made by joining seven small islands with landfill."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Singapura” mean?","a":"Lion city","x":["Tiger city","City by the sea"]}},"Q8646":{"t":"Green mountains, a crowded shore and a fragrant harbour","s":"From above, Hong Kong is surprisingly green: steep, forested hills and dozens of islands, with the city packed into thin strips along the shore. Victoria Harbour, a narrow blue channel, separates Kowloon from Hong Kong Island. Further west, look for the airport on a flat man-made platform and a long bridge striking out across the Pearl River estuary.","w":"Hilly green islands and a peninsula, dense city lining a narrow harbour; a rectangular airport island to the west.","f":["About 40 percent of Hong Kong's land is protected as country parks.","The bridge to Zhuhai and Macau is about 55 km long and dips into an undersea tunnel between two artificial islands.","The name means “fragrant harbour”, probably after the incense wood once shipped from here."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Hong Kong” mean?","a":"Fragrant harbour","x":["Pearl harbour","Deep harbour"]}},"Q1353":{"t":"Seven cities in one, on the banks of the Yamuna","s":"Delhi has been rebuilt again and again on the same stretch of the Yamuna, and tradition counts at least seven historic cities layered here. From above, the river runs down the eastern side, braided with sandbars in the dry season. A low, scrubby ridge — the last tip of the ancient Aravalli hills — cuts through the west. At night the glow spreads far across the plain.","w":"A huge sprawl on a flat plain, a braided river along its east side, a dark wooded ridge through the west.","f":["Old Delhi, or Shahjahanabad, was built by Shah Jahan, creator of the Taj Mahal; its Red Fort was finished in 1648.","The Aravalli Range, whose last spur ends in Delhi, is one of the oldest mountain ranges on Earth.","The Iron Pillar near the Qutb Minar has stood for about 1,600 years with remarkably little rust."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past Delhi?","a":"The Yamuna","x":["The Ganges","The Sutlej"]}},"Q269":{"t":"A green oasis city at the foot of the Tian Shan","s":"Tashkent spreads as a broad, leafy grid across an irrigated plain, fed by canals drawn from the Chirchiq River. To the north-east the land rises into the first ridges of the Tian Shan, snowy in winter and spring, and the turquoise Charvak Reservoir fills a mountain valley. Caravans on the Silk Road knew this oasis as Chach.","w":"A large green grid on a flat plain; mountains rising to the north-east with a turquoise reservoir in a valley.","f":["The name is usually read as “stone city” in Turkic.","Tashkent's metro, opened in 1977, was the first in Central Asia.","It is the most populous city in Central Asia."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Tashkent” most likely mean?","a":"Stone city","x":["Green city","Sun city"]}},"Q1861":{"t":"City of angels on a looping brown river","s":"The Chao Phraya winds through Bangkok in lazy brown loops, and the whole city sits on a delta so flat it barely rises above the sea. Around it, rice fields and square fish ponds stretch to the Gulf of Thailand, about 30 km south. Thais rarely say Bangkok: they call it Krung Thep, the city of angels.","w":"A vast flat grey city on a looping brown river, with a grid of green fields and ponds between it and the gulf.","f":["Bangkok's full ceremonial name is one of the longest place names in the world.","It became the capital in 1782, when King Rama I moved the court across the river from Thonburi.","Its old network of canals earned Bangkok the nickname “Venice of the East”."],"q":{"q":"What do Thais usually call Bangkok?","a":"Krung Thep","x":["Ayutthaya","Sukhothai"]}},"Q8684":{"t":"A wide river and granite peaks inside one capital","s":"Seoul is a bowl of city among steep granite mountains, split by the broad Han River. Up to a kilometre wide here, the Han is crossed by more than 25 bridges that show as fine stitches across the water. Joseon kings made it their capital in 1394: mountains behind, a river in front — ideal by the rules of geomancy.","w":"A broad river curving west through a dense city ringed by rocky, wooded mountains; bridges cross it every kilometre or two.","f":["Joseon kings ringed their capital with a stone wall about 18 km long, begun in 1396.","Bukhansan, a national park of granite peaks, rises right on the city's northern edge.","In Korean, the word “Seoul” simply means “capital”."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Seoul?","a":"The Han","x":["The Nakdong","The Taedong"]}},"Q1461":{"t":"A great round bay, a river and a lake behind the city","s":"Manila lines the eastern shore of a broad, almost circular bay, famous for its sunsets. The Pasig River threads through the city, linking the bay to Laguna de Bay, the largest lake in the Philippines. For 250 years Spanish galleons sailed from here to Mexico, carrying Chinese silk and porcelain across the Pacific.","w":"A dense city on the east shore of a wide round bay; a river winds east to a huge lake.","f":["The Spanish founded walled Intramuros in 1571; the Manila galleons ran to Acapulco from 1565 to 1815.","The name comes from nilad, a mangrove shrub that once grew along the river.","Laguna de Bay is very shallow: on average it is only about three metres deep."],"q":{"q":"Where did the Manila galleons sail to across the Pacific?","a":"Acapulco","x":["Lima","San Francisco"]}},"Q16959":{"t":"The big city farthest from any sea","s":"No large city on Earth lies farther from the ocean than Ürümqi — the nearest coastline is about 2,500 km away. It fills a valley on the northern foot of the Tian Shan, with the snowy massif of Bogda Peak rising to the east. North of the city the land flattens into the dry Dzungarian Basin.","w":"A long city in a valley below snowy mountains; the glaciers of Bogda Peak to the east, dry basin to the north.","f":["Bogda Peak is 5,445 m high — and still more than 5 km below you.","The Gurbantünggüt, China's second-largest desert, fills the basin north of the city.","The name comes from Mongolian and means “beautiful pasture”."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how far is Ürümqi from the nearest sea?","a":"About 2,500 km","x":["About 900 km","About 5,000 km"]}},"Q30002":{"t":"A round city on a plain watered since 256 BC","s":"Chengdu sits on a remarkably flat, green plain on the western edge of the Sichuan Basin, its ring roads drawing concentric circles. Just west, mountains rise abruptly towards the Tibetan Plateau. The plain owes its fertility to Dujiangyan, an irrigation system built around 256 BC that still splits the Min River into a fan of canals.","w":"A round city of concentric ring roads on a green plain; a wall of mountains rising sharply to the west.","f":["An old saying claims Sichuan's dogs bark at the sun because they see it so rarely through the haze.","Dujiangyan, about 50 km north-west of the city, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Chengdu has had a giant panda breeding centre since 1987."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient irrigation system still waters the Chengdu Plain?","a":"Dujiangyan","x":["The Grand Canal","The Lingqu Canal"]}},"Q35765":{"t":"Japan's old kitchen, with an airport out in the bay","s":"Osaka fills the flat land at the head of Osaka Bay, where the Yodo River splits into channels on its way to the sea. The built-up area runs on without a break towards Kobe and Kyoto. Out in the bay, look for a neat rectangle joined to the shore by a single long bridge: Kansai Airport, built on a man-made island.","w":"A continuous city around the head of a bay; a rectangular airport island offshore, linked by one bridge.","f":["Osaka ruled the rice trade in the Edo period so completely that it was called “the nation's kitchen”.","Toyotomi Hideyoshi began building Osaka Castle in 1583.","In neighbouring Sakai, the keyhole-shaped burial mound of Emperor Nintoku is about 486 m long."],"q":{"q":"What was Osaka nicknamed in the Edo period?","a":"The nation's kitchen","x":["The nation's forge","The nation's garden"]}},"Q987":{"t":"A planned imperial capital inside a far older city","s":"Within Delhi's endless sprawl, look for a greener, looser patch south of the old city: that is New Delhi. It was planned from scratch after 1911, when Britain decided to move its Indian capital here from Calcutta. Architects Edwin Lutyens and Herbert Baker laid out broad avenues meeting at roundabouts, with a ceremonial axis running east from Raisina Hill.","w":"A greener, more open patch in the middle of the Delhi sprawl, a few kilometres west of the Yamuna.","f":["New Delhi was formally inaugurated as the capital in 1931, twenty years after the move was announced.","Its plan is built from triangles and hexagons, with avenues radiating out from roundabouts.","The presidential palace on Raisina Hill has about 340 rooms."],"q":{"q":"When was New Delhi formally inaugurated as the capital?","a":"1931","x":["1911","1947"]}},"Q1355":{"t":"A garden city of lakes on a cool high plateau","s":"Bengaluru sits about 900 m up on the Deccan Plateau, which keeps it noticeably cooler than India's coastal cities. From above, look for dozens of lakes glinting across the sprawl: most are old reservoirs, or tanks, made centuries ago by damming shallow valleys. A local chieftain, Kempe Gowda, founded the city with a mud fort in 1537.","w":"A sprawling city on rolling upland, freckled with lakes of all sizes; bare granite hills rise to the north.","f":["Its many software companies have earned it the nickname “India's Silicon Valley”.","Lalbagh, a botanical garden begun under Hyder Ali in the 18th century, helped earn it the name “Garden City”.","In 2014 the official name changed from Bangalore to Bengaluru, as it has always been called in Kannada."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how high above sea level is Bengaluru?","a":"About 900 m","x":["About 100 m","About 2,000 m"]}},"Q5838":{"t":"A high mountain valley where Babur chose to rest","s":"Kabul lies about 1,800 m up, in a bowl of bare brown mountains, with rocky ridges rising right through the city itself. The Kabul River winds east from here towards the Khyber Pass and, eventually, the Indus. In winter the snowy wall of the Hindu Kush shines to the north. Babur, founder of the Mughal Empire, loved this valley and chose to be buried here.","w":"A dense city in a high, dry basin with bare ridges poking through it; snowy mountains to the north.","f":["Babur's tomb stands in Bagh-e Babur, a terraced garden he laid out himself.","At about 1,800 m, Kabul stands higher than the summit of Ben Nevis, Britain's highest mountain.","The Kabul River flows east into Pakistan and joins the Indus at Attock."],"q":{"q":"Which Mughal emperor chose to be buried in Kabul?","a":"Babur","x":["Akbar","Aurangzeb"]}},"Q3141":{"t":"A city at the head of a vast, nearly closed bay","s":"Melbourne sits at the top of Port Phillip Bay, a huge sheet of water almost sealed off from the ocean. Its entrance, the Rip, is only about 3 km wide and has a fierce reputation among sailors. The Yarra River curls into the centre from the east, where the forested Dandenong Ranges rise. A gold rush in the 1850s made this one of the richest cities in the world.","w":"A sprawling city around the head of a huge, almost round bay; wooded hills to the east, flat plains to the west.","f":["From 1901 to 1927 Australia's federal parliament sat in Melbourne, until Canberra was ready.","The city was founded in 1835 on the banks of the Yarra.","Port Phillip Bay covers nearly 2,000 km², yet is nowhere deeper than about 24 m."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how wide is the entrance to Port Phillip Bay?","a":"About 3 km","x":["About 30 km","About 300 m"]}},"Q1867":{"t":"A city poured into a basin of green mountains","s":"Taipei fills a flat basin like water in a bowl, with forested mountains rising on every side. To the north, the dormant volcanoes of Yangmingshan steam quietly, feeding hot springs on the city's edge. Three rivers join in the basin and leave through a single gap to the north-west as the Tamsui, heading for the Taiwan Strait.","w":"A dense city filling a round basin, green mountains all around, a river snaking out north-west to the sea.","f":["Taipei 101, finished in 2004, was the world's tallest building until 2010.","Qixing Mountain in Yangmingshan, about 1,120 m high, is a dormant volcano within the city limits.","The hot springs of Beitou, on the north side of the city, are heated by the same volcanic group."],"q":{"q":"Which river drains the Taipei Basin to the sea?","a":"The Tamsui","x":["The Zhuoshui","The Gaoping"]}},"Q37100":{"t":"A city on a narrow neck between two seas","s":"Auckland is squeezed onto a narrow isthmus: the Waitematā Harbour opens east to the Pacific, the Manukau Harbour west to the Tasman Sea. The city sits on a field of about 50 small volcanoes, and many survive as green grassy cones among the suburbs. Just offshore, look for Rangitoto, a symmetrical island that rose from the sea only about 600 years ago.","w":"A city on a thin neck of land between two harbours, dotted with green cones; a round, symmetrical island just offshore.","f":["At its narrowest the isthmus is so thin that Māori once hauled canoes across it from one harbour to the other.","Auckland's Māori name, Tāmaki Makaurau, is often translated as “Tāmaki desired by many”.","Rangitoto is both the youngest and the largest volcano in the Auckland field."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how many volcanoes make up the Auckland volcanic field?","a":"About 50","x":["About 5","About 500"]}},"Q15174":{"t":"A boomtown along Hong Kong's northern edge","s":"Shenzhen runs in a long ribbon along the coast, facing the green hills of Hong Kong across a narrow river. Before 1980 this was a quiet border district of paddies and fishing villages; that year it became one of China's first Special Economic Zones and began growing at astonishing speed. At night its lights run almost unbroken from the Pearl River estuary to the eastern peninsulas.","w":"A long band of city along the coast just north of Hong Kong; a bay with a bridge on the west side.","f":["The Shenzhen River forms much of the boundary with Hong Kong.","A bridge about 5 km long crosses Shenzhen Bay to Hong Kong's New Territories.","Mangroves still line Shenzhen Bay at Futian, a protected refuge for migrating birds."],"q":{"q":"In which year did Shenzhen become a Special Economic Zone?","a":"1980","x":["1949","1997"]}},"Q198370":{"t":"A city of scattered towns, and football's ancient home","s":"Zibo is less one city than a loose cluster of towns, strung out where the hills of central Shandong fall away to the North China Plain. One of them, Linzi, was the capital of the ancient state of Qi and among the largest cities of its age. Another, Boshan, has been known for ceramics and coloured glass for centuries.","w":"Several separate urban patches spread along the edge of low hills, with the flat plain opening to the north.","f":["In 2004 FIFA recognised cuju, played in Linzi over 2,000 years ago, as the earliest known form of football.","Linzi remained the capital of Qi for centuries, until the Qin conquest in 221 BC."],"q":{"q":"Zibo's Linzi district is honoured as the birthplace of which sport?","a":"Football","x":["Table tennis","Badminton"]}},"Q1858":{"t":"A thousand-year capital on the rust-brown river","s":"Hanoi sits on the right bank of the Red River, whose silt-laden water really does look rusty from above. The city is freckled with lakes, the biggest being West Lake in the north. All around, the flat, green Red River Delta is laid out in paddies. The Lý dynasty made this their capital in 1010, calling it Thăng Long, “ascending dragon”.","w":"A city on the south-west bank of a broad brown river, with a large lake on its north side, amid flat green paddies.","f":["The name Hà Nội means roughly “inside the river”; the city received it in 1831.","West Lake, the largest lake in Hanoi, covers about 500 hectares."],"q":{"q":"What was Hanoi called when it became the capital in 1010?","a":"Thăng Long","x":["Phú Xuân","Gia Định"]}},"Q1854":{"t":"River loops, a vast delta and a maze of mangroves","s":"The Saigon River loops through the city in tight, shining bends before heading for the sea. South-east of the centre, the land gives way to Cần Giờ, a dark green maze of mangroves and tidal channels. To the south-west spreads the Mekong Delta, one of the great rice bowls of Asia. Many residents still call the city by its old name, Saigon.","w":"A dense city on a looping river; dark mangrove channels to the south-east, flat green delta to the south-west.","f":["The Cần Giờ mangrove forest is a UNESCO biosphere reserve.","Before Vietnamese settlers arrived in the 17th century, a Khmer port called Prey Nokor stood here."],"q":{"q":"Which mangrove forest lies between Ho Chi Minh City and the sea?","a":"Cần Giờ","x":["The Sundarbans","Bhitarkanika"]}},"Q11739":{"t":"A Mughal city of gardens on the Punjab plain","s":"Lahore spreads across the flat Punjab plain, with the Ravi River curving past its north-western edge. Irrigation canals draw straight lines through the fields around it. For centuries this was one of the great cities of the Mughal Empire, and Akbar kept his capital here for more than a decade. The old walled city is still a tight knot of lanes near the river.","w":"A large flat city with a river on its north-west side and straight canals running through green fields.","f":["Akbar held his court at Lahore from 1585 to 1598.","Lahore Fort and the Shalimar Gardens are listed together as one UNESCO World Heritage site.","The walled city was once entered through about a dozen gates."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past Lahore?","a":"The Ravi","x":["The Jhelum","The Kabul"]}},"Q8660":{"t":"A megacity on a dry coast beside the Indus Delta","s":"Karachi sprawls over a dry, sand-coloured coast, with bare hills behind it and the Arabian Sea in front. Its harbour is sheltered by a low spit of land, and to the south-east the shore frays into mangrove creeks where the Indus Delta begins. In the 18th century this was a small fishing settlement; today it is Pakistan's largest city.","w":"A vast beige city on a straight dry coast; a sheltered harbour and mangrove creeks to the south-east, bare hills inland.","f":["Its old name was Kolachi, after a fishing village on this shore.","Karachi was Pakistan's capital from 1947 until 1959.","The Indus Delta mangroves to the south-east are among the largest mangrove forests in a dry climate."],"q":{"q":"What was Karachi's name as an 18th-century fishing village?","a":"Kolachi","x":["Thatta","Gwadar"]}},"Q18808":{"t":"A river-island city once called the capital of willows","s":"Pyongyang lies on both banks of the Taedong River, which flows here in wide, calm reaches around long, low islands. Beyond the city, flat farmland and low hills stretch towards the Yellow Sea, about 50 km to the south-west. The name means “flat land”, and in 427 the Goguryeo kingdom moved its capital here.","w":"A city on both banks of a broad river with long islands; the river winds south-west across flat farmland to the sea.","f":["An old name for the city is Ryugyong, “capital of willows”, after the trees along its riverbanks.","Goguryeo ruled from Pyongyang for more than two centuries, from 427 until 668."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Pyongyang?","a":"The Taedong","x":["The Han","The Yalu"]}},"Q5972":{"t":"Sumatra's biggest city, built on tobacco","s":"Medan spreads over the flat coastal plain of north-eastern Sumatra, a few kilometres inland from the Strait of Malacca. Around it, plantations lay out the land in vast, tidy grids. In the 19th century the leaf grown here, Deli tobacco, was prized worldwide as cigar wrapper, and a small village grew rich. To the south-west, volcanic cones rise towards the great caldera lake of Toba.","w":"A grey sprawl on a flat plain near the coast, ringed by gridded plantations; volcanic peaks on the south-west horizon.","f":["Medan is the largest city on Sumatra.","Lake Toba, south of the city, fills the caldera of a supervolcano that erupted about 74,000 years ago.","Deli tobacco, named after the local sultanate, was grown here for cigar wrappers from the 1860s."],"q":{"q":"Which crop made Medan rich in the 19th century?","a":"Tobacco","x":["Nutmeg","Cloves"]}},"Q1865":{"t":"A muddy river junction that tin turned into a capital","s":"Kuala Lumpur fills the Klang Valley, with the forested ridges of the Titiwangsa Mountains rising to the east. The city began in the 1850s as a rough camp of tin miners, and many of the lakes glinting around it are old flooded mine pits. To the south, look for Putrajaya, a planned government town wrapped around a large artificial lake.","w":"A dense city in a green valley, forested mountains to the east, scattered lakes around it and a planned town to the south.","f":["Kuala Lumpur means “muddy confluence”: it grew where the Gombak River meets the Klang.","The Petronas Twin Towers were the world's tallest buildings from 1998 to 2004.","Batu Caves, in a limestone hill on the city's northern edge, have been a Hindu shrine since the 1890s."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Kuala Lumpur” mean?","a":"Muddy confluence","x":["River of tin","Town of hills"]}},"Q183584":{"t":"A long, thin city squeezed along the Yellow River","s":"Lanzhou is a city with nowhere to spread: it runs for tens of kilometres along a narrow valley of the Yellow River, hemmed in by bare, ochre loess hills. From above it looks like a grey ribbon laid in a crease of the land. For Silk Road caravans, this was the key crossing of the Yellow River on the way west into the Hexi Corridor.","w":"A narrow grey ribbon of city following a winding river between bare yellow-brown hills.","f":["Zhongshan Bridge, finished in 1909, was the first permanent bridge across the Yellow River.","Before bridges, travellers crossed the river here on rafts buoyed by inflated sheepskins."],"q":{"q":"Which river runs through the middle of Lanzhou?","a":"The Yellow River","x":["The Yangtze","The Wei River"]}},"Q179608":{"t":"A green subtropical city in the loops of the Yong","s":"Nanning coils around the wide meanders of the Yong River, deep in China's subtropical south. The countryside around it is a patchwork of green hills and fields, many of them planted with sugarcane. The border with Vietnam is less than 200 km to the south-west, and since 2004 the city has hosted a yearly trade fair with South-East Asia.","w":"A city spread around broad river loops, with green hills and fields all around.","f":["Guangxi, whose capital is Nanning, grows more than half of China's sugarcane.","Its year-round greenery has earned Nanning the nickname “Green City”.","The Yong River flows on into the Xi River and eventually reaches the Pearl River Delta."],"q":{"q":"Which crop makes Guangxi China's biggest producer?","a":"Sugarcane","x":["Tea","Cotton"]}},"Q192271":{"t":"A high city among countless cone-shaped karst hills","s":"Guiyang sits about 1,100 m up on the Yunnan–Guizhou Plateau, and the land around it is crumpled into limestone cones and hollows. Districts squeeze in between the hills, so from above the city looks broken into pieces. An old saying claims Guizhou never has three sunny days in a row or three li of flat ground — look down and you'll see why.","w":"Patches of city scattered between green, cone-shaped hills on a high, crumpled plateau, often under cloud.","f":["Thanks to the altitude, Guiyang's summers are mild for southern China.","The name means “south of Mount Gui”: in Chinese, “yang” marks the sunny side of a hill."],"q":{"q":"Guiyang is the capital of which province?","a":"Guizhou","x":["Guangxi","Yunnan"]}},"Q11725":{"t":"A mountain city on the point where two rivers meet","s":"Chongqing's old centre is a steep, crowded peninsula at the meeting of two rivers: the Jialing from the north and the Yangtze. At the tip, their waters often run side by side in two colours before mixing. Around the city, long parallel ridges run north-east like the folds of a rumpled cloth. Hills, fog and hot summers have given it its nicknames: Mountain City and Fog City.","w":"A dense peninsula between two converging rivers, framed by long parallel ridges running north-east to south-west.","f":["The reservoir behind the Three Gorges Dam stretches roughly 600 km upstream, all the way to Chongqing.","Chongqing is counted among China's “furnaces” for its hot, humid summers.","The whole municipality of Chongqing is roughly the size of Austria."],"q":{"q":"Which river joins the Yangtze in central Chongqing?","a":"The Jialing","x":["The Min","The Han"]}},"Q68481":{"t":"Banyan City, in a green basin on the Min River","s":"Fuzhou lies in a basin ringed by green mountains, on the lower Min River just before it widens towards the sea. A large island in the river, Nantai, is itself covered in city. The streets are shaded by banyan trees, first planted here about a thousand years ago, and Fuzhou is still nicknamed Banyan City.","w":"A city in a mountain-ringed basin on a wide river, with a large built-up island in the middle of the river.","f":["The FAO lists Fuzhou's jasmine and tea culture as a Globally Important Agricultural Heritage System.","Hot springs rise beneath the city centre, and bathhouses have used them for centuries.","The Mawei shipyard, founded in 1866, was one of China's first modern shipyards."],"q":{"q":"Fuzhou's nickname comes from which tree?","a":"The banyan","x":["The camphor tree","The willow"]}},"Q16572":{"t":"Old Canton, at the head of the Pearl River Delta","s":"Guangzhou sits where the Pearl River breaks into the maze of channels of its delta, and from above it merges seamlessly with the cities around it. The green hump of Baiyun Mountain rises on the northern side. From 1757 to 1842 this was the one Chinese port where Western merchants were allowed to trade, and they knew it as Canton.","w":"A huge city on a branching river at the top of the delta, with a green mountain on its northern edge.","f":["A legend of five rams bringing rice to the people gave Guangzhou its nickname, the City of Rams.","The Canton Fair has been held here twice a year since 1957."],"q":{"q":"What did European traders call Guangzhou?","a":"Canton","x":["Amoy","Foochow"]}},"Q59218":{"t":"A patchwork of factory towns on the Pearl River","s":"Dongguan has no single centre: it is a patchwork of about thirty towns, factory estates and fish ponds on the east side of the Pearl River estuary, filling the gap between Guangzhou and Shenzhen. Countless toys, shoes and electronic gadgets have come out of its workshops. At its western edge, the Humen Bridge leaps across the river mouth.","w":"A sprawl of towns and ponds on the east shore of a wide estuary, with a long suspension bridge on its western edge.","f":["At Humen in 1839, Commissioner Lin Zexu had more than a thousand tonnes of seized opium destroyed.","The Humen Bridge, opened in 1997, has a main span of 888 m."],"q":{"q":"What did Commissioner Lin Zexu destroy at Humen in 1839?","a":"Opium","x":["Silver","Tea"]}},"Q10389":{"t":"A cool highland basin ringed by volcanoes","s":"Bandung fills a basin about 700 m up in the highlands of West Java, and the flat floor it sits on was once the bed of a great prehistoric lake. Volcanoes stand all around; the long, flat-topped one to the north is Tangkuban Perahu, the “upturned boat”. Its cool climate and elegant avenues once earned the city the nickname “Paris of Java”.","w":"A city spread across a flat basin floor, volcanoes on every side; a long, flat-topped mountain to the north.","f":["In 1955 Bandung hosted the Asian–African Conference, attended by 29 countries.","Tea plantations cover many of the volcanic slopes around the city.","Legend says Tangkuban Perahu is a boat kicked over in anger by the hero Sangkuriang."],"q":{"q":"What does the volcano name Tangkuban Perahu mean?","a":"Upturned boat","x":["Sleeping giant","Burning mountain"]}},"Q11462":{"t":"A shark, a crocodile and a bridge to Madura","s":"Surabaya lies on the flat north-east coast of Java, facing the narrow strait that separates it from the island of Madura. Look for the thin line of the Suramadu Bridge, about 5 km long, stitching the two islands together. Along the shore, square fish ponds glint in rows. The city's emblem shows a shark and a crocodile — sura and baya — locked in a fight.","w":"A large coastal city facing a narrow strait, with a long bridge across to an island and rows of fish ponds along the shore.","f":["When the Suramadu Bridge opened in 2009, it was the longest bridge in Indonesia.","Tanjung Perak, Surabaya's harbour, is one of Indonesia's busiest ports."],"q":{"q":"Surabaya's emblem shows a shark fighting which animal?","a":"A crocodile","x":["A tiger","An eagle"]}},"Q5826":{"t":"Where the Silk Road began, below the Qinling wall","s":"Xi'an lies on the flat Wei River plain, with the Qinling Mountains rising like a dark wall to the south. Its streets still follow a strict north–south grid around a rectangular old core. As Chang'an, this was the capital of the Tang dynasty, the eastern starting point of the Silk Road and one of the largest cities on Earth.","w":"A big gridded city on a flat plain; a steep mountain wall to the south, a river to the north.","f":["Xi'an's Ming-era city wall, about 14 km around, is one of the most complete old city walls in China.","The Terracotta Army was found in 1974 by farmers digging a well, about 35 km to the east.","The Qinling range is China's traditional dividing line between north and south."],"q":{"q":"What was Xi'an called as the Tang dynasty capital?","a":"Chang'an","x":["Luoyang","Kaifeng"]}},"Q72778":{"t":"Coal country's capital along the Fen River","s":"Taiyuan stretches north to south along the Fen River, in a long basin pinched between two mountain ranges on the Loess Plateau. The hills around it are dry and furrowed, yellow-brown for much of the year. Beneath Shanxi province lies some of China's richest coal, and the city grew as its industrial heart.","w":"A long city following a river down a narrow basin, bare furrowed hills on both sides.","f":["The first fortified town here, Jinyang, was built around 497 BC.","Shanxi is famous for its dark aged vinegar, made around Taiyuan for centuries."],"q":{"q":"Which river runs through Taiyuan?","a":"The Fen","x":["The Wei","The Luo"]}},"Q11746":{"t":"Three cities split by two rivers and a hundred lakes","s":"Wuhan is really three old cities — Wuchang, Hankou and Hanyang — divided by the meeting of the Yangtze and its tributary, the Han. From above, the rivers form a giant Y, and lakes glitter everywhere between the districts. The largest, East Lake, spreads its many arms over more than 30 km² on the eastern side.","w":"Two big rivers meeting in a Y through a vast city, with many lakes; a large branching lake to the east.","f":["The first bridge ever built across the Yangtze opened here in 1957.","In the 19th century, Russian merchants ran brick-tea factories in Hankou.","Its place at the heart of China's river network earned Wuhan the nickname “thoroughfare of nine provinces”."],"q":{"q":"Which river joins the Yangtze at Wuhan?","a":"The Han","x":["The Jialing","The Xiang"]}},"Q174091":{"t":"A long orange-grove island in the Xiang River","s":"Changsha lines both banks of the Xiang River, and in the middle of the river lies a remarkably long, thin island: Orange Isle, once covered in citrus groves. On the west bank rises the wooded hill of Yuelu. At its foot stands one of China's oldest academies, founded in 976 and still part of a university today.","w":"A city on both banks of a wide river with a long, narrow island in the middle; a wooded hill on the west bank.","f":["Orange Isle is about 5 km long, yet very narrow.","The Han-dynasty tombs of Mawangdui, found in Changsha in 1972, held a remarkably well-preserved noblewoman."],"q":{"q":"What is the long island in the Xiang River at Changsha called?","a":"Orange Isle","x":["Peach Isle","Lotus Isle"]}},"Q182852":{"t":"Eternal spring on the shore of a mountain lake","s":"Kunming sits almost 1,900 m up on the Yunnan plateau, at the northern end of Dian Lake, a long sheet of water whose western shore is lined by steep hills. Altitude and latitude together give it mild weather all year, and it is known as the City of Eternal Spring. The fields around it show the deep red soil typical of the plateau.","w":"A city at the north end of a long lake with steep hills along its western shore; red-soiled fields around.","f":["Dian Lake covers about 300 km², making it the largest lake in Yunnan.","Since the 1980s, black-headed gulls from Siberia have come to Kunming every winter.","The Stone Forest, a UNESCO-listed maze of limestone pinnacles, lies less than 100 km to the south-east."],"q":{"q":"What is Kunming's nickname?","a":"City of Eternal Spring","x":["City of Eternal Autumn","City of Winds"]}},"Q30340":{"t":"A rail crossroads beside a river that runs above the plain","s":"Zhengzhou spreads across the flat North China Plain just south of the Yellow River, which here runs wide, braided and sandy between high levees. Its bed has silted up so much that the river flows above the surrounding fields. People have lived here a long time: parts of the rammed-earth walls of a Shang dynasty city, some 3,500 years old, still stand in the centre.","w":"A big flat grid of a city with a wide, sandy, braided river running east–west just to the north.","f":["The Shaolin Temple, cradle of kung fu legend, lies in the Song Mountains less than 100 km to the west.","Zhengzhou grew up where China's main north–south and east–west railways cross."],"q":{"q":"Zhengzhou is the capital of which province?","a":"Henan","x":["Hebei","Hubei"]}},"Q11720":{"t":"The Manchu capital before Beijing, on a wintry plain","s":"Shenyang spreads across the flat plain of north-east China, with the Hun River curving along its southern side; in winter the river freezes and the fields turn white. In 1625 the Manchu leader Nurhaci made it his capital, and his successors ruled from here until they took Beijing in 1644. The Manchus called it Mukden.","w":"A large flat city on a wide plain, a river along its southern edge; snow-covered and grey in winter.","f":["The Mukden Palace, built from 1625, is a smaller cousin of Beijing's Forbidden City and a UNESCO site.","The name means “north of the Shen River”, an old name for the Hun."],"q":{"q":"What was Shenyang's Manchu name?","a":"Mukden","x":["Harbin","Kirin"]}},"Q170247":{"t":"City of Springs, between Mount Tai and the Yellow River","s":"Jinan is squeezed between low hills to the south and the Yellow River to the north. Water seeping through the limestone hills bubbles up in the city centre from dozens of springs, which feed Daming Lake and earned Jinan the name City of Springs. On clear days Mount Tai, foremost of China's five sacred mountains, rises about 60 km to the south.","w":"A city between green hills to the south and a wide river to the north, with a lake in its centre.","f":["Tradition counts 72 famous springs in Jinan; the best known is Baotu Spring.","Jinan means “south of the Ji”; since 1855 the Yellow River has flowed in the Ji River's old bed."],"q":{"q":"When did the Yellow River shift into its present course past Jinan?","a":"1855","x":["1755","1911"]}},"Q11736":{"t":"Beijing's gateway to the sea, edged with salt pans","s":"Tianjin lies on the flat coastal plain about 120 km south-east of Beijing, where the Hai River winds towards Bohai Bay. Along the shore, look for a geometric pattern of salt evaporation pans and harbour basins in varying shades. The name means “heavenly ford”, given in 1404 by the Yongle Emperor, who had crossed the river here.","w":"A large city on a flat plain, a winding river to the sea, and a coast of rectangular salt pans and harbour basins.","f":["After 1860 several foreign powers held concessions in Tianjin, and their European-style streets survive.","The Changlu salt works along this coast are among the oldest and largest in China."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Tianjin” mean?","a":"Heavenly ford","x":["Heavenly gate","Gate to the sea"]}},"Q171943":{"t":"Gateway to a lake that grows and shrinks with the seasons","s":"Nanchang straddles the Gan River just before it spreads out into the marshy plain of Poyang Lake, China's largest freshwater lake. In summer the lake swells into an inland sea; in winter it can shrink to a web of channels and grassland. Those winter wetlands host the great majority of the world's Siberian cranes.","w":"A city on both banks of a wide river; to the north-east, the huge, shifting expanse of Poyang Lake.","f":["Poyang Lake can cover several thousand km² in the wet season and shrink several times over in the dry.","The Tengwang Pavilion on the Gan River was first built in 653."],"q":{"q":"Which lake, China's largest freshwater lake, lies near Nanchang?","a":"Poyang Lake","x":["Dongting Lake","Lake Tai"]}},"Q16666":{"t":"The southern capital, between the Yangtze and green hills","s":"Nanjing sits on the south bank of the Yangtze, where the river swings north-east on its last stretch to the sea. The forested dome of Purple Mountain rises on the eastern edge, and Xuanwu Lake glints just inside the old walls. The first Ming emperor made this his capital in 1368, and its name still means “southern capital”.","w":"A large city on the south bank of the broad Yangtze, a green mountain to the east and a lake in its northern part.","f":["Nanjing's Ming city wall, about 35 km long, was one of the longest city walls ever built, and much of it survives.","Zhu Yuanzhang, founder of the Ming dynasty, is buried in a tomb on Purple Mountain."],"q":{"q":"Which dynasty built Nanjing's great city wall?","a":"The Ming","x":["The Tang","The Song"]}},"Q4970":{"t":"A poets' lake and a river with a racing tidal wave","s":"Hangzhou wraps around West Lake, a calm sheet of water held on three sides by green hills and praised by Chinese poets for more than a thousand years. South of the city the wide Qiantang River bends towards funnel-shaped Hangzhou Bay. Especially in autumn, a tidal bore charges up the river as a wall of water, drawing crowds to the banks.","w":"A city on the east shore of a lake with hills on three sides; a wide river curving to a funnel-shaped bay.","f":["Hangzhou was the capital of the Southern Song dynasty, and then one of the largest cities in the world.","The Grand Canal, running north to Beijing, begins here.","Longjing, or Dragon Well, one of China's most famous green teas, grows in the hills west of the lake."],"q":{"q":"Which river at Hangzhou is famous for its tidal bore?","a":"The Qiantang","x":["The Huangpu","The Min"]}},"Q34664":{"t":"A city on the fingers of a delta, by an island sea","s":"Hiroshima spreads over the delta of the Ōta River, whose channels fan out towards the sea like the fingers of a hand, splitting the city into long islands. Beyond lies the calm Seto Inland Sea, scattered with green islands. The name means “broad island”, and the city was founded in 1589, when the warlord Mōri Terumoto built his castle on the delta.","w":"A city on a delta of parallel river channels at the head of a bay, with wooded islands scattered offshore.","f":["Hiroshima Prefecture produces more than half of Japan's farmed oysters.","Itsukushima Shrine, on the island of Miyajima about 20 km south-west, is famous for its torii gate standing in the sea."],"q":{"q":"On the delta of which river is Hiroshima built?","a":"The Ōta","x":["The Yodo","The Shinano"]}},"Q92161":{"t":"China's car city on a plain of black earth","s":"Changchun lies in the middle of the Songliao Plain, a vast, flat expanse of fertile black soil that turns white under snow for months each winter. The city is laid out in a neat grid of wide avenues. In 1953 China's first automobile factory was founded here, and Changchun is still called the cradle of the country's car industry. Its name, ironically for so cold a place, means “long spring”.","w":"A gridded city on an endless flat plain of dark fields; a forested reservoir on its south-eastern edge.","f":["The first trucks built in the People's Republic rolled off the line here in 1956.","Jingyuetan, on the south-eastern edge, is a reservoir ringed by a vast planted forest."],"q":{"q":"What is Changchun best known for making?","a":"Cars","x":["Silk","Porcelain"]}},"Q569907":{"t":"Deer City on the Yellow River's great northern bend","s":"Baotou stretches along the plain between the bare Yin Mountains and the Yellow River, near the top of the river's great loop through Inner Mongolia. Across the water to the south, the dunes of the Kubuqi Desert begin. The city grew into a steel centre in the 1950s, and its Mongolian name is usually translated as “place with deer” — hence its nickname, Deer City.","w":"An industrial city strung along a plain between bare mountains to the north and a wide river to the south; dunes beyond.","f":["Bayan Obo, more than 100 km to the north, is the world's largest known deposit of rare-earth metals.","The Yellow River's loop around the Ordos Plateau is so big that the river flows north, then east, then south."],"q":{"q":"Baotou's Mongolian name refers to which animal?","a":"Deer","x":["Horses","Wolves"]}},"Q42956":{"t":"A railway town on a river that freezes into a festival","s":"Harbin spreads along the south bank of the Songhua River on the flat plains of the north-east. In winter the river freezes solid into a white road, and blocks cut from its ice are carved into the palaces of the famous Ice and Snow Festival. The city grew from 1898 around a railway built by Russia, and its onion-domed churches still recall those years.","w":"A large city on the south bank of a wide river with an island park opposite; the river white and frozen in winter.","f":["The average January temperature here is well below −15 °C.","Harbin's Ice and Snow Festival has been held every winter since 1985."],"q":{"q":"Harbin grew up around a railway built by which country?","a":"Russia","x":["Japan","Britain"]}},"Q37951":{"t":"A snowy grid city at the edge of Hokkaido's mountains","s":"Sapporo was laid out from 1869 on a neat grid, and you can still see its straight lines across the Ishikari Plain, with forested mountains rising right behind it to the west. Few large cities anywhere get as much snow: roughly five metres falls in a typical winter. The name comes from the Ainu language and is often read as “dry, great river”.","w":"A gridded city on a plain at the foot of forested mountains; in winter, everything white.","f":["In 1972 Sapporo hosted the first Winter Olympics held in Asia.","The Sapporo Snow Festival began in 1950 with six snow statues built by local students."],"q":{"q":"When did Sapporo host Asia's first Winter Olympics?","a":"1972","x":["1964","1998"]}},"Q1361":{"t":"A city of pearls and diamonds among granite boulders","s":"Hyderabad sits on the rocky Deccan Plateau, where giant granite boulders lie scattered as if dropped by hand. Look for a big lake right in the middle of the city: Hussain Sagar, built in the 16th century, which separates Hyderabad from its twin, Secunderabad. The Qutb Shahi sultans founded the city in 1591, and nearby Golconda was famed for its diamond trade.","w":"A vast city on a rocky plateau dotted with lakes, a large lake in the centre and a river through the south.","f":["The Koh-i-Noor diamond is believed to have come from mines in the Golconda region.","Its centuries-old pearl trade earned Hyderabad the nickname City of Pearls.","The Charminar, the city's four-minareted emblem, was built in 1591, the year Hyderabad was founded."],"q":{"q":"Which famous diamond is thought to come from the Golconda mines?","a":"The Koh-i-Noor","x":["The Cullinan","The Excelsior"]}},"Q1538":{"t":"The Peshwas' city in the lee of the Western Ghats","s":"Pune sits about 560 m up on the Deccan Plateau, where the Mula and Mutha rivers meet. Just to the west rise the steep, green Western Ghats, with a chain of reservoirs filling their valleys. In the 18th century this was the seat of the Peshwas, chief ministers who ran the Maratha Empire. Today it has so many colleges that it is called the Oxford of the East.","w":"A large city where two rivers meet on a plateau; green, steep hills and reservoirs to the west.","f":["Shaniwar Wada, the Peshwas' fortified palace, was completed in 1732.","The hill fort of Sinhagad stands about 30 km south-west, on a ridge of the Ghats."],"q":{"q":"Pune was the seat of which 18th-century rulers?","a":"The Peshwas","x":["The Mughals","The Nizams"]}},"Q1513":{"t":"Orange City, at the very heart of India","s":"Nagpur lies close to the geographic centre of India, and a Zero Mile Stone set up by British surveyors still marks the spot. From above it's a sprawling city on a gently rolling plateau, with several lakes on its western side. Orange orchards surround it, giving it the name Orange City, and the forests further out are home to tigers.","w":"A large city on a flat plateau with a few lakes on its western edge, surrounded by farmland and orchards.","f":["Nagpur's oranges are famous across India and carry a geographical indication tag.","Tiger reserves, including Pench and Tadoba, lie within a few hours' drive."],"q":{"q":"Nagpur's nickname comes from which fruit?","a":"Oranges","x":["Mangoes","Bananas"]}},"Q66485":{"t":"The Pink City, laid out on a grid in 1727","s":"Jaipur spreads across a dry plain in Rajasthan, with the rugged ridges of the Aravalli hills rising to the north and east, where old forts guard the passes. Maharaja Sawai Jai Singh II founded it in 1727 on a strict grid, unusual for its time. Its old town is painted terracotta pink, first done to welcome the Prince of Wales in 1876.","w":"A large city on a dry plain, rocky ridges with forts to the north and east, a lake at their foot.","f":["Jai Singh II was also an astronomer; his Jantar Mantar observatory is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","The walled city of Jaipur was added to the UNESCO World Heritage list in 2019."],"q":{"q":"For whose visit was Jaipur painted pink in 1876?","a":"The Prince of Wales","x":["Queen Victoria","The Viceroy of India"]}},"Q66568":{"t":"An old mill town on the banks of the Ganges","s":"Kanpur stretches along the south-west bank of the Ganges, which curves past its northern edge in wide, sandy channels. The land all around is the flat, fertile Gangetic plain, laid out in small fields. In the 19th century cotton mills and tanneries turned the town into an industrial hub nicknamed the Manchester of the East. Leather is still one of its big trades.","w":"A large flat city on the south bank of a broad river with sandbars, surrounded by a patchwork of small fields.","f":["Under British rule the city was known as Cawnpore.","Kanpur is one of India's biggest centres of leather tanning."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past Kanpur?","a":"The Ganges","x":["The Yamuna","The Gomti"]}},"Q80484":{"t":"Ancient Pataliputra, stretched along the wide Ganges","s":"Patna is long and thin: it runs for many kilometres along the south bank of the Ganges, which here spreads several kilometres wide in flood and breaks into channels around sandbars in the dry season. Look for the long bridge striking north across it. More than 2,000 years ago, as Pataliputra, this was the capital of the Maurya Empire and one of the greatest cities of the ancient world.","w":"A long, narrow city along the south bank of a very wide, braided river, with a long bridge crossing it.","f":["Around 300 BC the Greek envoy Megasthenes described Pataliputra's huge timber walls.","The Mahatma Gandhi Setu, opened in 1982, carries a road about 5.7 km across the Ganges."],"q":{"q":"What was Patna called as the Maurya capital?","a":"Pataliputra","x":["Taxila","Ujjain"]}},"Q1352":{"t":"Old Madras, facing the Bay of Bengal across a long beach","s":"Chennai lines a long, straight stretch of the Coromandel Coast, and its signature is Marina Beach: a pale strip of sand running for kilometres beside the Bay of Bengal. Two sluggish rivers, the Cooum and the Adyar, cut through the city to the sea. The English East India Company built Fort St George here in 1644, and the city of Madras grew up around it.","w":"A flat city along a long, straight sandy coast; harbour breakwaters in the north, two rivers entering the sea.","f":["Marina Beach is one of the longest urban beaches in the world.","The city's official name changed from Madras to Chennai in 1996.","Pulicat Lake, a large lagoon about 50 km north, draws flocks of flamingos in winter."],"q":{"q":"Which fort did the English build at Chennai in 1644?","a":"Fort St George","x":["Fort William","Fort St David"]}},"Q1070":{"t":"A sultan's city of kites on the Sabarmati","s":"Ahmedabad spreads across the flat plains of Gujarat on both banks of the Sabarmati, whose course through the city runs straight between long concrete embankments. Sultan Ahmed Shah founded it in 1411, and in 2017 it became the first Indian city on UNESCO's World Heritage list. Each January, at the Uttarayan festival, the sky above it fills with thousands of kites.","w":"A large flat city split by a river running straight between embankments; a planned capital with green avenues to the north.","f":["Gandhi lived at his Sabarmati Ashram on the riverbank from 1917 to 1930.","Gandhinagar, the planned capital of Gujarat, lies about 25 km to the north."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Ahmedabad?","a":"The Sabarmati","x":["The Narmada","The Tapi"]}},"Q4629":{"t":"Where most of the world's diamonds are cut","s":"Surat sits on a broad bend of the Tapi River, a few kilometres before it reaches the mudflats of the Arabian Sea coast. In Mughal times it was India's busiest port, and pilgrims sailed from here to Mecca. In the 1610s the English East India Company set up one of its earliest Indian trading posts here. Today the city's workshops cut and polish the great majority of the world's diamonds.","w":"A dense city on a wide river bend near a flat, muddy coast, with new districts spreading towards the sea.","f":["Around nine out of ten of the world's diamonds are cut and polished in Surat.","Surat is also one of India's largest centres of synthetic textiles."],"q":{"q":"Surat is famous for cutting and polishing what?","a":"Diamonds","x":["Emeralds","Pearls"]}},"Q1354":{"t":"A river-ringed megacity, once the capital of muslin","s":"Dhaka is almost an island: the Buriganga, Turag, Balu and Shitalakshya rivers wrap around it, and in the monsoon the fields beyond turn into sheets of water. Inside that ring the city is one of the most densely packed on Earth. In 1610 the Mughals made it the capital of Bengal, and its weavers produced muslin so fine it was likened to woven air.","w":"A very dense city on slightly higher ground ringed by rivers; in the monsoon, flooded fields all around.","f":["Dhaka is often called the rickshaw capital of the world, with hundreds of thousands of cycle rickshaws.","Under the Mughals the city was renamed Jahangirnagar, after Emperor Jahangir.","It has so many mosques that it is nicknamed the City of Mosques."],"q":{"q":"Mughal-era Dhaka was famed for which fine fabric?","a":"Muslin","x":["Silk brocade","Cashmere"]}},"Q376749":{"t":"Bangladesh's great port, where the hills meet the sea","s":"Chattogram sits at the mouth of the Karnaphuli River, where the flat delta of Bangladesh finally gives way to green hills rolling east towards Myanmar. It is the country's main seaport. North of the city, look along the shore for a row of large ships deliberately run aground on the mudflats: one of the world's biggest shipbreaking coasts. Portuguese traders called this Porto Grande.","w":"A port city at a river mouth, green hills to the east; a line of beached ships on the coast to the north.","f":["In the yards of Sitakunda, north of the city, ocean-going ships are taken apart on the beach, much of it by hand.","In 2018 the official English spelling changed from Chittagong to Chattogram, closer to the Bengali."],"q":{"q":"What did Portuguese traders call Chattogram?","a":"Porto Grande","x":["Porto Pequeno","Porto Novo"]}},"Q3183":{"t":"A lonely capital on a lake-like river of black swans","s":"Perth is one of the most isolated big cities on Earth: Adelaide, the nearest city of similar size, is over 2,000 km away. The Swan River widens here into broad blue basins that look more like lakes, with the city on their banks. To the east the land rises abruptly at the long, straight line of the Darling Scarp; to the west lie white beaches and Rottnest Island.","w":"A city around wide, lake-like river basins; a straight escarpment to the east, white beaches and an island to the west.","f":["Dutch explorer Willem de Vlamingh named the Swan River in 1697, after its black swans.","Rottnest Island, off the coast, is home to the quokka, a small marsupial with a smiling face.","The Swan River Colony, which grew into Perth, was founded in 1829."],"q":{"q":"Who named the Swan River after its black swans in 1697?","a":"Willem de Vlamingh","x":["Abel Tasman","Dirk Hartog"]}},"Q37995":{"t":"A golden-pagoda city between delta rivers","s":"Yangon lies on a tongue of land where the Yangon and Bago rivers meet, some 30 km from the Andaman Sea. West of it spreads the vast green Irrawaddy Delta, laced with channels. Two large lakes, Inya and Kandawgyi, glint among the northern districts. Near the second, on a low hill, stands the gilded Shwedagon Pagoda, the most sacred Buddhist site in Myanmar.","w":"A city on a point of land between converging rivers, with lakes in its northern half; a green delta stretching west.","f":["King Alaungpaya named the town Yangon, “end of strife”, in 1755.","Under British rule the city was known as Rangoon.","The Shwedagon's stupa is about 100 m tall and covered in gold plates."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Yangon” mean?","a":"End of strife","x":["City of gold","River mouth"]}},"Q572089":{"t":"Karst peaks, a dammed red river and long lives","s":"Hechi spreads across a rugged corner of north-western Guangxi, where forested limestone peaks crowd so tightly that towns and fields squeeze into the narrow valleys between them. The Hongshui, or Red Water River, winds through deep gorges here and is stepped with big dams. This is a homeland of several minority peoples, among them the Zhuang, Yao, Mulao and Maonan.","w":"A sea of steep, forested limestone peaks with small towns and fields tucked into the valleys; a dammed river in gorges.","f":["Bama county, in Hechi, is famous for its unusually large number of people who live past 100.","Hechi has China's only autonomous counties for the Mulao and the Maonan peoples.","The Longtan Dam on the Hongshui River, in Hechi, feeds one of China's largest hydropower stations."],"q":{"q":"Bama county in Hechi is famous for its many…","a":"Centenarians","x":["Twins","Tea houses"]}},"Q68814":{"t":"Orchards, a delta and round earthen fortresses","s":"Zhangzhou lies on the fertile plain of the Jiulong, or Nine Dragon River, which widens eastwards into an estuary with Xiamen at its mouth. The land around is a green quilt of orchards, known for lychees, pomelos and narcissus bulbs. In 1567 the Ming lifted their ban on overseas trade at Yuegang, a harbour on this river, and Zhangzhou's goods flowed to Manila and beyond.","w":"A city on a green delta plain; the river widens east into an estuary towards Xiamen, hills to the west.","f":["In the hills west of the city stand Fujian tulou, giant round earthen houses listed by UNESCO.","Zhangzhou's narcissus bulbs are sent all over China for the Lunar New Year."],"q":{"q":"What are the giant round earthen houses in Zhangzhou's hills called?","a":"Tulou","x":["Siheyuan","Yaodong"]}},"Q205697":{"t":"Kolkata's twin across the Hooghly, with a giant banyan","s":"Haora, or Howrah, faces Kolkata across the Hooghly River, and from above the two read as one city split by a brown ribbon of water. Bridges stitch them together, and the wide railway yards on this side mark Howrah Station, opened in 1854 and still one of India's busiest. Downstream, the botanic garden at Shibpur shelters the Great Banyan, a single tree grown into a small forest.","w":"The west-bank half of a city split by a brown river, with bridges across and wide railway yards on this side.","f":["The Great Banyan's aerial roots have become thousands of trunks, and its canopy covers well over a hectare.","Howrah Bridge, opened in 1943, was built without nuts and bolts — it is held together by rivets."],"q":{"q":"What famous tree grows in the botanic garden at Howrah?","a":"The Great Banyan","x":["A giant sequoia","A baobab"]}},"Q38283":{"t":"A fishing village that became Japan's great port","s":"Yokohama lines the western shore of Tokyo Bay, and from above it flows into Tokyo without a visible border. Its waterfront is a jigsaw of piers, container terminals and reclaimed land, crossed by the long Bay Bridge. In 1859 this was a small fishing village chosen as a port for foreign trade; within decades it had become Japan's gateway to the world.","w":"A port city on the west side of a large bay, merging into Tokyo; piers, reclaimed land and a bridge over the harbour.","f":["Japan's first railway, opened in 1872, ran between Tokyo's Shimbashi and Yokohama.","Yokohama is Japan's second most populous city, after Tokyo.","Its Chinatown is the largest in Japan."],"q":{"q":"In which year was Yokohama opened to foreign trade?","a":"1859","x":["1603","1905"]}},"Q244898":{"t":"A ring-shaped city wrapped around Taipei","s":"New Taipei is shaped like a ring: it completely surrounds Taipei, filling the rest of the basin and spilling over green mountains to Taiwan's northern coast. Look for the mouth of the Tamsui River, where an old harbour town faces the sunset, and the rocky capes of the far north. With nearly four million people, it is the most populous city in Taiwan.","w":"A band of city and green hills encircling Taipei, reaching the sea at a river mouth and at rocky northern capes.","f":["The Spanish built the first Fort San Domingo at Tamsui in 1628.","Fuguijiao, the northernmost point of Taiwan's main island, lies within the city.","Yehliu, on its north coast, is famous for mushroom-shaped rocks carved by the sea."],"q":{"q":"Who built the first Fort San Domingo at Tamsui in 1628?","a":"The Spanish","x":["The Portuguese","The French"]}},"Q16520":{"t":"A port city wedged between steep hills and the sea","s":"Busan is squeezed into the gaps between steep green ridges and a ragged, bay-cut coastline at Korea's south-eastern tip. To the west, the Nakdong River ends in a delta of sandbars and reed beds that shelters migrating birds. Long bridges leap across the bays. On a clear day, Japan's island of Tsushima is visible about 50 km offshore.","w":"A long city threaded between steep ridges along an indented coast; a river delta with sandbars to the west.","f":["Busan is South Korea's largest port and one of the busiest container ports in the world.","In the Joseon era, Busan hosted a walled Japanese trading post known as the Waegwan."],"q":{"q":"Which Japanese island can be seen from Busan on clear days?","a":"Tsushima","x":["Okinawa","Sado"]}},"Q74881":{"t":"A peninsula-tip port between two seas","s":"Dalian occupies the hilly southern tip of the Liaodong Peninsula, where the Bohai Sea meets the Yellow Sea. Its coast is cut into bays, headlands and harbours. Russia founded the modern city in 1898 under the name Dalny, meaning “distant”, and laid it out with round plazas and streets radiating from them like spokes.","w":"A hilly peninsula tip with a jagged coast of bays and harbours; open sea on both sides.","f":["Dalian's harbour stays largely free of ice in winter, a rarity in northern China.","Lüshun, once known as Port Arthur, sits at the very tip of the peninsula."],"q":{"q":"Under what name did Russia found Dalian in 1898?","a":"Dalny","x":["Port Arthur","Nikolaevsk"]}},"Q11751":{"t":"Castle city on a plain of three parallel rivers","s":"Nagoya spreads over the flat Nōbi Plain at the head of Ise Bay. Just to the west, three big rivers — the Kiso, Nagara and Ibi — run side by side to the sea like parallel strokes of a brush. Offshore to the south, the airport sits on its own man-made island. Tokugawa Ieyasu had Nagoya Castle built in the 1610s, and the city grew up around it.","w":"A large city on a flat plain at the head of a bay; three parallel rivers to the west, an airport island to the south.","f":["Atsuta Shrine in Nagoya is said to house the Kusanagi sword, one of Japan's three imperial regalia.","Toyota's home city lies just east of Nagoya, making the region the heart of Japan's car industry."],"q":{"q":"Which legendary sword is said to be kept at Atsuta Shrine?","a":"Kusanagi","x":["Masamune","Muramasa"]}},"Q28729":{"t":"Where Vasco da Gama reached India, under coconut palms","s":"Kozhikode sits on the Malabar Coast, a city half-hidden under a thick green blanket of coconut palms between the Arabian Sea and the rising Western Ghats. As Calicut, it was the busy capital of the Zamorin kings and a hub of the pepper trade. In 1498 Vasco da Gama landed nearby, completing the first sea voyage from Europe to India.","w":"A city under a dense green canopy of palms along a straight coast, with mountains rising to the east.","f":["Calico, a plain cotton cloth, takes its name from Calicut.","Zheng He's Chinese treasure fleets called at Calicut in the early 15th century."],"q":{"q":"Which cloth takes its name from Calicut?","a":"Calico","x":["Muslin","Chintz"]}},"Q171771":{"t":"Temple City at the edge of the Mahanadi delta","s":"Bhubaneswar sits on slightly raised red-earth land at the south-western edge of the Mahanadi delta, where green paddies stretch towards the Bay of Bengal. Hundreds of stone temples once stood here, and it is still called the Temple City. The modern town was planned from 1948 as the new capital of Odisha, right beside the ancient one.","w":"A city on low reddish uplands, with the green, river-laced delta spreading east and north towards the coast.","f":["The Lingaraja Temple, the city's greatest, dates largely from the 11th century.","The rock-cut caves of Udayagiri and Khandagiri, on the western edge, were carved for Jain monks over 2,000 years ago.","Chilika, India's largest coastal lagoon, lies about 100 km to the south-west."],"q":{"q":"What is Bhubaneswar's nickname?","a":"Temple City","x":["Pink City","Garden City"]}},"Q200054":{"t":"India's steel city, planned around a single works","s":"Jamshedpur grew around a steel plant founded in 1907 on the Chota Nagpur Plateau, where iron ore, coal and water lay close together. From above, look for the dark block of the steelworks near the meeting of the Subarnarekha and Kharkai rivers, with green planned neighbourhoods around it. The forested Dalma Hills to the north shelter wild elephants.","w":"A planned town where two rivers meet, a large dark industrial block at its centre, forested hills to the north.","f":["The city is named after Jamsetji Tata, who dreamed up the steelworks but died before it was built.","It was one of India's first planned industrial cities."],"q":{"q":"Jamshedpur is named after which industrialist?","a":"Jamsetji Tata","x":["G. D. Birla","Walchand Hirachand"]}},"Q5753":{"t":"Timur's blue-domed capital in a green Silk Road oasis","s":"Samarkand is a green oasis in the valley of the Zarafshan, whose name means “gold-scattering”, with brown mountain ridges rising to the south. On the city's northern edge lies Afrasiyab, a bare, empty mound that was the city for its first two thousand years. Timur made Samarkand his capital in 1370 and filled it with turquoise domes.","w":"A green oasis in a wide river valley, brown ridges to the south, a bare earthen mound on the northern edge.","f":["Timur's grandson Ulugh Beg built a great observatory here in the 1420s.","At around 2,700 years old, Samarkand is one of the oldest cities in Central Asia.","From the 8th century, Samarkand paper was prized across the Islamic world."],"q":{"q":"Which ruler made Samarkand his capital in 1370?","a":"Timur","x":["Genghis Khan","Babur"]}},"Q9310":{"t":"A small capital between steep mountains and a reef island","s":"Dili is a low, compact city on a narrow strip of flat land along Timor's north coast, with steep mountains rising close behind it. Across the strait, about 25 km to the north, lies Atauro Island, fringed with coral reefs. The Portuguese moved their colonial capital here in 1769, and Portuguese is still one of the country's official languages.","w":"A small city on a thin coastal plain, steep mountains behind, a long island across the water to the north.","f":["Surveys around Atauro have recorded some of the highest reef-fish diversity anywhere.","Timor lies in Wallacea, the transition zone between Asian and Australian wildlife."],"q":{"q":"When did the Portuguese move their Timor capital to Dili?","a":"1769","x":["1669","1869"]}},"Q37806":{"t":"A small harbour capital among reefs and lagoons","s":"Port Vila curls around a sheltered harbour on the south-west coast of Efate, one of about 80 islands of Vanuatu. From above, look for the turquoise of reefs and lagoons against dark green hills, and the small islets in the bay. Until 1980 these islands were the New Hebrides, ruled jointly by Britain and France — an unusual arrangement known as a condominium.","w":"A small town around a sheltered bay, turquoise reefs and lagoons along the shore, green hills behind.","f":["Vanuatu's national language, Bislama, is a creole largely based on English.","Land diving on Pentecost Island, to the north, is often described as the forerunner of bungee jumping."],"q":{"q":"What were Vanuatu's islands called before 1980?","a":"The New Hebrides","x":["New Caledonia","The Friendly Islands"]}},"Q9365":{"t":"A city named after a Monday market, below snowy peaks","s":"Dushanbe fills a broad green valley where the Varzob River tumbles out of the Hissar Range, whose snowy ridges rise just north of the city. A century ago this was a village known for its weekly bazaar, held on Mondays — and “dushanbe” is simply Tajik for Monday. East of here, the Vakhsh River is held back by some of the tallest dams on Earth.","w":"A green city in a wide valley at the foot of snowy mountains to the north, with a river flowing down through it.","f":["The Nurek Dam on the Vakhsh, about 75 km east, is around 300 m high.","From 1929 to 1961 the city was called Stalinabad."],"q":{"q":"What does “Dushanbe” mean in Tajik?","a":"Monday","x":["Market","Crossroads"]}},"Q1362":{"t":"A planned grid capital at the foot of the Margalla Hills","s":"Islamabad is one of the world's newer capitals, laid out from the 1960s on a neat grid of sectors, each with its own green belt. From above it looks tidy and leafy, pressed against the forested Margalla Hills — the first foothills of the Himalaya. Just south, its older twin Rawalpindi forms a much denser, busier sprawl.","w":"A neat grid of green sectors along the foot of wooded hills, with a lake to the east and a denser city to the south.","f":["Greek planner Constantinos Doxiadis drew up Islamabad's master plan.","Its sectors are named with a letter and a number, like squares on a chessboard.","Rawal Lake, on the eastern edge, is a reservoir that supplies water to both cities."],"q":{"q":"Who drew up Islamabad's master plan?","a":"Constantinos Doxiadis","x":["Le Corbusier","Edwin Lutyens"]}},"Q185453":{"t":"An ancient City of Saints on the hot Punjab plain","s":"Multan lies on the flat, irrigated plain of southern Punjab, with the braided Chenab River a few kilometres to the west. It is one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in South Asia, and its many Sufi shrines have earned it the name City of Saints. An old Persian couplet lists Multan's four gifts: dust, heat, beggars and graveyards.","w":"A large city on a flat plain of fields and canals, a wide braided river to the west, orchards around.","f":["The tomb of the Sufi saint Shah Rukn-e-Alam, built in the 14th century, is an octagonal brick landmark.","Multan is famous across Pakistan for its mangoes.","Blue-glazed tiles and pottery are a centuries-old local craft."],"q":{"q":"Multan is traditionally known as the City of…","a":"Saints","x":["Gardens","Lights"]}},"Q1640079":{"t":"A city of windcatchers on the banks of the Indus","s":"Hyderabad sits on a low limestone ridge on the east bank of the Indus, about 150 km upriver from Karachi. The river here is wide and braided, and a barrage beside the city spreads its water into canals that turn the desert green. The Kalhora ruler Ghulam Shah founded the city in 1768. Its old houses once bristled with rooftop windcatchers that funnelled breezes indoors.","w":"A city on a low ridge beside a wide, braided river, with a barrage across it and green canal-fed fields around.","f":["Hyderabad is known as the city of bangles for its many glass-bangle workshops.","The Kotri Barrage beside the city diverts Indus water into irrigation canals."],"q":{"q":"What did old houses in Hyderabad, Sindh, carry on their roofs?","a":"Windcatchers","x":["Water wheels","Bell towers"]}},"Q1113311":{"t":"A frontier city at the foot of the Khyber Pass","s":"Peshawar sits in a green, fertile basin ringed by bare brown mountains, just east of where the Khyber Pass climbs west towards Afghanistan. The Kabul River loops across the valley to the north. As Purushapura, it was the capital of the Kushan king Kanishka nearly two thousand years ago, and for centuries caravans rested here before or after the pass.","w":"A dense city on a green plain encircled by bare mountains, with a gap leading west into the hills.","f":["Kanishka raised a huge Buddhist stupa here, described by Chinese pilgrims as one of the tallest structures of its day.","The name of the old Qissa Khwani Bazaar means “storytellers' market”."],"q":{"q":"What was Peshawar called as the Kushan capital?","a":"Purushapura","x":["Pataliputra","Taxila"]}},"Q3037":{"t":"A valley that was once a lake, beneath the Himalaya","s":"Kathmandu fills an almost round valley about 1,400 m up, ringed by terraced green hills. Geologists confirm what legend says: the valley floor was once a lake, and its drained sediments made the soil here so fertile. On a clear day, a wall of snowy Himalayan peaks shines along the northern horizon.","w":"A round, densely built valley ringed by terraced hills; on clear days, a wall of snow peaks to the north.","f":["The valley holds seven groups of monuments on the UNESCO World Heritage list.","The city's name is traced to Kasthamandap, a wooden pavilion said to have been built from a single tree.","Everest, about 160 km to the east, is only some 2 km below your cruising altitude."],"q":{"q":"Legend and geology agree the Kathmandu Valley was once…","a":"A lake","x":["A desert","A glacier"]}},"Q36526":{"t":"A tropical capital on a surprisingly dry, brown coast","s":"Port Moresby wraps around a harbour on the south coast of New Guinea, and it looks surprisingly dry for the tropics: its hills turn brown and grassy in the dry season because they lie in a rain shadow. Coral reefs fringe the coast. Inland, the forested Owen Stanley Range rises to peaks of about 4,000 m. Captain John Moresby named the harbour in 1873 after his father.","w":"A town spread around a harbour and bays, brown grassy hills behind, reefs offshore and high mountains inland.","f":["Hanuabada, a village of houses on stilts over the harbour, is the traditional home of the Motu people.","Motu traders once sailed big lakatoi canoes along the coast on trading voyages called hiri."],"q":{"q":"Captain John Moresby named the harbour in 1873 after…","a":"His father","x":["His ship","Queen Victoria"]}},"Q40921":{"t":"A strip of town below Guadalcanal's green mountains","s":"Honiara is a thin strip of town along the north coast of Guadalcanal, squeezed between the sea and grassy foothills that climb towards jungle-covered mountains. The island's highest peaks, over 2,300 m, are the tallest in the Solomon Islands. Spanish navigator Álvaro de Mendaña reached these islands in 1568 and named them after King Solomon, hoping for gold.","w":"A narrow town along a coast, green hills and high mountains behind it, islands across the water to the north.","f":["The name Honiara is often said to come from a local phrase meaning “facing the south-east wind”.","Guadalcanal is named after a town in Andalusia, Spain, home of one of Mendaña's officers."],"q":{"q":"Who named the Solomon Islands in 1568?","a":"Álvaro de Mendaña","x":["Abel Tasman","James Cook"]}},"Q976":{"t":"Siberia's old university town, lace-carved in wood","s":"Tomsk sits on the high right bank of the Tom River, surrounded by dark taiga that stretches to every horizon. Cossacks founded a fort here in 1604 on the orders of Tsar Boris Godunov. In 1888 the city opened the first university in Siberia, and it is still a student town. Many old wooden houses are decorated with carvings as delicate as lace.","w":"A city on the high east bank of a winding river, surrounded by dark coniferous forest; white in winter.","f":["Tomsk State University, opened in 1888, was the first university in Asian Russia.","The city keeps hundreds of historic wooden houses with carved window frames."],"q":{"q":"When did Tomsk open Siberia's first university?","a":"1888","x":["1755","1918"]}},"Q7978":{"t":"Russia's easternmost town, at the mouth of a tundra river","s":"Anadyr is a small town on a point of land at the mouth of the Anadyr River, where it opens into a wide estuary of the Bering Sea. Around it lies treeless tundra on permafrost, white for much of the year. It is the easternmost town in Russia, just two and a half degrees west of the 180th meridian, and about 10,000 people live here.","w":"A small cluster of town on a point of land at a wide estuary, surrounded by flat, treeless tundra.","f":["The town was founded in 1889 under the name Novo-Mariinsk.","The estuary is frozen for much of the year, from autumn well into early summer."],"q":{"q":"What was Anadyr called when it was founded in 1889?","a":"Novo-Mariinsk","x":["Novo-Arkhangelsk","Nikolaevsk"]}},"Q887":{"t":"An ironworks town in the Urals, on the edge of Asia","s":"Yekaterinburg spreads across the low, forested hills of the Middle Urals, just east of the watershed traditionally taken as the border between Europe and Asia. It began in 1723 as an ironworks, and the dam built to power it still holds back the City Pond in the centre. The city is named after Catherine I, wife of Peter the Great.","w":"A big city among low forested hills, a dammed pond on the river in its centre and many lakes around.","f":["Monuments marking the Europe–Asia border stand on the ridge less than 20 km west of the city.","From 1924 to 1991 the city was called Sverdlovsk."],"q":{"q":"Yekaterinburg is named after which empress?","a":"Catherine I","x":["Catherine II","Elizabeth"]}},"Q183002":{"t":"An oil town among bogs and lakes on the great Ob","s":"Surgut stands on the right bank of the Ob, in the middle of the West Siberian Plain — one of the flattest, boggiest places on Earth, speckled with countless lakes. Around it lie some of Russia's largest oilfields; look for straight roads linking square well pads across the swamp. At night, isolated gas flares glow in the darkness.","w":"A city on a broad river amid endless bogs and lakes, crossed by straight roads; flares glowing at night.","f":["Founded in 1594, Surgut is one of the oldest Russian towns in Siberia.","Its bridge over the Ob, opened in 2000, hangs from a single tall pylon."],"q":{"q":"In which year was Surgut founded?","a":"1594","x":["1794","1961"]}},"Q6014":{"t":"A silver town where ribbons of pine cross the steppe","s":"Barnaul stands on the high left bank of the Ob, where fields and steppe begin to replace the taiga. Look for a strange pattern south-west of the city: long, narrow strips of dark pine forest running straight across the farmland, like ribbons laid on a table. The town grew from 18th-century smelting works, and for decades it produced most of Russia's silver.","w":"A city on the high bank of a wide river; long, straight dark strips of pine forest running diagonally across the fields.","f":["The ribbon pine forests follow ancient channels carved by meltwater, and some stretch for hundreds of kilometres.","The first smelting works here was set up in the 1730s by the industrialist Akinfiy Demidov."],"q":{"q":"Which river does Barnaul stand on?","a":"The Ob","x":["The Irtysh","The Yenisei"]}},"Q883":{"t":"Siberia's biggest city, born where a railway crossed the Ob","s":"Novosibirsk straddles the Ob, and its reason to exist is a bridge: in 1893 the Trans-Siberian Railway crossed the river here, and a settlement sprang up beside the works. In barely a century it became the largest city in Siberia. South of the city, a dam has turned the Ob into a reservoir some 200 km long that locals call the Ob Sea.","w":"A big city on both banks of a wide river crossed by several bridges; a vast, long reservoir stretches south.","f":["The city was called Novonikolayevsk until 1926.","Akademgorodok, a science town founded in the 1950s, lies in the pine forest beside the Ob Sea."],"q":{"q":"What was Novosibirsk called when it was founded?","a":"Novonikolayevsk","x":["Nikolsk","Novoarkhangelsk"]}},"Q35381":{"t":"A harbour city with brand-new land built into the sea","s":"Colombo lines the flat west coast of Sri Lanka, its harbour sheltered behind long breakwaters. Right beside it, look for a large, geometric block of new land pushed out into the Indian Ocean — a whole district built on reclaimed sand. Portuguese, Dutch and British traders each held the port in turn. Inland, green hills rise towards the mountains at the island's heart.","w":"A flat coastal city, a harbour behind breakwaters and a geometric block of reclaimed land jutting into the sea.","f":["Sri Lanka's official capital, Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte, borders Colombo on its eastern side.","Colombo's harbour is man-made: work on its first great breakwater began in 1875."],"q":{"q":"Which city is Sri Lanka's official capital, next to Colombo?","a":"Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte","x":["Kandy","Anuradhapura"]}},"Q1467":{"t":"Where Spain's story in the Philippines began","s":"Cebu City lies on the east coast of a long, narrow, mountainous island, facing flat Mactan Island across a slim channel crossed by bridges; the airport sits on Mactan. Magellan's expedition arrived here in 1521 and planted a cross that is still venerated. In 1565 Miguel López de Legazpi founded the first Spanish settlement in the archipelago on this shore.","w":"A city on the east coast of a long, hilly island, facing a flat island with an airport across a narrow channel.","f":["On Mactan in 1521, Magellan fell in a clash with the chief Lapu-Lapu, now honoured as a national hero.","Cebu is regarded as the oldest city in the Philippines."],"q":{"q":"In which year did Magellan's expedition plant a cross at Cebu?","a":"1521","x":["1492","1565"]}},"Q459787":{"t":"An old sugar port facing a mango island","s":"Iloilo spreads over flat land on the south coast of Panay, facing the hilly island of Guimaras across a strait only a few kilometres wide. Rivers and fish ponds break up the city's edges. When the port opened to world trade in 1855, sugar from nearby Negros poured through it, and grand merchant houses rose along its streets.","w":"A flat city on a coast facing a hilly island across a narrow strait, with rivers and fish ponds around.","f":["Guimaras, across the strait, is famous for its sweet mangoes.","Miag-ao Church, about 40 km to the west, is a UNESCO-listed Baroque church of yellow stone from the 18th century."],"q":{"q":"Iloilo faces which island across a narrow strait?","a":"Guimaras","x":["Mindoro","Bohol"]}},"Q1473":{"t":"A gulf city beneath the highest peak in the Philippines","s":"Davao spreads along the shore of the Davao Gulf in south-eastern Mindanao, with the green island of Samal just across the water. To the west rises Mount Apo, a volcanic peak of 2,954 m and the highest mountain in the Philippines. The land in between is carpeted with banana and fruit plantations, and Davao is famous for its durian.","w":"A long city on the shore of a wide gulf with an island just offshore; a large volcanic mountain to the west.","f":["Davao is the largest city in the Philippines by land area.","The rare Philippine eagle nests in the forests around Mount Apo."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest mountain in the Philippines?","a":"Mount Apo","x":["Mayon","Mount Pulag"]}},"Q132732":{"t":"A town beside one of the world's largest man-made lakes","s":"Bratsk sits beside an enormous reservoir that fills the valleys of the Angara and its tributaries, reaching through the taiga in long, branching arms. The dam, finished in the 1960s, powered one of the largest hydroelectric stations of its time. The town itself is scattered in separate districts along the shores. A Cossack fort stood here as early as 1631.","w":"A huge, many-armed reservoir winding through dark taiga, with a dam and scattered districts of town along its shores.","f":["The Bratsk Reservoir holds about 170 km³ of water, one of the largest reservoirs in the world by volume.","When it was completed, the Bratsk hydroelectric station was the most powerful in the world."],"q":{"q":"A dam on which river created the Bratsk Reservoir?","a":"The Angara","x":["The Lena","The Yenisei"]}},"Q6576":{"t":"The gateway to Baikal, on the lake's only outflow","s":"Irkutsk lies on both banks of the Angara, the only river that flows out of Lake Baikal, some 60 km upstream. A dam just above the city has widened the river into a long reservoir. On a clear day, look south-east for Baikal itself: a deep-blue crescent framed by mountains, the deepest lake on Earth. Irkutsk began as a fort in 1661 and grew rich on tea and furs.","w":"A city on both banks of a wide river with a reservoir upstream; the long, deep-blue arc of Lake Baikal to the south-east.","f":["Lake Baikal is over 1,600 m deep and holds about a fifth of the world's unfrozen surface fresh water.","Many Decembrists exiled after 1825 settled in Irkutsk and shaped its cultural life."],"q":{"q":"How many rivers flow out of Lake Baikal?","a":"One","x":["Three","More than 300"]}},"Q919":{"t":"A Yenisei city where the river never freezes","s":"Krasnoyarsk lines both banks of the mighty Yenisei, with forested hills and rocky outcrops rising to the south. Upstream, a huge dam releases water that is warmer than the winter air, so the river through the city stays open and steams even at −30 °C. The city began in 1628 as a Cossack fort named after a red clay cliff on the riverbank.","w":"A large city on both banks of a very wide river, forested hills and rock pillars to the south; in winter, open water steaming.","f":["Stolby, a reserve of giant granite pillars in the taiga, begins right on the city's southern edge.","The Krasnoyarsk Dam, about 30 km upstream, is one of the most powerful hydroelectric stations in Russia.","The city's Communal Bridge appears on the Russian 10-rouble banknote."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Krasnoyarsk refer to?","a":"A red riverbank cliff","x":["A beautiful spring","A red forest"]}},"Q678299":{"t":"An Arctic outpost where the Yenisei meets the Kara Sea","s":"Dikson is a tiny settlement on bare, rocky shores where the vast gulf of the Yenisei opens into the Kara Sea. For much of the year both sea and land are white, and the town is little more than a scatter of buildings on the tundra. Only about a thousand people live here, far above the Arctic Circle, where the sun does not rise for weeks in midwinter.","w":"A tiny scatter of buildings on a rocky Arctic shore at the mouth of a huge, ice-covered gulf.","f":["Nordenskiöld named the harbour in 1875 after Oscar Dickson, the Swedish merchant who funded his expeditions.","The polar night here lasts about two and a half months.","Its port has served ships on the Northern Sea Route."],"q":{"q":"Oscar Dickson, after whom Dikson is named, came from which country?","a":"Sweden","x":["Norway","Denmark"]}},"Q53139":{"t":"A sunny Transbaikal city between low forested ridges","s":"Chita lies in a broad valley where the Chita River meets the Ingoda, framed by low ridges of larch and pine. Transbaikalia is one of Russia's sunniest regions, and in winter the ground often stays bare under a hard, dry frost. From 1827 many exiled Decembrists were held here, and the wives who followed them helped turn a small fort into a town.","w":"A city in a wide valley between low wooded ridges, where two rivers meet; a large lake on its western edge.","f":["The Trans-Siberian Railway passes through Chita on its way to the Pacific.","The sun shines here for well over 2,000 hours a year."],"q":{"q":"Which famous exiles were held in Chita from 1827?","a":"The Decembrists","x":["The Streltsy","The Old Believers"]}},"Q959":{"t":"The Trans-Siberian's end, on a hilly peninsula of bays","s":"Vladivostok climbs over the hills of a peninsula between two bays of the Sea of Japan, and its harbour, the Golden Horn, was named after Istanbul's. Look south for the long, fine line of a bridge to Russky Island. The city was founded in 1860, and it is the eastern end of the Trans-Siberian Railway, about 9,300 km from Moscow.","w":"A hilly city on a peninsula between two bays, a curved harbour in the middle and a long bridge south to an island.","f":["The Russky Bridge, opened in 2012, had the longest cable-stayed span in the world: 1,104 m.","The name joins the Russian words for “rule” and “east”."],"q":{"q":"Vladivostok's harbour is named after which bay in Istanbul?","a":"The Golden Horn","x":["The Silver Horn","The Golden Gate"]}},"Q1993488":{"t":"A remote river port near the delta of the Yana","s":"Nizhneyansk — “Lower Yana” — is a tiny settlement on the tundra near where the Yana River splits into a delta and meets the Laptev Sea. From above, the land around is freckled with countless thaw lakes, frozen and white for most of the year. Only a few hundred people remain in what was once a busier river port on this remote Arctic coast.","w":"A tiny settlement on flat tundra dotted with lakes, near a river breaking into many channels towards the sea.","f":["Far upstream on the Yana lies Verkhoyansk, one of the coldest inhabited places in the Northern Hemisphere.","On the lower Yana, archaeologists have found traces of people living in the Arctic about 32,000 years ago."],"q":{"q":"Which famously cold town lies upstream on the Yana?","a":"Verkhoyansk","x":["Oymyakon","Norilsk"]}},"Q6610":{"t":"The coldest big city, on a river kilometres wide","s":"Yakutsk sits on the west bank of the Lena, which here spreads into a maze of channels and islands several kilometres wide. It is often called the coldest major city on Earth: January averages near −40 °C, and ice fog can hang over the streets for days. The whole city stands on permafrost, so most buildings rest on piles driven deep into the frozen ground.","w":"A city on the west bank of an extremely wide, island-studded river, flat taiga all around; ice fog in winter.","f":["Cossacks led by Pyotr Beketov founded a fort on the Lena here in 1632.","The Lena Pillars, a UNESCO-listed wall of rock towers, stand beside the river about 200 km upstream."],"q":{"q":"Why do most buildings in Yakutsk stand on piles?","a":"Permafrost","x":["Floods","Earthquakes"]}},"Q500895":{"t":"A polar port beside the vast fan of the Lena Delta","s":"Tiksi is a small Arctic port on a bay of the Laptev Sea, backed by bare, low mountains. Just to the west spreads one of the great sights of the north: the Lena Delta, a fan of hundreds of channels and thousands of lakes, around 30,000 km² in all. For most of the year the sea here is frozen, and in midwinter the sun stays below the horizon for weeks.","w":"A small port on an icy bay; to the west, a gigantic delta fanning out into countless channels and lakes.","f":["The Lena Delta is the largest delta in the Arctic and a protected nature reserve.","Tiksi has served ships on the Northern Sea Route since the 1930s."],"q":{"q":"Which river's vast delta lies just west of Tiksi?","a":"The Lena","x":["The Yenisei","The Kolyma"]}},"Q7968":{"t":"A port between two bays of the Sea of Okhotsk","s":"Magadan sits on a neck of land between two bays of the Sea of Okhotsk, Nagaev and Gertner, with bare, rounded mountains rising behind it. In winter the sea freezes and the coast turns a uniform white. For most travellers this is where the Kolyma region begins: a vast land of mountains, gold-bearing rivers and larch forest stretching inland.","w":"A city on a narrow neck of land between two bays, bare hills behind; sea ice along the coast in winter.","f":["The Kolyma Highway links Magadan with Yakutsk, about 2,000 km away.","The Kolyma basin inland has been one of Russia's main gold-mining regions since the 1930s."],"q":{"q":"Which sea does Magadan stand on?","a":"The Sea of Okhotsk","x":["The Bering Sea","The Sea of Japan"]}},"Q230628":{"t":"A mining city beside a giant open-pit crater","s":"Erdenet lies among rolling green hills in northern Mongolia, and next to it you can't miss the mine: a vast, terraced grey pit and a broad, pale tailings pond. The town was built from 1974 to serve one of the largest copper deposits in Asia. Its name means roughly “with treasure”.","w":"A small city in green steppe hills, beside a huge terraced grey pit and a large pale tailings pond.","f":["Besides copper, the Erdenet mine produces molybdenum, a metal used to harden steel.","Copper concentrate is one of Mongolia's main exports."],"q":{"q":"What is mainly mined at Erdenet?","a":"Copper","x":["Coal","Gold"]}},"Q23430":{"t":"The world's coldest capital, in a steppe valley","s":"Ulaanbaatar stretches east–west along the valley of the Tuul River, with the forested ridge of Bogd Khan Mountain rising to the south and pale ger districts spilling up the hills to the north. Around it the treeless steppe rolls to the horizon. With an average yearly temperature below freezing, it is the coldest national capital on Earth.","w":"A long city in a wide river valley, a dark forested mountain to the south, bare rolling steppe all around.","f":["The city began in 1639 as a moving monastery camp and only settled on this spot in 1778.","Bogd Khan Mountain has been protected since the 18th century, making it one of the oldest nature reserves in the world.","The name means “red hero”; the city took it in 1924."],"q":{"q":"Among the world's capitals, Ulaanbaatar is the…","a":"Coldest","x":["Highest","Driest"]}},"Q695360":{"t":"A tiny Koryak capital near the Okhotsk shore","s":"Palana is a small settlement in the valley of the Palana River, a few kilometres from the Sea of Okhotsk on Kamchatka's remote north-west coast. Around it lies a wild country of tundra, dwarf pine and snow-streaked ridges rising towards the Sredinny Range. It is the administrative centre of the Koryak district, homeland of the Koryak people, reindeer herders and sea hunters.","w":"A small settlement in a river valley near the sea, amid tundra and low mountains; snow lingers on the ridges into summer.","f":["Palana was the capital of the Koryak Autonomous Okrug until it merged into Kamchatka Krai in 2007.","The settlement is so remote that most visitors arrive by plane."],"q":{"q":"Palana lies in the homeland of which people?","a":"The Koryaks","x":["The Chukchi","The Nenets"]}},"Q7951":{"t":"A harbour city at the foot of two smoking volcanoes","s":"Petropavlovsk-Kamchatsky lines the shore of Avacha Bay, one of the finest natural harbours in the world, and behind it rise two giant cones: Koryaksky, about 3,456 m, and the often steaming Avachinsky. No road links the city to the rest of Russia; everything comes by sea or air. Vitus Bering founded it in 1740 and named it after his two ships.","w":"A city along a wide, nearly enclosed bay, with two big snow-streaked volcanic cones right behind it.","f":["Bering's ships were the St Peter and the St Paul — hence the city's name.","Kamchatka has around 30 active volcanoes, and several can be seen from the city on a clear day."],"q":{"q":"Bering named the city after his two ships: the St Peter and the…","a":"St Paul","x":["St Andrew","St Nicholas"]}},"Q7253":{"t":"Home of rendang, between the ocean and the volcanoes","s":"Padang lies on a narrow coastal plain in West Sumatra, pressed between the Indian Ocean and the steep green wall of the Barisan Mountains, with volcanoes rising inland. It is the main city of the Minangkabau people, known for houses whose roofs curve up like buffalo horns. Their cooking, from rendang to fiery curries, is served in “Padang restaurants” all over Indonesia.","w":"A city on a thin coastal plain, steep green mountains behind, the open ocean in front and islands offshore.","f":["The Minangkabau are among the largest matrilineal societies in the world: land passes down through the women.","Legend says the name Minangkabau means “victorious buffalo”."],"q":{"q":"Among the Minangkabau, family land passes down through…","a":"The mothers","x":["The fathers","The eldest sons"]}},"Q9361":{"t":"A leafy grid city beneath a wall of snowy peaks","s":"Bishkek lies on the flat floor of the Chuy Valley, laid out on a green grid of tree-lined streets. Immediately to the south the Kyrgyz Ala-Too range rises like a wall, its peaks above 4,000 m snowy for most of the year. The city grew from Pishpek, a clay fortress built by the Khanate of Kokand in 1825.","w":"A green, gridded city on a flat valley floor, with a steep wall of snowy mountains just to the south.","f":["From 1926 to 1991 the city was called Frunze.","Ala Archa National Park, a gorge of glaciers and juniper, begins only about 40 km south of the centre.","The Chuy River, north of the city, forms part of the border with Kazakhstan."],"q":{"q":"What was Bishkek called from 1926 to 1991?","a":"Frunze","x":["Pishpek","Verny"]}}},"history":{"via-appia":{"name":"Via Appia","era":"from 312 BC","t":"The Romans called it the queen of roads","s":"Beneath you runs the first of Rome's great highways, begun in 312 BC and pushed south until it reached the Adriatic port of Brindisi. Soldiers, traders and travellers all used it to reach the ships for Greece and the East. The paving stones are far too small to see, but south of Rome modern roads still trace its dead-straight line across the flat Pontine plain.","w":"Look for a ruler-straight road running south-east from Rome across a flat, chequered plain towards the coast at Terracina.","f":["It was begun by the censor Appius Claudius Caecus, who gave the road his name.","Near Rome the old road is still lined with tombs and umbrella pines, and parts of the Roman paving survive.","From Brindisi, ships crossed to Dyrrachium, where the Via Egnatia continued east towards Byzantium."],"q":{"q":"Which port was the final stop of the Via Appia?","a":"Brindisi","x":["Naples","Ostia"]}},"via-egnatia":{"name":"Via Egnatia","era":"2nd c. BC – Middle Ages","t":"Rome's highway across the Balkans to the Bosphorus","s":"Here Rome reached for the East. Built in the 2nd century BC, the Via Egnatia ran from the Adriatic coast of today's Albania over the mountains to Thessaloniki and on to Byzantium. It was the natural sequel to the Via Appia: sail across from Brindisi, then walk. The road itself is invisible from cruise, but you can follow its line past Lake Ohrid and along the Aegean shore.","w":"Find Lake Ohrid among the mountains, then the Gulf of Thessaloniki to the east; the road linked the two and ran on towards Istanbul.","f":["It was named after the Roman proconsul Gnaeus Egnatius, who had it built.","From the Adriatic to Byzantium the road covered roughly 1,100 km.","The Apostle Paul travelled along it on his way through Philippi and Thessaloniki."],"q":{"q":"Which lake did the Via Egnatia pass on its way east?","a":"Lake Ohrid","x":["Lake Balaton","Lake Scutari"]}},"via-francigena":{"name":"Via Francigena","era":"from the 9th–10th c.","t":"A medieval pilgrim road from Canterbury to Rome","s":"You are over the road that medieval pilgrims walked from England to Rome. In 990 Archbishop Sigeric of Canterbury wrote down each overnight stop on his way home from the Pope, and his list still defines the route. It crosses France, climbs the Alps at the Great St Bernard Pass and runs down through Tuscany. From above, trace it by the lakes, passes and river plains it threads between.","w":"Look for Lake Geneva, then the high Alpine notch of the Great St Bernard to its south, and the flat Po plain beyond.","f":["Sigeric's travel notes list 79 stages between Rome and the English Channel.","The Great St Bernard Pass, at 2,469 m, is the highest point on the route.","Pilgrims still walk it today, collecting stamps in a pilgrim passport."],"q":{"q":"Who recorded the stages of the Via Francigena in 990?","a":"Archbishop Sigeric","x":["Charlemagne","Pope Gregory the Great"]}},"camino-frances":{"name":"Camino de Santiago (Camino Francés)","era":"from the 9th c.","t":"A thousand years of footsteps to Santiago","s":"Below you runs Europe's best-loved pilgrim road. For more than a thousand years walkers have crossed the Pyrenees here and headed west across northern Spain to the tomb of St James in Santiago de Compostela, about 780 km away. From the air the route is easy to imagine: green mountains at both ends, and between them the Meseta, a high, flat plateau of golden wheat around Burgos and León.","w":"Look for the wide tawny plateau between Burgos and León, with the green Galician hills rising to the west.","f":["The scallop shell, symbol of St James, marks the way on walls and signposts.","In 1987 it became the first Cultural Route of the Council of Europe.","Hundreds of thousands of people now finish the pilgrimage each year."],"q":{"q":"Which symbol marks the Camino de Santiago?","a":"A scallop shell","x":["An olive branch","An oak leaf"]}},"amber-road":{"name":"Amber Road","era":"Bronze Age – Roman times","t":"Baltic gold carried south to Rome","s":"Imagine the Baltic shore as a gold mine. Amber washed up on its beaches, and for thousands of years traders carried it south through the Moravian Gate to the Danube and down to Aquileia on the Adriatic, where Roman workshops turned it into jewellery. The route itself left no line you can see, but its two ends are clear: the long sandy spits of the Baltic and the northern Adriatic coast.","w":"At the northern end, look for the thin sandy spits and lagoons of the Baltic coast near Gdańsk and Kaliningrad.","f":["Amber is fossilised tree resin, and Baltic amber is roughly 40 million years old.","Pliny the Elder tells of a Roman knight sent by Nero to the Baltic to buy amber in bulk.","Most of the world's mined amber still comes from the coast near Kaliningrad."],"q":{"q":"What is amber?","a":"Fossilised tree resin","x":["Petrified sea foam","Volcanic glass"]}},"hadrians-wall":{"name":"Hadrian's Wall","era":"AD 122 – early 5th c.","t":"Rome's northern frontier, coast to coast","s":"You are crossing the narrow neck of northern England, where Emperor Hadrian ordered a stone wall in AD 122. It ran from the Tyne to the Solway Firth, with small forts every Roman mile and garrisons drawn from all over the empire. The wall is only a few metres wide and cannot be seen from cruise, but the gap it guarded, between two estuaries, is plain.","w":"Trace the land from the Tyne estuary at Newcastle westward to the broad silver Solway Firth beyond Carlisle.","f":["The wall was about 117 km long, or 80 Roman miles.","Letters on wooden tablets found at nearby Vindolanda include a birthday party invitation from about AD 100.","It became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1987."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how long was Hadrian's Wall?","a":"About 117 km","x":["About 40 km","About 450 km"]}},"limes-germanicus":{"name":"Upper Germanic–Raetian Limes","era":"c. AD 100 – 260","t":"Rome's frontier line from the Rhine to the Danube","s":"Below you ran the edge of the Roman world. From the Rhine near Koblenz to the Danube near Regensburg, a palisade, ditch and hundreds of watchtowers marked where the empire ended and free Germania began. Much of it lies in forest today, invisible from any height. What you can see are the two great rivers it joined, and the wooded hills of the Taunus and Odenwald between them.","w":"Find the Rhine and the Danube; the frontier cut across the wooded uplands between them in a long, bent line.","f":["The frontier ran about 550 km and had some 900 watchtowers and 60 forts.","One section runs almost perfectly straight for about 80 km, ignoring hills and valleys.","At Saalburg near Frankfurt, a Roman fort was rebuilt around 1900 on its original foundations."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers did this Roman frontier connect?","a":"The Rhine and the Danube","x":["The Rhine and the Elbe","The Main and the Oder"]}},"danube-limes":{"name":"Danube Limes","era":"1st – 5th c.","t":"The river that was Rome's frontier","s":"For four centuries this river was a border. The Romans lined the Danube with forts and watchtowers, and several of today's capitals grew from their camps: Vienna, Budapest and Belgrade among them. Unlike most Roman frontiers, this one is easy to follow from the air, because the river itself was the line. Watch for the gorge of the Iron Gates, where the Danube squeezes through the Carpathians.","w":"Follow the broad Danube; the narrow Iron Gates gorge between Serbia and Romania is the most striking stretch.","f":["Vienna grew from the Roman camp of Vindobona, and Budapest from Aquincum.","Trajan's Bridge across the Danube, finished in AD 105, was over a kilometre long.","Its stone piers stood near today's Drobeta-Turnu Severin, and a few stumps still rise on the banks."],"q":{"q":"Which capital grew from the Roman camp of Vindobona?","a":"Vienna","x":["Budapest","Bratislava"]}},"hanseatic-route":{"name":"Hanseatic trade route (London–Novgorod)","era":"13th – 17th c.","t":"Merchant towns that ruled the northern seas","s":"You are over the old sea road of the Hanseatic League, a merchant alliance that for centuries controlled trade across the North Sea and the Baltic. Its ships carried cloth west to east, and furs, wax, grain and fish the other way, linking offices in London, Bruges, Bergen and Novgorod. Look for the Baltic coast below: the red-brick port towns along it were built on this trade.","w":"Follow the Baltic coast from Lübeck Bay eastward, with the long island of Gotland out to sea.","f":["Lübeck was the league's leading city, often called the Queen of the Hanse.","Hamburg's car plates still begin with HH, for Hansestadt Hamburg.","The Hanseatic Diet, the league's assembly, last met in 1669."],"q":{"q":"What does HH on Hamburg car plates stand for?","a":"Hansestadt Hamburg","x":["Hafen Hamburg","Hauptstadt Hamburg"]}},"via-domitia":{"name":"Via Domitia","era":"from 118 BC","t":"The first Roman road in Gaul, Italy to Spain","s":"Beneath you ran the first road Rome built in Gaul, laid out from 118 BC to link Italy with Spain. It climbed over the Alps at Montgenèvre, followed the Durance valley, crossed the Rhône near Beaucaire and ran along the coast plain past Nîmes and Narbonne to the Pyrenees. The road is invisible now, but the valleys, the river and the lagoon-fringed coast still show its path.","w":"Look for the Rhône delta and the chain of lagoons along the coast west of it, towards the Pyrenees.","f":["It was named after Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus, the Roman general who had it built.","In Narbonne a stretch of the original paving has been uncovered in the town's main square.","The Pont Julien, a Roman bridge near Apt, carried traffic on this route for about 2,000 years."],"q":{"q":"Which two lands did the Via Domitia link?","a":"Italy and Spain","x":["Italy and Britain","Gaul and Germania"]}},"canal-du-midi":{"name":"Canal du Midi","era":"1666 – 1681","t":"A 17th-century shortcut from Atlantic to Mediterranean","s":"Here a 17th-century engineer joined two seas. Pierre-Paul Riquet's canal runs from Toulouse to the Mediterranean, and with the Garonne it links the Atlantic to the Mediterranean without sailing round Spain. Its genius lies in the water supply, collected from the Montagne Noire and fed in at the canal's summit. At about 20 m wide it is too narrow to spot from cruise, but its corridor is clear.","w":"Look for the lowland corridor between the Pyrenees and the Massif Central, from Toulouse to the Étang de Thau lagoon.","f":["The canal is about 240 km long and still in use, now mostly by holiday boats.","At Béziers a staircase of eight locks lifts boats up a single hillside.","It became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1996."],"q":{"q":"Who built the Canal du Midi?","a":"Pierre-Paul Riquet","x":["Ferdinand de Lesseps","Gustave Eiffel"]}},"kiel-canal":{"name":"Kiel Canal","era":"opened 1895","t":"A shortcut that slices Germany's neck","s":"Look for a straight silver thread crossing the flat farmland of northern Germany. This is the Kiel Canal, opened in 1895 to link the North Sea with the Baltic so that ships no longer had to sail round Denmark. It runs 98 km from the Elbe estuary to Kiel. At about 100 m wide, it can be picked out from cruise height on a clear day.","w":"A thin straight waterline running north-east from the Elbe mouth at Brunsbüttel to the fjord at Kiel.","f":["By the number of ships, it is one of the busiest artificial waterways in the world.","The shortcut saves ships about 460 km compared with the voyage round Denmark.","At Rendsburg a transporter bridge from 1913 carries a gondola hanging beneath the railway."],"q":{"q":"Which peninsula does the Kiel Canal let ships avoid?","a":"Jutland","x":["Cotentin","Istria"]}},"orient-express":{"name":"Orient Express route","era":"1883 – 1977","t":"The legendary sleeper train from Paris to Constantinople","s":"Below you ran the most famous train in Europe. From 1883 the Orient Express carried passengers in sleeping cars from Paris towards Constantinople, and from 1889 all the way there by rail, through Munich, Vienna, Budapest and Belgrade. It made crossing half a continent feel like a stay in a grand hotel. From above, follow the Danube, then the Balkan valleys down to the Bosphorus.","w":"Follow the Danube past Vienna and Budapest, then valleys through Serbia and Bulgaria towards the Bosphorus.","f":["The train was created by the Belgian entrepreneur Georges Nagelmackers.","Istanbul's Sirkeci station, opened in 1890, was built as its terminus.","The through service from Paris to Istanbul ended in 1977."],"q":{"q":"Who founded the company that ran the Orient Express?","a":"Georges Nagelmackers","x":["George Pullman","Thomas Cook"]}},"bleriot-channel-1909":{"name":"Blériot's Channel flight (1909)","era":"25 July 1909","t":"The flight that shrank the English Channel","s":"On 25 July 1909 Louis Blériot took off from a field near Calais in a small monoplane and landed below Dover Castle about 37 minutes later. It was the first aeroplane flight across the English Channel, just six years after the Wright brothers first flew. He had no compass. The crossing you see below, with its white cliffs and busy shipping lanes, suddenly looked much narrower.","w":"Look for the white chalk cliffs at Dover facing the flat French coast near Calais, about 35 km apart.","f":["The flight won Blériot a £1,000 prize from London's Daily Mail.","A granite outline of his aeroplane marks the landing spot near Dover Castle.","His aircraft, the Blériot XI, is now in the Musée des Arts et Métiers in Paris."],"q":{"q":"How long did Blériot's Channel flight take?","a":"About 37 minutes","x":["About 12 minutes","About 2 hours"]}},"paris-meridian-arc":{"name":"Paris meridian arc (the metre survey)","era":"1792 – 1798","t":"Where the metre was measured on the ground","s":"You are crossing a line that gave the world the metre. In the 1790s two astronomers, Delambre and Méchain, measured the distance from Dunkirk to Barcelona by chains of triangles between church towers and hilltops. From it they fixed the metre as one ten-millionth of the distance from pole to equator. The line runs almost due north–south through Paris, invisible but exact.","w":"The arc runs nearly due north–south through Paris, from the Channel coast at Dunkirk to Barcelona.","f":["Delambre measured the northern half and Méchain the southern half, meeting at Rodez.","The result was cast as a platinum bar, the Metre of the Archives, in 1799.","They slightly misjudged the Earth's flattening, so their metre came out about 0.2 mm short."],"q":{"q":"Which unit was this survey meant to define?","a":"The metre","x":["The nautical mile","The kilogram"]}},"ancient-rome":{"name":"Ancient Rome","era":"753 BC – AD 476","t":"The city whose roads led everywhere","s":"Below you lies the heart of an empire that stretched from Scotland to Syria. At its peak ancient Rome was home to perhaps a million people, and its Forum, Colosseum and Pantheon still stand in the middle of the modern city. From cruise height the monuments merge into the sprawl, but the Tiber's lazy S-bends through the centre are the same river the city was founded on.","w":"Find the Tiber winding through the city in tight bends, about 25 km inland from the coast at Ostia.","f":["The Colosseum, opened in AD 80, could hold around 50,000 spectators.","The Pantheon's concrete dome, nearly 2,000 years old, is still the largest unreinforced one in the world.","By tradition, Rome was founded on 21 April 753 BC, and the city still celebrates that date."],"q":{"q":"In which year, by tradition, was Rome founded?","a":"753 BC","x":["509 BC","312 BC"]}},"pompeii":{"name":"Pompeii and Herculaneum","era":"buried AD 79, excavated since 1738","t":"Roman towns kept under ash for 1,700 years","s":"Look for the cone of Vesuvius above the Bay of Naples. In AD 79 it buried two Roman towns under ash and mud, and in doing so kept them almost intact: painted walls, bakeries, shop signs, even scribbled graffiti. Today they are the fullest picture we have of everyday Roman life. The streets are too small to see from here, but the volcano that made them famous is unmistakable.","w":"A broad volcanic cone with a dark crater, rising just east of the Bay of Naples; the ruins lie at its foot.","f":["Excavations at Pompeii began in 1748, and about a third of the town is still unexcavated.","Its walls are covered in ancient graffiti, from election slogans to jokes.","Charred papyrus scrolls from Herculaneum are now being read with X-ray scans and AI."],"q":{"q":"Which volcano buried Pompeii?","a":"Vesuvius","x":["Etna","Stromboli"]}},"acropolis-athens":{"name":"Acropolis of Athens","era":"5th c. BC","t":"The rock where Western architecture found its form","s":"In the middle of the Athens basin rises a flat-topped rock crowned with marble temples. The Parthenon was built on it in the 5th century BC, when Athens was experimenting with democracy, and its proportions have been copied on banks, museums and parliaments ever since. The temple itself is too small to pick out from cruise, but the city's bowl between the mountains and the sea is easy to see.","w":"Look for a sprawling pale city in a bowl of mountains, opening south-west onto the Saronic Gulf and Piraeus.","f":["The Parthenon was built between 447 and 432 BC and dedicated to Athena.","Its columns bulge slightly and its floor curves upward, so it looks perfectly straight to the eye.","The marble came from Mount Pentelicus, about 16 km away."],"q":{"q":"To which goddess was the Parthenon dedicated?","a":"Athena","x":["Hera","Artemis"]}},"delphi":{"name":"Delphi","era":"8th c. BC – 4th c. AD","t":"The ancient Greeks' centre of the world","s":"Look for the massif of Mount Parnassus, often white on top, above the Gulf of Corinth. On its southern slope sat Delphi, where for a thousand years Greeks came to ask Apollo's oracle about wars, colonies and marriages. They believed it marked the centre of the world. The temple terraces are invisible from here, but the mountain and the silvery olive plain running down to the sea are clear.","w":"A high, often snowy mountain block north of the Gulf of Corinth, with a wide olive plain below its south side.","f":["A carved stone called the omphalos, or navel, marked Delphi as the centre of the world.","The Pythian Games held here included music and poetry contests as well as athletics.","Winners of the Pythian Games were crowned with laurel, the plant sacred to Apollo."],"q":{"q":"Which god's oracle spoke at Delphi?","a":"Apollo","x":["Zeus","Poseidon"]}},"olympia":{"name":"Olympia","era":"776 BC – AD 393","t":"Where the Olympic Games began","s":"In a green valley in the western Peloponnese, the ancient Greeks held the Olympic Games for more than a thousand years. Wars were paused so athletes could travel safely, and victors went home with an olive wreath and lasting fame. Every modern Olympic flame is still lit here from the sun's rays. From cruise, look for the wide Alfeios river valley winding to the Ionian Sea.","w":"Find the broad valley of the Alfeios river running west to the Ionian coast in the western Peloponnese.","f":["By tradition the first Games were held in 776 BC.","The stadium track is about 192 m long, the length of an ancient stade.","Its gold-and-ivory statue of Zeus was one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World."],"q":{"q":"What did ancient Olympic victors receive?","a":"An olive wreath","x":["A gold medal","A laurel wreath"]}},"knossos":{"name":"Knossos","era":"c. 1900 – 1350 BC","t":"The palace behind the legend of the labyrinth","s":"Just inland from Heraklion on Crete's north coast stood the greatest palace of Europe's first advanced civilisation, the Minoans. Its maze of rooms, light wells and painted walls may lie behind the Greek myth of King Minos, the labyrinth and the Minotaur. From above you won't see the ruins, but you can see Crete's long mountainous spine and the busy coast where the Minoans built their sea-trading world.","w":"On Crete's north coast, look for the city of Heraklion; the palace lies a few kilometres inland to its south.","f":["The British archaeologist Arthur Evans excavated Knossos from 1900.","Evans rebuilt parts of the palace in concrete, which is still debated today.","Clay tablets from Knossos written in Linear B turned out to be an early form of Greek."],"q":{"q":"Who excavated Knossos from 1900?","a":"Arthur Evans","x":["Heinrich Schliemann","Howard Carter"]}},"mycenae":{"name":"Mycenae","era":"c. 1600 – 1100 BC","t":"The citadel of Homer's King Agamemnon","s":"On a hill above the Argolid plain stood Mycenae, the fortress that gave its name to Greece's Late Bronze Age. Homer made it the home of King Agamemnon, leader of the Greeks at Troy. Its walls are built of boulders so huge that later Greeks thought giants had laid them. From cruise, look for the flat green plain of Argos opening onto its gulf.","w":"Find the Gulf of Argolis in the north-east Peloponnese; the citadel stands on the plain's northern edge.","f":["The walls are called Cyclopean, after the one-eyed giants said to have built them.","Its famous Lion Gate, carved around 1250 BC, is one of the oldest monumental sculptures in Europe.","The gold 'Mask of Agamemnon' found here in 1876 is about 300 years older than the Trojan War of legend."],"q":{"q":"What guards the main entrance to Mycenae?","a":"Two carved lions","x":["Two sphinxes","A bronze bull"]}},"stonehenge":{"name":"Stonehenge and Avebury","era":"c. 3000 – 1500 BC","t":"Stone circles aligned with the midsummer sun","s":"Below you, on the rolling chalk of Salisbury Plain, stands the world's most famous prehistoric monument. Stonehenge was built in stages over some 1,500 years; its axis points to sunrise at midsummer and sunset at midwinter. Nearby Avebury has an even larger stone circle, with a village built inside it. The stones are far too small to see from here, but the pale downland is easy to spot.","w":"Look for open, pale-green chalk downland north of Salisbury, with few trees and scattered military ranges.","f":["Its smaller bluestones were brought about 240 km from the Preseli Hills in Wales.","The biggest sarsen stones weigh around 25–30 tonnes each.","Avebury's outer circle is so big that part of the village sits inside it."],"q":{"q":"Where did Stonehenge's bluestones come from?","a":"The Preseli Hills, Wales","x":["Cornwall","The Lake District"]}},"carnac":{"name":"Carnac stones","era":"c. 4500 – 3300 BC","t":"Rows of standing stones marching across Brittany","s":"On the south coast of Brittany, thousands of standing stones stand in long parallel rows, some stretching more than a kilometre. They were raised in the Neolithic, long before Stonehenge's great sarsens, and nobody knows exactly why. Local legend says they are Roman soldiers turned to stone. The rows are too small to see, but the Quiberon peninsula and the island-studded Gulf of Morbihan are plain.","w":"Look for the thin Quiberon peninsula pointing south, with the island-filled Gulf of Morbihan just east of it.","f":["More than 3,000 standing stones survive around Carnac.","The Ménec alignment alone has over 1,000 stones in 11 rows.","The word 'menhir', for a standing stone, comes from Breton: 'long stone'."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how many standing stones survive at Carnac?","a":"Over 3,000","x":["About 300","About 30,000"]}},"lascaux":{"name":"Lascaux and the Vézère valley","era":"c. 17,000 years ago","t":"Ice Age horses and bulls painted in the dark","s":"Somewhere in the wooded valleys below, a cave holds some of the finest art of the Ice Age: horses, deer and huge black bulls painted about 17,000 years ago. It was found in 1940 by four local teenagers. The Vézère valley around it is packed with prehistoric sites. From the air you see only green limestone hills and a winding river, just as the painters did.","w":"Look for the meandering Vézère river among wooded limestone hills, north-east of the wider Dordogne valley.","f":["The cave holds about 600 paintings and around 1,500 engravings.","It closed to visitors in 1963 to protect the art; people now visit exact replicas nearby.","The Vézère valley has 147 prehistoric sites and 25 decorated caves on UNESCO's list."],"q":{"q":"Who found the Lascaux cave in 1940?","a":"Four teenagers","x":["A team of archaeologists","A shepherd"]}},"newgrange":{"name":"Newgrange (Brú na Bóinne)","era":"c. 3200 BC","t":"A tomb older than the pyramids, lit by midwinter sun","s":"In a great bend of the River Boyne, north of Dublin, stands a huge round mound faced with white quartz. Newgrange was built around 3200 BC, before Stonehenge's stone circle and the pyramids of Giza. Once a year, at the midwinter sunrise, a beam of light travels down its passage and lights the inner chamber. The mound is small from here, but the loop of the Boyne is visible.","w":"Find the River Boyne west of Drogheda; the monuments sit inside a tight southern loop of the river.","f":["The sunbeam enters through a 'roof-box' above the doorway and lasts about 17 minutes.","Its passage is about 19 m long and ends in a chamber with a stone corbelled roof.","Neighbouring Knowth has more megalithic art than any other site in western Europe."],"q":{"q":"What lights up Newgrange's chamber each year?","a":"The midwinter sunrise","x":["The midsummer sunset","The spring full moon"]}},"skara-brae":{"name":"Neolithic Orkney (Skara Brae)","era":"c. 3100 – 2500 BC","t":"A stone-age village with its furniture still in place","s":"On Orkney's main island, a Stone Age village lay hidden under sand until a storm uncovered it in 1850. Skara Brae's houses still have their stone beds, hearths and dressers, left as if their owners had just stepped out. Nearby stand the Ring of Brodgar and the great tomb of Maeshowe. From above, look for Orkney's green, treeless islands and the two lochs at their heart.","w":"Look for low green islands north of Scotland's tip; the monuments lie around two joined lochs on the largest one.","f":["Skara Brae was lived in for about 600 years.","At midwinter the setting sun shines down the entrance passage of Maeshowe.","The Ring of Brodgar originally had about 60 stones; around 27 still stand."],"q":{"q":"What uncovered Skara Brae in 1850?","a":"A storm","x":["A farmer's plough","A landslide"]}},"pont-du-gard":{"name":"Pont du Gard","era":"1st c. AD","t":"Rome's aqueduct bridge, three tiers high","s":"In the gorge of the Gardon river, a Roman bridge of three stacked arcades carried water to the city of Nîmes. It was one part of an aqueduct about 50 km long that fell only a few metres over its whole length, a feat of surveying. The bridge is too small to see from cruise, but the green Gardon gorge between Nîmes and Avignon is where to look.","w":"Look between Nîmes and Avignon, where the Gardon river cuts a green gorge before reaching the Rhône.","f":["At about 49 m, it is the tallest aqueduct bridge the Romans built.","The aqueduct drops only about 12 m over its 50 km, around 25 cm per kilometre.","The stones were laid without mortar in the main arches."],"q":{"q":"Which Roman city did the Pont du Gard supply?","a":"Nîmes","x":["Arles","Marseille"]}},"mont-saint-michel":{"name":"Mont-Saint-Michel","era":"from 966","t":"An abbey on a rock, cut off by the tide","s":"Look for a vast grey-and-silver bay on the Normandy–Brittany border. In its middle sits a tiny rocky island topped by a Gothic abbey, founded as a Benedictine monastery in 966. The bay has some of the biggest tides in Europe, and at high water the sea surrounds the rock. From cruise the abbey is a dot, but the patterned sandflats and river channels are striking.","w":"A broad bay of sandflats and winding channels where Normandy meets Brittany, with a small island near its southern shore.","f":["The tidal range in the bay can reach about 14 m.","In 2014 a light bridge replaced the old causeway so the sea can flow around the rock again.","It became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1979, with its bay."],"q":{"q":"How high can the tide rise in Mont-Saint-Michel Bay?","a":"Up to about 14 m","x":["Up to about 2 m","Up to about 40 m"]}},"aachen":{"name":"Aachen, Charlemagne's capital","era":"c. 800","t":"The spa town where Charlemagne ruled Europe","s":"Near the corner where Germany, Belgium and the Netherlands meet lies Aachen, Charlemagne's favourite residence. Around 800 he built a domed chapel here that became the heart of his empire, and for six centuries German kings were crowned in it. The Romans had come for the hot springs, which still flow. From above, look for the city on the edge of the green Eifel uplands.","w":"Look for the city just east of where Germany, Belgium and the Netherlands meet, at the edge of wooded uplands.","f":["About 30 German kings were crowned in Aachen between 936 and 1531.","In 1978 its cathedral was among the first 12 sites on UNESCO's World Heritage list.","The Roman name Aquae Granni referred to the town's hot springs."],"q":{"q":"Whose chapel became Aachen Cathedral?","a":"Charlemagne's","x":["Otto the Great's","Frederick Barbarossa's"]}},"ravenna":{"name":"Ravenna","era":"5th – 6th c.","t":"The last capital of the Western Roman Empire","s":"On the flat Adriatic coast of Italy, Ravenna was for a time the capital of the Western Roman Empire, then of Ostrogothic kings and Byzantine governors. They filled its churches with glittering mosaics that still look fresh after 1,500 years, among the finest anywhere. The city was once a harbour; the sea has since retreated. From above, look for the flat coast, pine woods and lagoons.","w":"Look for a low, straight coastline with dark pine forests and, to the north, the wide lagoons of Comacchio.","f":["The Western Roman emperors moved their court to Ravenna in 402.","The mosaics of San Vitale show Emperor Justinian and Empress Theodora with their courts.","The poet Dante died in Ravenna in 1321 and is buried there."],"q":{"q":"Which poet is buried in Ravenna?","a":"Dante","x":["Petrarch","Virgil"]}},"venice-republic":{"name":"Republic of Venice","era":"c. 697 – 1797","t":"A merchant republic built on a lagoon","s":"Look for a fish-shaped city floating in a lagoon, joined to the mainland by a long bridge. For about a thousand years Venice was an independent republic ruled by elected doges, and its galleys carried spices, silk and glass between Europe and the East. Few places are as easy to recognise from the air: the lagoon, the barrier islands and the city's dense tangle of canals.","w":"A compact fish-shaped city in a shallow lagoon, linked to the mainland by a long straight causeway.","f":["The city stands on more than 100 small islands linked by around 400 bridges.","Its Arsenal was one of the largest shipyards of the pre-industrial world.","Marco Polo, whose travels made China famous in Europe, was a Venetian merchant."],"q":{"q":"Who ended the Venetian Republic in 1797?","a":"Napoleon","x":["Louis XIV","Louis XVI"]}},"split-diocletian":{"name":"Diocletian's Palace, Split","era":"c. AD 305","t":"An emperor's retirement home that became a city","s":"When Emperor Diocletian gave up the throne around AD 305, he retired to a fortress-palace he had built on the Dalmatian coast. After Rome fell, local people moved inside its walls, and a whole town grew within them. Today shops, homes and a cathedral fill the Roman palace. From above, look for Split on its peninsula, facing the long islands of Brač and Hvar.","w":"A city on a peninsula of the Croatian coast, with the long islands of Brač and Hvar lying offshore.","f":["Diocletian was the first Roman emperor to step down of his own accord.","Split's cathedral was built in the emperor's own mausoleum.","Around 3,000 people still live inside the old palace walls."],"q":{"q":"Which emperor retired to a palace at Split?","a":"Diocletian","x":["Constantine","Hadrian"]}},"constantinople":{"name":"Constantinople","era":"AD 330 – 1453","t":"Imperial capital on two continents","s":"Below you is the city Constantine made the new Rome in 330. As Constantinople it was the capital of the Byzantine Empire for over a thousand years, and then of the Ottoman sultans, guarding the only sea route between the Black Sea and the Mediterranean. Its dome of Hagia Sophia amazed visitors for centuries. From the air, look for the Bosphorus and the curved Golden Horn.","w":"Look for the narrow Bosphorus strait and the curved inlet of the Golden Horn cutting into the old city.","f":["Hagia Sophia, finished in 537, was the world's largest cathedral for nearly a thousand years.","The city's land walls, built in the 5th century, ran about 6 km from sea to sea.","Before Constantine, the city was the Greek colony of Byzantium, founded around 660 BC."],"q":{"q":"Which emperor refounded Byzantium as his capital in 330?","a":"Constantine the Great","x":["Justinian","Theodosius"]}},"alhambra-granada":{"name":"Nasrid Granada and the Alhambra","era":"1238 – 1492","t":"A red palace-city under the snowy Sierra Nevada","s":"Look for the Sierra Nevada, often snow-capped, rising behind the city of Granada. Here the Nasrid dynasty ruled the last Muslim kingdom of the Iberian Peninsula until 1492, and built the Alhambra, a hilltop palace of carved stucco, tiles and courtyard fountains. The palace cannot be seen from cruise, but the mountain wall behind it, higher than any other in mainland Spain, is hard to miss.","w":"A long mountain range, white-topped in winter and spring, with Granada spread on the plain at its north-western foot.","f":["The name Alhambra comes from the Arabic for 'the red one', after the colour of its walls.","The fountain in the Court of the Lions rests on the backs of 12 marble lions.","Mulhacén in the Sierra Nevada, at 3,479 m, is the highest peak in mainland Spain."],"q":{"q":"What does the name 'Alhambra' mean?","a":"The red one","x":["The high one","The garden"]}},"cordoba-umayyad":{"name":"Umayyad Córdoba","era":"756 – 1031","t":"Once one of Europe's greatest cities","s":"In the 10th century Córdoba, on the Guadalquivir, was the capital of an Umayyad caliphate and one of the largest and most learned cities in Europe. Its Great Mosque, with a forest of columns and red-and-white striped arches, still stands, with a cathedral built right in the middle of it. From the air, look for the city on the river, with the dark Sierra Morena rising to the north.","w":"Find the Guadalquivir valley; the city lies on the river at the foot of the wooded Sierra Morena hills.","f":["The mosque's prayer hall has over 800 columns of marble, jasper and granite.","A Renaissance cathedral was built inside the mosque in the 16th century.","The ruined palace-city of Medina Azahara lies just west of Córdoba."],"q":{"q":"What was built inside Córdoba's Great Mosque?","a":"A cathedral","x":["A bullring","A royal palace"]}},"belem-lisbon":{"name":"Belém, Lisbon: the Age of Discovery","era":"15th – 16th c.","t":"The riverbank where the sea route to India began","s":"From this riverbank at the mouth of the Tagus, Portuguese ships set out in the 15th and 16th centuries to map Africa's coast and reach India by sea. Vasco da Gama sailed from here in 1497. Spice money paid for the ornate Jerónimos Monastery and the Belém Tower that guarded the harbour. From above, look for the broad Tagus estuary and the long red suspension bridge across it.","w":"Look for the wide Tagus estuary opening to the Atlantic, crossed by a long suspension bridge west of Lisbon's centre.","f":["Vasco da Gama is buried in the Jerónimos Monastery.","The Belém Tower was completed around 1519 to guard the harbour entrance.","The monastery's intricate style, called Manueline, uses carved ropes, shells and sea creatures."],"q":{"q":"Who sailed from Belém in 1497 to find a sea route to India?","a":"Vasco da Gama","x":["Ferdinand Magellan","Bartolomeu Dias"]}},"bergen-bryggen":{"name":"Bryggen, Bergen","era":"c. 1360 – 1754","t":"The Hanseatic wharf of wooden gables","s":"In the city of Bergen, surrounded by fjords and mountains, German merchants of the Hanseatic League ran one of their four great foreign offices for almost 400 years. They traded grain for stockfish, cod dried on racks in the Arctic wind. Their row of tall wooden gabled houses, Bryggen, still lines the harbour. From above, look for the city on its inlet, ringed by steep hills.","w":"A city on a sheltered inlet of Norway's west coast, ringed by steep hills and fjords, often under cloud.","f":["The stockfish came from the Lofoten Islands, hundreds of kilometres to the north.","Bryggen has burned several times and was rebuilt after a fire in 1702 in the old style.","It became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1979."],"q":{"q":"What did Bergen's Hanseatic merchants mainly export?","a":"Stockfish (dried cod)","x":["Amber","Silver"]}},"visby":{"name":"Visby","era":"12th – 14th c.","t":"A walled Hanseatic town in the middle of the Baltic","s":"On the west coast of Gotland, in the middle of the Baltic, stands Visby, a Hanseatic trading town still enclosed by its medieval wall. Its merchants grew rich on trade between Russia and western Europe, and filled the town with stone churches, many now roofless ruins. From the air, look for the long, low island of Gotland, the largest in Sweden.","w":"A long, low limestone island in the open Baltic; Visby lies on its western shore, facing Sweden.","f":["Visby's ring wall is about 3.4 km long and still has 27 of its towers.","Gotland has yielded more Viking-age silver hoards than anywhere else in Scandinavia.","The town became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1995."],"q":{"q":"On which island does Visby stand?","a":"Gotland","x":["Öland","Bornholm"]}},"thingvellir":{"name":"Þingvellir","era":"930 – 1798","t":"An open-air parliament in a crack between continents","s":"North-east of Reykjavík lies a lake in a rift valley where two tectonic plates pull apart. In 930 Iceland's settlers began meeting here each summer for the Althing, an open-air assembly that made laws and settled disputes. It gathered here until 1798. From above you can see what they could not: the lake and the long fissures running through the lava, where North America and Eurasia drift apart.","w":"Look for Iceland's largest natural lake, north-east of Reykjavík, with dark lava fissures running along its north shore.","f":["The Althing is often called one of the oldest parliaments in the world.","In the year 1000 the assembly here decided that Iceland would adopt Christianity.","Across Iceland, the two plates drift apart by about 2 cm a year."],"q":{"q":"In which year was the Althing founded?","a":"930","x":["874","1262"]}},"gutenberg-mainz":{"name":"Mainz, Gutenberg's press","era":"c. 1450","t":"Where movable type began Europe's print revolution","s":"Look for the point where the Main flows into the Rhine. On its west bank lies Mainz, where around 1450 Johannes Gutenberg began printing with movable metal type. His Bible, finished around 1455, showed books could be made in hundreds of copies instead of one at a time. Within 50 years printing presses were running in hundreds of European towns. The confluence below is easy to spot.","w":"Find the confluence of the Main and the Rhine, with Mainz on the west bank and Wiesbaden opposite.","f":["About 180 copies of the Gutenberg Bible were printed; 49 are known to survive.","Gutenberg's system combined metal type, oil-based ink and a press adapted from wine presses.","Mainz began as the Roman fortress of Mogontiacum."],"q":{"q":"Which book did Gutenberg print around 1455?","a":"The Bible","x":["The Iliad","The Quran"]}},"greenwich-meridian":{"name":"Greenwich prime meridian","era":"chosen in 1884","t":"Longitude zero, the line every map counts from","s":"On a hill above the Thames stands the Royal Observatory, founded in 1675. In 1884 delegates from 25 nations agreed that its meridian would be longitude zero for the world. Every position on your flight map is measured east or west of it, and world time was once counted from it. From above, look for the Thames looping round the Isle of Dogs; Greenwich lies on the south bank.","w":"Look for the Thames's tight loop around the Isle of Dogs in east London; Greenwich Park lies just south of it.","f":["The Royal Observatory was founded by King Charles II in 1675.","The zero line used by GPS lies about 100 m east of the brass line at the observatory.","The 1884 decision was made at a conference in Washington, D.C."],"q":{"q":"When was Greenwich chosen as the prime meridian?","a":"1884","x":["1675","1851"]}},"montgolfier-annonay":{"name":"Annonay, the first hot-air balloon","era":"4 June 1783","t":"Where two paper-makers launched the age of flight","s":"In the hills west of the Rhône lies Annonay, home of the Montgolfier brothers, paper-makers. On 4 June 1783 they filled a large cloth-and-paper envelope with hot air in the town square and watched it rise more than a kilometre. Within months their balloons carried a sheep, a duck and a rooster, and then the first human passengers. Every flight since traces back here.","w":"Look for the Rhône valley running south from Lyon; Annonay lies in the wooded hills a little to its west.","f":["The first human flight in a Montgolfier balloon took place over Paris on 21 November 1783.","At Versailles in September 1783, a sheep, a duck and a rooster flew and landed safely.","In French, a hot-air balloon is still called a 'montgolfière'."],"q":{"q":"What was the Montgolfier family's trade?","a":"Paper-making","x":["Silk weaving","Wine-making"]}},"ironbridge":{"name":"Ironbridge Gorge","era":"1709 – 1779","t":"A valley that helped start the Industrial Revolution","s":"In this wooded gorge of the River Severn, the Industrial Revolution found its fuel and its symbol. In 1709 Abraham Darby first smelted iron with coke instead of charcoal, making iron cheap. In 1779 his grandson raised the world's first major cast-iron bridge across the river. The bridge is too small to see from cruise, but the Severn's deep valley west of Telford is visible.","w":"Find the River Severn winding south-east from Shrewsbury; the gorge lies just south of the new town of Telford.","f":["The Iron Bridge has a span of about 30 m and is still standing.","Its parts were joined with carpentry techniques, such as dovetails, because iron building was so new.","The gorge became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1986."],"q":{"q":"What is the Iron Bridge made of?","a":"Cast iron","x":["Wrought iron","Steel"]}},"malta-temples":{"name":"Megalithic temples of Malta","era":"c. 3600 – 2500 BC","t":"Stone temples older than the pyramids","s":"On the small islands of Malta and Gozo, farmers of the Stone Age built great stone temples more than 5,000 years ago, earlier than Stonehenge's circle or the pyramids of Giza. They are among the oldest free-standing buildings on Earth. The temples are too small to see, but the islands themselves, golden-brown in the blue Mediterranean south of Sicily, are easy to find.","w":"Look for two small golden-brown islands, Malta and Gozo, about 90 km south of Sicily.","f":["Local legend says the Ġgantija temples on Gozo were built by a giantess.","The Ħal Saflieni Hypogeum is an underground temple and burial site cut into solid rock.","Seven of the temples are on the UNESCO World Heritage list."],"q":{"q":"Who, in local legend, built the Ġgantija temples?","a":"A giantess","x":["Phoenician sailors","The Knights of St John"]}},"etruria":{"name":"Etruria","era":"8th – 1st c. BC","t":"Land of the Etruscans, Rome's mysterious teachers","s":"Below you lies the land of the Etruscans, who ruled central Italy before Rome and taught the young city much of its religion, engineering and style. They built hill towns, drained marshes and painted their tombs with banquets, dancers and musicians. Their language is still only partly understood. From above, look for the green Tuscan hills and the round volcanic lakes that dot the land north of Rome.","w":"Look for round crater lakes such as Bolsena and Bracciano among green hills between Rome and Florence.","f":["Tuscany takes its name from the Etruscans, whom the Romans called Tusci.","The painted tombs of Tarquinia and Cerveteri are on the UNESCO World Heritage list.","Lake Bolsena fills an old volcanic caldera and is the largest volcanic lake in Europe."],"q":{"q":"Which Italian region is named after the Etruscans?","a":"Tuscany","x":["Umbria","Lombardy"]}},"magna-graecia":{"name":"Magna Graecia","era":"8th – 3rd c. BC","t":"When southern Italy was part of the Greek world","s":"From the 8th century BC, Greek settlers sailed west and founded cities along the coasts of southern Italy and Sicily, so many that the Romans called the region Greater Greece. Pythagoras taught here, and cities like Syracuse and Taranto rivalled Athens. From above, look for the boot's toe and heel and the deep Gulf of Taranto between them, the sea the colonists crossed.","w":"Look for the toe and heel of Italy's boot, with the wide Gulf of Taranto curving between them.","f":["Naples began as the Greek colony of Neapolis, 'the new city'.","The word 'sybarite', for a lover of luxury, comes from the rich colony of Sybaris.","The Doric temples at Paestum are among the best-preserved Greek temples anywhere."],"q":{"q":"What does Naples' Greek name, Neapolis, mean?","a":"New city","x":["Sea city","Holy city"]}},"nuragic-sardinia":{"name":"Nuragic Sardinia","era":"c. 1800 – 238 BC","t":"An island of thousands of Bronze Age stone towers","s":"Sardinia is dotted with stone towers called nuraghi, built by a Bronze Age people who left no writing. Thousands survive, from single cones to fortress-villages with walls and courtyards, and archaeologists still debate what they were for. The towers are too small to see from cruise, but the island's rugged granite mountains and empty uplands, where many of them stand, are clear below.","w":"A large, rugged, lightly populated island south of Corsica, with granite mountains in the east.","f":["More than 7,000 nuraghi are known across the island.","Su Nuraxi at Barumini, a nuraghe village, became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1997.","Nuragic smiths made small bronze figures of warriors, priests and animals."],"q":{"q":"What is a nuraghe?","a":"A stone tower","x":["A burial mound","A reed boat"]}},"al-andalus":{"name":"Al-Andalus","era":"711 – 1492","t":"Eight centuries of Muslim Iberia","s":"For almost eight centuries much of the Iberian Peninsula was al-Andalus, ruled by Muslim dynasties from Córdoba, Seville and Granada. Its scholars preserved and extended Greek learning, and its farmers brought rice, oranges and sugar cane to Europe. Christians, Muslims and Jews lived side by side, not always peacefully. From above, look for the Guadalquivir valley and the endless dotted rows of olive trees around it.","w":"Look for the broad Guadalquivir valley and hillsides covered in regular dotted rows of olive groves.","f":["The philosophers Averroes and Maimonides were both born in Córdoba in the 12th century.","The name of the region of Andalusia comes from al-Andalus.","Many Spanish words, such as 'aceite' (oil) and 'azúcar' (sugar), come from Arabic."],"q":{"q":"Which Spanish region takes its name from al-Andalus?","a":"Andalusia","x":["Aragon","Extremadura"]}},"dacia":{"name":"Dacia","era":"1st c. BC – AD 271","t":"The gold-rich mountain kingdom inside the Carpathians","s":"Inside the great curve of the Carpathians lay Dacia, a kingdom of mountain fortresses and gold mines. In AD 106 it became a Roman province under Trajan, and for the next 165 years Roman settlers mixed with the Dacians. Their Latin grew into Romanian. From above, look for the arc of the Carpathians, often snow-streaked, wrapped around the green Transylvanian plateau.","w":"Look for the curved chain of the Carpathians enclosing a broad, rolling plateau of farms and forest.","f":["The Dacian capital, Sarmizegetusa Regia, stood at about 1,200 m in the Orăștie Mountains.","Trajan's Column in Rome tells the story of the Roman campaigns in Dacia in a spiral of carved scenes.","The Romans withdrew from Dacia around AD 271."],"q":{"q":"Which language grew from the Latin spoken in Dacia?","a":"Romanian","x":["Hungarian","Albanian"]}},"sapmi":{"name":"Sápmi, land of the Sámi","era":"thousands of years – today","t":"Homeland of the Sámi, across four countries","s":"Below you stretches Sápmi, the homeland of the Sámi, the Indigenous people of the far north of Europe. It spans Norway, Sweden, Finland and Russia's Kola Peninsula. For centuries many Sámi followed reindeer between winter forests and summer pastures by the coast and in the fells, and some still do. From above you see their landscape: lakes, bogs, birch and pine, and long winter snow.","w":"Look for a vast land of lakes, low fells and forest, white for much of the year, north of the Gulf of Bothnia.","f":["Sámi National Day, 6 February, marks the first Sámi assembly held in Trondheim in 1917.","Norway, Sweden and Finland each have an elected Sámi Parliament.","The joik is a traditional Sámi song, often sung to evoke a person or a place."],"q":{"q":"What is a joik?","a":"A traditional Sámi song","x":["A reindeer sled","A winter tent"]}},"incense-route":{"name":"Incense Route","era":"7th c. BC – 2nd c. AD","t":"Camel caravans carrying frankincense to Rome","s":"Below you ran the road of frankincense and myrrh. Camel caravans carried these precious resins from the south of Arabia north along the Red Sea hills, oasis to oasis, to Petra and the Mediterranean port of Gaza. Temples across the ancient world burned incense, so the trade made the desert kingdoms rich. From above you see what the caravans crossed: bare mountains, lava fields and sand.","w":"Look for dark volcanic hills and sandy valleys running north–south inland from the Red Sea coast.","f":["Frankincense is the dried resin of a small desert tree, Boswellia.","The Nabataeans of Petra grew rich by taxing and guiding the caravans.","The caravan journey from southern Arabia to Gaza took around two months."],"q":{"q":"What is frankincense?","a":"A tree resin","x":["Ground seashells","Dried flower petals"]}},"royal-road":{"name":"Persian Royal Road","era":"5th c. BC","t":"The highway of the Persian kings, Sardis to Susa","s":"In the 5th century BC the Persian king Darius the Great linked the edges of his empire with a road about 2,700 km long, from Sardis near the Aegean to his capital at Susa. Relay stations with fresh horses lined the way, so royal messengers could cross it in about a week. From above, trace it across the Anatolian plateau, over the Euphrates and Tigris and down to the plains of Iran.","w":"Across eastern Turkey, look for the upper Euphrates and Tigris valleys the road crossed on its way to Mesopotamia.","f":["Herodotus wrote that travellers on foot needed about 90 days for the full journey.","He counted 111 relay stations along the road.","His praise of the couriers, that neither snow nor rain stayed them, is carved on a New York post office."],"q":{"q":"Which Persian king built the Royal Road?","a":"Darius the Great","x":["Cyrus the Great","Xerxes"]}},"suez-canal":{"name":"Suez Canal","era":"opened 1869","t":"A ruler-straight line joining two seas","s":"Look for a thin straight line cutting through the desert between the Mediterranean and the Red Sea. Opened in 1869, the Suez Canal spared ships the long voyage round Africa. It has no locks: the sea simply flows through, pausing in the Bitter Lakes halfway. From cruise height it is one of the clearest human-made lines on Earth, often with a convoy of ships strung along it.","w":"A straight blue line from Port Said south to Suez, widening into the Great Bitter Lake halfway along.","f":["The canal was built under the French diplomat Ferdinand de Lesseps.","It is about 193 km long and was later widened, with a parallel channel added along part of it in 2015.","It cut the sea route between Britain and India by about 40 percent."],"q":{"q":"Who led the building of the Suez Canal?","a":"Ferdinand de Lesseps","x":["Pierre-Paul Riquet","Isambard Kingdom Brunel"]}},"troy":{"name":"Troy","era":"c. 3000 BC – AD 500","t":"The city behind Homer's Iliad","s":"Below you, at the mouth of the Dardanelles, lies the mound of Troy. For thousands of years people rebuilt a city here, layer on layer, and one of those cities may be the Troy of Homer's Iliad. It guarded the sea route between the Aegean and the Black Sea. The mound is too small to see, but the narrow strait and the river plain beside it are clear.","w":"Look for the entrance of the Dardanelles strait; the site lies just inland on its Asian side, above a flat river plain.","f":["Archaeologists have found at least nine cities built on top of one another.","Heinrich Schliemann's 'Priam's Treasure' turned out to be about a thousand years older than Homer's Troy.","Troy became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1998."],"q":{"q":"Who began excavating Troy in the 1870s?","a":"Heinrich Schliemann","x":["Arthur Evans","Howard Carter"]}},"ephesus":{"name":"Ephesus","era":"10th c. BC – 15th c.","t":"A great Roman port now stranded far inland","s":"Ephesus was one of the largest cities of the Roman East, with a marble main street, a theatre for about 25,000 people and the grand Library of Celsus. It was a port, but the river silted up its harbour, and the coast now lies kilometres away. From above you can read that story: a flat green plain of river silt where the sea once came in, west of the town of Selçuk.","w":"Look for a flat green river plain west of Selçuk that runs out to the sea near Kuşadası.","f":["Its Temple of Artemis was one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World.","The front of the Library of Celsus, built in the 2nd century AD, has been rebuilt from its original stones.","Ephesus became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2015."],"q":{"q":"Which Wonder of the Ancient World stood at Ephesus?","a":"The Temple of Artemis","x":["The Colossus of Rhodes","The Pharos lighthouse"]}},"pergamon":{"name":"Pergamon","era":"3rd – 2nd c. BC","t":"A hilltop capital that gave its name to parchment","s":"On a steep hill above the town of Bergama stood Pergamon, capital of a rich Hellenistic kingdom. Its kings built a great library, a famous altar and one of the steepest theatres of the ancient world, cut into the slope. The library needed so much writing material that Pergamon became known for parchment. From above, look for the green plain of the Bakırçay river, inland from the Aegean.","w":"Look for the Bakırçay river plain about 25 km inland from the Aegean coast, north of İzmir.","f":["The word 'parchment' comes from the name of Pergamon.","The Pergamon Altar was taken to Berlin in the 19th century and gave its name to a museum there.","The theatre's seating drops down the hillside in about 80 rows."],"q":{"q":"Which writing material is named after Pergamon?","a":"Parchment","x":["Papyrus","Paper"]}},"sardis":{"name":"Sardis, city of the first coins","era":"7th – 6th c. BC","t":"Where money first became coins","s":"In this fertile valley stood Sardis, capital of the Lydian kingdom. Around the 7th century BC the Lydians began stamping lumps of electrum, a natural gold–silver alloy, into coins, one of the first uses of coinage anywhere. Their last king, Croesus, became a byword for wealth. The city later marked the start of the Persian Royal Road. From above, look for the broad green Gediz valley east of İzmir.","w":"Look for the broad green Gediz valley east of İzmir, with a mountain ridge rising along its southern side.","f":["The river Pactolus, which ran through Sardis, was said to carry gold dust.","The phrase 'as rich as Croesus' comes from the last king of Lydia.","The ruins include one of the largest ancient synagogues ever found."],"q":{"q":"Which king of Sardis became a byword for wealth?","a":"Croesus","x":["Midas","Darius"]}},"gobekli-tepe":{"name":"Göbekli Tepe","era":"c. 9500 – 8000 BC","t":"Temples built before farming began","s":"On a hill above the Harran plain, hunter-gatherers raised great stone pillars carved with foxes, snakes and vultures about 11,500 years ago. That is before pottery, before writing, and before farming had taken hold. Göbekli Tepe turned the old idea that villages came first and monuments later upside down. The pillars are invisible from cruise, but the wide, now irrigated plain around Şanlıurfa is easy to see.","w":"Look for the wide plain north of the Syrian border near Şanlıurfa, green in patches with irrigated fields.","f":["Its T-shaped pillars stand up to about 5.5 m tall.","It is some 6,000 years older than Stonehenge.","The site was recognised as prehistoric by the archaeologist Klaus Schmidt in 1994."],"q":{"q":"Who built Göbekli Tepe?","a":"Hunter-gatherers","x":["Bronze Age farmers","Roman engineers"]}},"catalhoyuk":{"name":"Çatalhöyük","era":"c. 7100 – 5700 BC","t":"A Stone Age town with no streets","s":"On the flat Konya plain stood one of the world's earliest large towns, home to thousands of people more than 9,000 years ago. Its mud-brick houses were packed wall to wall with no streets between them, so people walked across the rooftops and climbed down ladders into their homes. From above, the plain still looks as flat as a table, with the volcano Hasan Dağı rising to the east.","w":"Look for the vast flat Konya plain, with the lone twin-peaked volcano Hasan Dağı standing to its east.","f":["Houses were entered through a hole in the roof.","A wall painting found here may show a town with an erupting volcano behind it.","It became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2012."],"q":{"q":"How did people enter houses at Çatalhöyük?","a":"Through the roof","x":["Through side tunnels","Through front doors"]}},"hattusa":{"name":"Hattusa, the Hittite capital","era":"c. 1650 – 1200 BC","t":"Below you: the land where the Hittite empire rose","s":"On these bare hills of central Anatolia stood Hattusa, capital of the Hittites, who ruled much of the Bronze Age Near East and dealt as equals with Egypt. Its walls, gates carved with lions and sphinxes, and an archive of clay tablets survive. Among them is one of the oldest peace treaties known. From above, look for the great loop of the Kızılırmak river that wraps around the Hittite heartland.","w":"Look for the long arc of the Kızılırmak, Turkey's longest river, curving around dry hills east of Ankara.","f":["The Hittite king and Egypt's Ramesses II signed a peace treaty around 1259 BC; a copy hangs at UN headquarters.","Hittite is the oldest written Indo-European language known.","Archaeologists have found around 30,000 clay tablets at Hattusa."],"q":{"q":"Which pharaoh signed a peace treaty with the Hittites?","a":"Ramesses II","x":["Tutankhamun","Akhenaten"]}},"petra":{"name":"Petra","era":"4th c. BC – 7th c. AD","t":"A city carved into rose-red cliffs","s":"In the rugged mountains east of the Wadi Araba lies Petra, capital of the Nabataeans, who grew rich on the incense caravans. They carved temples and tombs straight into the rose-red sandstone and caught every drop of rain in channels and cisterns. You reach its most famous facade, the Treasury, through a narrow gorge. From cruise, look for the jagged, reddish mountain wall above the long, dead-flat rift valley.","w":"Look for jagged reddish mountains rising steeply east of the Wadi Araba, the flat rift valley between the Dead Sea and Aqaba.","f":["The gorge leading into Petra, the Siq, is about 1.2 km long and in places only a few metres wide.","The Swiss traveller Johann Ludwig Burckhardt brought Petra to Europe's attention in 1812.","A Victorian poem called it 'a rose-red city half as old as time'."],"q":{"q":"Who brought Petra to Europe's attention in 1812?","a":"Johann Ludwig Burckhardt","x":["Heinrich Schliemann","Richard Burton"]}},"palmyra":{"name":"Palmyra","era":"1st – 3rd c. AD","t":"The caravan city of the desert palms","s":"In the middle of the Syrian desert, a spring and a grove of palms made Palmyra a stop for caravans travelling between the Mediterranean and the Euphrates. Its merchants grew so rich that they built a long colonnaded avenue, grand temples and tower tombs, mixing Greek, Roman and Persian styles. From above, look for the green patch of the oasis, sharp against the pale desert around it.","w":"A small green oasis in a vast pale desert, at the foot of a line of low bare hills.","f":["In the 3rd century Queen Zenobia made Palmyra the centre of a short-lived empire.","Palmyrene merchants traded in silk, spices and perfumes from as far as China and India.","The city's own language was a dialect of Aramaic, written in its own script."],"q":{"q":"Which queen made Palmyra the heart of an empire?","a":"Zenobia","x":["Cleopatra","Dido"]}},"baalbek":{"name":"Baalbek","era":"1st c. BC – 3rd c. AD","t":"Temples built of some of the largest stones ever moved","s":"In the Beqaa valley, between two mountain ranges, the Romans built one of their grandest temple complexes, Heliopolis, today's Baalbek. Six towering columns of the Temple of Jupiter still stand, and the Temple of Bacchus beside them is among the best preserved in the Roman world. Its foundations include blocks of hundreds of tonnes each. From above, the long flat valley between two ridges is unmistakable.","w":"Look for a long, flat green valley running north–south between two parallel mountain ranges, often snow-capped in winter.","f":["Three stones in the temple's base, the Trilithon, weigh about 800 tonnes each.","Even larger blocks, never moved, still lie in the ancient quarry nearby.","The Temple of Jupiter's surviving columns are about 20 m tall."],"q":{"q":"About how heavy is each stone of Baalbek's Trilithon?","a":"About 800 tonnes","x":["About 8 tonnes","About 80 tonnes"]}},"qumran":{"name":"Qumran and the Dead Sea Scrolls","era":"c. 250 BC – AD 68; found 1947","t":"Caves that kept the oldest Bible manuscripts","s":"Look down at the Dead Sea, the lowest water on Earth. In 1947 a Bedouin shepherd, searching the cliffs on its north-western shore, found clay jars holding ancient scrolls. Over the next decade the caves around Qumran yielded hundreds of manuscripts, including the oldest known copies of books of the Hebrew Bible. The caves are invisible from here, but the sea and its steep cliffs are striking.","w":"Look for the Dead Sea's long blue northern basin and the steep bare cliffs along its western shore.","f":["The scrolls were written roughly between 250 BC and AD 68.","The Great Isaiah Scroll is over 7 m long and nearly complete.","The surface of the Dead Sea lies about 430 m below sea level."],"q":{"q":"Who found the first Dead Sea Scrolls in 1947?","a":"A Bedouin shepherd","x":["A British archaeologist","A travelling monk"]}},"babylon":{"name":"Babylon","era":"c. 1900 – 300 BC","t":"The ancient world's byword for a great city","s":"On the Euphrates south of Baghdad lie the ruins of Babylon. Under Hammurabi it gave the world one of its first written law codes; under Nebuchadnezzar II it became a city of blue-glazed gates and a towering ziggurat that may lie behind the story of the Tower of Babel. From above, look for the Euphrates and the dark ribbon of palm groves and fields that follows it through the desert.","w":"Look for the Euphrates winding through dry land, lined with dark green palm groves, about 85 km south of Baghdad.","f":["Hammurabi's law code, from about 1754 BC, was carved on a black stone stele now in the Louvre.","The blue-tiled Ishtar Gate has been rebuilt in a museum in Berlin.","Babylon became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2019."],"q":{"q":"Which king's law code came from Babylon?","a":"Hammurabi's","x":["Gilgamesh's","Sargon's"]}},"ur":{"name":"Ur","era":"c. 3800 – 500 BC","t":"A Sumerian city still watched over by its ziggurat","s":"In the desert of southern Iraq rises the stepped bulk of the Great Ziggurat of Ur, built more than 4,000 years ago. Ur was one of the richest cities of Sumer, a port on the Euphrates close to the Gulf. The river and the coast have since moved away, leaving it stranded in the sand. From cruise the ziggurat is a speck, but the edge of the green river plain against the desert is clear.","w":"Look for the boundary between the green Euphrates plain near Nasiriyah and the empty desert just south-west of it.","f":["The ziggurat was built by King Ur-Nammu around 2100 BC and partly restored in modern times.","Its royal tombs, dug in the 1920s, held gold helmets, lyres and the mosaic box called the Standard of Ur.","Tradition names 'Ur of the Chaldees' as the birthplace of Abraham."],"q":{"q":"Who built the Great Ziggurat of Ur?","a":"Ur-Nammu","x":["Nebuchadnezzar II","Hammurabi"]}},"uruk":{"name":"Uruk","era":"c. 4000 – 300 BC","t":"One of the first cities, where writing began","s":"Here, in what is now desert, stood Uruk, one of the first true cities in the world. By about 3000 BC tens of thousands of people lived inside its walls, and its temple officials began pressing marks into clay tablets to keep accounts: some of the earliest writing anywhere. Legend made Gilgamesh its king. From above, look for the old dry channels of the Euphrates wandering across the plain.","w":"Look for faint old river channels and the edge of green farmland in the desert east of Samawah.","f":["The earliest tablets from Uruk, from about 3300 BC, are mostly records of grain, beer and livestock.","Its city walls, credited in legend to Gilgamesh, ran about 9 km around.","The Epic of Gilgamesh is one of the oldest surviving works of literature."],"q":{"q":"Which legendary hero was king of Uruk?","a":"Gilgamesh","x":["Hammurabi","Sargon"]}},"nineveh":{"name":"Nineveh","era":"c. 700 – 612 BC (as capital)","t":"Assyria's capital and its great library","s":"On the east bank of the Tigris, opposite Mosul, lie the mounds of Nineveh, once the largest city in the world. Assyrian kings lined its palaces with carved stone reliefs and built canals and aqueducts to water its gardens. King Ashurbanipal gathered a library of clay tablets, and among them was the Epic of Gilgamesh. From above, look for the Tigris flowing south through a city on both banks.","w":"Look for the Tigris running north–south through the city of Mosul; the ancient mounds lie on its eastern bank.","f":["Ashurbanipal's library held tens of thousands of clay tablets, now mostly in the British Museum.","In 1872 a tablet from it was found to tell a flood story much like Noah's.","Sennacherib's canals to Nineveh crossed a valley on the Jerwan aqueduct, one of the oldest known."],"q":{"q":"Whose library at Nineveh held the Epic of Gilgamesh?","a":"Ashurbanipal's","x":["Nebuchadnezzar's","Darius's"]}},"persepolis":{"name":"Persepolis","era":"c. 518 – 330 BC","t":"The ceremonial capital of the Persian kings","s":"On a stone terrace at the foot of a mountain, the Persian kings built Persepolis to receive envoys from across their empire. Its stairways are carved with long processions of Medes, Egyptians, Indians and many others bringing gifts. It was burned in 330 BC after Alexander the Great took it, and its columns still stand among the ruins. From above, look for the wide Marvdasht plain north of Shiraz.","w":"Look for the broad, farmed Marvdasht plain about 60 km north-east of Shiraz, edged by bare mountains.","f":["Darius the Great began building Persepolis around 518 BC.","Its reliefs show envoys of more than 20 peoples bringing gifts to the king.","The tomb of Cyrus the Great stands at nearby Pasargadae."],"q":{"q":"Which king founded Persepolis?","a":"Darius the Great","x":["Cyrus the Great","Xerxes"]}},"yazd":{"name":"Yazd, city of windcatchers","era":"5th c. – today","t":"A mud-brick city that learned to live in the desert","s":"Between two great deserts lies Yazd, one of the oldest living cities of Iran. Its old town is built of mud brick, with towers called windcatchers that funnel cool air into the houses below. Water came from qanats, underground channels tunnelled from distant mountains. From cruise, look for a green oasis city on a vast beige plain, with the Shir Kuh mountains to its south-west.","w":"Look for an oasis city on a wide beige desert plain, with the high Shir Kuh mountains to the south-west.","f":["The historic city of Yazd became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2017.","A Zoroastrian fire temple in Yazd keeps a flame said to have burned since AD 470.","Qanat shafts show from the air as long dotted lines across the desert, best seen from lower altitude."],"q":{"q":"What is a badgir in Yazd?","a":"A windcatcher tower","x":["An ice house","A water mill"]}},"giza":{"name":"Pyramids of Giza","era":"c. 2600 – 2500 BC","t":"The last Wonder of the ancient world still standing","s":"Where the green Nile valley meets the desert, on the western edge of Cairo, stand the pyramids of Giza. The Great Pyramid of Khufu was the tallest human-made structure on Earth for about 3,800 years. Nearby Saqqara holds the step pyramid of Djoser, the first of them all. In clear air, look for three small pale triangles at the desert's edge, right beside the city.","w":"Look where Cairo's sprawl meets the desert to the south-west; the pyramids sit on the plateau's edge like pale specks.","f":["The Great Pyramid was originally about 146 m tall.","It is the only one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World that still stands.","Its sides face the four compass points with an error of only a fraction of a degree."],"q":{"q":"Which pharaoh built the Great Pyramid?","a":"Khufu","x":["Khafre","Ramesses II"]}},"thebes":{"name":"Thebes: Karnak and the Valley of the Kings","era":"c. 2000 – 1000 BC","t":"Temples on one bank, royal tombs on the other","s":"At Luxor the Nile divides two worlds. On the east bank stood Thebes, capital of Egypt's New Kingdom, with the vast temples of Karnak and Luxor. On the west bank, in desert valleys behind the cliffs, the pharaohs cut their tombs, including Tutankhamun's. From cruise the monuments are too small, but the contrast is striking: a narrow green valley, then sudden bare desert and cliffs.","w":"A thin green strip of fields along the Nile, with the city of Luxor on the east bank and pale cliffs to the west.","f":["Karnak is one of the largest religious complexes ever built.","More than 60 tombs have been found in the Valley of the Kings.","Howard Carter found Tutankhamun's nearly intact tomb there in 1922."],"q":{"q":"Who found Tutankhamun's tomb in 1922?","a":"Howard Carter","x":["Flinders Petrie","Auguste Mariette"]}},"abu-simbel":{"name":"Abu Simbel","era":"c. 1264 BC; moved 1964–1968","t":"Temples cut into a mountain, then moved","s":"Look for the huge blue sheet of Lake Nasser in the Nubian desert. On its shore sit the temples of Ramesses II, fronted by four seated giants, each about 20 m tall. When the Aswan High Dam was about to flood them in the 1960s, an international effort led by UNESCO cut the temples into blocks and rebuilt them higher up. The temples are too small to see, but the lake is vast.","w":"A long, branching blue lake in the desert near Egypt's southern border; the temples lie on its western shore.","f":["The temples were cut into over 1,000 blocks and rebuilt about 65 m higher.","Twice a year, the rising sun reaches deep inside to light statues in the inner sanctuary.","The rescue campaign helped inspire UNESCO's World Heritage list."],"q":{"q":"Why were the Abu Simbel temples moved?","a":"To escape a dam's lake","x":["To avoid earthquakes","To make way for a road"]}},"alexandria-ancient":{"name":"Ancient Alexandria","era":"331 BC – 7th c. AD","t":"The city of the Lighthouse and the Great Library","s":"Alexander the Great founded this city on the Mediterranean in 331 BC, and it became the capital of the Ptolemies and the scholarly centre of the ancient world. Its Great Library gathered books from everywhere, and its lighthouse on the island of Pharos was one of the Seven Wonders. From above, look for a long city stretched along the coast between the sea and a lake, west of the Nile delta.","w":"A city strung out along the coast for many kilometres, between the sea and Lake Mariout, west of the Nile delta.","f":["The Pharos lighthouse stood for centuries; a 15th-century fort now occupies its site.","Eratosthenes estimated the Earth's circumference here around 240 BC, remarkably well.","Cleopatra VII, the last Ptolemaic ruler of Egypt, reigned from Alexandria."],"q":{"q":"Who measured the Earth's size from Alexandria?","a":"Eratosthenes","x":["Archimedes","Pythagoras"]}}}}